// ---------- Catechism notes: Part One (continued), §§ 74–1065 ----------
function cccN(id,part,p,u,t,gist,sum,key,r,th,ask,q,psy){CCC_NOTES.push({id,part,p,u,t,sum,key,r,th,ask,q,psy});CCC_GIST[id]=gist;}
const CQ=(q,o,a,why)=>({q,o,a,why});
cccN("c15","p1s1c2a2","74–83","__PJ.HTM","Tradition and Scripture","God's revelation reaches us through Scripture and Tradition, which flow from one source and are handed on together.",
 `<p>God wants all people to come to the truth, so what he revealed in Christ had to be handed on whole to every generation. Christ commanded the apostles to preach the Gospel; they did so by their preaching, example and institutions (Tradition), and some of them, with others, wrote it down under the Holy Spirit (Scripture). To keep it intact, they left bishops as their successors.</p><p>Tradition and Scripture are not two sources but two ways of transmitting one deposit of faith, flowing from the same divine wellspring. Both are received with equal reverence. Apostolic Tradition (with a capital T) is distinct from the many local theological, liturgical or devotional traditions, which can change.</p>`,
 [["Apostolic Tradition","The living transmission of the Gospel from the apostles, through the Church, by the Holy Spirit."],["Deposit of faith","Everything God revealed, entrusted by the apostles to the whole Church."]],
 [["2 Thess 2:14: hold the traditions, by word or by letter","2-thessalonians,2,14,14"],["2 Tim 2:2: entrust it to faithful men","2-timothy,2,2,2"]],
 [["ST III q.25 a.3 ad 4","Thomas notes that the apostles, prompted by the Spirit, handed on to the churches many things they did not leave in writing, kept in the Church's practice."]],
 "Which do you lean on more naturally: the Bible or the Church's teaching? How does this section change that picture?",
 CQ("How do Scripture and Tradition relate?",["They are rival sources that often disagree","Two ways of handing on one deposit of faith, from the same source","Tradition replaces Scripture"],1,"They flow from the same divine wellspring and make up one sacred deposit (CCC 80, 97)."));
cccN("c16","p1s1c2a2","84–100","__PM.HTM","Who interprets the faith?","The whole Church holds the faith, but the bishops in communion with the Pope are its authentic interpreters, as servants of the Word.",
 `<p>The deposit of faith is entrusted to the whole Church: the faithful hold fast to it in the apostles' teaching, communion, the breaking of bread and prayer. The task of giving an authentic interpretation belongs to the Magisterium, the bishops in communion with the successor of Peter. The Magisterium is not above the Word of God but serves it, teaching only what has been handed on.</p><p>Dogmas are lights on the path of faith; there is an organic connection between our spiritual life and them. The whole body of the faithful, anointed by the Spirit, cannot err in belief when it agrees in faith and morals (the <i>sense of faith</i>). Understanding of revelation grows through contemplation and study, the experience of spiritual realities, and the preaching of the bishops.</p>`,
 [["Magisterium","The Church's living teaching office: the bishops in communion with the Pope."],["Sensus fidei","The supernatural sense of faith of the whole people of God."]],
 [["Acts 2:42: the apostles' teaching and fellowship","acts,2,42,42"],["Luke 10:16: he that heareth you heareth me","luke,10,16,16"]],
 [["ST II-II q.1 a.10","It belongs to the Pope to publish a new edition of the creed, so that the faith may be held by all as one."],["ST II-II q.5 a.3","Faith adheres to the First Truth as shown in Scripture and as taught by the Church."]],
 "What would happen to the Bible's meaning if there were no living authority to interpret it?",
 CQ("Is the Magisterium above the Word of God?",["Yes, it decides what is true","No, it serves the Word and teaches only what has been handed on","Only in matters of morality"],1,"The Magisterium is not superior to the Word of God but is its servant (CCC 86)."));
cccN("c17","p1s1c2a3","101–108","__PO.HTM","Scripture: God's word in human words","God is the author of Scripture, and he wrote it through human authors who were true authors too; so it teaches without error the truth God wanted put in writing for our salvation.",
 `<p>In Scripture God speaks to us in a human way, just as the eternal Word took on our flesh. Through all its words, God speaks only one Word: his Son. That is why the Church venerates the Scriptures as she venerates the Lord's Body, and nourishes the faithful from both.</p><p>God inspired the human authors: he used them with their own abilities, so that they wrote everything and only what he wanted. The books therefore teach firmly, faithfully and without error the truth God wanted recorded for our salvation. Christianity is not a "religion of the book" but of the living Word, Christ.</p>`,
 [["Inspiration","The Holy Spirit's action on the human writers so that God is the author of what they wrote."],["Inerrancy","Scripture teaches without error the truth God intended for our salvation."]],
 [["2 Tim 3:16-17: all scripture inspired of God","2-timothy,3,16,17"],["2 Pet 1:20-21: holy men spoke, inspired by the Spirit","2-peter,1,20,21"]],
 [["ST I q.1 a.10","God is the author of Scripture; since he can express meaning not only by words but by things themselves, Scripture has a literal and a spiritual sense."]],
 "How would you read the Bible differently if you remembered that God is speaking to you through it today?",
 CQ("Why does the Catechism say Christianity is not a \"religion of the book\"?",["Because the Bible is unimportant","Because it is the religion of the living Word, Christ","Because Tradition matters more"],1,"Our faith is in the Word incarnate and living, not a written and mute word (CCC 108)."));
cccN("c18","p1s1c2a3","109–119","__PQ.HTM","How to read the Bible","Read Scripture as one whole, within the living Tradition and the analogy of faith, attentive to both its literal and its spiritual senses.",
 `<p>To understand Scripture we must look for what the human authors really meant and what God wanted to reveal through their words. That means attending to literary forms, times and cultures. And because the same Spirit inspired it, it must be read in the Spirit in which it was written, by three criteria: the content and unity of the whole of Scripture; the living Tradition of the whole Church; and the analogy of faith (how the truths of faith hang together).</p><p>Tradition distinguishes the literal sense (what the words mean, the basis of the rest) from the spiritual senses: allegorical (how events point to Christ), moral (how we should act) and anagogical (how they lead us to eternity).</p>`,
 [["Literal sense","What the words mean as intended by the author; all other senses rest on it."],["Analogy of faith","The coherence of the truths of faith among themselves and within God's plan."]],
 [["Luke 24:25-27: beginning at Moses and all the prophets","luke,24,25,27"],["1 Cor 10:1-11: these things were done in a figure","1-corinthians,10,1,11"]],
 [["ST I q.1 a.10","Thomas's classic account of the four senses: literal, allegorical, moral and anagogical, with the literal as foundation."]],
 "Pick one Gospel scene. What is its literal meaning? What does it say to you morally? How does it point to heaven?",
 CQ("Which are the three criteria for reading Scripture \"in the Spirit\"?",["Feelings, reason and tradition","The unity of Scripture, the living Tradition, and the analogy of faith","History, archaeology and language"],1,"CCC 112–114 names these three."));
cccN("c19","p1s1c2a3","120–141","__PR.HTM","The canon, and the two Testaments","The Church discerned the canon: 46 books of the Old Testament and 27 of the New; both Testaments are God's word, and they explain each other.",
 `<p>Apostolic Tradition led the Church to recognise which books belong in Scripture: 46 in the Old Testament and 27 in the New. The Old Testament is indispensable: its books are inspired and keep their lasting value, preparing for Christ and teaching about God and humanity. The Gospels are the heart of Scripture, because they are the main witness to Jesus.</p><p>The two Testaments are a unity: the New lies hidden in the Old, and the Old is unveiled in the New (St Augustine). The Church urges all the faithful to read Scripture often, because "ignorance of the Scriptures is ignorance of Christ" (St Jerome).</p>`,
 [["Canon","The complete list of inspired books."],["Typology","Reading Old Testament persons and events as foreshadowing Christ."]],
 [["Luke 24:44: all things written of me in the law, the prophets and the psalms","luke,24,44,44"],["John 20:31: written that you may believe","john,20,31,31"]],
 [["ST I-II q.107 a.3","The New Law is contained in the Old as the tree in the seed."]],
 "Catholic Bibles have seven more Old Testament books than most Protestant Bibles. Which do you know? (Tobit, Judith, 1–2 Maccabees, Wisdom, Sirach, Baruch.)",
 CQ("How many books are in the Catholic canon?",["66","73","81"],1,"46 Old Testament books and 27 New Testament books: 73 in all (CCC 120)."));
cccN("c20","p1s1c3","142–152","__PU.HTM","The obedience of faith","Faith is our personal yes to God who reveals himself: trusting him with mind and will, as Abraham and Mary did.",
 `<p>By revelation the invisible God speaks to us as friends; the fitting response is faith. By faith we submit our intellect and will to God, freely. To obey (from the Latin <i>ob-audire</i>) is to "hear or listen to". Abraham is the model: he went out not knowing where. Mary is its most perfect embodiment: "be it done unto me according to thy word."</p><p>Faith is first a personal adherence to God, and at the same time a free assent to the whole truth he has revealed. It means believing in God, in his Son (we can believe in Jesus because he is God), and in the Holy Spirit, who reveals Jesus to us.</p>`,
 [["Obedience of faith","Freely submitting mind and will to God who reveals; literally, \"listening\"."]],
 [["Rom 16:26: for the obedience of faith","romans,16,26,26"],["Heb 11:8: Abraham went out, not knowing whither","hebrews,11,8,8"],["Luke 1:38: be it done to me","luke,1,38,38"]],
 [["ST II-II q.2 a.2","To believe has three aspects: believing that God exists, believing God (trusting him who speaks), and believing into God (tending towards him)."]],
 "Where in your life is God asking you, like Abraham, to go out \"not knowing whither\"?",
 CQ("What does \"obedience\" literally mean in Latin?",["To fear","To hear or listen to","To follow rules"],1,"Ob-audire: to hear or listen to (CCC 144)."));
cccN("c21","p1s1c3","153–165","__PX.HTM","What faith is like","Faith is a gift of grace and a truly human act: certain, seeking understanding, free, necessary for salvation, and a foretaste of heaven.",
 `<p>Faith is a gift: a supernatural virtue infused by God, with the interior help of the Spirit. Yet it is also an authentically human act, not contrary to freedom or reason. It is more certain than any human knowledge, because it rests on God who cannot lie, even if what it believes can seem obscure. Faith seeks understanding, and there can never be a real conflict between faith and reason, since the same God gives both.</p><p>Faith must be free: no one may be forced to believe. It is necessary for salvation. It can be lost, so we must nourish it with the word of God and beg the Lord to increase it. Faith is already the beginning of eternal life, even though we often walk in darkness and trial.</p>`,
 [["Theological virtue","A virtue given by God, directed to God: faith, hope and charity."],["Fides quaerens intellectum","\"Faith seeking understanding\" (St Anselm)."]],
 [["Mark 9:23: I do believe; help my unbelief","mark,9,23,23"],["Heb 11:1: faith is the substance of things hoped for","hebrews,11,1,1"]],
 [["ST II-II q.2 a.9","The act of believing is meritorious because it comes from the will moved by grace."],["ST II-II q.4 a.8","Faith is more certain than science or wisdom in its cause, God's truth, even if less clear to us."]],
 "Have you ever felt faith and reason pulling in different directions? What resolved it, or what would?",
 CQ("Can there be a true conflict between faith and reason?",["Yes, often","No, because the same God gives both","Only in science"],1,"Faith and reason both come from God, who cannot deny himself (CCC 159)."));
cccN("c22","p1s1c3","166–184","__PY.HTM","We believe: the faith of the Church","Faith is personal but never private: I believe because the Church believes, and I receive the faith from her as from a mother.",
 `<p>No one can believe alone, just as no one can live alone. We receive faith from others and hand it on. "I believe" is the faith of each believer; "We believe" is the faith of the Church, confessed by the bishops in council and by the assembly at Mass. The Church is the mother of our new birth, and we say with St Cyprian that no one can have God for Father who does not have the Church for Mother.</p><p>We do not believe in formulas but in the realities they express. Yet formulas matter: they let us express and hand on the faith. Across languages, cultures and centuries, the Church confesses one faith, received from one Lord.</p>`,
 [["Creed","A summary of the faith; from the Latin credo, \"I believe\"."]],
 [["Eph 4:4-6: one Lord, one faith, one baptism","ephesians,4,4,6"]],
 [["ST II-II q.1 a.2 ad 2","The act of the believer does not terminate in the statement but in the reality: the formulas express the truth we reach through them."]],
 "Who are the people through whom you received the faith? Thank God for one of them by name.",
 CQ("Why does the Catechism say faith is not private?",["Because it must be shown on social media","Because we receive it from the Church and believe with her","Because only priests have real faith"],1,"I believe because the Church believes; faith is received and handed on (CCC 166–169)."));
cccN("c23","p1s2c1","185–197","__P14.HTM","The creeds","The creeds are short summaries of the faith; the Apostles' and Nicene Creeds are the two most important.",
 `<p>From the beginning the Church expressed her faith in brief formulas, called creeds or "symbols" (from the Greek for a token of recognition). The Apostles' Creed is the ancient baptismal creed of the Church of Rome. The Nicene-Constantinopolitan Creed comes from the first two ecumenical councils (325 and 381) and is shared by the Churches of East and West.</p><p>The Catechism follows the Apostles' Creed, in three parts: God the Father and creation; Jesus Christ and redemption; the Holy Spirit and our sanctification. Reciting the Creed with faith is entering into communion with God and with the whole Church.</p>`,
 [["Symbol of faith","Another name for a creed: a sign by which believers recognise one another."]],
 [["1 Cor 15:3-5: I delivered unto you first of all","1-corinthians,15,3,5"]],
 [["ST II-II q.1 a.9","It was necessary to gather the truths of faith into a creed, so that they could be more easily proposed to all."]],
 "Pray the Creed slowly today, pausing after each line.",
 CQ("The Catechism's Part One follows which creed?",["The Athanasian Creed","The Apostles' Creed","The Creed of Pius IV"],1,"It follows the Apostles' Creed, supplemented by the Nicene (CCC 196)."));
cccN("c24","p1s2c1","198–231","__P16.HTM","I believe in God","There is only one God, who revealed his name, I AM, and who is Truth and Love.",
 `<p>The whole Creed rests on its first line. God revealed himself to Israel as the only God: "Hear, O Israel, the Lord our God is one Lord." To Moses he revealed his name, I AM WHO AM: the one who simply is, who is always faithful, and who is near. Out of reverence Israel did not pronounce it.</p><p>God is Truth: his words cannot deceive. God is Love: his love for Israel is like a father's, a mother's, a bridegroom's. Believing in one God changes everything: we recognise his greatness, live in thanksgiving, see the dignity of every person, use created things rightly, and trust him in every circumstance.</p>`,
 [["YHWH","The divine name revealed to Moses: \"I AM WHO AM\" (Exod 3:14)."]],
 [["Deut 6:4-5: Hear, O Israel","deuteronomy,6,4,5"],["Exod 3:13-15: I AM WHO AM","exodus,3,13,15"],["1 John 4:8: God is charity","1-john,4,8,8"]],
 [["ST I q.13 a.11","\"He who is\" is the most proper name of God, because God's essence is his very existence."],["ST I q.11 a.3","God is one, because he is infinite and simple."]],
 "Which consequence of believing in one God most needs to show in your week: gratitude, respect for others, or trust?",
 CQ("What name did God reveal to Moses?",["The Almighty","I AM WHO AM","The Holy One of Israel"],1,"Exod 3:14; CCC 206, 213."));
cccN("c25","p1s2c1","232–267","__P17.HTM","The Father, and the Holy Trinity","The central mystery of the faith: one God in three Persons, Father, Son and Holy Spirit.",
 `<p>Christians are baptised in the name (singular) of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit. The Trinity is the central mystery of Christian faith and life: God's own inner life, which we could never have known unless he revealed it.</p><p>Jesus reveals God as Father in a new sense: Father of his eternal Son. The Spirit is revealed as another Person, proceeding from the Father and the Son. The Church's councils used words like "substance", "person" and "relation" to say it precisely: we do not believe in three gods, but one God in three Persons, really distinct by their relations of origin. All God's works are common to the three, yet each shows what is proper to him. The goal of everything is that we enter the Trinity's perfect unity.</p>`,
 [["Consubstantial","Of one and the same substance: the Son is God as the Father is God."],["Person","In God, a distinct \"who\": Father, Son and Spirit, distinguished by their relations."]],
 [["Matt 28:19: in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Ghost","matthew,28,19,19"],["John 14:16-17: another Paraclete","john,14,16,17"],["2 Cor 13:13: the grace of our Lord","2-corinthians,13,13,13"]],
 [["ST I q.29 a.4","\"Person\" in God signifies a relation as subsisting."],["ST I q.32 a.1","The Trinity cannot be proven by reason; arguments for it only show it is not impossible."]],
 "Next time you make the Sign of the Cross, notice that you name three Persons and one Name.",
 CQ("How are the three divine Persons distinguished?",["By their power","By their relations of origin","By their roles in creation only"],1,"They differ only in relation to each other: begetting, being begotten, proceeding (CCC 254–255)."));
cccN("c26","p1s2c1","268–278","__P18.HTM","The Almighty","God's power is universal, loving and mysterious; he shows it most of all by forgiving.",
 `<p>Of all God's attributes, only omnipotence is named in the Creed. His power is universal (he made everything and rules everything), loving (he is our Father), and mysterious: it can seem absent when evil happens. Yet faith sees it most in the weakness of the Cross and the Resurrection.</p><p>Nothing is impossible with God. The liturgy says he shows his almighty power above all by having mercy and forgiving. Only faith can embrace the ways of this power, which is "made perfect in infirmity".</p>`,
 [["Omnipotence","God's power to do all things that are possible."]],
 [["Luke 1:37: no word shall be impossible with God","luke,1,37,37"],["2 Cor 12:9: power is made perfect in infirmity","2-corinthians,12,9,9"]],
 [["ST I q.25 a.3","God can do everything that does not involve a contradiction; whatever is truly possible."],["ST I q.25 a.3 ad 3","God's omnipotence is shown chiefly in sparing and having mercy."]],
 "Where do you find it hardest to believe God is almighty? Bring that place to him.",
 CQ("How does God show his almighty power \"above all\", according to the liturgy?",["By miracles","By mercy and forgiveness","By punishing evil"],1,"CCC 270, quoting the Collect of the 26th Sunday."));
cccN("c27","p1s2c1","279–324","__P19.HTM","God the Creator, providence and evil","God freely created everything from nothing, out of love, keeps it in being, and guides it by his providence; evil does not have the last word.",
 `<p>Creation is the foundation of all God's plans. God created freely, by his wisdom and love, from nothing, not from himself or from pre-existing matter. The world is ordered and good, and it depends entirely on God: he upholds it in being at every moment. God's providence guides all things towards their goal, often through his creatures' free cooperation.</p><p>Why, then, is there evil? No single quick answer suffices: the whole of Christian faith is the answer. God permits physical evil in a world still "on the way", and moral evil because he respects freedom. He is in no way the cause of moral evil, yet he can bring good out of it, as he did supremely from the Cross.</p>`,
 [["Creation ex nihilo","Creation \"out of nothing\": not from God's substance or from prior matter."],["Providence","God's wise and loving guidance of all creatures to their end."]],
 [["Gen 1:1-31: in the beginning","genesis,1,1,31"],["2 Macc 7:28: God made them out of nothing","2-maccabees,7,28,28"],["Rom 8:28: all things work together unto good","romans,8,28,28"]],
 [["ST I q.45 a.1","Creation is the bringing of all being into existence from nothing."],["ST I q.2 a.3 ad 1","God allows evil only because he is so good and powerful that he can bring good even out of evil (from Augustine)."]],
 "Think of a past evil that God brought some good out of. Thank him for it.",
 CQ("Is God the cause of moral evil?",["Yes, he wills everything","No, he permits it, respecting freedom, and can draw good from it","Evil is an illusion"],1,"God is in no way the cause of moral evil (CCC 311–312)."));
cccN("c28","p1s2c1","325–354","__P1A.HTM","Heaven and earth: angels and the visible world","God made all things visible and invisible: the angels, and a world whose every creature has its own goodness.",
 `<p>The existence of angels, spiritual non-bodily beings, is a truth of faith. They are personal and immortal creatures, servants and messengers of God, who surround Christ and accompany the Church. From infancy to death human life is surrounded by their care; each believer has an angel as protector and shepherd.</p><p>In the visible world each creature has its own goodness and reflects something of God's wisdom; they are interdependent and ordered in a hierarchy, with the human person as the summit. Respect for creation flows from this. The Sabbath shows that creation is for worship.</p>`,
 [["Angel","A purely spiritual, personal and immortal creature; the name means \"messenger\"."]],
 [["Ps 90:11-12 (91): he hath given his angels charge over thee","psalms,90,11,12"],["Matt 18:10: their angels in heaven","matthew,18,10,10"]],
 [["ST I q.50 a.1","The perfection of the universe required intellectual creatures without bodies."],["ST I q.113 a.2","Each human being has a guardian angel."]],
 "Pray the Angel of God prayer today, and thank your guardian angel.",
 CQ("Is the existence of angels a truth of faith?",["No, it's a pious opinion","Yes, witnessed by Scripture and Tradition","Only in the Old Testament"],1,"CCC 328."),null);
cccN("c29","p1s2c1","355–384","__P1B.HTM","The human person","Made in God's image, each human being is a unity of body and soul, male or female, called to friendship with God.",
 `<p>Of all visible creatures, only the human person is able to know and love the Creator, and is willed by God for his or her own sake. Being made in God's image gives every person dignity: not just something but someone, capable of self-knowledge, self-giving and communion.</p><p>The human person is one being, body and soul together. The soul is the spiritual principle, created immediately by God and immortal; it is the "form" of the body. Man and woman are created equal in dignity and for each other. Our first parents were created in friendship with God, in a state of holiness and justice.</p>`,
 [["Soul","The spiritual principle in us; it is the form of the body, and it is immortal."],["Image of God","What makes every person a \"someone\" capable of knowing and loving God."]],
 [["Gen 1:26-28: to our image and likeness","genesis,1,26,28"],["Gen 2:7: breathed into his face the breath of life","genesis,2,7,7"]],
 [["ST I q.76 a.1","The intellectual soul is the substantial form of the body: we are not souls trapped in bodies, but one being."],["ST I q.93 a.4","The image of God is found in the mind's capacity to know and love God."]],
 "Who do you find it hard to see as made in God's image? Pray for them today.",
 CQ("How does the Church describe the relation of soul and body?",["The soul is trapped in the body","The soul is the form of the body: one human nature","The body is an illusion"],1,"Body and soul are not two natures united, but form one nature (CCC 365)."),
 "Psychology often studies \"mind\" and \"brain\" separately. The Catholic view of the person as one unified being (hylomorphism) fits well with research showing how deeply bodily states such as sleep, nutrition and stress shape thought and emotion, and vice versa.");
cccN("c30","p1s2c1","385–421","__P1C.HTM","The Fall and original sin","Our first parents' sin wounded human nature; we inherit that wound, but Christ, the new Adam, is greater.",
 `<p>The reality of sin can only be understood in relation to God. Scripture tells of a primeval event: our first parents, tempted by the devil, let their trust in God die and disobeyed. They lost original holiness and justice not only for themselves but for all.</p><p>Original sin is a sin "contracted", not "committed": a state, not an act. Human nature is not totally corrupted, but wounded: subject to ignorance, suffering, death and an inclination to sin (concupiscence). Baptism takes away original sin, though its consequences remain as a struggle. And the first gospel (Gen 3:15) already announces the victory of a new Adam and a new Eve.</p>`,
 [["Original sin","The deprivation of original holiness and justice, transmitted to all by birth."],["Concupiscence","The inclination to sin that remains after Baptism."]],
 [["Gen 3:1-15: the Fall","genesis,3,1,15"],["Rom 5:12-19: by one man sin entered","romans,5,12,19"]],
 [["ST I-II q.82 a.3","Original sin is materially concupiscence, formally the lack of original justice."],["ST I-II q.85 a.3","Sin leaves four wounds in nature: ignorance, malice, weakness and disordered desire."]],
 "Which of Thomas's four wounds (ignorance, malice, weakness, disordered desire) do you notice most in yourself?",
 CQ("Is original sin something we committed?",["Yes, personally","No, it is contracted, a state, not a personal act","It doesn't exist"],1,"It is \"contracted\" and not \"committed\" (CCC 404)."),
 "Thomas's \"four wounds\" map onto modern findings on cognitive bias (ignorance), weak self-control (weakness) and strong impulses (disordered desire). Psychology describes the symptoms; the doctrine names the cause and the cure.");
cccN("c31","p1s2c2","422–455","__P1D.HTM","Jesus, Christ, Son of God, Lord","Each title says who Jesus is: Saviour, the Anointed One, God's only Son, and Lord.",
 `<p>The heart of catechesis is a Person: Jesus of Nazareth, the eternal Son of God made man. His name means "God saves". He is the Christ (the Messiah, "anointed one"), who fulfils the Messianic hope of Israel in his triple office of priest, prophet and king.</p><p>He is the only Son of God, not by adoption but by nature, as Peter confessed. And he is Lord: the title used in the Greek Old Testament for God's own name. To call Jesus "Lord" is to confess his divinity; from the beginning Christians prayed to him, and his lordship extends over the world and history.</p>`,
 [["Messiah / Christ","\"Anointed one\": the one consecrated by God as priest, prophet and king."],["Kyrios","\"Lord\": the Greek word for God's name, applied to Jesus."]],
 [["Matt 1:21: thou shalt call his name Jesus","matthew,1,21,21"],["Matt 16:16: thou art Christ, the Son of the living God","matthew,16,16,16"],["Phil 2:9-11: Jesus Christ is Lord","philippians,2,9,11"]],
 [["ST III q.22 a.1","Christ is supremely a priest, because he unites God and humanity and offers himself."],["ST III q.37 a.2","The name Jesus was fittingly given because it expresses the purpose of the Incarnation: salvation."]],
 "Which title of Jesus do you use most in prayer? Try praying with a different one this week.",
 CQ("What does the name \"Jesus\" mean?",["Anointed","God saves","Son of God"],1,"CCC 430."));
cccN("c32","p1s2c2","456–483","__P1J.HTM","The Word became flesh","The eternal Son became truly man without ceasing to be truly God: one Person in two natures.",
 `<p>Why did the Word become flesh? To save us by reconciling us with God; so that we might know God's love; to be our model of holiness; and to make us "partakers of the divine nature".</p><p>The Church spent centuries defending this mystery against errors that denied either his divinity or his humanity. The councils (Nicaea, Ephesus, Chalcedon, Constantinople) taught that Jesus is one divine Person with two natures, divine and human, without confusion or separation. He has a true human soul, with human knowledge and a human will, freely in harmony with his divine will; and a human heart that loves us.</p>`,
 [["Incarnation","The Son of God taking a human nature to accomplish our salvation."],["Hypostatic union","The union of two natures, divine and human, in the one Person of the Son."]],
 [["John 1:14: the Word was made flesh","john,1,14,14"],["2 Pet 1:4: partakers of the divine nature","2-peter,1,4,4"],["Phil 2:6-7: emptied himself","philippians,2,6,7"]],
 [["ST III q.1 a.2","The Incarnation was the most fitting way to heal us: it strengthens faith, raises hope, enkindles charity and shows the dignity of human nature."],["ST III q.2 a.2","The union of the Word and humanity took place in the Person, not in the nature."]],
 "Jesus has a human heart that knows and loves you personally (CCC 478). How does that change how you pray?",
 CQ("How many Persons and natures are in Christ?",["Two Persons, one nature","One Person, two natures","One Person, one nature"],1,"One divine Person in two natures, divine and human (CCC 464–469)."));
cccN("c33","p1s2c2","484–511","__P1K.HTM","Born of the Virgin Mary","Mary was prepared by God from her conception, conceived Jesus by the Holy Spirit, and remained a virgin: the Mother of God.",
 `<p>God chose Mary to be the mother of his Son. To be able to give a free assent of faith, she was enriched with grace: from the first instant of her conception she was preserved from original sin (the Immaculate Conception), by the merits of Christ. By grace she remained free of every personal sin.</p><p>Because her Son is God, Mary is truly the Mother of God (<i>Theotokos</i>). Jesus was conceived by the Holy Spirit alone, without a human father; the Church confesses Mary's perpetual virginity. Her "yes" makes her the new Eve, the mother of all the living in the order of grace.</p>`,
 [["Theotokos","\"God-bearer\": Mother of God, defined at Ephesus (431)."],["Immaculate Conception","Mary's preservation from original sin from her conception, by Christ's merits."]],
 [["Luke 1:26-38: the Annunciation","luke,1,26,38"],["Matt 1:18-25: conceived of the Holy Ghost","matthew,1,18,25"]],
 [["ST III q.35 a.4","Mary is rightly called Mother of God, because a mother is mother of the person she bears, and the person is divine."],["ST III q.28 a.3","Thomas defends Mary's virginity after the birth."]],
 "How would you explain to a non-Catholic friend why Mary is called \"Mother of God\"?",
 CQ("What does the Immaculate Conception refer to?",["Jesus' conception","Mary's preservation from original sin from her conception","The Virgin Birth"],1,"CCC 490–492. (A common confusion!)"));
cccN("c34","p1s2c2","512–570","__P1L.HTM","The mysteries of Christ's life","Everything in Jesus' life, from the manger to the entry into Jerusalem, reveals the Father and is for us.",
 `<p>The Creed only mentions Christ's birth and death, but his whole life is a revelation of the Father: his words and deeds, silences and sufferings. All of it is for us; he lived it as our model, and he lets us live in him what he lived.</p><p>The Catechism walks through the mysteries: his birth in poverty, the Epiphany, the Presentation, the hidden life at Nazareth, his baptism and temptations, his preaching of the Kingdom with signs and miracles, the choice of the Twelve and the keys given to Peter, the Transfiguration, and his ascent to Jerusalem. The Rosary is one way of entering these mysteries.</p>`,
 [["Mystery (of Christ's life)","An event in Jesus' life that reveals God and has power to save us."],["Kingdom of God","God's reign, begun in Jesus and his Church, fulfilled at the end of time."]],
 [["Luke 2:51-52: he went down to Nazareth","luke,2,51,52"],["Mark 1:14-15: the kingdom of God is at hand","mark,1,14,15"],["Matt 17:1-9: the Transfiguration","matthew,17,1,9"]],
 [["ST III qq.27–59","Thomas's \"life of Christ\" treatise studies each mystery in order: conception, birth, baptism, temptation, teaching, miracles, transfiguration, passion and exaltation."],["ST III q.40 a.1","Christ chose to live among people, not as a hermit, to show the truth and bring us to God."]],
 "Which mystery of Christ's life do you most need to sit with this week?",
 CQ("Why does the Catechism say Christ's whole life matters, not just his death?",["Because the Creed lists every event","Because all of it reveals the Father and is lived for us","Because his death is less important"],1,"His whole life is revelation, redemption and recapitulation (CCC 516–518)."));
cccN("c35","p1s2c2","571–594","__P1M.HTM","Jesus and Israel","Jesus fulfilled the Law, honoured the Temple and revealed himself as God who forgives sins: the deepest reason for the conflict.",
 `<p>The Paschal mystery is at the centre of the Good News. Some of Israel's leaders opposed Jesus because of his attitude to the Law, the Temple and faith in one God. Jesus did not abolish the Law but fulfilled it perfectly, and he loved the Temple as his Father's house, even foretelling its destruction and identifying himself with it.</p><p>Above all, by forgiving sins he acted as God, which seemed blasphemous to those who did not believe. The Church does not hold the Jewish people collectively responsible for Jesus' death. All sinners were the authors of his Passion, and Christians bear the heavier responsibility.</p>`,
 [["Paschal mystery","Christ's Passion, death, Resurrection and Ascension: our \"Passover\"."]],
 [["Matt 5:17-19: not to destroy but to fulfil","matthew,5,17,19"],["Mark 2:5-12: who can forgive sins but God?","mark,2,5,12"]],
 [["ST III q.47 a.5","Thomas distinguishes degrees of guilt in Christ's death: the leaders acted with more knowledge, the crowd more in ignorance."]],
 "Why might it matter that the Church says our sins, not \"the Jews\", crucified Christ?",
 CQ("Does the Church hold the Jewish people collectively responsible for Jesus' death?",["Yes","No; all sinners were its authors, Christians especially","Only the leaders of the time and their descendants"],1,"CCC 597–598."));
cccN("c36","p1s2c2","595–630","__P1O.HTM","He died, crucified, and was buried","Jesus freely offered his life on the Cross as the one perfect sacrifice that reconciles us with God.",
 `<p>Jesus' death was not a chance event but part of God's plan, foretold in the Scriptures: he "died for our sins". God took the initiative of redeeming love. Jesus freely embraced the Father's will, and at the Last Supper he anticipated his free offering of his life, giving it to the apostles in the Eucharist.</p><p>His death is the unique and definitive sacrifice. By his obedience unto death he made satisfaction for our sins, replacing our disobedience with love. He calls us to take up our cross and follow him. He was truly buried, and remained united to his divine Person even in the tomb.</p>`,
 [["Redemption","Christ's buying us back from sin and death by his death and Resurrection."],["Satisfaction","Making amends for sin through an offering of love greater than the offence."]],
 [["Isa 53:4-12: he was wounded for our iniquities","isaiah,53,4,12"],["John 10:17-18: I lay down my life","john,10,17,18"],["1 Pet 2:24: by whose stripes you were healed","1-peter,2,24,24"]],
 [["ST III q.48 a.2","Christ's Passion was a superabundant satisfaction, because of the greatness of his love, the dignity of his life and the extent of his suffering."],["ST III q.46 a.3","The Cross was the most fitting way to save us: it shows God's love and calls forth ours."]],
 "Look at a crucifix for two minutes. What does it say to you about love?",
 CQ("Was Jesus' death an accident of history?",["Yes, a tragic mistake","No, part of God's plan, freely accepted by Jesus out of love","Only a political execution"],1,"CCC 599–609."));
cccN("c37","p1s2c2","631–658","__P1Q.HTM","He descended to the dead and rose again","Christ went down to the realm of the dead to free the just, and rose bodily on the third day: the crowning truth of our faith.",
 `<p>"He descended into hell" means that Jesus truly died and went to the realm of the dead (Sheol, Hades) to open heaven's gates for the just who had gone before him. He did not go to free the damned.</p><p>The Resurrection is a real event, historically attested (the empty tomb, the appearances), yet it also transcends history. The risen body is the same body that was crucified, but now glorious, no longer bound by space and time. The Resurrection confirms all Christ did and taught, fulfils the promises, shows his divinity, and is the source of our own justification and future resurrection.</p>`,
 [["Hades / Sheol","The realm of the dead, where the just awaited the Redeemer."],["Glorified body","The risen body: real and the same, but transformed by the Spirit."]],
 [["1 Pet 3:18-19: he preached to those spirits in prison","1-peter,3,18,19"],["1 Cor 15:3-8, 14: if Christ be not risen","1-corinthians,15,3,14"],["Luke 24:36-43: handle and see","luke,24,36,43"]],
 [["ST III q.52 a.5","Christ's descent freed the holy fathers from the \"limbo\" of the just."],["ST III q.54 a.1","Christ rose with a true body, the same body."]],
 "\"If Christ be not risen, your faith is vain\" (1 Cor 15:17). What difference does the Resurrection make to how you face death?",
 CQ("Did Jesus descend into hell to free the damned?",["Yes","No, to free the just who had gone before him","He did not descend at all"],1,"CCC 633."));
cccN("c38","p1s2c2","659–682","__P1T.HTM","He ascended and will come again","Christ ascended into heaven, intercedes for us, and will come in glory to judge the living and the dead.",
 `<p>After forty days, the risen Christ entered heaven with his humanity: where the Head has gone, the members hope to follow. Seated at the Father's right hand, he reigns and intercedes for us as our high priest, and pours out the Holy Spirit.</p><p>His Kingdom is already present in the Church, but not yet fulfilled. Before his return, the Church will pass through a final trial. On the last day he will come in glory to judge the living and the dead; then each person's relation to God and neighbour will be laid bare. Christ is the Judge because he is the Redeemer: those who reject his grace judge themselves.</p>`,
 [["Parousia","Christ's glorious return at the end of time."]],
 [["Acts 1:9-11: this Jesus shall so come","acts,1,9,11"],["Heb 7:25: always living to make intercession","hebrews,7,25,25"],["Matt 25:31-46: the last judgement","matthew,25,31,46"]],
 [["ST III q.57 a.6","Christ's Ascension causes our salvation: it raises our hope, prepares our place, and he intercedes for us."],["ST III q.59 a.2","Judgement belongs to Christ also as man, because of his humanity's union with God."]],
 "If Christ came today, what would you want him to find you doing?",
 CQ("Why is Christ the Judge?",["Because he is angry with sinners","Because he is the Redeemer; those who refuse his grace judge themselves","Because the Father refuses to judge"],1,"CCC 679."));
cccN("c39","p1s2c3","683–747","__P1Y.HTM","I believe in the Holy Spirit","The Holy Spirit, the Lord and giver of life, makes Christ present and builds up the Church.",
 `<p>No one can say "Jesus is Lord" except in the Holy Spirit. The Spirit is the first to awaken faith in us, though he is the last revealed. The Son and the Spirit are sent together: the Spirit reveals the Son, and the Son glorifies the Father.</p><p>The Spirit has many names (Paraclete, Spirit of truth) and symbols: water, anointing, fire, cloud and light, the seal, the hand, the finger, the dove. He spoke through the prophets, overshadowed Mary, and anointed Jesus. At Pentecost he was poured out on the Church, and now he builds, animates and sanctifies her, distributing gifts and charisms for the good of all.</p>`,
 [["Paraclete","\"One called to the side\": advocate, helper, consoler."],["Charism","A special grace of the Spirit given to build up the Church."]],
 [["1 Cor 12:3: no man can say the Lord Jesus but by the Holy Ghost","1-corinthians,12,3,3"],["John 16:13-14: the Spirit of truth","john,16,13,14"],["Acts 2:1-4: Pentecost","acts,2,1,4"]],
 [["ST I q.37 a.1","\"Love\" is a proper name of the Holy Spirit: he proceeds as the love of the Father and the Son."],["ST I q.38 a.2","\"Gift\" is likewise a proper name of the Spirit."]],
 "Which symbol of the Spirit (water, fire, wind, dove, seal) speaks to you most? Why?",
 CQ("Which symbol is NOT listed by the Catechism for the Holy Spirit?",["Fire","The dove","The lamb"],2,"The Lamb is a title of Christ; the Spirit's symbols include water, anointing, fire, cloud, seal, hand, finger and dove (CCC 694–701)."));
cccN("c40","p1s2c3","748–780","__P26.HTM","The Church in God's plan","The Church is God's plan from the beginning: prefigured, prepared in Israel, founded by Christ, revealed by the Spirit, and fulfilled in glory.",
 `<p>Christ is the light of the nations, and that light shines on the face of the Church. "Church" (<i>ekklesia</i>) means a convocation, the assembly of those God calls. She is described by many images: the sheepfold, God's field, God's building, our mother, the bride of Christ.</p><p>The Church was foreshadowed from the beginning, prepared in the history of Israel, instituted by Christ (above all in the Paschal mystery, when the Church was born from his pierced side), and revealed by the Spirit at Pentecost. She will be perfected in glory. She is at once visible and spiritual, human and divine, and she is the sacrament of salvation: the sign and instrument of communion with God and of unity among people.</p>`,
 [["Ekklesia","\"Those called out\": the assembly God convokes."],["Sacrament of salvation","The Church as sign and instrument of union with God and unity among all people."]],
 [["Matt 16:18: upon this rock I will build my church","matthew,16,18,18"],["John 19:34: blood and water from his side","john,19,34,34"],["Eph 5:25-27: Christ loved the church","ephesians,5,25,27"]],
 [["ST III q.64 a.2 ad 3","The sacraments, from which the Church is built, flowed from Christ's side as he hung on the Cross."]],
 "Which image of the Church (flock, field, building, mother, bride) helps you love her more?",
 CQ("What does \"Church\" (ekklesia) mean?",["A building for worship","A convocation: those God calls together","A hierarchy"],1,"CCC 751."));
cccN("c41","p1s2c3","781–810","__P28.HTM","People of God, Body of Christ, Temple of the Spirit","The Church is God's people, Christ's body with him as head, and the Spirit's temple.",
 `<p>The Church is the People of God: one becomes a member not by birth but by faith and Baptism. This people shares Christ's offices as priest, prophet and king; its law is love, its mission to be salt and light, its destiny the Kingdom.</p><p>The Church is also the Body of Christ: he is its head, its members are united to him and to one another, especially through the Eucharist, with a diversity of gifts and a special place for the poor and suffering. And the Church is the Temple of the Holy Spirit, who is the soul of the Mystical Body and the source of its life and unity.</p>`,
 [["Mystical Body","The Church as the body of which Christ is the head."],["Common priesthood","The priesthood of all the baptised: offering their lives to God."]],
 [["1 Pet 2:9: a kingly priesthood","1-peter,2,9,9"],["1 Cor 12:12-27: you are the body of Christ","1-corinthians,12,12,27"],["1 Cor 3:16: you are the temple of God","1-corinthians,3,16,16"]],
 [["ST III q.8 a.1","Christ is the head of the Church by his dignity, his perfect grace, and his power to give grace to the members."],["ST III q.8 a.1 ad 3","The Holy Spirit is compared to the heart, invisibly animating the Church."]],
 "\"If one member suffer, all suffer\" (1 Cor 12:26). Who in your parish or group is suffering right now?",
 CQ("Who is called the \"soul\" of the Mystical Body?",["The Pope","The Holy Spirit","Mary"],1,"CCC 797, quoting St Augustine and Pius XII."));
cccN("c42","p1s2c3","811–870","__P29.HTM","One, holy, catholic and apostolic","These four marks are gifts Christ gives the Church and tasks she must live out.",
 `<p>The Church is <b>one</b>: one Lord, one faith, one baptism, held together by charity, the profession of faith, common worship and apostolic succession. The one Church of Christ subsists in the Catholic Church; yet many elements of truth and sanctification exist outside her visible bounds, and the desire for Christian unity is a gift and call of Christ.</p><p>She is <b>holy</b>, though containing sinners. She is <b>catholic</b> (universal), possessing the fullness of the means of salvation and sent to all. Outside the Church there is no salvation, which, rightly understood, means all salvation comes from Christ through his Church, while those who through no fault of their own don't know him can still be saved. She is <b>apostolic</b>, built on the apostles and still taught and governed by their successors.</p>`,
 [["Subsists in","The one Church of Christ is found in its fullness in the Catholic Church (Lumen Gentium 8)."],["Ecumenism","Work and prayer for the unity of all Christians."]],
 [["John 17:20-23: that they all may be one","john,17,20,23"],["Eph 2:19-20: built on the apostles and prophets","ephesians,2,19,20"]],
 [["Thomas, Commentary on the Apostles' Creed, art. 9","Thomas explains the Church's marks: one (in faith, hope and charity), holy (washed and anointed by Christ), catholic (in place, people and time) and firm, because it is built on Christ and the apostles."]],
 "Pray for Christian unity today, naming a Christian friend from another church.",
 CQ("What does \"outside the Church there is no salvation\" mean, according to the Catechism?",["Only Catholics can be saved","All salvation comes from Christ through his Church; those inculpably ignorant of him can still be saved","Nothing at all"],1,"CCC 846–848."));
cccN("c43","p1s2c3","871–945","__P2A.HTM","Hierarchy, laity and consecrated life","All the baptised share Christ's mission: the ordained serve as shepherds, the laity sanctify the world, and the consecrated witness to the Kingdom.",
 `<p>Among all the faithful there is a true equality of dignity, and a diversity of ministries. Christ instituted the hierarchy to serve: the college of bishops with the Pope as head, who teach, sanctify and govern, with priests and deacons as their co-workers. The Pope is the perpetual and visible source of unity.</p><p>The lay faithful have the vocation to seek the Kingdom by engaging in temporal affairs and ordering them to God: in families, work, politics and culture. Consecrated life (religious, hermits, consecrated virgins, secular institutes) professes the evangelical counsels of poverty, chastity and obedience as a sign of the world to come.</p>`,
 [["Infallibility","The charism by which the Pope, and bishops in union with him, cannot err when defining doctrine of faith or morals."],["Evangelical counsels","Poverty, chastity and obedience, freely vowed."]],
 [["Matt 16:18-19: the keys of the kingdom","matthew,16,18,19"],["Matt 5:13-16: salt of the earth, light of the world","matthew,5,13,16"]],
 [["ST II-II q.186 a.7","The three vows sum up the religious state, removing obstacles to perfect charity."],["ST II-II q.184 a.3","Perfection consists essentially in charity; the counsels are means to it."]],
 "Where is your \"field\" as a lay person (studies, work, family)? How can you bring Christ there?",
 CQ("What is the specific vocation of the laity?",["To run parish committees","To seek the Kingdom by engaging in and ordering temporal affairs to God","To become priests"],1,"CCC 898."));
cccN("c44","p1s2c3","946–962","__P2B.HTM","The communion of saints","The Church is a communion of holy things and holy people, in heaven, in purgatory and on earth.",
 `<p>"Communion of saints" has two meanings: communion in holy things (the faith, the sacraments, charisms and charity) and communion among holy persons. The Church exists in three states: some are pilgrims on earth, some are being purified after death, and some are in glory.</p><p>These are united by charity. The saints in heaven intercede for us; we honour them and ask their prayers. We pray for the dead, so that they may be freed from their sins. And on earth, the least of our acts done in charity benefit all.</p>`,
 [["Communion of saints","The union of all the faithful, living and dead, in Christ."]],
 [["Heb 12:1: so great a cloud of witnesses","hebrews,12,1,1"],["2 Macc 12:46: pray for the dead","2-maccabees,12,46,46"],["Rev 5:8: the prayers of the saints","revelation,5,8,8"]],
 [["ST II-II q.83 a.11","The saints in heaven pray for us, and their prayer is more effective because of their greater charity."]],
 "Choose a saint to be your friend this month. Ask for their prayers each day.",
 CQ("What are the three \"states\" of the Church?",["Clergy, laity, religious","Pilgrims on earth, those being purified, and those in glory","Past, present and future"],1,"CCC 954."));
cccN("c45","p1s2c3","963–975","__P2C.HTM","Mary, Mother of the Church","Mary is the Church's model and mother in the order of grace; she was taken body and soul into glory.",
 `<p>Mary's role is inseparable from her Son's. From the Annunciation to the Cross she was united with him, and he gave her to the beloved disciple as mother. She is therefore mother of the Church, and the model of its faith and charity.</p><p>At the end of her earthly life she was assumed body and soul into heavenly glory, a singular share in her Son's Resurrection. Her motherhood continues: she intercedes for us. Devotion to her is intrinsic to Christian worship, but it differs essentially from the adoration given to God alone.</p>`,
 [["Assumption","Mary's being taken up body and soul into glory (defined 1950)."],["Hyperdulia","The special veneration given to Mary; different in kind from adoration (latria), given to God alone."]],
 [["John 19:25-27: behold thy mother","john,19,25,27"],["Luke 1:48: all generations shall call me blessed","luke,1,48,48"],["Rev 12:1: a woman clothed with the sun","revelation,12,1,1"]],
 [["ST III q.25 a.5","Mary is given hyperdulia, a veneration above that of the saints but not the worship due to God."]],
 "Pray one Hail Mary slowly, for someone who needs a mother's care.",
 CQ("Do Catholics worship Mary?",["Yes","No; she is venerated, while adoration is for God alone","Only on feast days"],1,"CCC 971."));
cccN("c46","p1s2c3","976–987","__P2D.HTM","The forgiveness of sins","Christ gave the Church power to forgive sins, first in Baptism and then in the sacrament of Penance.",
 `<p>The Creed links the forgiveness of sins with the Holy Spirit and the Church. Baptism is the first and chief sacrament of forgiveness: it unites us to Christ and takes away all sin. But since we remain weak, Christ gave his Church the power of the keys, to forgive the sins of the baptised in the sacrament of Penance.</p><p>There is no sin, however serious, that the Church cannot forgive. No one is so wicked that they cannot hope for forgiveness if their repentance is honest. Christ wills that the doors of forgiveness always be open to anyone who turns back.</p>`,
 [["Power of the keys","The authority Christ gave the apostles and their successors to bind and loose, including to forgive sins."]],
 [["John 20:22-23: whose sins you shall forgive","john,20,22,23"],["Matt 16:19: whatsoever thou shalt loose","matthew,16,19,19"]],
 [["ST III q.84 a.10","Penance can be repeated as often as we fall, because God's mercy has no limit."]],
 "Is there a sin you secretly think is too big to be forgiven? Read CCC 982 and believe it.",
 CQ("Is there any sin the Church cannot forgive to a repentant person?",["Yes, murder","No, none","Only sins against the Church"],1,"CCC 982."));
cccN("c47","p1s2c3","988–1019","__P2G.HTM","The resurrection of the body","Our bodies will rise again, transformed, as Christ's did; so we must honour the body and prepare for a holy death.",
 `<p>We believe not only that the soul lives on, but that our mortal bodies will come to life again. This was hard to accept from the beginning, yet it is central: if there is no resurrection, Christ is not risen. All the dead will rise at the last day: those who have done good to the resurrection of life, those who have done evil to the resurrection of judgement.</p><p>How? We cannot imagine it; but it will be our own body, glorified. Death is the end of earthly life and the consequence of sin, but Christ has transformed it: for the Christian, dying is going home to the Lord. The Church encourages us to prepare for the hour of death.</p>`,
 [["Resurrection of the flesh","The raising of our bodies on the last day, reunited with our souls."]],
 [["John 11:25: I am the resurrection","john,11,25,26"],["1 Cor 15:35-44: it is sown a natural body","1-corinthians,15,35,44"],["Phil 3:20-21: he will reform the body of our lowness","philippians,3,20,21"]],
 [["Supplement q.79 a.1 (from Thomas's Sentences commentary)","We will rise with the same body, because the soul is naturally the form of a body; a soul without its body is not a complete human being."]],
 "Pray for a holy death today: yours and that of someone who is dying.",
 CQ("Do Christians believe only in the immortality of the soul?",["Yes","No, also in the resurrection of the body","Neither"],1,"CCC 988–990."));
cccN("c48","p1s2c3","1020–1037","__P2K.HTM","Judgement, heaven, purgatory and hell","At death each person is judged; heaven is perfect communion with God, purgatory a final purification, and hell self-exclusion from God.",
 `<p>At death each person receives an eternal recompense in a particular judgement: entrance into heaven (directly or through purification) or eternal damnation.</p><p><b>Heaven</b> is the ultimate end and fulfilment of our deepest longings: to see God face to face, with all the blessed. <b>Purgatory</b> is the final purification of those who die in God's grace but still imperfectly purified; the Church prays for them, especially in the Mass. <b>Hell</b> is the state of definitive self-exclusion from communion with God, chosen by dying in mortal sin without repenting. God predestines no one to hell; the warnings about it are a call to responsibility and conversion.</p>`,
 [["Particular judgement","The judgement of each soul immediately after death."],["Beatific vision","Seeing God face to face: the essence of heaven."]],
 [["1 Cor 13:12: then face to face","1-corinthians,13,12,12"],["1 Cor 3:13-15: saved, yet so as by fire","1-corinthians,3,13,15"],["Matt 25:41-46: everlasting punishment, life everlasting","matthew,25,41,46"]],
 [["ST I-II q.3 a.8","Final happiness is the vision of the divine essence."],["ST I-II q.87 a.3","Mortal sin, unrepented, merits eternal punishment because it turns away from the eternal good."]],
 "Pray for the souls in purgatory today, especially someone you knew.",
 CQ("Does God predestine anyone to hell?",["Yes","No; hell is self-exclusion by persisting in mortal sin until the end","Hell does not exist"],1,"CCC 1037."));
cccN("c49","p1s2c3","1038–1065","__P2P.HTM","The Last Judgement, the new creation, and Amen","At the end, Christ will judge all, and God will make a new heaven and a new earth; the Creed ends with Amen, \"so be it\".",
 `<p>The Last Judgement will reveal the full meaning of creation and history, and that God's justice triumphs over all the injustices committed. It calls us to conversion while there is time.</p><p>At the end of time the Kingdom will come in its fullness: God will renew the universe, and humanity and the cosmos will be transformed. This hope does not weaken our concern for this world but strengthens it. The Creed ends with "Amen", a word of faith and trust: so be it. Christ himself is the "Amen", the faithful witness.</p>`,
 [["Amen","A Hebrew word expressing firmness and trust: \"so be it\", \"truly\"."]],
 [["Rev 21:1-5: behold I make all things new","revelation,21,1,5"],["Rom 8:19-23: creation groaneth","romans,8,19,23"],["2 Cor 1:20: in him Amen","2-corinthians,1,20,20"]],
 [["SCG IV.97","After the judgement, the world will be renewed: creation itself shares in the glory of the redeemed."]],
 "Say the Amen at Mass this Sunday as a real \"yes\".",
 CQ("What does \"Amen\" mean?",["The end","So be it: an expression of faith and trust","Peace"],1,"CCC 1062–1064."));

// ---------- Catechism notes: Part Two, The Celebration of the Christian Mystery (§§ 1066–1690) ----------
cccN("c50","p2s1","1066–1112","__P2T.HTM","The liturgy: work of the Holy Trinity","In the liturgy the Father blesses us, Christ acts through signs, and the Holy Spirit makes it all present: it is the work of God and of his people.",
 `<p>"Liturgy" originally meant a public work, a service done for the people. In Christian tradition it means the people of God's participation in the work of God: Christ continues the work of our redemption in, with and through his Church. It is the summit towards which the Church's activity tends and the font from which all her power flows.</p><p>The Father is the source and goal of the liturgy: every blessing comes from him. Christ, now glorified, acts through the sacraments he instituted, and he is present in the assembly, in the word, in the minister and above all in the Eucharist. The Holy Spirit prepares us to meet Christ, recalls his mystery, makes it present, and unites us in communion.</p>`,
 [["Liturgy","The public worship of the Church; literally, \"a work of (or for) the people\"."],["Epiclesis","The prayer asking the Father to send the Spirit, so that the offerings become Christ's Body and Blood."]],
 [["Eph 1:3-6: blessed us with every spiritual blessing","ephesians,1,3,6"],["Matt 18:20: there am I in the midst of them","matthew,18,20,20"]],
 [["ST II-II q.81 a.7","External acts of worship are needed because we are body and soul: through signs the mind is raised to God."]],
 "Next time you go to Mass, notice when you're addressing the Father, the Son and the Spirit.",
 CQ("What is the liturgy called by the Second Vatican Council?",["An optional devotion","The summit and font of the Church's life","A private prayer"],1,"Sacrosanctum Concilium 10; CCC 1074."));
cccN("c51","p2s1","1113–1134","__P2Z.HTM","What the sacraments are","The sacraments are effective signs of grace, instituted by Christ and entrusted to the Church, by which divine life is given to us.",
 `<p>The whole liturgical life of the Church revolves around the seven sacraments. They are "of Christ" (he instituted them), "of the Church" (by her and for her), "of faith" (they presuppose, nourish and express it), "of salvation" (they really confer the grace they signify), and "of eternal life" (they anticipate glory).</p><p>They act <i>ex opere operato</i>: by the very fact that the action is performed, because it is Christ who acts, not by the holiness of the minister. Yet the fruit depends also on the dispositions of the one who receives. Baptism, Confirmation and Holy Orders also confer a permanent "character", so they are never repeated.</p>`,
 [["Sacrament","An effective sign of grace, instituted by Christ and entrusted to the Church, by which divine life is given."],["Ex opere operato","\"By the work worked\": the sacrament acts by Christ's power, not by the minister's worthiness."]],
 [["Mark 16:15-16: he that believeth and is baptised","mark,16,15,16"],["John 3:5: born again of water and the Holy Ghost","john,3,5,5"]],
 [["ST III q.60 a.2","A sacrament is a sign of a sacred thing insofar as it sanctifies us."],["ST III q.65 a.1","Thomas shows why there are seven sacraments: they match the stages of natural life (birth, growth, nourishment, healing, social life)."]],
 "Which sacrament have you received most recently? What did Christ do in you through it?",
 CQ("What does \"ex opere operato\" mean?",["The sacrament depends on the priest's holiness","The sacrament acts by Christ's power when it is rightly celebrated","The sacrament works even without faith or desire"],1,"CCC 1128. The fruit still depends on the recipient's dispositions."));
cccN("c52","p2s1","1135–1209","__P36.HTM","Celebrating: who, how, when, where","The whole Christ, head and members, celebrates, through signs, words and music, in the rhythm of the Church's year, in churches that are houses of prayer.",
 `<p><b>Who</b> celebrates? The whole Christ: the heavenly liturgy of angels and saints, and the assembly on earth, each with its own role. <b>How?</b> Through signs and symbols drawn from creation, human life and salvation history; through word and action; through singing, music and holy images.</p><p><b>When?</b> Sunday, the Lord's Day, is the foundation of the liturgical year. Easter is the "feast of feasts". The year unfolds the whole mystery of Christ, and the Liturgy of the Hours sanctifies the day. <b>Where?</b> Worship is not tied to one place, since Christ is the true temple, but churches are signs of the Church on earth and houses of prayer. Different liturgical traditions (Latin, Byzantine and others) express the one mystery.</p>`,
 [["Liturgical year","The yearly unfolding of Christ's mystery, from Advent to Christ the King."],["Rite","A liturgical tradition, such as the Latin, Byzantine, Coptic or Syro-Malabar."]],
 [["Rev 4:1-11: the heavenly liturgy","revelation,4,1,11"],["Ps 117:24 (118): this is the day which the Lord hath made","psalms,117,24,24"]],
 [["ST II-II q.91 a.2","Singing in worship is fitting because it stirs devotion, provided it raises the mind to God."]],
 "Look at the liturgical calendar for this week. Which feast could you mark in some small way?",
 CQ("What is the \"feast of feasts\"?",["Christmas","Easter","Pentecost"],1,"CCC 1169."));
cccN("c53","p2s2","1210–1284","__P3E.HTM","Baptism","Baptism makes us children of God: it takes away all sin, gives new life in the Spirit, and joins us to Christ and his Church.",
 `<p>The seven sacraments touch all the stages of life. Baptism, Confirmation and the Eucharist are the sacraments of Christian initiation. Baptism is the gateway: it is the bath of rebirth, prefigured by creation, the flood, the Red Sea crossing and the Jordan, and instituted by Christ.</p><p>Its essential rite is immersion in water, or pouring water on the head, while invoking the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit. Infants are baptised in the faith of the Church. In case of necessity anyone can baptise. Baptism is necessary for salvation for those who have heard the Gospel; God is not bound by the sacraments, however, and the Church trusts in his mercy for those who die without it. Its effects: forgiveness of all sin, new birth, membership of the Church, and an indelible character.</p>`,
 [["Indelible character","A permanent spiritual seal configuring us to Christ; Baptism is never repeated."],["Baptism of desire","Salvation of those who, without Baptism, sincerely seek God or die desiring Baptism."]],
 [["Matt 28:19: baptising them","matthew,28,19,19"],["Rom 6:3-4: buried together with him by baptism","romans,6,3,4"],["Acts 2:38: be baptised for the remission of sins","acts,2,38,38"]],
 [["ST III q.66 a.3","Water is the fitting matter of Baptism because it washes, cools and is everywhere available."],["ST III q.68 a.2","Someone who desires Baptism but dies before receiving it can be saved by that desire."]],
 "Do you know the date of your Baptism? Find out, and celebrate it this year.",
 CQ("Who can baptise in an emergency?",["Only a priest","Only a deacon or priest","Anyone, with the right intention, water and the Trinitarian formula"],2,"CCC 1256."));
cccN("c54","p2s2","1285–1321","__P3P.HTM","Confirmation","Confirmation completes Baptism, pouring out the Holy Spirit in fullness and strengthening us to witness to Christ.",
 `<p>Confirmation perfects baptismal grace. It roots us more deeply in our divine sonship, unites us more firmly to Christ, increases the gifts of the Spirit, binds us more perfectly to the Church, and gives special strength to spread and defend the faith.</p><p>Its essential rite is the anointing with chrism on the forehead, with the laying on of hands and the words "Be sealed with the Gift of the Holy Spirit." In the Latin Church the ordinary minister is the bishop, which shows the link with the apostles; in the Eastern Churches the priest confirms immediately after Baptism. Like Baptism it gives a character and is received only once.</p>`,
 [["Chrism","Perfumed oil consecrated by the bishop, used in Baptism, Confirmation and Ordination."],["Seal","The mark of the Spirit, showing we belong totally to Christ."]],
 [["Acts 8:14-17: they laid their hands upon them","acts,8,14,17"],["2 Cor 1:21-22: he hath sealed us","2-corinthians,1,21,22"]],
 [["ST III q.72 a.1","Confirmation is to Baptism as growth is to birth: it gives the fullness of the Spirit for spiritual adulthood."],["ST III q.72 a.5","It imprints a character, deputing us to profess the faith publicly."]],
 "Which gift of the Holy Spirit (wisdom, understanding, counsel, fortitude, knowledge, piety, fear of the Lord) do you most need now?",
 CQ("Who is the ordinary minister of Confirmation in the Latin Church?",["The parish priest","The bishop","A catechist"],1,"CCC 1313."));
cccN("c55","p2s2","1322–1344","__P3W.HTM","The Eucharist: source and summit","The Eucharist is the source and summit of the Christian life, instituted at the Last Supper and celebrated by the Church from the beginning.",
 `<p>The Eucharist completes Christian initiation. In it Christ makes present his one sacrifice and gives himself as food. It contains the whole spiritual good of the Church, Christ himself, our Pasch. The Church calls it by many names: Eucharist (thanksgiving), the Lord's Supper, the Breaking of Bread, the Holy Sacrifice, Holy Communion, Holy Mass.</p><p>Christ prepared it through the signs of bread and wine, the manna and the multiplication of loaves, and instituted it on the night he was betrayed: "Do this in memory of me." From the first community of Jerusalem, Christians gathered on the first day of the week to break bread.</p>`,
 [["Eucharist","\"Thanksgiving\": the sacrament of Christ's Body and Blood."],["Mass","From the dismissal (missa), sending the faithful to do God's will."]],
 [["Luke 22:19-20: do this for a commemoration of me","luke,22,19,20"],["John 6:52-59: my flesh is meat indeed","john,6,52,59"],["Acts 2:42: the breaking of bread","acts,2,42,42"]],
 [["ST III q.65 a.3","The Eucharist is the greatest sacrament, because it contains Christ himself, and all the other sacraments are ordered to it as their end."]],
 "Arrive five minutes early to Mass this week and pray about what you're about to receive.",
 CQ("Why is the Eucharist the \"source and summit\"?",["Because it is the oldest sacrament","Because it contains Christ himself, the whole spiritual good of the Church","Because it is celebrated most often"],1,"CCC 1324."));
cccN("c56","p2s2","1345–1419","__P40.HTM","The Mass: sacrifice, presence, communion","The Mass makes present Christ's one sacrifice; he is truly, really and substantially present; and in communion he unites us to himself and to each other.",
 `<p>The Mass has kept the same structure since the second century: the Liturgy of the Word and the Liturgy of the Eucharist, together forming one act of worship. The Eucharist is <b>thanksgiving</b> and praise to the Father, the <b>memorial</b> that makes Christ's sacrifice present (not repeated, but made present), and a <b>sacrifice</b> in which the Church offers herself with Christ.</p><p>Christ is present in many ways, but in the Eucharist uniquely: truly, really and substantially, Body and Blood, soul and divinity. By consecration the whole substance of bread and wine becomes his Body and Blood: <i>transubstantiation</i>. Holy Communion deepens our union with Christ, separates us from sin, builds the Church, and commits us to the poor. To receive, one must be in a state of grace; anyone conscious of grave sin should go to Confession first.</p>`,
 [["Transubstantiation","The change of the whole substance of bread and wine into Christ's Body and Blood, while the appearances remain."],["Real presence","Christ's true, real and substantial presence in the Eucharist."]],
 [["1 Cor 11:23-29: discerning the body of the Lord","1-corinthians,11,23,29"],["1 Cor 10:16-17: the bread which we break","1-corinthians,10,16,17"]],
 [["ST III q.75 a.4","Thomas's explanation of transubstantiation: the substance changes, the accidents (appearances) remain."],["Adoro te devote","Thomas's Eucharistic hymn: \"sight, touch and taste in thee are each deceived; the ear alone most safely is believed\"."]],
 "At the consecration this Sunday, silently pray: \"My Lord and my God.\"",
 CQ("What is \"transubstantiation\"?",["Bread and wine become a symbol","The substance becomes Christ's Body and Blood, while the appearances remain","Christ is present alongside the bread"],1,"CCC 1376, from the Council of Trent."));
cccN("c57","p2s2","1420–1470","__P45.HTM","Penance and Reconciliation","Christ gave the Church a sacrament to forgive sins after Baptism: contrition, confession and satisfaction, with the priest's absolution.",
 `<p>The new life of Baptism does not remove our weakness, so Christ instituted this sacrament for the baptised who sin. It has many names: conversion, Penance, Confession, forgiveness, Reconciliation. It answers Christ's call to conversion, which continues all through Christian life.</p><p>Interior penance (a radical reorientation of life) comes first; it shows itself in fasting, prayer and almsgiving. The sacrament has the acts of the penitent (contrition, confession of all mortal sins not yet confessed, and satisfaction) and God's action through the priest's absolution. Confession of venial sins is strongly recommended. The priest is bound by the absolute seal of confession. Its effects: reconciliation with God and with the Church, remission of eternal punishment, peace of conscience and spiritual strength.</p>`,
 [["Contrition","Sorrow of soul and detestation for sin, with resolve not to sin again. \"Perfect\" if from love of God; \"imperfect\" (attrition) if from fear, still enough for the sacrament."],["Seal of confession","The absolute secrecy binding every confessor."]],
 [["John 20:21-23: receive ye the Holy Ghost","john,20,21,23"],["Luke 15:11-24: the prodigal son","luke,15,11,24"],["Ps 50:3-14 (51): have mercy on me, O God","psalms,50,3,14"]],
 [["ST III q.90 a.2","The three integral parts of penance: contrition, confession and satisfaction."],["ST III q.86 a.4","After forgiveness some temporal punishment may remain, which satisfaction helps to repair."]],
 "When did you last go to Confession? If it's been a while, look up your parish times this week.",
 CQ("Which sins must be confessed?",["All sins, including every venial sin","All mortal sins not yet confessed; venial sins are recommended","Only sins against others"],1,"CCC 1456–1458."),
 "Contrition is not shame or self-loathing, which psychology links with avoidance and lower wellbeing. It is sorrow for a wrong done, together with hope; research separates the two in a similar way (guilt tied to an action tends to motivate repair, shame about the self tends to motivate hiding: Tangney & Dearing, 2002).");
cccN("c58","p2s2","1471–1498","__P4G.HTM","Indulgences, and how Confession is celebrated","An indulgence remits temporal punishment due to forgiven sin, drawn from the Church's treasury of grace.",
 `<p>Sin has a double consequence: grave sin deprives us of communion with God (eternal punishment), and every sin involves an unhealthy attachment to creatures needing purification (temporal punishment). Forgiveness removes the eternal punishment; temporal punishment remains to be purified, in this life or in purgatory.</p><p>An indulgence is a remission of temporal punishment for sins already forgiven, which the Church grants from the treasury of the merits of Christ and the saints. It can be partial or plenary, and can be applied to ourselves or the dead. Confession is ordinarily celebrated individually, or in a communal celebration with individual confession; general absolution is only for grave necessity.</p>`,
 [["Temporal punishment","The purification still needed after forgiveness, because of the unhealthy attachment that sin leaves."],["Plenary indulgence","Remission of all temporal punishment; requires Confession, Communion, prayer for the Pope's intentions and freedom from attachment to sin."]],
 [["2 Macc 12:43-46: that they may be loosed from sins","2-maccabees,12,43,46"],["Col 1:24: I fill up those things that are wanting","colossians,1,24,24"]],
 [["Supplement q.25 a.1 (from Thomas's Sentences commentary)","Indulgences have real value, because of the unity of the Mystical Body and the superabundant merits of Christ and the saints."]],
 "Look at the indulgence notes on the Mass page. Is there one you could gain for someone who has died?",
 CQ("What does an indulgence remit?",["Sins not yet confessed","Temporal punishment due to sins already forgiven","The need for Confession"],1,"CCC 1471."));
cccN("c59","p2s2","1499–1532","__P4J.HTM","Anointing of the Sick","Christ comes close to the seriously ill and the elderly in this sacrament, giving strength, peace, union with his Passion, and forgiveness.",
 `<p>Illness confronts us with our limits and can lead to anguish or to God. Christ healed the sick as a sign of the Kingdom and sent his disciples to anoint and heal. James describes the presbyters praying over the sick and anointing them.</p><p>The sacrament is for those in danger of death from illness or old age, and it can be repeated. Only priests and bishops give it, anointing the forehead and hands with blessed oil. Its graces: strength, peace and courage; union with the Passion of Christ; grace for the Church; forgiveness of sins if one cannot confess; restoration of health if it helps salvation; and preparation for the final journey. Viaticum, Communion for the dying, is the last sacrament of the Christian.</p>`,
 [["Viaticum","\"Food for the journey\": Holy Communion given to the dying."]],
 [["Jas 5:14-15: is any man sick among you?","james,5,14,15"],["Mark 6:12-13: anointed with oil many that were sick","mark,6,12,13"]],
 [["ST III q.65 a.1","Anointing corresponds to the healing that completes recovery: it removes the remnants of sin and strengthens the soul."]],
 "Is someone you know seriously ill? Encourage them, or their family, to ask for the Anointing.",
 CQ("Is the Anointing of the Sick only for people about to die?",["Yes","No, for anyone in danger of death from illness or old age, and it can be repeated","Only after an accident"],1,"CCC 1514–1515."));
cccN("c60","p2s2","1533–1600","__P4Q.HTM","Holy Orders","Through Holy Orders, Christ's mission to the apostles continues in bishops, priests and deacons, who serve the Church in his name.",
 `<p>Holy Orders and Matrimony are directed to the salvation of others. Holy Orders is the sacrament of apostolic ministry, in three degrees: bishop (the fullness of the sacrament), priest and deacon. The ministerial priesthood is essentially different from the common priesthood of the faithful, and it is at its service.</p><p>The essential rite is the bishop's laying on of hands with the prayer of consecration. Only a validly ordained bishop can ordain. Only a baptised man validly receives the sacrament; the Church considers itself bound by Christ's choice of the Twelve. In the Latin Church priests are normally chosen from men who will remain celibate. Orders confers an indelible character: the ordained acts <i>in persona Christi</i>.</p>`,
 [["In persona Christi","\"In the person of Christ\": the priest acts as Christ the head in the sacraments."],["Apostolic succession","The unbroken line of bishops from the apostles."]],
 [["Heb 5:1-6: every high priest taken from among men","hebrews,5,1,6"],["2 Tim 1:6: the grace which is in thee by the imposition of my hands","2-timothy,1,6,6"]],
 [["ST III q.63 a.3","The character of Orders configures the priest to Christ's priesthood."],["ST Supplement q.34 a.1","Orders exists so that some may hand on the sacraments to others."]],
 "Pray today for your parish priest, by name, and for vocations from your community.",
 CQ("What are the three degrees of Holy Orders?",["Pope, cardinal, bishop","Bishop, priest, deacon","Priest, religious, lay minister"],1,"CCC 1536, 1554."));
cccN("c61","p2s2","1601–1666","__P50.HTM","Marriage","Marriage is a lifelong covenant between a man and a woman, ordered to their good and to children; between the baptised it is a sacrament.",
 `<p>God himself is the author of marriage. Its vocation is written in human nature. Sin wounded it, the Law protected it, and Christ restored it to its original meaning, raising it to a sacrament: a sign of his love for the Church.</p><p>The spouses themselves are the ministers, exchanging consent freely before the Church. The essential properties of marriage are unity and indissolubility, and it is open to fertility. A valid, consummated sacramental marriage cannot be dissolved; a declaration of nullity (annulment) says that a true marriage never existed. The family is the "domestic church", the first school of Christian life.</p>`,
 [["Indissolubility","A valid sacramental marriage cannot be dissolved by any human power."],["Domestic church","The Christian family as the Church in miniature."]],
 [["Gen 2:18-24: they shall be two in one flesh","genesis,2,18,24"],["Matt 19:3-9: what God hath joined","matthew,19,3,9"],["Eph 5:25-32: a great sacrament","ephesians,5,25,32"]],
 [["SCG III.123","Marriage must be lifelong and exclusive because it is ordered to raising children and to the friendship of the spouses, the greatest friendship."],["ST Supplement q.42 a.1","Marriage is a sacrament because it signifies the union of Christ and the Church."]],
 "If you're preparing for marriage someday, what virtue would you like to grow in first?",
 CQ("Who are the ministers of the sacrament of Marriage in the Latin Church?",["The priest","The spouses themselves","The witnesses"],1,"CCC 1623."));
cccN("c62","p2s2","1667–1690","__P58.HTM","Sacramentals and Christian funerals","Sacramentals (blessings, holy water, rosaries) prepare us for grace; Christian funerals commend the dead to God.",
 `<p>Sacramentals are sacred signs instituted by the Church, which resemble the sacraments and dispose us to receive grace and to sanctify everyday life. Blessings come first: of persons, meals, objects and places. Some consecrate permanently (as with a church or a chalice). Exorcism is also a sacramental. Popular piety (relics, pilgrimages, the Rosary, medals) is to be encouraged, guided by the liturgy.</p><p>For the Christian, death is the last Passover. The funeral rite commends the dead to God's mercy, supports the community in hope, and expresses communion with the departed. It includes the greeting of the community, the Word, the Eucharist, and the farewell.</p>`,
 [["Sacramental","A sacred sign instituted by the Church to prepare us for grace."]],
 [["1 Tim 4:4-5: sanctified by the word of God and prayer","1-timothy,4,4,5"],["1 Thess 4:12-17 (13-18): that you be not sorrowful","1-thessalonians,4,12,17"]],
 [["ST III q.65 a.1 ad 6","Thomas distinguishes the sacraments from other sacred signs and actions that prepare for them."]],
 "Bless yourself with holy water next time you enter a church, recalling your Baptism.",
 CQ("How do sacramentals differ from sacraments?",["They are the same","They are instituted by the Church and prepare us for grace, rather than conferring it as sacraments do","They are only for priests"],1,"CCC 1667, 1670."));

// ---------- Catechism notes: Part Three, Life in Christ (§§ 1691–2557) ----------
cccN("c63","p3s1c1","1691–1715","__P5D.HTM","Christian, recognise your dignity","The moral life starts not with rules but with who we are: made in God's image, redeemed by Christ, called to beatitude.",
 `<p>"Christian, recognise your dignity" (St Leo the Great). Part Three begins with who we are. Incorporated into Christ by Baptism, Christians are called to live a life worthy of the Gospel, a life in the Spirit.</p><p>The human person is made in the image of God: endowed with a spiritual soul, intellect and free will, and directed from conception to eternal beatitude. Wounded by original sin, we remain inclined to evil and subject to error; but Christ, by his Passion, has freed us and given us new life in the Spirit.</p>`,
 [["Moral life","Living our dignity as God's children through free, good acts, under grace."]],
 [["Phil 1:27: conversation worthy of the gospel","philippians,1,27,27"],["Gen 1:27: to the image of God he created him","genesis,1,27,27"]],
 [["ST I-II prologue","The second part of the Summa studies the human person as God's image, the source of his own actions with free will and power over his works."]],
 "How would your choices this week change if you remembered your dignity as God's child?",
 CQ("Where does the Catechism begin its teaching on morality?",["With a list of sins","With the dignity of the person made in God's image","With civil law"],1,"CCC 1691–1700."));
cccN("c64","p3s1c1","1716–1729","__P5I.HTM","The Beatitudes and our desire for happiness","God put a desire for happiness in every heart, and only he can satisfy it; the Beatitudes describe that happiness and the way to it.",
 `<p>The Beatitudes are at the heart of Jesus' preaching. They depict his own face and his charity; they express the vocation of the faithful; and they shed light on the actions and attitudes of the Christian life.</p><p>The desire for happiness is of divine origin: God has placed it in the human heart in order to draw us to himself, who alone can satisfy it. Christian beatitude means seeing God, entering into his joy and rest. It purifies our hearts of bad instincts and teaches that true happiness is not in riches, fame, power, science or any creature, but in God alone.</p>`,
 [["Beatitude","Blessedness: the happiness of seeing God and sharing his life."]],
 [["Matt 5:1-12: the Beatitudes","matthew,5,1,12"],["2 Pet 1:4: partakers of the divine nature","2-peter,1,4,4"]],
 [["ST I-II q.2 a.8","Happiness cannot consist in any created good, but only in God."],["ST I-II q.69 a.1","The Beatitudes are acts flowing from the virtues and gifts, which are rewarded with beatitude."]],
 "Which Beatitude describes where you are now? Which do you most need?",
 CQ("Who alone can satisfy our desire for happiness?",["Success","God","Other people"],1,"CCC 1718."));
cccN("c65","p3s1c1","1730–1748","__P5M.HTM","Freedom","Freedom is the power to act deliberately; it reaches perfection when directed to God, and the more we do good, the freer we become.",
 `<p>God created us rational, giving us dignity as persons who can initiate and control our own actions. Freedom is the power, rooted in reason and will, to act or not to act, and so to shape one's own life. It makes us responsible for our acts to the extent that they are voluntary.</p><p>Freedom is not the right to say or do anything. The more one does good, the freer one becomes; choosing evil is an abuse of freedom that leads to slavery to sin. Responsibility can be diminished or even removed by ignorance, inadvertence, duress, fear, habits, inordinate attachments and other psychological or social factors. Christ's grace does not compete with our freedom: it sets it free.</p>`,
 [["Imputability","The degree to which an act can be attributed to someone as its responsible author."]],
 [["Gal 5:1, 13: called unto liberty","galatians,5,13,13"],["John 8:31-36: the truth shall make you free","john,8,31,36"]],
 [["ST I-II q.6 a.8","Ignorance can make an act involuntary, depending on whether it precedes, accompanies or follows the will."],["ST I q.83 a.1","Man has free will because he acts by judgement of reason, not by instinct alone."]],
 "Is there a habit that makes you less free? What small step would increase your freedom?",
 CQ("Which can diminish responsibility for an act, according to CCC 1735?",["Only external force","Ignorance, duress, fear, habit, inordinate attachments and other psychological or social factors","Nothing"],1,"CCC 1735."),
 "CCC 1735 is one of the clearest bridges to clinical psychology: compulsive behaviours, addiction, trauma responses and severe anxiety can genuinely reduce freedom, and so culpability, without making the acts good. That's why moral judgement of acts and pastoral judgement of persons are distinct.");
cccN("c66","p3s1c1","1749–1761","__P5Q.HTM","The morality of human acts","An act is good when its object, intention and circumstances are all good; some acts are always wrong, whatever the intention.",
 `<p>Freedom makes us moral subjects. The morality of human acts depends on three sources: the <b>object</b> chosen (what is done), the <b>intention</b> (why it is done), and the <b>circumstances</b> (which can increase or reduce goodness or guilt but cannot change the object's moral quality).</p><p>A morally good act requires all three to be good. A good intention does not make a bad act good: the end does not justify the means. Some acts, such as blasphemy, murder and adultery, are always gravely wrong by their object, independently of circumstances and intentions. It is wrong to do evil so that good may come of it.</p>`,
 [["Object","What the acting person chooses to do, as understood by reason."],["Intrinsically evil","An act always wrong by its object, whatever the intention or circumstances."]],
 [["Rom 3:8: let us do evil that good may come? God forbid","romans,3,8,8"]],
 [["ST I-II q.18 a.4","An act's goodness comes from its object, end and circumstances; it is good only if all are good (\"good from a whole cause, evil from any defect\")."]],
 "Think of a time you justified a wrong act by a good intention. What would the three sources say?",
 CQ("Can a good intention make a wrong act good?",["Yes, always","No; the end does not justify the means","Only in emergencies"],1,"CCC 1753, 1759."));
cccN("c67","p3s1c1","1762–1775","__P5U.HTM","The passions (emotions)","Emotions are part of being human; in themselves neither good nor bad, they become morally good when reason and will direct them to the good.",
 `<p>The passions, or emotions, are movements of feeling that incline us to act or not act in regard to something felt or imagined to be good or evil. Love, aroused by the attraction of the good, is the most fundamental; others include desire and fear, joy and sadness, and anger.</p><p>In themselves the passions are neither good nor evil. They are morally qualified only insofar as they depend on reason and will. Moral perfection is not to suppress them but to integrate them: the more they are directed by right reason to the good, the more they contribute to a good act. Strong feelings are not decisive for the morality or holiness of a person; they are a reservoir of images and affections in which the moral life is expressed.</p>`,
 [["Passion","In Thomas's sense, an emotion: a movement of the sensory appetite in response to good or evil."]],
 [["Mark 14:33-36: he began to fear and to be heavy","mark,14,33,36"],["Eph 4:26: be angry, and sin not","ephesians,4,26,26"]],
 [["ST I-II qq.22–48","Thomas's treatise on the passions names eleven basic emotions: love, hatred, desire, aversion, joy, sorrow, hope, despair, fear, daring and anger."],["ST I-II q.24 a.3","Passions that follow and are guided by reason increase the goodness of an act."]],
 "What emotion was strongest in you today? What good was it pointing to?",
 CQ("Are emotions in themselves sinful?",["Yes, especially anger","No; they are morally qualified only as they are governed by reason and will","Only negative ones"],1,"CCC 1767."),
 "Thomas's view anticipates modern emotion science: appraisal theories see emotions as evaluations of something as good or bad for us, and emotion-regulation research (Gross, 1998) describes reshaping emotions through attention and reappraisal, close to Thomas's \"political\" rule of reason over the passions, rather than \"despotic\" suppression.");
cccN("c68","p3s1c1","1776–1802","__P5Y.HTM","Conscience","Conscience is reason's judgement about the goodness of an act; it must be formed, followed, and corrected when it errs.",
 `<p>Deep within, we discover a law we did not give ourselves but must obey: conscience, our most secret core and sanctuary, where we are alone with God. Conscience is a judgement of reason by which a person recognises the moral quality of a concrete act, before, during and after.</p><p>Conscience must be informed and enlightened: formation is a lifelong task, guided by the Word of God, prayer, the advice of others, and the Church's teaching. We must always obey the certain judgement of our conscience. But conscience can err: ignorance can be culpable (when a person takes little trouble to find the truth) or not. Some rules always apply: never do evil that good may come of it; the Golden Rule; and respect for others' consciences.</p>`,
 [["Conscience","A judgement of practical reason about the moral quality of a concrete act."],["Erroneous conscience","A conscience that judges wrongly; culpable if one neglected to seek the truth."]],
 [["Rom 2:14-16: the work of the law written in their hearts","romans,2,14,16"],["1 John 3:19-21: if our heart reprehend us not","1-john,3,19,21"]],
 [["ST I-II q.19 a.5","Acting against conscience is always wrong, even when conscience errs."],["ST I-II q.19 a.6","Yet an erroneous conscience does not always excuse: not if the ignorance is voluntary or negligent."]],
 "What sources help you form your conscience? Which could you use more?",
 CQ("Must we always follow a certain conscience?",["No, only if the Church agrees","Yes, though we must also form it, since it can err","Only in small matters"],1,"CCC 1790–1794."),
 "Moral psychology shows that intuitions can be quick and biased, with reasons supplied afterwards (Haidt, 2001). The Church's insistence on forming conscience, slowly, with others and with the truth, is a safeguard against exactly this.");
cccN("c69","p3s1c1","1803–1845","__P64.HTM","The virtues","Virtues are firm habits of doing good: four cardinal (human) virtues, three theological virtues, and the Spirit's gifts and fruits.",
 `<p>A virtue is a habitual and firm disposition to do the good. Human virtues are acquired by education, deliberate acts and perseverance, and purified by grace. Four play a pivotal ("cardinal") role: <b>prudence</b> (right reason in action), <b>justice</b> (giving God and neighbour their due), <b>fortitude</b> (firmness in difficulties) and <b>temperance</b> (moderating the attraction of pleasures).</p><p>The theological virtues, <b>faith</b>, <b>hope</b> and <b>charity</b>, are infused by God and relate directly to him; charity is the greatest, the form of all the virtues. The seven gifts of the Holy Spirit make us docile to his promptings, and his fruits (charity, joy, peace, patience…) are the first-fruits of eternal glory.</p>`,
 [["Cardinal virtues","Prudence, justice, fortitude and temperance: the \"hinges\" (cardo) of the moral life."],["Theological virtues","Faith, hope and charity: given by God, directed to God."]],
 [["Wis 8:7: she teacheth temperance, prudence, justice and fortitude","wisdom,8,7,7"],["1 Cor 13:13: faith, hope, charity; the greatest is charity","1-corinthians,13,13,13"],["Gal 5:22-23: the fruit of the Spirit","galatians,5,22,23"]],
 [["ST I-II q.61 a.2","Why there are exactly four cardinal virtues: reason itself (prudence), operations (justice), and the passions to be restrained (temperance) or strengthened (fortitude)."],["ST I-II q.68 a.1","The gifts of the Spirit dispose us to follow his promptings readily."]],
 "Which cardinal virtue is weakest in you? Choose one small daily act to train it.",
 CQ("Which are the cardinal virtues?",["Faith, hope, charity, humility","Prudence, justice, fortitude, temperance","Wisdom, piety, counsel, fear of the Lord"],1,"CCC 1805."),
 "Virtue is built like habits are built: by repeated acts in a stable context. Lally et al. (2010, European Journal of Social Psychology, N = 96) found new habits took a median of about 66 days to become automatic, with wide variation; missing a single day did not undo progress. Thomas would agree that virtue grows by acts, and that grace heals and raises it.");
cccN("c70","p3s1c1","1846–1876","__P69.HTM","Sin","Sin is an offence against God and a failure in love; mortal sin destroys charity, venial sin wounds it, and God's mercy is greater than both.",
 `<p>The Gospel is the revelation of God's mercy to sinners. To receive it we must admit our faults. Sin is an offence against reason, truth and right conscience; a failure in genuine love for God and neighbour caused by a perverse attachment to certain goods.</p><p>Sins can be distinguished by their object, by the virtues they oppose, or by whether they concern God, neighbour or self. <b>Mortal sin</b> requires grave matter, full knowledge and deliberate consent; it destroys charity and, unrepented, leads to exclusion from the Kingdom. <b>Venial sin</b> weakens charity. Repeated sins produce vices; the capital sins are pride, avarice, envy, wrath, lust, gluttony and sloth. We can also cooperate in others' sins, and sin can create "structures of sin" in society.</p>`,
 [["Mortal sin","Sin of grave matter, committed with full knowledge and deliberate consent."],["Capital sins","Seven vices that give rise to other sins: pride, avarice, envy, wrath, lust, gluttony, sloth (acedia)."]],
 [["Rom 5:20: where sin abounded, grace did more abound","romans,5,20,20"],["1 John 5:16-17: a sin unto death","1-john,5,16,17"],["Luke 15:1-10: the lost sheep","luke,15,1,10"]],
 [["ST I-II q.71 a.6","Sin is \"a word, deed or desire contrary to the eternal law\" (Augustine)."],["ST I-II q.88 a.1","Mortal and venial sin differ as a turning away from God and a disorder about the means while still ordered to God."]],
 "Which capital sin is the root of most of your struggles? Pray for the opposite virtue.",
 CQ("Which three conditions make a sin mortal?",["Grave matter, full knowledge, deliberate consent","Harm to others, publicity, repetition","Breaking a commandment, intention, regret"],0,"CCC 1857."));
cccN("c71","p3s1c2","1877–1896","__P6F.HTM","The person and society","We are made for communion; society is needed, but the person is its origin and purpose, and conversion of heart comes before the reform of structures.",
 `<p>The human vocation is to show forth God's image and be transformed into the image of the Son. That vocation has a communal character: there is a resemblance between the union of the divine Persons and the fraternity people are to build. Society is necessary, and the person is and ought to be its principle, subject and end.</p><p>Subsidiarity means that a higher community should not take over what a smaller one can do, but should support it. Inner conversion of heart is needed for lasting changes in society, though changing unjust institutions is also required.</p>`,
 [["Subsidiarity","Higher bodies should not absorb what lower ones (families, communities) can do, but should help them."]],
 [["Gen 2:18: it is not good for man to be alone","genesis,2,18,18"],["John 17:21-22: that they may be one, as we also are one","john,17,21,22"]],
 [["ST I-II q.90 a.2","Law is ordered to the common good, because the person is part of a community."]],
 "Which small community (family, residence, group) could you strengthen this week?",
 CQ("What does \"subsidiarity\" mean?",["The state should do everything","Higher bodies shouldn't take over what smaller communities can do, but help them","Everyone for themselves"],1,"CCC 1883."));
cccN("c72","p3s1c2","1897–1927","__P6J.HTM","Authority, the common good and participation","Legitimate authority comes from God and serves the common good; every citizen is called to participate responsibly.",
 `<p>Every human community needs authority to ensure order and promote the common good. Its basis is in human nature and ultimately in God. Authority is exercised legitimately only when it seeks the common good by morally licit means; unjust laws do not bind in conscience.</p><p>The common good is the sum of the conditions of social life that allow people to reach their fulfilment more fully and easily. It includes respect for the person, social well-being and development, and peace (stability and security of a just order). Everyone should take part, according to their position and role, in promoting it: through personal responsibility at work and at home, and through public life.</p>`,
 [["Common good","The social conditions that allow everyone to flourish more fully and easily."]],
 [["Rom 13:1-7: be subject to higher powers","romans,13,1,7"],["Acts 5:29: we ought to obey God rather than men","acts,5,29,29"]],
 [["ST I-II q.96 a.4","Unjust laws, contrary to the human good or to God, are more violence than law and do not bind in conscience."]],
 "Name one way you could take part in the common good of your city, university or parish.",
 CQ("Does an unjust law bind in conscience?",["Yes, always","No","Only if it's a constitution"],1,"CCC 1902–1903; ST I-II q.96 a.4."));
cccN("c73","p3s1c2","1928–1948","__P6N.HTM","Social justice and solidarity","Social justice flows from the dignity of every person; differences are a call to share, and solidarity is a Christian virtue.",
 `<p>Society ensures social justice when it provides conditions allowing associations and individuals to obtain what is their due. Social justice can only be achieved in respect for the transcendent dignity of each person. Everyone must see their neighbour as "another self", above all the poor and the excluded.</p><p>All people share the same nature, origin and dignity, so every form of discrimination is to be overcome; yet differences of talents are part of God's plan, so that we need and share with one another. Sinful inequalities contradict the Gospel. Solidarity, or "friendship", is a direct demand of human and Christian brotherhood, expressed in the fair distribution of goods and pay for work.</p>`,
 [["Solidarity","Firm commitment to the common good, because we are all responsible for all."]],
 [["Matt 25:40: as long as you did it to one of these my least brethren","matthew,25,40,40"],["Gal 3:28: neither Jew nor Greek","galatians,3,28,28"]],
 [["ST II-II q.58 a.6","Legal (general) justice directs every virtue towards the common good."]],
 "In South Africa, where is the call to solidarity most urgent around you?",
 CQ("What does the Catechism say about unjust (sinful) inequalities?",["They are God's will","They contradict the Gospel","They are only economic"],1,"CCC 1938."));
cccN("c74","p3s1c3","1949–1986","__P6S.HTM","The moral law: natural, Old and New","God's law is a fatherly instruction: the natural law written in our hearts, the Law of Moses, and the New Law of grace.",
 `<p>Moral law is the work of divine Wisdom: fatherly instruction that prescribes the ways leading to happiness. <b>The natural law</b> is our participation in God's wisdom; it is universal and unchangeable, the foundation of moral and civil law. <b>The Old Law</b>, summed up in the Ten Commandments, is the first stage of revealed law; it prepares for the Gospel.</p><p><b>The New Law</b>, or Law of the Gospel, is the perfection of the divine law: it is the grace of the Holy Spirit given through faith in Christ, working through charity. It is expressed above all in the Sermon on the Mount and is a law of love, grace and freedom. The evangelical counsels are added to the commandments for those called to them.</p>`,
 [["Natural law","The light of reason by which we know what is good and evil; our share in God's eternal law."],["New Law","The grace of the Holy Spirit, given through faith in Christ."]],
 [["Rom 2:14-15: the law written in their hearts","romans,2,14,15"],["Matt 5:17: not to destroy, but to fulfil","matthew,5,17,17"],["Rom 13:10: love is the fulfilling of the law","romans,13,10,10"]],
 [["ST I-II q.91 a.2","The natural law is nothing else than the rational creature's participation in the eternal law."],["ST I-II q.106 a.1","The New Law is chiefly the grace of the Holy Spirit, given to those who believe in Christ."]],
 "Thomas says the New Law is first of all grace, not a list of rules. How does that change how you see the commandments?",
 CQ("What is the New Law \"chiefly\"?",["A stricter set of rules","The grace of the Holy Spirit, given through faith in Christ","The Church's canon law"],1,"CCC 1965–1966; ST I-II q.106 a.1."));
cccN("c75","p3s1c3","1987–2029","__P6Y.HTM","Grace and justification","God justifies us by grace, freely, through faith and Baptism; our merit is itself his gift, and all are called to holiness.",
 `<p>The grace of the Holy Spirit can justify us: cleanse us from sin and communicate God's righteousness through faith and Baptism. Justification is the most excellent work of God's love; it is God's free initiative, yet it involves our free cooperation.</p><p><b>Grace</b> is favour, the free and undeserved help God gives us to respond to his call. Sanctifying grace is a stable gift that heals and divinises us; actual graces are God's interventions at particular moments. <b>Merit</b>: in strict justice we can merit nothing from God, but by his loving plan, our good works done in grace merit reward, because it is God who crowns his own gifts in us. All Christians are called to holiness: the fullness of Christian life and the perfection of charity.</p>`,
 [["Justification","Being made righteous: forgiveness of sins and inner renewal, by grace."],["Sanctifying grace","The habitual gift of God's own life in the soul."]],
 [["Rom 3:21-26: justified freely by his grace","romans,3,21,26"],["Eph 2:8-10: by grace you are saved","ephesians,2,8,10"],["Phil 2:12-13: it is God who worketh in you","philippians,2,12,13"]],
 [["ST I-II q.113 a.9","The justification of the ungodly is a greater work than the creation of heaven and earth."],["ST I-II q.114 a.1","We merit only by God's ordination: he crowns his own gifts."]],
 "Thank God today for one grace you know you did not deserve.",
 CQ("Can we merit grace from God in strict justice?",["Yes, by good works","No; merit itself is God's gift, by his loving plan","Only saints can"],1,"CCC 2007–2011."));
cccN("c76","p3s1c3","2030–2051","__P73.HTM","The Church, mother and teacher; the precepts","The Church guides our moral life by her teaching, and the five precepts set the minimum of prayer and moral effort.",
 `<p>We live our vocation in the Church, where we receive the Word, grace and the example of holiness. The Magisterium teaches on morals too, including the natural law, because observing it is necessary for salvation. Christians should respect and follow it, forming their conscience by it.</p><p>The five precepts of the Church are an indispensable minimum: attend Mass on Sundays and holy days and rest from servile work; confess your sins at least once a year; receive Communion at least during the Easter season; observe the days of fasting and abstinence; and help provide for the Church's needs. A Christian life lived faithfully is itself a witness that draws others.</p>`,
 [["Precepts of the Church","Five basic obligations setting the minimum for Christian life."]],
 [["Luke 10:16: he that heareth you heareth me","luke,10,16,16"],["Matt 5:16: so let your light shine","matthew,5,16,16"]],
 [["ST II-II q.147 a.3","The Church can establish fasts as a fitting determination of the natural duty to do penance."]],
 "Which precept do you find hardest? What would help you keep it?",
 CQ("How often must Catholics confess (at minimum)?",["Weekly","At least once a year (when conscious of grave sin)","Never"],1,"CCC 2042."));
cccN("c77","p3s2c1","2052–2082","__P78.HTM","The Ten Commandments","The commandments are God's word to his people, a path of life, summed up by Jesus in love of God and neighbour.",
 `<p>"Teacher, what must I do?" Jesus answers: keep the commandments, and follow me. The Ten Commandments (Decalogue, \"ten words\") were given by God on Sinai within the covenant: they express what it means to belong to him. They are a privileged expression of the natural law.</p><p>Jesus summed them up in two: love God with all your heart, and your neighbour as yourself. The first three concern love of God, the other seven love of neighbour. They form an organic unity: to break one is to offend against all. They bind gravely, and what God commands he makes possible by his grace.</p>`,
 [["Decalogue","The Ten Commandments; literally \"ten words\"."]],
 [["Exod 20:1-17: the Ten Commandments","exodus,20,1,17"],["Matt 19:16-21: if thou wilt enter into life","matthew,19,16,21"],["Matt 22:37-40: on these two commandments","matthew,22,37,40"]],
 [["ST I-II q.100 a.3","All the moral precepts reduce to the Ten Commandments, as conclusions to principles."],["ST I-II q.100 a.8","The precepts of the Decalogue admit no dispensation."]],
 "Pray through the Ten Commandments as an examination of conscience tonight.",
 CQ("How did Jesus sum up the Commandments?",["Do no harm","Love God with all your heart, and your neighbour as yourself","Obey the Law of Moses exactly"],1,"CCC 2055."));
cccN("c78","p3s2c1","2083–2141","__P7A.HTM","The First Commandment","Worship God alone: by faith, hope and charity, adoration and prayer; avoid idolatry, superstition and irreligion.",
 `<p>"I am the Lord your God… you shall have no other gods before me." The first commandment calls us to believe in God, hope in him and love him above all. It requires worship: adoration, prayer, sacrifice, promises and vows. It includes the right to religious freedom: no one may be forced to act against their conscience in religious matters.</p><p>It forbids superstition (attributing power to practices apart from the right interior attitude), idolatry (divinising what is not God: power, money, pleasure, the state), divination and magic (astrology, horoscopes, mediums), irreligion (sacrilege, simony), atheism and agnosticism. Veneration of sacred images is not idolatry, because honour paid to an image passes to the one it portrays.</p>`,
 [["Idolatry","Giving to a creature the worship due to God alone; includes money, power or pleasure."],["Simony","Buying or selling spiritual things."]],
 [["Exod 20:2-5: thou shalt not have strange gods","exodus,20,2,5"],["Matt 4:10: the Lord thy God shalt thou adore","matthew,4,10,10"]],
 [["ST II-II q.81 a.6","Religion is the virtue by which we give God the worship due to him; it is the greatest moral virtue."],["ST II-II q.94 a.1","Idolatry is giving divine worship to a creature."]],
 "What \"other god\" competes most with the true God in your life?",
 CQ("Is venerating an icon or statue idolatry?",["Yes","No; the honour passes to the one portrayed","Only if it's expensive"],1,"CCC 2132."));
cccN("c79","p3s2c1","2142–2167","__P7I.HTM","The Second Commandment","Honour God's holy name: never use it to blaspheme, swear falsely, or curse.",
 `<p>"You shall not take the name of the Lord your God in vain." Among all the words of revelation, God's name is unique; it is entrusted only to those who trust him. We should use it only to bless, praise and glorify. Promises made to others in God's name engage his honour.</p><p>The commandment forbids blasphemy (speaking against God, the Church, the saints or holy things), false oaths and perjury, and using God's name to justify crimes. Christians are baptised in the name of the Trinity and receive their own name in Baptism: every name is sacred, and God calls each of us by name.</p>`,
 [["Blasphemy","Words of hatred, reproach or defiance against God, the Church or holy things."],["Perjury","Making a false promise under oath."]],
 [["Exod 20:7: thou shalt not take the name of the Lord in vain","exodus,20,7,7"],["Matt 5:33-37: swear not at all","matthew,5,33,37"],["Isa 43:1: I have called thee by thy name","isaiah,43,1,1"]],
 [["ST II-II q.13 a.2","Blasphemy is always a mortal sin by its kind, because it is directly against God's goodness."],["ST II-II q.89 a.3","An oath is lawful when made with truth, judgement and justice."]],
 "When you hear God's name misused this week, silently say: \"Blessed be the name of the Lord.\"",
 CQ("What does the Second Commandment protect?",["The Sabbath","God's holy name","Our parents"],1,"CCC 2142."));
cccN("c80","p3s2c1","2168–2195","__P7N.HTM","The Third Commandment","Keep the Lord's Day holy: Christians celebrate Sunday, the day of the Resurrection, with Mass, rest and charity.",
 `<p>"Remember the Sabbath day, to keep it holy." The Sabbath recalled creation and the liberation from Egypt; Jesus honoured it and is its Lord. For Christians, Sunday, the first day and the day of the Resurrection, fulfils the Sabbath.</p><p>The Sunday Eucharist is the foundation of Christian practice. The faithful are obliged to take part in Mass on Sundays and holy days of obligation (or the evening before); deliberately failing to do so is a grave sin, unless excused for a serious reason such as illness or caring for infants. Sunday is also a day of rest, of family, of charity and of reflection. Christians should seek recognition of Sunday as a legal holiday, while respecting others' freedom.</p>`,
 [["Lord's Day","Sunday, the day of Christ's Resurrection."]],
 [["Exod 20:8-11: remember the sabbath","exodus,20,8,11"],["Mark 2:27-28: the sabbath was made for man","mark,2,27,28"],["Acts 20:7: the first day of the week","acts,20,7,7"]],
 [["ST II-II q.122 a.4 ad 1","The commandment is moral and natural in requiring that some time be given to God; the particular day is ceremonial and fixed by law."]],
 "Plan this Sunday: Mass, rest, and one act of charity.",
 CQ("Why do Christians keep Sunday rather than Saturday?",["Tradition only","Sunday is the day of Christ's Resurrection, fulfilling the Sabbath","It's more convenient"],1,"CCC 2174–2175."));
cccN("c81","p3s2c2","2196–2257","__P7Q.HTM","The Fourth Commandment","Honour your father and mother: the family is the original cell of society; it also covers our duties to authorities and theirs to us.",
 `<p>The fourth commandment opens the second table and concerns love of neighbour, beginning with the family. A man and a woman united in marriage, with their children, form a family; it is the original cell of social life and a domestic church.</p><p>Children owe their parents respect (filial piety), gratitude, obedience while they live at home, and care in old age. Parents must educate their children in faith and virtue, respect their vocation, and are the first heralds of the faith. The commandment extends to all legitimate authority: citizens should cooperate with the common good, pay taxes, vote and defend the country; authorities must serve the dignity of persons. One must refuse obedience to authorities when their commands are contrary to the moral order.</p>`,
 [["Filial piety","The respect, gratitude and care children owe their parents."]],
 [["Exod 20:12: honour thy father and thy mother","exodus,20,12,12"],["Eph 6:1-4: fathers, provoke not your children","ephesians,6,1,4"],["Sir 3:1-16: honour thy father","sirach,3,1,16"]],
 [["ST II-II q.101 a.1","Piety (pietas) is the virtue of giving honour and service to parents and country, as principles of our being."]],
 "Do one concrete thing this week to honour your parents or a parent figure.",
 CQ("Must one obey an authority that commands something immoral?",["Yes, always","No; one must obey God rather than men","Only if it's the government"],1,"CCC 2242."));
cccN("c82","p3s2c2","2258–2283","__P7Y.HTM","The Fifth Commandment: respect for life","Human life is sacred from conception to natural death; the deliberate killing of an innocent person is always gravely wrong.",
 `<p>Human life is sacred because from its beginning it involves God's creative action and remains in a special relationship with him. No one can claim the right directly to destroy an innocent human being. Legitimate defence is permitted, and can be a grave duty for those responsible for others. Regarding the death penalty, the Catechism (revised in 2018) teaches it is inadmissible.</p><p>Abortion and euthanasia are gravely contrary to the moral law. Human life must be protected from conception; embryos must be treated as persons. Suicide contradicts our natural inclination to life and love of self, neighbour and God; yet grave psychological disturbance, anguish or fear can diminish responsibility, and we should not despair of the salvation of those who have taken their own lives. The Church prays for them.</p>`,
 [["Legitimate defence","Protecting oneself or others, even if it causes the aggressor's death as an unintended effect."],["Euthanasia","Ending the life of the sick or dying, by act or omission, to eliminate suffering."]],
 [["Exod 20:13: thou shalt not kill","exodus,20,13,13"],["Gen 4:10: the voice of thy brother's blood","genesis,4,10,10"],["Ps 138:13-16 (139): thou hast protected me from my mother's womb","psalms,138,13,16"]],
 [["ST II-II q.64 a.7","Thomas's principle of double effect: self-defence is lawful when one intends to save one's life and the death of the aggressor is not intended."],["ST II-II q.64 a.6","It is never lawful to kill the innocent."]],
 "Who in your community most needs life to be protected and cared for: the unborn, the sick, the elderly, the lonely?",
 CQ("What does the Catechism say about those who die by suicide?",["They are certainly lost","Grave psychological disturbance can diminish responsibility; we should not despair of their salvation and should pray for them","Nothing"],1,"CCC 2282–2283. If you are struggling, please talk to someone; in South Africa SADAG can help (sadag.org)."),
 "CCC 2282 explicitly recognises that \"grave psychological disturbances, anguish, or grave fear\" can diminish responsibility: a clear point of contact with clinical understanding of suicidal crisis. The pastoral response is mercy, prayer and practical care.");
cccN("c83","p3s2c2","2284–2330","__P80.HTM","The Fifth Commandment: dignity and peace","Respect for persons includes not leading others into sin, caring for health, respecting the body, and working for peace.",
 `<p><b>Scandal</b>, an attitude or behaviour that leads another to sin, is grave when it comes from those who teach or lead. Life and health are gifts: we should take reasonable care of them, while rejecting a cult of the body. Drug use (except for strictly therapeutic reasons), abuse of food, alcohol or medicine, and reckless driving offend against this commandment.</p><p>Scientific research must respect the person: experiments without consent, organ trafficking and degrading treatment are forbidden. The dead must be treated with respect. Jesus calls us to <b>peace</b>, the tranquillity of order. Anger and hatred desiring another's evil are sins against charity. War must be avoided by every means; the strict conditions of legitimate defence by military force are the \"just war\" criteria.</p>`,
 [["Scandal","Leading another to sin by attitude or behaviour."],["Just war criteria","Strict conditions for legitimate defence by force: lasting grave damage, other means ineffective, serious prospect of success, no graver evils produced."]],
 [["Matt 18:6: whosoever shall scandalise one of these little ones","matthew,18,6,6"],["Matt 5:21-24: whosoever is angry with his brother","matthew,5,21,24"],["Matt 5:9: blessed are the peacemakers","matthew,5,9,9"]],
 [["ST II-II q.40 a.1","Thomas's conditions for a just war: legitimate authority, a just cause, and right intention."],["ST II-II q.29 a.1","Peace is \"the tranquillity of order\" (Augustine)."]],
 "Is there someone you are angry with? Pray for their good today.",
 CQ("What is \"scandal\" in the moral sense?",["Gossip","An attitude or behaviour that leads another to sin","A public embarrassment"],1,"CCC 2284."));
cccN("c84","p3s2c2","2331–2359","__P84.HTM","The Sixth Commandment: sexuality and chastity","Sexuality is a good gift, part of the whole person; chastity integrates it in love, and everyone is called to it in their state of life.",
 `<p>God is love and created man and woman in his image, so sexuality affects every aspect of the person and our capacity to love. Each person should acknowledge and accept their sexual identity; man and woman are equal in dignity and complementary.</p><p>Chastity is the successful integration of sexuality within the person: an apprenticeship in self-mastery, which is a training in human freedom. It is a moral virtue, a gift of God and the fruit of spiritual effort, lived differently by married, single and consecrated people. Offences against chastity include lust, masturbation, fornication, pornography, prostitution and rape. Persons with homosexual tendencies must be accepted with respect, compassion and sensitivity; they too are called to chastity. Chastity blossoms in friendship.</p>`,
 [["Chastity","The virtue that integrates sexuality within the whole person, in love and self-mastery."]],
 [["Gen 1:27: male and female he created them","genesis,1,27,27"],["Matt 5:27-28: whosoever shall look on a woman to lust after her","matthew,5,27,28"],["1 Cor 6:18-20: glorify God in your body","1-corinthians,6,18,20"]],
 [["ST II-II q.151 a.1","Chastity is a virtue because it subjects the body's desires to reason."],["ST II-II q.153 a.5","Lust's \"daughters\" include blindness of mind, rashness, inconstancy and self-love: it damages judgement itself."]],
 "Chastity \"blossoms in friendship\" (CCC 2347). Which friendships help you live it?",
 CQ("Who is called to chastity?",["Only priests and religious","Everyone, according to their state of life","Only the unmarried"],1,"CCC 2348–2349."));
cccN("c85","p3s2c2","2360–2400","__P86.HTM","Marriage: fidelity and fruitfulness","The love of husband and wife is faithful and fruitful; adultery, divorce, contraception and cohabitation offend against it.",
 `<p>Conjugal love has two ends: the good of the spouses and the transmission of life. These two meanings, unitive and procreative, cannot be separated. Spouses are called to responsible parenthood; periodic continence (natural family planning) respects this, whereas contraception separates the two meanings and is wrong. Children are a gift, not a right; techniques that dissociate the sexual act from procreation are morally unacceptable.</p><p>Offences against the dignity of marriage include adultery, divorce (which claims to break a valid contract), polygamy, incest, so-called free unions and trial marriages. The separated and the divorced who have not remarried can be exemplary witnesses; the divorced and civilly remarried remain members of the Church and should be accompanied with care.</p>`,
 [["Unitive and procreative","The two inseparable meanings of the marital act."],["Responsible parenthood","Generous and prudent decisions about having children, respecting the moral law."]],
 [["Gen 1:28: increase and multiply","genesis,1,28,28"],["Heb 13:4: marriage honourable in all","hebrews,13,4,4"],["Mal 2:14-16: let none despise the wife of his youth","malachi,2,14,16"]],
 [["SCG III.122","Thomas argues that sexual acts are rightly ordered when they respect their natural end and the good of the children who may come from them."],["ST Supplement q.49 a.2","The three goods of marriage (Augustine): offspring, fidelity and the sacrament."]],
 "What questions do you have about the Church's teaching on marriage? Bring them to a priest or a trusted mentor.",
 CQ("What are the two meanings of the marital act that must not be separated?",["Pleasure and duty","Unitive and procreative","Physical and emotional"],1,"CCC 2366."));
cccN("c86","p3s2c2","2401–2463","__P89.HTM","The Seventh Commandment","You shall not steal: respect others' goods, the universal destination of goods, just work and wages, care for creation and love for the poor.",
 `<p>The goods of creation are destined for the whole human race; the right to private property is legitimate but subordinate to that universal destination. The commandment forbids theft, keeping borrowed or lost things, fraud, unjust wages, tax evasion, forgery, waste and damage to public property. Stolen goods must be restored. It also requires care for animals and creation.</p><p>The Church's social doctrine judges economic and social matters: work is a duty and a right, wages must be just, and the economy exists for the person. Rich nations have duties of solidarity toward poor nations. Love for the poor, expressed in the works of mercy, is part of the Church's constant tradition.</p>`,
 [["Universal destination of goods","Created goods are meant for all; private property serves this end."],["Works of mercy","Corporal (feeding the hungry, sheltering the homeless…) and spiritual (counselling, consoling, forgiving…)."]],
 [["Exod 20:15: thou shalt not steal","exodus,20,15,15"],["Luke 19:8: if I have wronged any man, I restore him fourfold","luke,19,8,8"],["Jas 5:1-6: the hire of the labourers","james,5,1,6"]],
 [["ST II-II q.66 a.7","In extreme necessity all things are common: taking what one needs to survive is not theft."],["ST II-II q.66 a.2","Private property is lawful, but as to use, we should hold goods as common, sharing with those in need."]],
 "Which work of mercy could you do this week?",
 CQ("Is private property absolute, according to the Catechism?",["Yes","No; it is subordinate to the universal destination of goods","Property is wrong"],1,"CCC 2403."));
cccN("c87","p3s2c2","2464–2513","__P8H.HTM","The Eighth Commandment","Live in the truth: no lies, false witness, detraction or calumny; respect others' reputation, and use the media responsibly.",
 `<p>God is the source of all truth; Jesus is the Truth. The disciple is called to live in the truth, with simplicity of life, and to bear witness to it, even to martyrdom.</p><p>The commandment forbids false witness and perjury, rash judgement, detraction (revealing others' faults without valid reason), calumny (lies harming reputation), flattery, boasting, irony aimed at belittling, and lying: speaking a falsehood with the intention of deceiving. Damage must be repaired. Not everyone has the right to every truth: secrets and professional confidences must be kept, and the seal of confession is inviolable. Media must serve truth and the common good. Truth is also expressed in beauty, especially sacred art.</p>`,
 [["Detraction","Revealing another's real faults to those who didn't know, without objectively valid reason."],["Calumny","Harming another's reputation by lies."]],
 [["Exod 20:16: thou shalt not bear false witness","exodus,20,16,16"],["John 14:6: I am the way, and the truth","john,14,6,6"],["Eph 4:25: putting away lying, speak ye the truth","ephesians,4,25,25"]],
 [["ST II-II q.110 a.3","Every lie is a sin, because words are naturally signs of thought; to say what one doesn't think abuses speech."],["ST II-II q.73 a.2","Backbiting is grave because it takes away a person's good name, a great good."]],
 "Spend one day this week without exaggerating, flattering or gossiping. What did you notice?",
 CQ("What is \"detraction\"?",["Telling a lie about someone","Revealing someone's real faults without valid reason","Refusing to speak"],1,"CCC 2477."));
cccN("c88","p3s2c2","2514–2533","__P8P.HTM","The Ninth Commandment","Purity of heart: guard your desires, thoughts and gaze, because the pure in heart shall see God.",
 `<p>St John distinguishes three kinds of covetousness: lust of the flesh, lust of the eyes and pride of life. The ninth commandment forbids carnal concupiscence; the tenth, coveting another's goods.</p><p>The heart is the seat of moral personality. "Blessed are the pure in heart." Purity of heart is won by the virtue and gift of chastity, purity of intention, purity of vision (disciplining feelings and imagination, refusing complicity in impure thoughts) and prayer. Purity requires modesty, which protects the intimate centre of the person and guides how we look at others. Society needs a purification of the social climate, in media and culture.</p>`,
 [["Modesty","Protects the intimate centre of the person; refusing to unveil what should remain hidden."]],
 [["Matt 5:8: blessed are the clean of heart","matthew,5,8,8"],["1 John 2:16: concupiscence of the flesh, of the eyes, pride of life","1-john,2,16,16"],["Ps 50:12 (51): create a clean heart in me","psalms,50,12,12"]],
 [["ST II-II q.8 a.7","The gift of understanding corresponds to the beatitude of the pure in heart: it purifies the mind to see God."]],
 "What do you allow into your heart through your eyes (screens, media)? What one change would help?",
 CQ("Which Beatitude corresponds to the Ninth Commandment?",["Blessed are the poor in spirit","Blessed are the pure in heart","Blessed are the meek"],1,"CCC 2518."));
cccN("c89","p3s2c2","2534–2557","__P8T.HTM","The Tenth Commandment","Do not covet: avoid greed and envy, seek poverty of heart, and desire God above all.",
 `<p>The tenth commandment concerns the intentions of the heart: it forbids greed, avarice and the desire to acquire earthly goods without limit, and envy, sadness at the sight of another's goods. Envy is a capital sin and can lead to the gravest crimes; it is overcome by goodwill, humility and rejoicing in others' progress.</p><p>The Holy Spirit turns our desires to the good. Jesus calls his disciples to prefer him above everything and everyone, to poverty of heart: detachment from riches. "Blessed are the poor in spirit." The deepest desire of the human heart is expressed in the words: "I want to see God."</p>`,
 [["Envy","Sadness at another's good and the desire to have it, even unjustly."],["Poverty of heart","Detachment from riches, so that God is our treasure."]],
 [["Exod 20:17: thou shalt not covet","exodus,20,17,17"],["Matt 6:19-21: where thy treasure is","matthew,6,19,21"],["Matt 5:3: blessed are the poor in spirit","matthew,5,3,3"]],
 [["ST II-II q.36 a.1","Envy is sorrow at another's good insofar as it seems to lessen one's own excellence."],["ST II-II q.118 a.1","Covetousness is an immoderate love of possessing."]],
 "Whose success do you find hard to celebrate? Pray for them by name.",
 CQ("What is the deepest desire of the human heart, according to CCC 2557?",["To be rich","\"I want to see God\"","To be loved by everyone"],1,"CCC 2557, quoting St Teresa of Avila."),
 "Social comparison (Festinger, 1954) is a basic human tendency, and constant upward comparison, for example on social media, is associated with lower wellbeing. The tenth commandment's remedies, gratitude and rejoicing in others' good, have parallels in research on gratitude practices.");

// ---------- Catechism notes: Part Four, Christian Prayer (§§ 2558–2865) ----------
cccN("c90","p4s1","2558–2622","__P8Z.HTM","What prayer is, and how God taught it","Prayer is the raising of the heart to God: a gift, a covenant and a communion, revealed from Abraham and the Psalms to Jesus and Mary.",
 `<p>"For me, prayer is a surge of the heart, a simple look turned toward heaven" (St Thérèse). Prayer is the raising of one's mind and heart to God. It is a <b>gift</b> (we pray from humility, as beggars), a <b>covenant</b> (it comes from the heart, the place of encounter), and a <b>communion</b> with the Trinity.</p><p>God calls every person to prayer. In the Old Testament it is revealed through Abraham, Moses, David, Elijah and the Psalms, the masterwork of prayer. In the fullness of time Jesus prays: he prays to his Father, teaches his disciples to pray with faith and perseverance, and hears prayer addressed to him. Mary's prayer, at the Annunciation and Cana, is a model.</p>`,
 [["Prayer","The raising of mind and heart to God, or the requesting of good things from him."]],
 [["Ps 41:2-3 (42): my soul thirsteth for God","psalms,41,2,3"],["Luke 11:1-13: Lord, teach us to pray","luke,11,1,13"],["Matt 26:39: not as I will, but as thou wilt","matthew,26,39,39"]],
 [["ST II-II q.83 a.1","Prayer is an act of reason, expressing our desire to God: \"the interpreter of our desire\"."],["ST II-II q.83 a.2","We pray not to change God's plan but to obtain what he has planned to give through our prayer."]],
 "What does a \"surge of the heart\" towards God look like for you?",
 CQ("Why do we pray if God already knows what we need?",["To inform him","To obtain what he has planned to give through our prayer","There is no reason"],1,"ST II-II q.83 a.2; CCC 2559–2565."));
cccN("c91","p4s1","2623–2649","__P95.HTM","The forms of prayer","Blessing and adoration, petition, intercession, thanksgiving and praise: the five basic forms of Christian prayer.",
 `<p>The Holy Spirit teaches the Church the basic forms of prayer. <b>Blessing and adoration</b>: our response to God's gifts, and the homage of the creature before the Creator. <b>Petition</b>: asking, beginning with asking forgiveness, and above all for the Kingdom. <b>Intercession</b>: praying for others, even for enemies, as Jesus does.</p><p><b>Thanksgiving</b>: every event and need can become an offering of thanks. <b>Praise</b>: giving glory to God simply because he is, for his own sake. The Eucharist contains and expresses all the forms of prayer.</p>`,
 [["Adoration","Acknowledging God as Creator and Lord; the first attitude of the creature."],["Intercession","Asking on behalf of others."]],
 [["1 Tim 2:1-4: supplications, prayers, intercessions, thanksgivings","1-timothy,2,1,4"],["1 Thess 5:16-18: in all things give thanks","1-thessalonians,5,16,18"],["Ps 150:1-6: praise ye the Lord","psalms,150,1,5"]],
 [["ST II-II q.83 a.17","Thomas's four parts of prayer from 1 Timothy: supplications, prayers, intercessions and thanksgivings."]],
 "Today, pray using all five forms, one sentence each.",
 CQ("Which prayer contains and expresses all the forms of prayer?",["The Rosary","The Eucharist","The Our Father"],1,"CCC 2643."));
cccN("c92","p4s1","2650–2696","__P9C.HTM","Where prayer comes from, and who teaches it","We learn to pray from the Word, the liturgy and the virtues; we pray to the Father, through Jesus, in the Spirit, with Mary; and the family is the first school.",
 `<p>Prayer is not a spontaneous impulse only: we must learn it. The wellsprings of prayer are the Word of God, the liturgy, and the theological virtues; we also pray "today", in the present moment, where God meets us.</p><p>The way of prayer: we pray to the Father, through Jesus (the Jesus Prayer and devotion to the Sacred Heart), in the Holy Spirit ("Come, Holy Spirit"), in communion with Mary (the Hail Mary, the Rosary). Guides for prayer include the saints and the many spiritualities of the Church; the Christian family is the first place of education in prayer; ordained ministers, religious, catechists and prayer groups help too. Favourable places include a prayer corner at home, churches and monasteries.</p>`,
 [["Jesus Prayer","\"Lord Jesus Christ, Son of God, have mercy on me, a sinner\"."],["Spirituality","A particular tradition of living and praying the Gospel (e.g. Benedictine, Dominican, Ignatian)."]],
 [["Luke 18:13: O God, be merciful to me a sinner","luke,18,13,13"],["Luke 1:28, 42: Hail, full of grace","luke,1,28,28"],["Rom 8:26: the Spirit helpeth our infirmity","romans,8,26,26"]],
 [["ST II-II q.83 a.4","We pray to God alone as the source of grace, and to the saints as intercessors."]],
 "Set up or tidy a small prayer corner at home this week.",
 CQ("Where is the \"first place\" of education in prayer?",["The parish","The Christian family","The school"],1,"CCC 2685."));
cccN("c93","p4s1","2697–2724","__P9J.HTM","Vocal prayer, meditation and contemplation","Three expressions of prayer: vocal prayer with words, meditation that seeks with the mind, and contemplation, a gaze of faith fixed on Jesus.",
 `<p>We need to pray at specific times so as to learn to pray at all times. Christian tradition has three major expressions of prayer. <b>Vocal prayer</b>, with words spoken or sung, unites body and soul; Jesus taught one, the Our Father. <b>Meditation</b> is a quest: the mind seeks to understand the why and how of the Christian life, often with Scripture, icons or spiritual writers, to move to the conversion of the heart.</p><p><b>Contemplative prayer</b> is "nothing else than a close sharing between friends" (St Teresa of Avila): a gaze of faith fixed on Jesus, a silent love. It is a gift, received in humility and poverty. "I look at him and he looks at me," said a peasant to St John Vianney.</p>`,
 [["Meditation","Prayer that engages thought, imagination, emotion and desire to reflect on God's word."],["Contemplation","A loving gaze of faith on God; a gift of silent communion."]],
 [["Matt 6:6: pray to thy Father in secret","matthew,6,6,6"],["Ps 1:2: on his law he shall meditate day and night","psalms,1,2,2"],["Luke 10:38-42: Mary hath chosen the best part","luke,10,38,42"]],
 [["ST II-II q.180 a.4","Contemplation is principally the contemplation of divine truth, the goal of all human life."],["ST II-II q.83 a.12","Vocal prayer is useful because words stir up inner devotion."]],
 "Try five minutes of silent prayer today: just look at Jesus (perhaps before a crucifix) and let him look at you.",
 CQ("How does St Teresa of Avila describe contemplative prayer?",["A difficult technique","A close sharing between friends","Emptying the mind of everything"],1,"CCC 2709."),
 "Christian meditation is not the same as secular mindfulness: it is directed to a Person, not only attention to the present. Still, both train attention, and attention-training is linked with better emotion regulation; Christian contemplation adds the relationship that gives silence its meaning.");
cccN("c94","p4s1","2725–2758","__P9O.HTM","The battle of prayer","Prayer is a battle against ourselves and the tempter: distractions, dryness and discouragement are overcome by humility, trust and perseverance.",
 `<p>Prayer is both a gift of grace and a determined response on our part: it always presupposes effort. Objections come from false ideas of prayer (that it is psychology, or useless), from the world's values, and from our failures. The difficulties: <b>distraction</b> (turn back to the Lord humbly), <b>dryness</b> (stay faithful, like the grain of wheat), and <b>acedia</b>, a form of depression due to lax asceticism.</p><p>We may feel our prayer isn't heard. But God knows what we need; we should ask for the Spirit, trust as children, and persevere, praying always. "Pray constantly." Jesus' own prayer at the Last Supper, the priestly prayer (John 17), embraces the whole economy of salvation.</p>`,
 [["Acedia","Spiritual sloth: a disgust or listlessness about spiritual things."],["Dryness","Prayer without felt consolation; a time of faithful perseverance."]],
 [["1 Thess 5:17: pray without ceasing","1-thessalonians,5,17,17"],["Luke 18:1-8: always to pray, and not to faint","luke,18,1,8"],["John 17:1-26: the priestly prayer","john,17,1,26"]],
 [["ST II-II q.35 a.1","Acedia is sorrow about spiritual good, the good God offers us."],["ST II-II q.83 a.13","Prayer can be meritorious even with involuntary distractions, if one's first intention was to pray."]],
 "What is your biggest obstacle in prayer: distraction, dryness or busyness? Make one plan against it.",
 CQ("Does a distracted prayer still count?",["No","Yes; if we intended to pray, we turn back humbly, and it can still please God","Only if it's short"],1,"CCC 2729; ST II-II q.83 a.13."),
 "The Catechism distinguishes spiritual acedia from illness. Persistent low mood, loss of interest and exhaustion can also be clinical depression, which deserves professional care; spiritual guidance and medical help work together, not against each other.");
cccN("c95","p4s2","2759–2802","__P9V.HTM","The Our Father: \"Our Father, who art in heaven\"","The Lord's Prayer is the summary of the whole Gospel; we dare to call God \"Abba, Father\", together, and \"heaven\" means his majesty and our homeland.",
 `<p>When the disciples asked "Lord, teach us to pray," Jesus gave them the Our Father. It is "the summary of the whole Gospel" (Tertullian) and the most perfect of prayers (St Thomas). It is at the centre of the Scriptures and of the liturgy, prayed at Baptism and in every Mass.</p><p>"We dare to say": only Jesus could teach us to call God "Abba". It expresses filial trust and humble boldness. "Our" expresses a new relationship and our communion with all believers, and it excludes individualism. "Who art in heaven" does not mean a place but God's majesty and his presence in the hearts of the just; heaven is the Father's house, our true homeland.</p>`,
 [["Abba","Aramaic for \"Father\", the intimate word Jesus used."]],
 [["Matt 6:9-13: thus therefore shall you pray","matthew,6,9,13"],["Gal 4:6: crying Abba, Father","galatians,4,6,6"],["Rom 8:15: the spirit of adoption","romans,8,15,15"]],
 [["ST II-II q.83 a.9","The Lord's Prayer is the most perfect of prayers: it asks for everything we may rightly desire, in the order in which we should desire it."]],
 "Pray the Our Father very slowly, one phrase per breath.",
 CQ("Who called the Our Father \"the most perfect of prayers\"?",["St Augustine","St Thomas Aquinas","St Francis"],1,"CCC 2763, quoting ST II-II q.83 a.9."));
cccN("c96","p4s2","2803–2865","__PA6.HTM","The seven petitions, and Amen","Three petitions for God's glory, four for our needs: hallowed be thy name, thy kingdom come, thy will be done; our daily bread, forgiveness, protection in temptation, deliverance from evil.",
 `<p>After placing ourselves in God's presence, the Spirit inspires seven petitions. The first three draw us towards the Father's glory: <b>hallowed be thy name</b> (that his holiness be recognised and lived in us), <b>thy kingdom come</b> (Christ's return and the Kingdom's growth now), <b>thy will be done</b> (that we unite our will to his).</p><p>The last four present our needs to his grace: <b>give us this day our daily bread</b> (material bread, the Word and the Eucharist, with solidarity for the hungry), <b>forgive us our trespasses as we forgive</b> (the outpouring of mercy can't reach us unless we forgive), <b>lead us not into temptation</b> (do not let us yield), and <b>deliver us from evil</b>, from the Evil One. The final Amen means: let it be so.</p>`,
 [["Petition","A request; the Our Father has seven."],["Doxology","A prayer of praise: \"For the kingdom, the power and the glory are yours…\""]],
 [["Matt 6:9-15: the Lord's Prayer and forgiveness","matthew,6,9,15"],["John 6:32-35: I am the bread of life","john,6,32,35"],["Jas 1:13-14: God tempteth no man","james,1,13,14"]],
 [["ST II-II q.83 a.9","Thomas shows the order of the petitions: first the end (God's glory and our sharing in it), then the means (bread, forgiveness, freedom from temptation and evil)."],["Thomas, Commentary on the Lord's Prayer","Thomas's Lenten sermons explain each petition and pair them with the gifts of the Holy Spirit."]],
 "Which petition of the Our Father do you most need to mean this week?",
 CQ("How many petitions does the Our Father have?",["Five","Seven","Ten"],1,"CCC 2803–2806."));

// ---------- Catechism notes: the "Go deeper" layer, §§ 1–184 ----------
// For each section: what each group of paragraphs teaches, common misunderstandings, a session plan for catechists,
// short sayings from the Fathers and saints, and further check questions. Summaries are in our own words.
const CCC_DEEP={};
function cccD(id,o){CCC_DEEP[id]=o;}
cccD("c1",{paras:[["1","God is perfect and blessed in himself; he created us freely, out of goodness, to share his blessed life. He is near to us at every moment, and after sin he gathered humanity into his family, the Church, through his Son and in the Spirit, so that we might become his adopted children."],["2","To make this call heard everywhere, Christ sent the apostles to proclaim the Gospel: \"Go, teach all nations.\" Strengthened by this mission, they went out preaching, and the Lord confirmed their word with signs."],["3","Those who welcome the call are urged by Christ's love to proclaim it in turn. This treasure, received from the apostles, has been faithfully kept by their successors, and all Christians are called to hand it on from generation to generation, by professing it, living it in fraternal sharing and celebrating it in liturgy and prayer."]],
 mis:[["God created us because he was lonely or needed something.","God lacks nothing. He created out of sheer goodness, to share his own happiness (§ 1)."],["Passing on the faith is the job of priests and catechists.","Every believer who has welcomed Christ's call is sent to hand it on (§ 3)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants grasp that the whole Catechism answers one question: what is human life for? God made us to share his blessed life.",discuss:["What do most people around you think life is for?","What does it mean that God \"lacks nothing\", and why is that good news for us?","Who first passed the faith on to you, and how?"],act:"Ask each person to write their own one-sentence answer to \"Why am I here?\" before reading § 1, then compare it with § 1 afterwards."},
 saints:[["St Irenaeus (Against Heresies IV.20.7)","The glory of God is a human being fully alive; and the life of man is the vision of God."],["St Augustine (Confessions I.1)","You have made us for yourself, and our heart is restless until it rests in you."]],
 more:[CQ("To whom did Christ entrust the mission of proclaiming the Gospel?",["Only to Peter","To the apostles","To kings and rulers"],1,"§ 2: he sent forth the apostles."),CQ("According to § 3, how is the faith handed on?",["Only by teaching","By professing it, living it in fraternal sharing, and celebrating it in liturgy and prayer","By memorising texts"],1,"§ 3.")]});
cccD("c2",{paras:[["4","Catechesis is the whole of the Church's efforts to make disciples: to help people believe that Jesus is the Son of God, so that believing they may have life in his name, and to educate and build them up in that life."],["5","It is an education in the faith of children, young people and adults, especially in the teaching of Christian doctrine, given in an organic and systematic way, to initiate people into the fullness of Christian life."],["6","Catechesis is linked with, but distinct from, other elements of the Church's pastoral mission: the first proclamation of the Gospel, the search for reasons for belief, the experience of Christian living, celebration of the sacraments, integration into the Church community, and apostolic and missionary witness."],["7–8","Catechesis is bound up with the whole life of the Church: her growth depends on it. Periods of renewal in the Church have also been intense periods of catechesis; the great bishops and Fathers (Cyril of Jerusalem, Augustine, John Chrysostom, Ambrose and others) were great catechists."],["9–10","The Council of Trent led to the Roman Catechism (1566); Vatican II inspired a renewal of catechesis; and the 1985 Synod asked for a catechism of the whole Catholic doctrine, which John Paul II published in 1992."]],
 mis:[["Catechesis is just classes for children before First Communion or Confirmation.","It is lifelong: for children, young people and adults, aimed at the fullness of Christian life (§ 5)."],["Catechesis is only information.","It aims at faith and life in Christ, not only knowledge (§ 4)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants see catechesis as making disciples, not only passing on facts, and see themselves in the long line of those who hand on the faith.",discuss:["What is the difference between knowing about Jesus and being his disciple?","Why do you think renewal in the Church has always come with renewed catechesis (§ 8)?","Which elements of § 6 have shaped your own faith most?"],act:"Draw a \"faith family tree\": the people who handed the faith to you, and those you might hand it on to."},
 saints:[["St Cyril of Jerusalem (Catecheses)","His catechetical lectures in the 4th century prepared adults for Baptism at Easter, and are still read today."],["St Augustine (On Catechising the Uninstructed)","Augustine advised catechists above all to teach with joy (hilaritas), because God loves a cheerful giver."]],
 more:[CQ("Which catechism followed the Council of Trent?",["The Baltimore Catechism","The Roman Catechism","The Catechism of the Catholic Church"],1,"§ 9: the Roman Catechism (1566)."),CQ("Who is catechesis for?",["Children only","Children, young people and adults","Converts only"],1,"§ 5.")]});
cccD("c3",{paras:[["11","The Catechism aims to present an organic synthesis of the essential and fundamental contents of Catholic doctrine, on faith and morals, in the light of Vatican II and the whole Tradition. Its principal sources are Scripture, the Fathers, the liturgy and the Magisterium. It is meant to be a point of reference for national and diocesan catechisms."],["12","It is addressed above all to those responsible for catechesis: first the bishops, as teachers of the faith and pastors; through them, to those who compose catechisms, to priests and catechists; and it will also be useful reading for all the faithful."]],
 mis:[["The Catechism is a replacement for the Bible.","Scripture is one of its principal sources; it presents the faith in a synthesis, constantly quoting Scripture (§ 11)."],["Local catechisms are unnecessary now.","The Catechism is a reference point for local catechisms, which adapt it to particular people (§§ 11, 24)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants know what kind of book the Catechism is, what it draws from, and how to approach it.",discuss:["Why might it help Catholics in South Africa and in Brazil to share one reference text?","What are the four main sources the Catechism draws on?","How is a reference book read differently from a novel?"],act:"Open the Catechism (vatican.va) and look up any topic in the index. Notice the footnotes: which sources are cited?"},
 saints:[["St John Paul II (Fidei Depositum, 1992)","He called the Catechism \"a sure norm for teaching the faith\"."]],
 more:[CQ("The Catechism is meant to be a point of reference for…",["Civil laws","Local catechisms","Seminary exams only"],1,"§ 11."),CQ("Which is NOT listed as a principal source?",["Scripture","The Fathers","Popular opinion"],2,"§ 11: Scripture, the Fathers, the liturgy and the Magisterium.")]});
cccD("c4",{paras:[["13","The Catechism follows the traditional order of the great catechisms, built on four pillars: the baptismal profession of faith (the Creed), the sacraments of faith, the life of faith (the Commandments), and the prayer of the believer (the Our Father)."],["14","Part One: those who belong to Christ by faith and Baptism must confess their baptismal faith before others. It speaks first of Revelation and faith, then summarises the gifts of God in the Creed."],["15","Part Two explains how God's salvation, accomplished once for all through Christ and the Spirit, is made present in the liturgy, especially the seven sacraments."],["16","Part Three deals with our final end, created in God's image: beatitude, and the ways to reach it, by right conduct freely chosen with the help of God's law and grace, fulfilling the twofold commandment of love in the Ten Commandments."],["17","Part Four deals with the meaning and importance of prayer, ending with a commentary on the seven petitions of the Lord's Prayer: the sum of all the goods we should hope for."]],
 mis:[["The Catechism is a list of rules.","Only one of its four parts is about moral life, and even that begins with grace and beatitude (§ 16)."],["Prayer is an optional extra at the end.","Prayer is one of the four pillars; faith that isn't prayed is incomplete (§ 17)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants can name the four parts of the Catechism and see how they hold together: believed, celebrated, lived, prayed.",discuss:["Which of the four pillars is strongest in your parish? Which is weakest?","Why does the Catechism put the Creed before the Commandments?","How does Sunday Mass include all four pillars?"],act:"Draw a house with four pillars and label them. Under each, write one thing you already do and one you'd like to grow in."},
 saints:[["St Thomas Aquinas (Lenten sermons, 1273)","Thomas preached on the Creed, the Our Father, the Hail Mary and the Commandments: knowledge of what to believe, what to desire and what to do is all a person needs for salvation."]],
 more:[CQ("Which part of the Catechism comments on the Our Father?",["Part One","Part Three","Part Four"],2,"§ 17."),CQ("Part Two is about…",["The Creed","The liturgy and the sacraments","Moral life"],1,"§ 15.")]});
cccD("c5",{paras:[["18","The Catechism is conceived as an organic presentation of the whole faith; it should be read as a unity. Numbers in the margin (cross-references) refer to other paragraphs on the same theme, and an analytical index lets one follow each theme across the whole book."],["19","Scripture texts are often not quoted word for word but referenced (\"cf.\"); for deeper understanding the texts themselves should be consulted."],["20","Passages in small print give historical or apologetic background, or supplementary explanations."],["21","Small-print quotations from patristic, liturgical, magisterial or hagiographical sources enrich the presentation; they have often been chosen for direct catechetical use."],["22","At the end of each thematic unit, \"In Brief\" texts summarise the essentials in condensed formulas, meant to be easy to memorise."]],
 mis:[["You can understand a doctrine from one paragraph.","The Catechism is meant to be read as a whole, following its cross-references (§ 18)."],["The small print is unimportant.","It contains rich quotations from the Fathers, saints and liturgy, often chosen for catechesis (§ 21)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants know how to navigate the Catechism: cross-references, footnotes, small print and In Brief.",discuss:["Have you ever been misunderstood when only part of what you said was quoted? How does that apply to doctrine?","Why might the \"In Brief\" sections be useful for memorising?"],act:"Pick paragraph 1213 (Baptism). Follow two cross-references in the margin and note how they deepen it."},
 saints:[["St Vincent of Lérins (Commonitorium, 434)","Hold fast to what has been believed everywhere, always and by all."]],
 more:[CQ("What do the numbers in the margin do?",["Count pages","Point to other paragraphs on the same theme","Show the date of each teaching"],1,"§ 18."),CQ("What do Scripture references marked \"cf.\" mean?",["The text is quoted exactly","The text is referred to, and should be read for deeper understanding","The text is disputed"],1,"§ 19.")]});
cccD("c6",{paras:[["23","The Catechism emphasises the exposition of doctrine. It aims to deepen understanding of faith and is oriented to the maturing of faith, its putting down roots in life and its shining forth in conduct."],["24","By design it does not adapt doctrine to different cultures, ages, spiritual maturity or social and ecclesial conditions. Those adaptations belong to local catechisms and, still more, to those who instruct the faithful."],["25","A pastoral principle concludes the Prologue: the whole concern of doctrine and its teaching must be directed to the love that never ends. Whether something is proposed for belief, hope or action, the love of our Lord must always be made accessible, so that every Christian virtue springs from love and tends to love (quoting the Roman Catechism)."]],
 mis:[["Teaching doctrine and showing love are opposites.","All doctrine is directed to love (§ 25); true charity includes the truth."],["A catechist should simply read the Catechism aloud.","Adaptation to the people in front of you is the catechist's work (§ 24)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Catechists understand their own role: faithful to the doctrine, attentive to their group, and always leading to love.",discuss:["How would you explain the Trinity differently to a 10-year-old and to a university student?","Can you think of a teaching that seems strict but is really about love?","What does it mean that doctrine should \"take root in life\" (§ 23)?"],act:"Take one doctrine (e.g. the Real Presence) and plan how you'd present it to children, teens and adults."},
 saints:[["St Thérèse of Lisieux (Story of a Soul)","\"My vocation is love\": she found her place in the Church's heart."]],
 more:[CQ("Who is responsible for adapting the Catechism to different groups?",["No one","Local catechisms and those who instruct the faithful","Only the Pope"],1,"§ 24."),CQ("Doctrine and its teaching are directed to…",["Exams","The love that never ends","Rules"],1,"§ 25.")]});
cccD("c7",{paras:[["26","When we profess our faith we begin with \"I believe\" or \"We believe\". Before treating the faith itself, the Catechism considers the person who believes (Chapter One), God who reveals (Chapter Two), and the response of faith (Chapter Three)."],["27","The desire for God is written in the human heart, because we are created by God and for God; he never ceases to draw us. Only in God will we find the truth and happiness we never stop seeking. Our dignity rests above all on this call to communion with God."],["28","Throughout history people have expressed their search for God in religious beliefs and behaviour: prayers, sacrifices, rituals, meditations. These expressions, despite their ambiguities, are so universal that we can call the human being a religious being."],["29","This intimate bond with God can be forgotten, overlooked or explicitly rejected, for many reasons: revolt against evil in the world, religious ignorance or indifference, worldly cares and riches, the bad example of believers, currents of thought hostile to religion, and the sinner's attitude of hiding from God."],["30","\"Let the hearts of those who seek the Lord rejoice.\" Even if we forget or reject God, he never ceases to call every person to seek him and find life and happiness. This search demands every effort of intellect, a sound will, an upright heart, and the witness of others who teach us to seek God."]],
 mis:[["Religion is just a cultural invention.","The universal search for God reflects how we are made: for God (§§ 27–28)."],["Atheists are simply bad people.","The Catechism lists many causes of unbelief, including the bad example of believers (§ 29)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants recognise their own longing for meaning as a longing for God, and understand why people turn away.",discuss:["Where do you see people searching for something more (music, sport, relationships, success)?","Which reason for unbelief in § 29 do you encounter most?","How can our own example help or hinder someone's search (§ 29)?"],act:"Play or read a popular song about longing. Discuss: what is the singer really looking for?"},
 saints:[["St Augustine (Confessions I.1)","Our heart is restless until it rests in you."]],
 more:[CQ("According to § 29, which can lead people to reject God?",["Only intellectual arguments","Revolt against evil, ignorance, worldly cares, the bad example of believers, and others","Nothing; everyone believes"],1,"§ 29."),CQ("What does seeking God require, according to § 30?",["Only feelings","Effort of intellect, a sound will, an upright heart, and the witness of others","A theology degree"],1,"§ 30.")]});
cccD("c8",{paras:[["31","Created in God's image, the person who seeks God discovers certain \"ways\" of coming to know him. These are not proofs in the sense of the natural sciences, but \"converging and convincing arguments\" that give real certainty about the truth."],["32","The world: from motion and becoming, contingency, and the world's order and beauty, one can come to know God as the origin and end of the universe (Rom 1:19–20). St Augustine: question the beauty of the earth, the sea, the sky; they answer, \"See, we are beautiful.\""],["33","The human person: our openness to truth and beauty, our sense of moral goodness, freedom and the voice of conscience, our longing for the infinite and for happiness, all point to a spiritual soul, which cannot be explained by matter alone. The soul, \"the seed of eternity we bear in ourselves\", can have its origin only in God."],["34","The world and the human person attest that they contain within themselves neither their first principle nor their final end; they participate in Being itself, which alone is without origin or end. So, in different ways, we can come to know God, the first cause and final end of all things."],["35","Our faculties make us capable of knowing the existence of a personal God. But for us to enter into real intimacy with him, God willed to reveal himself and give us the grace to welcome that revelation in faith. The proofs of God's existence can predispose us to faith and help show that faith is not opposed to reason."]],
 mis:[["Belief in God is blind and irrational.","Reason can reach real certainty that God exists through converging arguments (§§ 31, 35)."],["If God can be proved, we don't need faith.","Reason knows that God is; only revelation and faith open his inner life and friendship with him (§ 35)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants can present the two \"starting points\" (the world and the human person) for knowing God, and explain why they are reasonable.",discuss:["Why does anything exist rather than nothing?","Where do your sense of right and wrong and your longing for meaning come from?","Why does knowing that God exists differ from knowing him?"],act:"Go outside (or look at photos of nature). Each person chooses one thing and asks: what does this tell me about its Maker?"},
 saints:[["St Augustine (Sermon 241)","Question the beauty of the earth, the sea, the sky… they all answer: \"See, we are beautiful.\" Who made them, if not the Beautiful One?"],["St Thomas Aquinas (ST I q.2 a.3)","The Five Ways: motion, causation, contingency, degrees of perfection, and order in nature."]],
 more:[CQ("What are the two starting points for the \"ways\" to God?",["Feelings and dreams","The world and the human person","The Bible and the Church"],1,"§§ 32–33."),CQ("What can the arguments for God's existence do, according to § 35?",["Replace faith","Predispose us to faith and show that faith is not opposed to reason","Prove the Trinity"],1,"§ 35.")]});
cccD("c9",{paras:[["36","The Church holds and teaches that God, the first principle and last end of all things, can be known with certainty from the created world by the natural light of human reason (First Vatican Council). Without this capacity we could not welcome God's revelation. We have it because we are made in his image."],["37","In the historical conditions in which we find ourselves, however, we experience many difficulties in coming to know God by reason alone: the senses and imagination get in the way, as do disordered appetites from original sin. People easily persuade themselves that what they would not like to be true is false or doubtful (Pius XII, Humani Generis)."],["38","That is why we need to be enlightened by God's revelation, not only about truths that exceed our understanding, but also about religious and moral truths that are not in principle beyond reason, so that they can be known by everyone with ease, with firm certainty, and with no admixture of error."]],
 mis:[["The Church distrusts reason.","The Church defends reason's power to know God with certainty (§ 36)."],["Revelation is only needed for mysteries like the Trinity.","It also helps us know truths reason could reach, easily, surely and without error (§ 38)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand both the power and the limits of reason, and why revelation is a gift even for moral truths.",discuss:["Can you think of a moral truth whole societies have got wrong?","Why do we sometimes \"persuade ourselves\" that what is inconvenient is false (§ 37)?","How does revelation make the truth accessible to people without time for philosophy?"],act:"List three moral truths almost everyone agrees on and three that are disputed. Discuss how revelation helps with the second list."},
 saints:[["St Thomas Aquinas (ST I q.1 a.1)","Without revelation, the truth about God would be known only by a few, after a long time, and with many errors mixed in."],["St John Paul II (Fides et Ratio, 1998)","Faith and reason are like two wings on which the human spirit rises to the contemplation of truth."]],
 more:[CQ("Which council taught that God can be known with certainty by reason?",["Trent","Vatican I","Nicaea"],1,"§ 36."),CQ("According to § 37, what makes knowing God by reason difficult?",["Nothing","The senses, imagination and disordered appetites, and our tendency to deny what is inconvenient","Science"],1,"§ 37.")]});
cccD("c10",{paras:[["39","In defending reason's ability to know God, the Church expresses her confidence in the possibility of speaking about him to all people: to other religions, to philosophy and science, and to unbelievers and atheists."],["40","Since our knowledge of God is limited, our language about him is equally so. We can name God only by taking creatures as our starting point, in accordance with our limited human ways of knowing and thinking."],["41","All creatures bear a certain resemblance to God, especially the human person made in his image. Their manifold perfections (truth, goodness, beauty) reflect God's infinite perfection, so we can name God from them, \"for from the greatness and beauty of created things comes a corresponding perception of their Creator\" (Wis 13:5)."],["42","God transcends all creatures. We must therefore continually purify our language of everything limited, image-bound or imperfect, so as not to confuse God, \"the inexpressible, the incomprehensible, the invisible, the ungraspable\", with our human representations. Our words always fall short."],["43","Yet in speaking about God like this, our language really does attain God himself, though it cannot express him in his infinite simplicity. Between Creator and creature no similitude can be expressed without implying an even greater dissimilitude (Lateran IV); and \"concerning God, we cannot grasp what he is, but only what he is not\" (St Thomas)."]],
 mis:[["Since God is beyond words, nothing we say about him is true.","Our language really attains God, though it never captures him fully (§ 43)."],["Calling God \"Father\" means God is male.","Such words come from creatures and must be purified of their limits; God is neither man nor woman (§§ 42, 239)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand analogy: our words about God are true but always fall short.",discuss:["What does \"good\" mean when we say a meal is good, a person is good, and God is good?","Why can't any image of God be complete?","How could this help when talking with people of other religions (§ 39)?"],act:"Each person writes one word for God and then two ways it is true of God, and one way God is infinitely more than that word."},
 saints:[["St Thomas Aquinas (SCG I.30)","Concerning God, we cannot grasp what he is, but only what he is not, and how other things stand in relation to him."],["St Augustine (Sermon 117)","If you have understood, then what you have understood is not God."]],
 more:[CQ("What did the Fourth Lateran Council teach about likeness between Creator and creature?",["They are identical","Every likeness implies an even greater unlikeness","There is no likeness at all"],1,"§ 43."),CQ("Why must we purify our language about God?",["Because it is offensive","Because God transcends all creatures and our words are limited","Because the Church forbids images"],1,"§ 42.")]});
cccD("c11",{paras:[["44","The human person is by nature and vocation a religious being: coming from God and going towards God, we live a fully human life only if we freely live in our bond with God."],["45","We are made to live in communion with God, in whom we find happiness. (St Augustine: \"When I am completely united to you, there will be no more sorrow or trials.\")"],["46","When we listen to the message of creation and to the voice of conscience, we can arrive at certainty about the existence of God, the cause and end of everything."],["47","The Church teaches that the one true God can be known with certainty from his works, by the natural light of reason."],["48","We really can name God, starting from the perfections of his creatures, which are likenesses of the infinitely perfect God, even if our limited language cannot exhaust the mystery."],["49","Without the Creator, the creature vanishes (Vatican II, Gaudium et Spes 36). That is why believers know that the love of Christ urges them to bring the light of the living God to those who do not know him or who reject him."]],
 mis:[["Religion is an optional hobby for some people.","We are religious by nature and vocation; a fully human life includes our bond with God (§ 44)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants can summarise Chapter One in their own words and connect it to evangelisation.",discuss:["Which of the six \"In Brief\" points would you most like a friend to understand?","What does \"without the Creator, the creature vanishes\" mean (§ 49)?"],act:"In pairs, explain Chapter One to each other in two minutes, then check against §§ 44–49."},
 saints:[["St Augustine (Confessions X.28)","When I am completely united to you, there will be no more sorrow or trials; entirely full of you, my life will be complete."]],
 more:[CQ("What two \"voices\" can lead us to certainty about God's existence, according to § 46?",["Parents and teachers","Creation and conscience","Scripture and Tradition"],1,"§ 46.")]});
cccD("c12",{paras:[["50","Reason can know God with certainty from his works. But there is another order of knowledge that we cannot reach by our own powers at all: divine Revelation. By a free decision God reveals himself and gives himself to us, revealing his own mystery: his plan of loving goodness, formed from all eternity in Christ."],["51","It pleased God, in his goodness and wisdom, to reveal himself and make known the mystery of his will: that through Christ, the Word made flesh, we have access to the Father in the Holy Spirit and come to share in the divine nature (Dei Verbum 2)."],["52","God, who \"dwells in unapproachable light\", wants to communicate his own divine life to the people he freely created, to adopt them as his sons and daughters in his only Son. By revealing himself, God wishes to make us capable of responding to him, knowing him and loving him far beyond our own natural capacity."],["53","The divine plan of revelation is realised simultaneously \"by deeds and words which are intrinsically bound up with each other\". It involves a specific divine pedagogy: God communicates himself gradually, preparing us in stages to welcome the supernatural revelation that culminates in Christ. St Irenaeus speaks of God and humanity becoming accustomed to each other."]],
 mis:[["Revelation is mainly a set of rules handed down from above.","God reveals himself and his loving plan, to make us his children (§§ 51–52)."],["God revealed everything at once.","He revealed himself gradually, by deeds and words, as a patient teacher (§ 53)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants see revelation as God's personal self-gift, given step by step, aimed at friendship and adoption.",discuss:["Why would God reveal himself gradually rather than all at once?","Why do deeds and words belong together in revelation (and in our own witness)?","What is the difference between knowing facts about someone and being invited into their family?"],act:"Make a timeline of how you got to know a close friend: first impressions, deeper conversations, trust. Compare it with God's \"pedagogy\" (§ 53)."},
 saints:[["St Irenaeus (Against Heresies III.20.2)","The Word of God dwelt in man and became the Son of man, to accustom man to perceive God and to accustom God to dwell in man."]],
 more:[CQ("What is God's purpose in revealing himself, according to § 52?",["To show his power","To communicate his divine life and adopt us as his children","To test us"],1,"§ 52."),CQ("Revelation is realised by…",["Words alone","Deeds and words intrinsically bound together","Visions alone"],1,"§ 53.")]});
cccD("c13",{paras:[["54","God, who creates and conserves all things by his Word, gives us constant evidence of himself in created realities. From the beginning he also manifested himself to our first parents, inviting them to intimate communion and clothing them with grace and justice."],["55","This revelation was not broken off by our first parents' sin. After the fall, God buoyed them up with the hope of salvation by promising redemption (Gen 3:15), and he has never ceased to show his care for the human race."],["56–58","After sin shattered the unity of the human race, God at once sought to save humanity part by part. The covenant with Noah after the flood expresses the principle of the divine economy towards the \"nations\" (gathered in their lands, languages and families). This covenant remains in force during the times of the Gentiles, until the universal proclamation of the Gospel. Scripture honours great figures among the nations, such as Abel, Melchizedek, Noah, Daniel and Job."],["59–61","To gather scattered humanity, God calls Abram from his country and makes him Abraham, \"the father of a multitude of nations\". The people descended from Abraham would be the trustee of the promise made to the patriarchs, the chosen people, called to prepare for the gathering of all God's children. The patriarchs, prophets and other Old Testament figures have always been venerated as saints in the Church's liturgy."],["62–63","After the patriarchs, God formed Israel as his people by freeing them from slavery in Egypt. He established the covenant of Mount Sinai and gave them his law through Moses, so that they would recognise him as the one true God and wait for the promised Saviour. Israel is the priestly people of God, the \"first to hear the word of God\", our \"elder brothers\" in the faith of Abraham."],["64","Through the prophets, God forms his people in the hope of salvation, the expectation of a new and everlasting covenant, written on hearts. The poor and humble of the Lord carry this hope; holy women such as Sarah, Rebecca, Rachel, Miriam, Deborah, Hannah, Judith and Esther kept it alive, and Mary is its purest figure."]],
 mis:[["The Old Testament is replaced and no longer matters.","It is a real stage of God's revelation; Israel remains the people to whom God first spoke, our \"elder brothers\" (§ 63)."],["God only cares about Israel, or only about Christians.","The covenant with Noah embraces all nations, and the call of Abraham is for the gathering of all (§§ 58–60)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants can tell the story of salvation from Adam to the prophets and see Christ as its goal.",discuss:["What does each stage (Adam, Noah, Abraham, Moses, the prophets) show us about God?","Why does the Catechism call the Jewish people our \"elder brothers\"?","Which Old Testament woman in § 64 would you like to learn more about?"],act:"Lay out cards with Adam and Eve, Noah, Abraham, Moses, David, the prophets and Mary. Participants put them in order and say what God promised at each stage."},
 saints:[["St Augustine (Questions on the Heptateuch 2.73)","The New Testament lies hidden in the Old, and the Old is unveiled in the New."]],
 more:[CQ("After the Fall, did God stop revealing himself?",["Yes, until Abraham","No; he promised redemption and never ceased to care for humanity","Yes, until Moses"],1,"§ 55."),CQ("What did God give Israel at Sinai?",["A king","The covenant and the Law through Moses","The Temple"],1,"§ 62.")]});
cccD("c14",{paras:[["65","\"In many and various ways God spoke of old to our fathers by the prophets; but in these last days he has spoken to us by a Son\" (Heb 1:1–2). Christ, the Son made man, is the Father's one, perfect and unsurpassable Word. In him the Father has said everything, and there will be no other word than this one. St John of the Cross: having given us his Son, his only Word, God has spoken everything at once, and has nothing more to say."],["66","The Christian economy, as the new and definitive covenant, will never pass away; no new public revelation is to be expected before the glorious manifestation of our Lord. Yet even though Revelation is already complete, it has not been made completely explicit; it remains for Christian faith gradually to grasp its full significance over the course of the centuries."],["67","Throughout the ages there have been \"private\" revelations, some recognised by the Church. They do not belong to the deposit of faith: their role is not to improve or complete Christ's definitive Revelation, but to help live it more fully in a certain period of history. Guided by the Magisterium, the sense of the faithful discerns what in them is an authentic call of Christ. Christian faith cannot accept \"revelations\" that claim to surpass or correct the Revelation fulfilled in Christ."],["68–73","In Brief: by love God revealed himself and gave himself to us; he revealed himself gradually through deeds and words; beyond the witness of creation he revealed himself to our first parents, made a covenant with Noah, chose Abraham, formed Israel, and has revealed himself fully by sending his own Son, the definitive Word."]],
 mis:[["Catholics must believe in Fatima, Lourdes or Medjugorje.","No private revelation belongs to the deposit of faith; even approved ones are only aids (§ 67)."],["New religions can add to or correct Christ.","No new public revelation will come before Christ's return; claims to surpass him cannot be accepted (§§ 66–67)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand that Christ is God's complete Word, and can explain the place of private revelations.",discuss:["If God has \"said everything\" in Christ, why does the Church still grow in understanding?","How would you answer a friend who says a new prophet has corrected Jesus?","What role can approved apparitions (like Lourdes) play in a Catholic's life?"],act:"Read Hebrews 1:1–4 together. Underline every phrase that shows Jesus is greater than the prophets."},
 saints:[["St John of the Cross (Ascent of Mount Carmel II.22)","In giving us his Son, his only Word, God has spoken everything to us at once in this single Word, and he has nothing more to say."]],
 more:[CQ("Is revelation completely explicit already?",["Yes, nothing more to understand","No; the Church gradually grasps its full significance over the centuries","There is no more revelation of any kind"],1,"§ 66."),CQ("What is the role of approved private revelations?",["To complete Christ's revelation","To help people live Christ's revelation more fully in a certain period","To replace the Gospels"],1,"§ 67.")]});
cccD("c15",{paras:[["74","God \"desires all men to be saved and to come to the knowledge of the truth\" (1 Tim 2:4), that is, of Christ. So Christ must be proclaimed to all peoples, and Revelation must be transmitted to the ends of the earth."],["75–76","Christ commanded the apostles to preach the Gospel. It was handed on in two ways: orally, by the apostles, who handed on by preaching, example and institutions what they had received from Christ's lips, way of life and works and from the Holy Spirit; and in writing, by the apostles and others associated with them who, inspired by the Holy Spirit, committed the message of salvation to writing."],["77","To preserve the Gospel full and alive in the Church, the apostles left bishops as their successors, handing over their own teaching role to them. This apostolic preaching, expressed especially in the inspired books, was to be preserved in a continuous line of succession until the end of time."],["78–79","This living transmission, accomplished in the Holy Spirit, is called Tradition. Through it the Church, in her doctrine, life and worship, perpetuates and transmits to every generation all that she herself is and all that she believes. The Father's self-communication through his Word in the Spirit remains present and active in the Church."],["80–82","Tradition and Scripture are bound closely together: flowing from the same divine wellspring, they form one thing and move towards the same goal. Scripture is the speech of God put down in writing under the Spirit's breath; Tradition transmits in its entirety the Word of God entrusted to the apostles. So the Church does not derive her certainty about revealed truths from Scripture alone; both must be accepted and honoured with equal devotion and reverence."],["83","The Tradition in question comes from the apostles and hands on what they received from Jesus and the Holy Spirit. It must be distinguished from the various theological, disciplinary, liturgical or devotional traditions born in local churches over time, which can be retained, modified or abandoned under the Magisterium's guidance."]],
 mis:[["\"Tradition\" means old customs.","Apostolic Tradition is the living transmission of the Gospel; customs (small-t traditions) can change (§ 83)."],["Catholics add Tradition to the Bible as a second source.","Scripture and Tradition flow from one source and form one deposit (§ 80)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants can explain what Tradition is, how it relates to Scripture, and the difference between Tradition and traditions.",discuss:["The New Testament was written after the Church began. What does that tell us?","Give an example of a small-t tradition that has changed (e.g. Friday abstinence rules, the language of the Mass).","How would you respond to someone who says \"only the Bible\"?"],act:"Read 2 Thess 2:14 and 2 Tim 2:2 together. Map out the \"chain\" of handing on: Jesus → apostles → faithful men → others."},
 saints:[["St Irenaeus (Against Heresies III.3)","Irenaeus listed the bishops of Rome from Peter onwards as proof that the apostolic teaching had been faithfully handed on."],["St Basil (On the Holy Spirit 27)","Some things we hold from written teaching, others from apostolic tradition handed down to us in secret, and both have the same force."]],
 more:[CQ("In what two ways was the Gospel handed on, according to § 76?",["Orally and in writing","In Latin and Greek","By kings and priests"],0,"§ 76."),CQ("Which can be modified or abandoned?",["Apostolic Tradition","Local theological, liturgical or devotional traditions","Scripture"],1,"§ 83.")]});
cccD("c16",{paras:[["84","The apostles entrusted the \"sacred deposit\" of faith, contained in Scripture and Tradition, to the whole Church. By adhering to it, the entire holy people, united to its pastors, remains faithful to the apostles' teaching, to communion, to the breaking of bread and the prayers."],["85–87","The task of giving an authentic interpretation of the Word of God has been entrusted to the living teaching office of the Church alone, that is, to the bishops in communion with the successor of Peter. Yet this Magisterium is not superior to the Word of God, but its servant: it teaches only what has been handed on, listening devoutly, guarding it scrupulously and explaining it faithfully. Mindful of \"He who hears you, hears me\", the faithful receive its teachings with docility."],["88–90","The Magisterium exercises its authority to the fullest when it defines dogmas, truths contained in Revelation or having a necessary connection with them. There is an organic connection between our spiritual life and the dogmas: they are lights along the path of faith. Their mutual connection reveals the coherence of the whole; there is a \"hierarchy\" of truths, since they vary in their relation to the foundation of faith."],["91–93","All the faithful share in understanding and handing on revealed truth; they have received the anointing of the Spirit. The whole body of the faithful cannot err in matters of belief: this supernatural appreciation of faith (sensus fidei) is shown when, from the bishops to the last of the faithful, they manifest universal consent in matters of faith and morals."],["94–95","Thanks to the Spirit, understanding of the realities and words of the deposit of faith grows: through contemplation and study by believers, the intimate understanding of spiritual realities they experience, and the preaching of the bishops. Tradition, Scripture and the Magisterium are so connected that one cannot stand without the others; together, each in its own way, they contribute effectively to salvation."],["96–100","In Brief: what Christ entrusted to the apostles they handed on by preaching and writing; Tradition and Scripture form one deposit; the Church does not draw her certainty from Scripture alone; the whole people of God, by the sensus fidei, unfailingly adheres to the faith; the task of authentic interpretation belongs to the Magisterium."]],
 mis:[["The Magisterium can invent new doctrines.","It is the servant of the Word and teaches only what has been handed on (§ 86)."],["The sensus fidei means majority opinion or polls.","It is the Spirit-given sense of the whole people, bishops included, in universal agreement (§ 92)."],["All doctrines are equally central.","There is a hierarchy of truths in relation to the foundation of faith (§ 90)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand the roles of the Magisterium, the sensus fidei and development of doctrine, and how Scripture, Tradition and Magisterium work together.",discuss:["Why does a living Church need a living teaching authority?","How is the sense of the faithful different from a vote?","Can doctrine develop without changing? (Think of an acorn and an oak.)"],act:"Draw a three-legged stool labelled Scripture, Tradition and Magisterium. Discuss what happens if one leg is removed (§ 95)."},
 saints:[["St Vincent of Lérins (Commonitorium 23)","Doctrine progresses as the body grows: developing in years, but remaining the same in nature and meaning."],["St John Henry Newman (Essay on Development, 1845)","To live is to change, and to be perfect is to have changed often: genuine development preserves the type and continuity of the idea."]],
 more:[CQ("Who has the task of authentic interpretation of the Word of God?",["Each individual","The bishops in communion with the Pope","Biblical scholars"],1,"§ 85."),CQ("Dogmas are described as…",["Chains","Lights along the path of faith","Optional opinions"],1,"§ 89.")]});
cccD("c17",{paras:[["101","To reveal himself, God speaks to us in human words. God's words, expressed in human language, have become like human speech, just as the Word of the eternal Father took on human flesh."],["102–104","Through all the words of Scripture God speaks only one single Word, his Son. That is why the Church has always venerated the Scriptures as she venerates the Lord's Body, never ceasing to offer the faithful the bread of life from the one table of God's Word and Christ's Body. In Scripture the Church finds her nourishment and strength; in the sacred books the Father meets his children with great love and speaks with them."],["105","God is the author of Sacred Scripture. Holy Mother Church holds that the books of both Testaments, whole and entire, with all their parts, are sacred and canonical, because written under the inspiration of the Holy Spirit, they have God as their author."],["106","God inspired the human authors. In composing the books he chose certain men who, all the while he employed them in this task, made full use of their own faculties and powers, so that, though he acted in them and by them, it was as true authors that they consigned to writing whatever he wanted written, and no more."],["107","The inspired books teach the truth: since all that the inspired authors affirm should be regarded as affirmed by the Holy Spirit, the books of Scripture firmly, faithfully and without error teach that truth which God, for the sake of our salvation, wished to see confided to the Sacred Scriptures."],["108","Still, the Christian faith is not a \"religion of the book\". It is the religion of the Word of God, not a written and mute word, but the Word incarnate and living. If Scripture is not to remain a dead letter, Christ must, through the Spirit, open our minds to understand it."]],
 mis:[["Inspiration means God dictated word for word.","The human authors used their own faculties as true authors (§ 106)."],["Inerrancy means Scripture is a science textbook.","It teaches without error the truth God wanted recorded for our salvation (§ 107)."],["Catholics don't really value the Bible.","The Church venerates Scripture as she venerates the Lord's Body (§ 103)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants can explain inspiration and inerrancy accurately, and approach Scripture as God speaking to them.",discuss:["How can a book be fully God's word and fully human words?","What kind of truth does Genesis 1 teach? What kind does it not try to teach?","How does the Mass feed us from \"one table\" of Word and Eucharist (§ 103)?"],act:"Compare Luke 1:1–4 (a human author describing his research) with 2 Tim 3:16 (inspiration). Discuss how both are true at once."},
 saints:[["St Jerome (Commentary on Isaiah, prologue)","Ignorance of the Scriptures is ignorance of Christ."],["St Ambrose (On the Duties of Clergy I.20)","We speak to God when we pray; we listen to him when we read the divine sayings."]],
 more:[CQ("How many \"Words\" does God speak through all of Scripture, according to § 102?",["Many words","One single Word, his Son","Seventy-three"],1,"§ 102."),CQ("Were the human authors true authors?",["No, only secretaries","Yes, using their own faculties, while God acted in and through them","Only some of them"],1,"§ 106.")]});
cccD("c18",{paras:[["109–110","In Scripture God speaks to us in a human way. To interpret it correctly, the reader must be attentive to what the human authors truly wanted to affirm and to what God wanted to reveal through their words. To discover the authors' intention, one must take into account the conditions of their time and culture, the literary genres in use, and the modes of feeling, speaking and narrating then current."],["111","But since Scripture is inspired, there is another and no less important principle: it must be read and interpreted in the light of the same Spirit by whom it was written. Vatican II gives three criteria."],["112–114","1. Be attentive to the content and unity of the whole Scripture: however different its books, it is a unity by reason of God's plan, of which Christ is the centre. 2. Read Scripture within the living Tradition of the whole Church: Scripture is written principally in the Church's heart rather than on parchment. 3. Be attentive to the analogy of faith: the coherence of the truths of faith among themselves and within the whole plan of Revelation."],["115–118","According to an ancient tradition, there are two senses of Scripture: the literal and the spiritual, the latter subdivided into the allegorical, moral and anagogical senses. The literal sense is the meaning conveyed by the words, discovered by sound exegesis; all other senses are based on it. The allegorical sense sees events in relation to Christ (the Red Sea as a sign of Baptism); the moral sense leads us to act justly; the anagogical sense views realities in terms of their eternal significance (the Church on earth as a sign of the heavenly Jerusalem). A medieval couplet sums it up: the letter speaks of deeds; allegory, what to believe; the moral, what to do; anagogy, where we are heading."],["119","It is the task of exegetes to work towards a better understanding of Scripture, so that the Church's judgement may mature; but all that concerns the interpretation of Scripture is ultimately subject to the judgement of the Church."]],
 mis:[["Catholics can't read the Bible for themselves.","The Church strongly encourages personal reading, interpreted within the Church's living faith (§§ 113, 133)."],["The spiritual sense can ignore what the text actually says.","All other senses are based on the literal sense (§ 116)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants learn the three criteria and the four senses, and practise them on a passage.",discuss:["Why does knowing the literary genre (poetry, history, parable) matter?","Why is it dangerous to read one verse on its own?","What changes when you read Scripture as part of the Church rather than alone?"],act:"Read Exodus 14:21–31 (the Red Sea). Find its literal, allegorical (Baptism), moral (trust in God) and anagogical (passing to the promised land of heaven) senses together."},
 saints:[["Augustine of Dacia (13th century)","The letter teaches what happened; allegory what to believe; the moral sense what to do; anagogy where to go."],["St Thomas Aquinas (ST I q.1 a.10)","All the senses are founded on the literal; nothing necessary to faith is contained in the spiritual sense that is not taught clearly elsewhere in the literal sense."]],
 more:[CQ("All the other senses of Scripture are based on which sense?",["The moral sense","The literal sense","The anagogical sense"],1,"§ 116."),CQ("The anagogical sense concerns…",["What to do","Eternal realities: where we are heading","Historical facts"],1,"§ 117.")]});
cccD("c19",{paras:[["120","It was by apostolic Tradition that the Church discerned which writings are to be included in the list of the sacred books. This complete list is called the canon: 46 (or 45, if Jeremiah and Lamentations are counted as one) for the Old Testament and 27 for the New."],["121–123","The Old Testament is an indispensable part of Scripture. Its books are divinely inspired and retain a permanent value, for the Old Covenant has never been revoked. Its economy was oriented to prepare for the coming of Christ; though it contains matters imperfect and provisional, it bears witness to the whole divine pedagogy of God's saving love. Christians venerate it as true Word of God; the Church has always rejected the idea (Marcionism) that it should be discarded because the New has rendered it void."],["124–127","The New Testament, whose central object is Jesus Christ, hands on the ultimate truth of God's Revelation. The Gospels are the heart of all the Scriptures, as the principal witness to the life and teaching of Jesus. They were formed in three stages: the life and teaching of Jesus; the oral tradition of the apostles; and the written Gospels, composed by the evangelists, who selected, synthesised and explained, always telling the honest truth about Jesus. The fourfold Gospel holds a unique place in the Church's liturgy and the lives of the saints."],["128–130","The unity of the two Testaments: the Church, from apostolic times, has illuminated this unity by typology, which discerns in God's works of the Old Covenant prefigurations of what he accomplished in Christ. Christians therefore read the Old Testament in the light of Christ crucified and risen; yet it keeps its own value. As St Augustine said, the New lies hidden in the Old and the Old is unveiled in the New."],["131–133","Scripture in the life of the Church: such is the force and power of the Word of God that it is the support and energy of the Church and the food of the soul. The study of the sacred page should be the very soul of theology; preaching, catechesis and all Christian instruction should be nourished by it. The Church forcefully exhorts all the faithful to learn the surpassing knowledge of Christ by frequent reading of the divine Scriptures: \"Ignorance of the Scriptures is ignorance of Christ\" (St Jerome)."],["134–141","In Brief: all Scripture is one book, Christ; God is its author; it must be interpreted in the Church, attending to the literal and spiritual senses; the Church accepts 46 Old and 27 New Testament books; the four Gospels occupy a central place; the two Testaments form a unity; and the Church honours the Scriptures as she honours the Lord's Body."]],
 mis:[["The Old Testament is just for Jews, and Christians can ignore it.","It is an indispensable part of Scripture, never to be discarded (§§ 121–123)."],["Catholics added books to the Bible.","The Church's canon (with the deuterocanonical books) was recognised from the early centuries and confirmed at Trent; it follows the Greek Old Testament used by the apostles."],["The Gospels are myths invented much later.","They arose from Jesus' life, the apostles' preaching, and the evangelists' faithful writing (§ 126)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants know the canon, the place of the Gospels, the unity of the Testaments, and are encouraged to read Scripture daily.",discuss:["Which Old Testament stories can you connect to Christ (typology)?","What are the three stages in the formation of the Gospels?","What helps or hinders you from reading Scripture every day?"],act:"Typology match: pair Old Testament events (Passover lamb, manna, Jonah in the fish, the bronze serpent) with New Testament fulfilments (the Cross, the Eucharist, the Resurrection, John 3:14)."},
 saints:[["St Jerome (Commentary on Isaiah, prologue)","Ignorance of the Scriptures is ignorance of Christ."],["St Augustine (Questions on the Heptateuch 2.73)","The New Testament lies hidden in the Old; the Old is made manifest in the New."]],
 more:[CQ("What are the three stages in the formation of the Gospels?",["Writing, editing, printing","Jesus' life and teaching, the apostles' oral tradition, the written Gospels","Prophecy, fulfilment, interpretation"],1,"§ 126."),CQ("Which ancient error rejected the Old Testament?",["Arianism","Marcionism","Pelagianism"],1,"§ 123.")]});
cccD("c20",{paras:[["142–143","By his Revelation the invisible God, from the fullness of his love, addresses us as friends, to invite us into his company. The adequate response to this invitation is faith. By faith we completely submit our intellect and our will to God, giving our assent to God who reveals."],["144","To obey (ob-audire, \"to hear or listen to\") in faith is to submit freely to the word that has been heard, because its truth is guaranteed by God, who is Truth itself."],["145–147","Abraham is the model: he went out, not knowing where he was to go; he lived as a stranger in the promised land; by faith Sarah conceived the son of the promise; and by faith he offered his only son in sacrifice. He fulfils the definition of faith in Hebrews 11:1 and is the \"father of all who believe\". The Old Testament is rich in witnesses to this faith (Hebrews 11)."],["148–149","The Virgin Mary most perfectly embodies the obedience of faith. By faith she welcomed the tidings and promise of the angel, believing that \"nothing will be impossible with God\". Elizabeth called her blessed because she believed. Throughout her life and to the Cross, her faith never wavered; that is why the Church venerates in Mary the purest realisation of faith."],["150","Faith is first of all a personal adherence of the whole person to God. At the same time, and inseparably, it is a free assent to the whole truth that God has revealed. It is right and just to entrust oneself wholly to God and to believe absolutely what he says; it would be futile and false to place such faith in a creature."],["151–152","For a Christian, believing in God cannot be separated from believing in the One he sent, his beloved Son: we can believe in Jesus Christ because he is himself God, the Word made flesh. And one cannot believe in Jesus without sharing in his Spirit: it is the Holy Spirit who reveals to us who Jesus is. The Church never ceases to proclaim her faith in one only God: Father, Son and Holy Spirit."]],
 mis:[["Faith is a feeling.","Faith involves the whole person, intellect and will, giving free assent to God who reveals (§ 143)."],["Faith is only a set of beliefs, or only a relationship.","It is both, inseparably: personal adherence to God and assent to what he reveals (§ 150)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand faith as personal trust and free assent, modelled by Abraham and Mary.",discuss:["Have you trusted someone enough to act without knowing the outcome?","What does Mary's faith at the Cross teach us about faith in hard times (§ 149)?","Why would it be wrong to put absolute faith in a creature (§ 150)?"],act:"Trust walk: one person, blindfolded, is guided by a partner's voice only. Debrief: what made it possible to trust? Connect with Abraham (Heb 11:8)."},
 saints:[["St Augustine (Sermon 215)","Mary conceived Christ in her heart by faith before she conceived him in her womb."]],
 more:[CQ("Who is called the \"father of all who believe\"?",["Moses","Abraham","Peter"],1,"§ 146."),CQ("Who most perfectly embodies the obedience of faith?",["Abraham","The Virgin Mary","St Paul"],1,"§ 148.")]});
cccD("c21",{paras:[["153","When Peter confessed Jesus as the Christ, Jesus declared that this was revealed by the Father. Faith is a gift of God, a supernatural virtue infused by him: to believe, we need the grace of God and the interior help of the Holy Spirit."],["154–155","Believing is possible only by grace, yet it is also an authentically human act: trusting God and holding to his truths is not contrary to human freedom or reason. Even in human relations it is not contrary to our dignity to believe what others tell us. In faith the intellect and will cooperate with grace; St Thomas: believing is an act of the intellect assenting to divine truth by command of the will moved by God through grace."],["156","What moves us to believe is not that revealed truths appear true in the light of reason, but the authority of God himself, who can neither deceive nor be deceived. Yet so that our faith might be in accord with reason, God gave outward proofs of revelation: the miracles of Christ and the saints, prophecies, the Church's growth and holiness, her fruitfulness and stability. These are motives of credibility."],["157–159","Faith is certain, more certain than all human knowledge, because founded on God, though revealed truths can seem obscure to reason; \"ten thousand difficulties do not make one doubt\" (Newman). Faith seeks understanding: it is intrinsic to faith to want to know better the One in whom one has believed. Faith and reason cannot truly conflict, since the same God reveals mysteries and gives the light of reason; methodical research, if truly scientific and respectful of moral norms, never conflicts with faith."],["160–162","Faith must be free: no one is to be forced to embrace the faith against his will; Christ invited, he did not coerce. Faith is necessary for salvation (\"without faith it is impossible to please God\"). Faith is a free gift that can be lost: to persevere we must nourish it with the word of God, beg the Lord to increase it, and let it work through charity."],["163–165","Faith is the beginning of eternal life: it makes us taste in advance the joy of the beatific vision. Now, however, we walk by faith, not by sight; our experience of evil, suffering and death can shake it. Then we turn to the witnesses of faith: Abraham, who hoped against hope; Mary, who walked in the night of faith; and the \"great cloud of witnesses\"."]],
 mis:[["Having doubts means I have no faith.","Difficulties are not the same as doubt; faith can be tested and grow (§§ 157, 164)."],["Science disproves faith.","Faith and reason cannot truly contradict each other; honest research never conflicts with faith (§ 159)."],["Faith, once received, can never be lost.","It is a gift that must be nourished, or it can be lost (§ 162)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants can describe the characteristics of faith and respond calmly to the claim that faith is irrational or opposed to science.",discuss:["What is the difference between a difficulty and a doubt (Newman)?","Name scientists who were also people of faith (e.g. Georges Lemaître, a priest who proposed the Big Bang theory).","How do you nourish your faith (§ 162)?"],act:"List \"motives of credibility\" (§ 156) you find convincing: a miracle, a saint's life, the Church's survival. Share one."},
 saints:[["St Anselm (Proslogion 1)","I do not seek to understand so that I may believe; I believe so that I may understand."],["St John Henry Newman (Apologia, 1864)","Ten thousand difficulties do not make one doubt."]],
 more:[CQ("What moves us to believe, according to § 156?",["That revealed truths seem obvious","The authority of God who reveals, who can neither deceive nor be deceived","Peer pressure"],1,"§ 156."),CQ("Can faith be forced on someone?",["Yes, for their good","No; faith must be free","Only for children"],1,"§ 160.")]});
cccD("c22",{paras:[["166","Faith is a personal act, the free response of the person to God's initiative. But it is not an isolated act: no one can believe alone, just as no one can live alone. We have received faith from others and should hand it on. Each believer is a link in the great chain of believers."],["167","\"I believe\" (Apostles' Creed) is the faith of the Church professed personally by each believer, especially at Baptism. \"We believe\" (Nicene Creed) is the faith of the Church confessed by the bishops in council or, more generally, by the liturgical assembly."],["168–169","It is the Church that believes first, and so bears, nourishes and sustains my faith. Everywhere it is the Church that first confesses the Lord. Salvation comes from God alone; but because we receive the life of faith through the Church, she is our mother: \"We believe the Church as the mother of our new birth.\""],["170–171","We do not believe in formulas, but in the realities they express (St Thomas: the act of faith ends not in the statement but in the reality). Yet we approach these realities with the help of formulas, which permit us to express and hand on the faith. The Church, \"pillar and bulwark of the truth\", guards the faith once delivered to the saints, and teaches us the language of faith."],["172–175","Through the centuries, in so many languages, cultures and nations, the Church has confessed one faith, received from one Lord, transmitted by one Baptism. St Irenaeus: the Church, though scattered throughout the world, carefully guards this preaching and faith as if living in one house, with one soul and one heart. Languages differ, but the content of the Tradition is one and the same."],["176–184","In Brief: faith is a personal adherence of the whole person to God who reveals himself; we believe in no one but God; faith is a supernatural gift; believing is a human act; faith is certain, necessary for salvation, and a foretaste of the vision of God; it is the Church who believes first and hands on the faith."]],
 mis:[["My faith is a private matter between me and God.","Faith is personal but never isolated: received from the Church and lived with her (§ 166)."],["Creeds and formulas don't matter; only the heart does.","We believe in realities, not formulas, but formulas let us express and hand on the faith truly (§ 170)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants appreciate the communal nature of faith and the value of the Church's words of faith.",discuss:["Why can't anyone believe completely alone?","What is the difference between \"I believe\" and \"We believe\"?","How does it feel to say the same Creed as Christians across the world and through history?"],act:"Pray the Creed together slowly, each person reading one line, to show how the Church's faith is carried together."},
 saints:[["St Cyprian (On the Unity of the Church 6)","No one can have God for Father who does not have the Church for Mother."],["St Irenaeus (Against Heresies I.10.2)","The Church, though scattered through the whole world, carefully preserves the faith as if dwelling in one house, with one soul and one heart."]],
 more:[CQ("\"We believe\" is the faith confessed by…",["One person alone","The bishops in council or the liturgical assembly","Only theologians"],1,"§ 167."),CQ("Does faith end in the formula or in the reality it expresses?",["The formula","The reality","Neither"],1,"§ 170.")]});

// ---------- Catechism notes: "Go deeper" layer, §§ 185–682 (the Creed: the Father and the Son) ----------
cccD("c23",{paras:[["185–188","Whoever says \"I believe\" says \"I pledge myself to what we believe.\" From the beginning the Church expressed her faith in brief formulas for all. These are called professions of faith, \"creeds\" (from credo), or \"symbols\" of faith: the Greek symbolon was half of a broken object, matched with the other half as a sign of recognition."],["189–191","The first profession of faith is made at Baptism. Because Baptism is given \"in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit\", the Creed has three parts: God the Father and creation; Jesus Christ and redemption; the Holy Spirit and our sanctification. Traditionally its statements are called \"articles\", like the joints of a body."],["192–195","Many creeds have arisen through the centuries, in response to the needs of different eras. None can be considered superseded. Two have a special place: the Apostles' Creed, the ancient baptismal symbol of the Church of Rome, a faithful summary of the apostles' faith; and the Nicene-Constantinopolitan Creed, from the first two ecumenical councils (325, 381), still common to the great Churches of East and West."],["196–197","The Catechism follows the Apostles' Creed, completed by references to the Nicene. As at Baptism, to say the Creed in faith is to enter into communion with the Father, Son and Spirit, and with the whole Church that hands on the faith. St Ambrose called it a spiritual seal and a treasure for the heart."]],
 mis:[["The Creed is just a list of opinions.","It is a pledge of the whole person to what the Church believes, received at Baptism (§§ 185, 197)."],["Old creeds are outdated.","None of the creeds can be considered superseded or irrelevant (§ 193)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants know what a creed is, why it has three parts, and the place of the Apostles' and Nicene Creeds.",discuss:["Why might early Christians have needed a short summary of the faith?","What is the link between the Creed and Baptism?","Which line of the Creed do you find most moving? Most difficult?"],act:"Break a card in two; give each half to different people, and let them find their match. Explain symbolon: the Creed is how believers recognise one another."},
 saints:[["St Ambrose (Explanation of the Creed 1)","The Creed is the spiritual seal, our heart's meditation and an ever-present guardian; it is, unquestionably, the treasure of our soul."],["St Cyril of Jerusalem (Catecheses 5.12)","The Creed was not composed by human choice: the most important points were collected from all of Scripture to make one complete teaching of the faith."]],
 more:[CQ("Where is the first profession of faith made?",["At Confirmation","At Baptism","At First Communion"],1,"§ 189."),CQ("What did the Greek symbolon originally mean?",["A prayer","Half of a broken object, used as a sign of recognition","A painted icon"],1,"§ 188.")]});
cccD("c24",{paras:[["198–202","Our profession begins with God, the First and the Last, the beginning and end of everything. \"I believe in God\" is the most fundamental affirmation. God revealed himself to Israel as the only One: \"Hear, O Israel, the Lord our God is one Lord.\" Jesus himself confirms that God is the one Lord. To confess that Jesus is Lord does not divide this oneness."],["203–209","God revealed himself to Israel by making his name known. To Moses, from the bush that burns without being consumed, he says \"I AM WHO AM\". This name is both a revelation and a kind of refusal: God is mysterious, he is not one we can master. Yet he is the God who is near, faithful from age to age, and ready to save. Out of reverence Israel did not pronounce the name YHWH, replacing it with \"Lord\" (Adonai, in Greek Kyrios), the title later given to Jesus."],["210–213","After Israel's sin with the golden calf, God reveals himself as \"merciful and gracious, slow to anger, abounding in steadfast love and faithfulness\". The name \"I Am\" expresses God's faithfulness: despite our unfaithfulness he keeps his steadfast love. Over the centuries Israel's faith deepened its understanding: God alone IS, without origin and without end; all creatures receive their existence from him."],["214–217","God, HE WHO IS, revealed himself as abounding in steadfast love (hesed) and faithfulness (emet). God is Truth: his words cannot deceive, so we can abandon ourselves to him. His truth is his wisdom, which orders the whole creation. When he reveals himself, the teaching from his mouth is \"true instruction\"; sending his Son, he came to bear witness to the truth."],["218–221","Israel discovered that God had only one reason for choosing them: his gratuitous love. His love is compared to a father's love for his son, stronger than a mother's for her children, and a bridegroom's for his bride. It is everlasting. St John goes further: \"God is love.\" God's very being is love, an eternal exchange of love among Father, Son and Spirit, which he destines us to share."],["222–231","Believing in one God has consequences for our whole life: knowing his greatness and majesty; living in thanksgiving; knowing the unity and true dignity of all people; making good use of created things; and trusting God in every circumstance, even adversity. St Teresa's prayer sums it up: \"Let nothing trouble you… God alone suffices.\""]],
 mis:[["Christians believe in three gods.","We confess one God; belief in the Trinity does not divide God's oneness (§§ 202, 253)."],["The God of the Old Testament is harsh and the God of the New is loving.","Already in Exodus God reveals himself as merciful, gracious and abounding in steadfast love (§ 210)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand the divine name, God as Truth and Love, and the practical consequences of believing in one God.",discuss:["What does it say about God that his name is \"I AM\"?","Why can we trust what God says (§ 215)?","Which consequence of believing in one God (§§ 223–227) most needs to change your week?"],act:"Pray St Teresa of Avila's bookmark prayer (\"Let nothing trouble you…\") together; ask each person to name what \"troubles\" them and offer it to God."},
 saints:[["St Teresa of Avila (her bookmark)","Let nothing trouble you, let nothing frighten you. All things pass; God does not change. Patience obtains all. Whoever has God lacks nothing. God alone suffices."],["St Augustine (Confessions III.6)","You were more inward to me than my most inward part, and higher than my highest."]],
 more:[CQ("How did Israel speak the divine name YHWH out of reverence?",["They said it loudly","They replaced it with \"Lord\" (Adonai)","They wrote it only in Greek"],1,"§ 209."),CQ("Which is NOT a consequence of believing in one God (§§ 222–227)?",["Living in thanksgiving","Recognising the dignity of all people","Fearing that creation is evil"],2,"Faith in one God leads to good use of created things, not contempt for them (§ 226).")]});
cccD("c25",{paras:[["232–237","Christians are baptised in the name (not \"names\") of the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit. The Trinity is the central mystery of Christian faith and life: God's own inner life, the source of all other mysteries, and the light that illumines them. It is a mystery in the strict sense, one that could never be known unless revealed. God's works (\"economy\") reveal who he is in himself (\"theology\"), and who he is in himself illuminates his works."],["238–242","Many religions call God \"Father\" as creator, or for his care. But Jesus revealed that God is Father in an unheard-of sense: not only as Creator, but eternally Father in relation to his only Son, who is eternally Son only in relation to the Father. So the apostles confess Jesus to be the Word, the image of the invisible God. Following this tradition, the Council of Nicaea (325) confessed that the Son is \"consubstantial\" (homoousios) with the Father: one only God with him."],["243–248","Before his Passover Jesus announced \"another Paraclete\", the Holy Spirit, who is a divine Person distinct from Jesus and the Father. The Spirit's eternal origin is revealed in his mission: he is sent by the Father in the name of the Son, and by the Son from the Father. The Council of Constantinople (381) confessed the Spirit as \"the Lord, the giver of life\", worshipped and glorified with the Father and the Son. The Latin tradition says the Spirit proceeds from the Father \"and the Son\" (filioque); the Eastern tradition says \"from the Father through the Son\". Rightly understood, these express the same faith."],["249–256","The Church worked out her Trinitarian faith over the first centuries, borrowing terms from philosophy. \"Substance\" (essence, nature) names the divine being in its unity; \"person\" (hypostasis) names Father, Son and Spirit in their real distinction; \"relation\" names the fact that their distinction lies only in their relations to one another. The Trinity is one: not three gods, but one God in three Persons, each wholly God. The Persons are really distinct: Father begets, Son is begotten, Spirit proceeds. And they are relative to one another: their distinction does not divide the divine unity."],["257–260","The whole divine economy (creation, salvation, sanctification) is the common work of the three Persons, for they have one and the same nature and operation. Yet each shows what is proper to him: the Father, from whom all things come; the Son, through whom; the Spirit, in whom. The ultimate goal of the whole divine economy is that creatures enter into the perfect unity of the Blessed Trinity; even now we are called to be a dwelling for it."],["261–267","In Brief: the mystery of the Trinity is the central mystery of faith; the Incarnation and the sending of the Spirit reveal it; the Church confesses one God in three Persons, consubstantial; the divine Persons are inseparable in what they are and in what they do; and by Baptism we are called to share in the life of the Trinity, here in faith and after death in eternal light."]],
 mis:[["The Trinity is like one person wearing three masks (Father in the Old Testament, Son in the Gospels, Spirit now).","This is the ancient error of modalism; the three Persons are really distinct, always (§ 254)."],["The Son and Spirit are lesser, created beings.","Each Person is wholly God, consubstantial with the Father (§§ 242, 253)."],["The Trinity can be proven by reason, or is a mathematical contradiction.","It is a mystery known only by revelation, beyond reason but not against it: one in nature, three in persons (§ 237)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants can state the doctrine of the Trinity accurately, avoid the classic errors, and see the Trinity as the goal of their life.",discuss:["Why does Baptism use the singular \"name\"?","Why do analogies like water, ice and steam fall short (and which error do they suggest)?","What does it mean that we are made to share the life of the Trinity (§ 260)?"],act:"Pray the Sign of the Cross and the Glory Be very slowly. Then ask each person to write one prayer addressed to the Father, one to the Son and one to the Spirit."},
 saints:[["St Gregory of Nazianzus (Oration 40.41)","I cannot think of the One without being enlightened by the splendour of the Three; I cannot distinguish the Three without being carried back to the One."],["St Elizabeth of the Trinity (prayer, 1904)","O my God, Trinity whom I adore, help me to forget myself entirely so as to dwell in you."]],
 more:[CQ("What did the Council of Nicaea teach about the Son?",["He is a creature","He is consubstantial (one in being) with the Father","He became God at his baptism"],1,"§ 242."),CQ("The divine Persons are distinguished by…",["Their power","Their relations of origin","Their age"],1,"§§ 254–255.")]});
cccD("c26",{paras:[["268","Of all the divine attributes, only omnipotence is named in the Creed: confessing it has great bearing on our lives. God's power is universal, loving and mysterious; only faith can discern it when it is \"made perfect in weakness\"."],["269","Scripture repeatedly confesses God's universal power: he is \"the Mighty One of Jacob\", \"the Lord of hosts\". Nothing is impossible with God; he disposes his works according to his will. He is master of history, governing hearts and events."],["270–271","God is the Father Almighty, whose fatherhood and power shed light on each other: he shows his almighty power by caring for our needs, by adopting us as children, and above all by his infinite mercy, freely forgiving sins. His power is in no way arbitrary: in God power, essence, will, intellect, wisdom and justice are all identical, so nothing can be in his power that cannot be in his just will or wise intellect."],["272–274","Faith in the Father Almighty can be tested by the experience of evil and suffering; God can seem absent and incapable of stopping evil. But God revealed his almighty power in the most mysterious way in his Son's voluntary humiliation and Resurrection, by which he conquered evil. Christ crucified is \"the power of God and the wisdom of God\". Nothing is better able to strengthen our faith and hope than holding fast to the conviction that nothing is impossible with God."],["275–278","With Job we confess that God can do all things; with Mary, that nothing is impossible with God. If we do not believe that God's love is almighty, how can we believe that the Father could create us, the Son redeem us and the Spirit sanctify us?"]],
 mis:[["If God is almighty, he can do absurd things like make a square circle.","Omnipotence means God can do everything possible; a contradiction is not a \"thing\" (§ 271; ST I q.25 a.3)."],["Suffering proves that God is not all-powerful.","God's power is most fully revealed in the weakness of the Cross and the victory of the Resurrection (§ 272)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand God's power as fatherly and merciful, and can face the objection from suffering.",discuss:["How is God's power different from human power?","Why does the liturgy say God shows his power most by mercy?","Where have you seen strength in weakness (2 Cor 12:9)?"],act:"Read Luke 1:26–38 and Job 42:1–6. List what each says about God's power."},
 saints:[["Collect of the 26th Sunday in Ordinary Time","O God, who manifest your almighty power above all by pardoning and showing mercy…"],["St Thomas Aquinas (ST I q.25 a.3 ad 3)","God's omnipotence is shown especially in sparing and having mercy, because by it he leads us to share in his infinite good."]],
 more:[CQ("In God, power is identical with…",["Arbitrary will","His essence, will, wisdom and justice","Force"],1,"§ 271."),CQ("Where did God reveal his almighty power in the most mysterious way?",["In the creation of stars","In his Son's humiliation and Resurrection","In the Flood"],1,"§ 272.")]});
cccD("c27",{paras:[["279–281","\"In the beginning God created the heavens and the earth.\" Creation is the foundation of all God's saving plans, the beginning of the history of salvation that culminates in Christ. The Easter Vigil begins with the account of creation."],["282–289","Catechesis on creation is of major importance: it concerns the very foundations of human and Christian life, answering the basic questions: Where do we come from? Where are we going? What is our origin and end? Scientific studies of the age of the cosmos and the development of life are welcome and invite greater admiration for the Creator; but the question of origins goes beyond science. Human reason can find an answer: God's existence can be known through his works. Revelation adds certainty and depth. Genesis 1–3 occupies a unique place, expressing in solemn language the truths of creation: its origin and end in God, its order and goodness, the vocation of the human person, the drama of sin and the hope of salvation."],["290–292","\"In the beginning God created\": God alone created the universe freely, directly, without any help. The Son and the Spirit share in creation: the Father made all things through the Word, and in the Spirit, the Creator Spirit. Creation is the common work of the Holy Trinity."],["293–294","The world was made for the glory of God: not to increase his glory, but to show it forth and communicate it. God created out of goodness, to share his beatitude. \"The glory of God is man fully alive\" (Irenaeus)."],["295–301","God created the world according to his wisdom, not by necessity or chance. He needs nothing pre-existent: he creates \"out of nothing\" (2 Macc 7:28). He creates an ordered and good world, and he transcends it, while being present to it more intimately than it is to itself. Creation is not abandoned: God upholds and sustains it in being at every moment; without him it would fall back into nothingness."],["302–308","Creation has its own goodness, but it was created \"in a state of journeying\" towards an ultimate perfection. Divine providence is the dispositions by which God guides creation towards that perfection. Scripture is unanimous: God's care is concrete and immediate. Jesus asks for childlike trust. God is sovereign master of his plan, but to carry it out he also uses his creatures' cooperation: he grants them the dignity of acting on their own, of being causes, and the power of freely sharing in his providence by work and prayer."],["309–314","If God is good and almighty, why does evil exist? No quick answer suffices; the whole of Christian faith is the answer: the goodness of creation, the drama of sin, the patient love of God, the covenants, the Incarnation, the gift of the Spirit, the Church, the sacraments, the call to blessedness. God's world is \"in a state of journeying\": with physical good there is also physical evil, as long as creation has not reached perfection. Angels and people, as free creatures, can go astray, and moral evil entered the world. God is in no way the cause of moral evil; he permits it, respecting freedom, and knows how to draw good from it. From the greatest moral evil, the Son's death, he brought the greatest good, our redemption. We will fully know God's ways only when we see him face to face."]],
 mis:[["The Church rejects science, especially evolution and the Big Bang.","Scientific discoveries about the age and development of the cosmos invite greater admiration for the Creator (§ 283); the question of ultimate origins goes beyond science (§ 284)."],["God made the world and then left it to run on its own (deism).","God upholds everything in being at every moment and guides it by providence (§§ 301–303)."],["God causes evil.","He permits it, respects freedom, and can bring good out of it (§§ 311–312)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants grasp creation from nothing, providence, and the Christian response to the problem of evil.",discuss:["What is the difference between \"how\" the universe developed (science) and \"why\" there is anything at all (faith)?","Where have you seen providence in your life, perhaps only in hindsight?","How would you comfort, rather than only argue with, someone who has lost faith because of suffering?"],act:"Read Genesis 50:20 (Joseph) and Romans 8:28. Ask each person to share, if willing, a time God brought good out of something painful."},
 saints:[["St Augustine (Enchiridion 11)","Almighty God, because he is supremely good, would never allow any evil in his works unless he were so powerful and good as to bring good even out of evil."],["St Catherine of Siena (Dialogue 138)","Everything comes from love; all is ordained for the salvation of man; God does nothing without this goal in mind."]],
 more:[CQ("Does science contradict the doctrine of creation?",["Yes","No; it invites greater admiration for the Creator, though the question of ultimate origins goes beyond it","Science has nothing to say about nature"],1,"§§ 283–284."),CQ("God's world is described as created…",["Already perfect","In a state of journeying towards ultimate perfection","As an illusion"],1,"§ 302.")]});
cccD("c28",{paras:[["325–327","The Creed professes God as creator of heaven and earth, \"of all that is, seen and unseen\". \"Heaven and earth\" means all that exists; \"heaven\" can also mean the firmament, God's own \"place\", and the place of the spiritual creatures, the angels. The Fourth Lateran Council (1215) taught that God from the beginning made both orders of creatures, the spiritual and the corporeal."],["328–330","The existence of spiritual, non-bodily beings, called angels, is a truth of faith. St Augustine: \"Angel\" is the name of their office, not their nature; by nature they are spirits, by office messengers. With their whole beings they are servants and messengers of God. As purely spiritual creatures, they have intelligence and will: they are personal and immortal, surpassing in perfection all visible creatures."],["331–333","Christ is the centre of the angelic world: they are his angels, created through and for him. They have been present since creation and throughout the history of salvation, announcing it from afar or near and serving its accomplishment: closing the gates of paradise, protecting Lot, saving Hagar, staying Abraham's hand, leading God's people, announcing births and callings. Gabriel announced the births of John and of Jesus. Angels surround the whole life of the Incarnate Word."],["334–336","The whole life of the Church benefits from the angels' help. In the liturgy the Church joins them in adoring God (the \"Holy, holy, holy\"). From infancy to death human life is surrounded by their watchful care and intercession. Beside each believer stands an angel as protector and shepherd, leading him to life (St Basil)."],["337–344","God himself created the visible world in all its richness, diversity and order. Scripture presents it symbolically as six days of divine work concluded by the Sabbath rest. Each creature has its own goodness and perfection, reflecting a ray of God's infinite wisdom; so we must respect the particular goodness of every creature. God wills the interdependence of creatures: no creature is self-sufficient. The beauty of the universe reflects the Creator's beauty. There is a hierarchy of creatures, with the human person as the summit of the Creator's work; and there is solidarity among all creatures, since all have the same Creator."],["345–349","The Sabbath, the end of the work of the six days, shows that creation is ordered to worship: \"Nothing is to be preferred to the work of God\" (St Benedict). For us a new day has dawned: the day of Christ's Resurrection, the eighth day, on which the first creation finds its meaning and fulfilment in the new creation."]],
 mis:[["Angels are dead people who \"get their wings\".","Angels are a distinct kind of creature, purely spiritual; the saints remain human (§ 330)."],["Animals and nature have no value except their usefulness to us.","Each creature has its own goodness; we must respect creation (§ 339)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand the reality and role of angels and the goodness, order and interdependence of the visible world.",discuss:["Why might God create purely spiritual beings?","What does the interdependence of creatures (§ 340) say about how we treat the environment?","How does the Sabbath show what creation is for?"],act:"Learn and pray the Angel of God prayer and the Prayer to St Michael together; share an experience of feeling protected."},
 saints:[["St Augustine (Enarrationes in Psalmos 103)","\"Angel\" is the name of their office, not of their nature. If you seek the name of their nature, it is \"spirit\"; if you seek the name of their office, it is \"angel\"."],["St Basil (Against Eunomius III.1)","Beside each believer stands an angel as protector and shepherd, leading him to life."]],
 more:[CQ("What does the word \"angel\" name, according to St Augustine?",["Their nature","Their office: messenger","Their appearance"],1,"§ 329."),CQ("Who is the centre of the angelic world?",["St Michael","Christ","Mary"],1,"§ 331.")]});
cccD("c29",{paras:[["355–357","\"God created man in his own image… male and female he created them.\" The human person occupies a unique place in creation: made in God's image; uniting the spiritual and material worlds in his own nature; created male and female; and established in God's friendship. Of all visible creatures only the human person is able to know and love the Creator, is willed for his own sake, and is called to share by knowledge and love in God's own life. Being in God's image, each person has the dignity of a person: not just something, but someone."],["358–361","God created everything for man, but man was created to serve and love God and to offer all creation back to him. Only in the mystery of the Word made flesh does the mystery of man become clear (Gaudium et Spes 22): Christ, the \"new Adam\", fully reveals man to himself. Because of its common origin the human race forms a unity; all peoples are one family, bound by a law of solidarity and charity."],["362–365","The human person is a being at once corporeal and spiritual. \"Soul\" in Scripture often means human life or the whole person, but also what is most intimate and valuable, that by which we are most especially in God's image: the spiritual principle in us. The human body shares in the dignity of the image of God: it is a human body precisely because it is animated by a spiritual soul. The unity of soul and body is so profound that the soul is the \"form\" of the body: spirit and matter are not two natures united, but form one single nature."],["366–368","Every spiritual soul is created immediately by God, not produced by the parents, and it is immortal: it does not perish when separated from the body at death, and will be reunited with the body at the final resurrection. The \"heart\" in Scripture means the depths of one's being, where the person decides for or against God."],["369–373","Man and woman have been created, which is to say, willed by God: in perfect equality as human persons, and in their respective beings as man and woman. Both reflect the Creator's wisdom and goodness. God is neither man nor woman; he is pure spirit. Man and woman were made \"for each other\": not that God left them half-made, but for a communion of persons, each a \"helpmate\" to the other. In marriage they are called to transmit life and to be stewards of creation, not in arbitrary domination but as God's representatives."],["374–379","The first man was created good and established in friendship with God and in harmony with himself and creation, in a state of \"original holiness and justice\". As long as he remained in intimacy with God, he would not have to suffer or die. The inner harmony of the person, between man and woman, and between the first couple and creation, made up original justice. The sign of this familiarity with God is the garden. All this harmony was lost by sin."]],
 mis:[["The soul is a ghost trapped inside a body.","The soul is the form of the body; together they make one human nature (§ 365)."],["Men are more in God's image than women.","Man and woman are equal in dignity, both created in God's image (§ 369)."],["God is male.","God is neither man nor woman; he is pure spirit (§ 370)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants appreciate human dignity, the unity of body and soul, and the equal dignity and complementarity of man and woman.",discuss:["What is the difference between being \"something\" and \"someone\"?","How does the unity of body and soul change how we treat our bodies?","Why is it important that the soul is created directly by God (§ 366)?"],act:"Read Psalm 138:13–16 (139) and Genesis 1:26–31. Each person writes one sentence beginning \"Because I am made in God's image, I…\""},
 saints:[["St Catherine of Siena (Dialogue 13)","What made you establish man in so great a dignity? Certainly the incalculable love by which you have looked on your creature in yourself; you have fallen in love with it."],["Vatican II (Gaudium et Spes 22)","Only in the mystery of the Word made flesh does the mystery of man take on light."]],
 more:[CQ("Is the human soul produced by the parents?",["Yes","No; every spiritual soul is created immediately by God","It evolves gradually"],1,"§ 366."),CQ("What was the state of our first parents before sin called?",["Natural innocence","Original holiness and justice","Perfect knowledge"],1,"§ 375.")]});
cccD("c30",{paras:[["385–390","God is infinitely good and all his works are good. Yet no one escapes the experience of suffering, evil in nature, and moral evil. Sin is present in human history; trying to ignore it or give it other names is futile. Only the light of revelation clarifies it: sin is an abuse of the freedom God gives to persons so that they may love him and one another. The doctrine of original sin is the \"reverse side\" of the Good News that Jesus is Saviour of all. The account of the fall in Genesis 3 uses figurative language, but affirms a primeval event, a deed that took place at the beginning of human history."],["391–395","Behind our first parents' disobedient choice lurks a seductive voice opposed to God. Scripture and Tradition see in this being a fallen angel, Satan or the devil. The angels were created good by God, but became evil by their own free and irrevocable rejection of God. Their sin cannot be forgiven, not because of any defect in God's mercy, but because of the irrevocable character of their choice. The devil's power is not infinite; he is only a creature. Although he acts in the world out of hatred for God, he cannot prevent the building up of God's reign. It is a great mystery that providence permits his activity, but \"we know that in everything God works for good with those who love him.\""],["396–401","God created man in his image and established him in his friendship; as a spiritual creature, man can live this friendship only in free submission to God. The prohibition against eating \"of the tree of the knowledge of good and evil\" symbolises the limits we must freely recognise. Tempted by the devil, man let his trust in his Creator die in his heart and, abusing his freedom, disobeyed. All subsequent sin would be disobedience towards God and lack of trust in his goodness. Man preferred himself to God. The consequences: the harmony of original justice was destroyed; the union of man and woman became subject to tensions and domination; harmony with creation was broken; and death entered human history."],["402–406","All people are implicated in Adam's sin (Rom 5:12–19). How? The whole human race is in Adam \"as one body of one man\". Adam and Eve received original holiness and justice not for themselves alone but for all human nature; by sinning they transmitted a human nature deprived of original holiness and justice. That is why original sin is called \"sin\" only in an analogical sense: it is a sin \"contracted\" and not \"committed\", a state and not an act. Human nature is not totally corrupted: it is wounded in its natural powers, subject to ignorance, suffering and death, and inclined to sin (concupiscence). Baptism, by imparting Christ's life of grace, erases original sin and turns a person back towards God, but the consequences for nature remain, calling us to spiritual battle."],["407–409","The doctrine of original sin gives lucid discernment of our situation and actions in the world. Ignoring that we have a wounded nature inclined to evil gives rise to serious errors in education, politics, social action and morals. Original sin and all personal sins put the world in a sinful condition: the whole of human history has been a hard battle against the powers of evil."],["410–412","After the fall, man was not abandoned by God. God calls him and announces in a mysterious way the coming victory over evil and his restoration from the fall: the \"Protoevangelium\" (Gen 3:15). Tradition sees here the New Adam, who by his obedience unto death makes amends superabundantly, and Mary, the New Eve. Why did God not prevent the first man from sinning? St Leo: Christ's inexpressible grace gave us blessings better than those the demon's envy took away. St Thomas: nothing prevents human nature being raised up to something greater even after sin. Hence the Exsultet: \"O happy fault, which gained for us so great a Redeemer!\""]],
 mis:[["Original sin means a newborn baby is personally guilty of Adam's act.","Original sin is \"contracted\", a state, not a personal act; it is sin only analogically (§ 404)."],["Original sin means human nature is totally corrupt.","Our nature is wounded, not totally corrupted (§ 405)."],["The devil is God's evil equal.","He is only a creature, powerful but not infinite, and cannot stop God's Kingdom (§ 395)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand original sin accurately (state, not act; wounded, not corrupted) and see it in the light of Christ's victory.",discuss:["Where do you see the effects of original sin in the world? In yourself?","Why do false ideas about human nature lead to errors in politics and education (§ 407)?","What does the Exsultet mean by \"O happy fault\"?"],act:"Read Genesis 3:1–15 and mark: the temptation (distrust), the act (disobedience), the consequences (shame, blame, broken relationships), and the promise (v. 15). Then read Romans 5:17–19."},
 saints:[["Exsultet (Easter Vigil)","O truly necessary sin of Adam, destroyed completely by the death of Christ! O happy fault that earned so great, so glorious a Redeemer!"],["St Leo the Great (Sermon 73.4)","Christ's inexpressible grace gave us blessings better than those the demon's envy had taken away."]],
 more:[CQ("At its root, what was the first sin?",["Eating forbidden fruit for pleasure","Letting trust in the Creator die, and disobeying","Laziness"],1,"§ 397."),CQ("What does Baptism do regarding original sin?",["Nothing","Erases it, though its consequences for nature remain","Removes concupiscence completely"],1,"§ 405.")]});
cccD("c31",{paras:[["422–429","\"When the time had fully come, God sent forth his Son.\" This is the Gospel: the good news of Jesus Christ, the Son of God. At the heart of catechesis we find a Person: Jesus of Nazareth, the only Son from the Father, who suffered and died for us and now, after rising, is living with us forever. To catechise is to reveal in Christ the whole of God's plan, and to lead people into communion with him. Whoever is called to teach Christ must first seek the surpassing worth of knowing him."],["430–435","Jesus means in Hebrew \"God saves\". The name expresses both his identity and his mission: since God alone can forgive sins, it is God who, in Jesus his eternal Son made man, will save his people. In Jesus, God recapitulates all of his history of salvation. The name of Jesus is at the heart of Christian prayer; liturgical prayers conclude \"through our Lord Jesus Christ\"."],["436–440","Christ comes from the Greek for the Hebrew Messiah, \"anointed\". In Israel those consecrated to God for a mission were anointed: kings, priests and sometimes prophets. Jesus fulfilled the divine mission of priest, prophet and king; the Spirit anointed him at his conception and baptism. Jesus accepted the title Messiah, but with reserve, because many understood it politically; he revealed its true meaning in the Son of Man who came to give his life as a ransom."],["441–445","In the Old Testament \"son of God\" was a title given to angels, the chosen people, and kings. But when Peter confessed Jesus as \"the Christ, the Son of the living God\", Jesus said this came from the Father: Peter recognised the transcendent character of his divine sonship. Jesus himself calls himself \"the only Son of God\", and the Father declares at the Baptism and Transfiguration: \"This is my beloved Son.\" After the Resurrection his divine sonship is fully manifest."],["446–451","In the Greek Old Testament, the divine name YHWH is rendered Kyrios, \"Lord\". The New Testament uses \"Lord\" both for the Father and for Jesus, recognising him as God. Jesus attributes the title to himself; the disciples address him with it; and the first Christians confess that \"Jesus is Lord\", ascribing to him the power, honour and glory due to God. The lordship of Christ means that no earthly power can claim the absolute submission due to God alone. Christian prayer is marked by the title \"Lord\": \"Marana tha\", \"Come, Lord!\""]],
 mis:[["\"Christ\" is Jesus' surname.","It is a title: the Anointed One, the Messiah (§ 436)."],["\"Son of God\" just means a very holy man.","Jesus is the only Son of God in a unique, divine sense (§§ 441–445)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants can explain each title of Jesus and what it reveals about him.",discuss:["What does each title (Jesus, Christ, Son of God, Lord) add to our picture of him?","Why did Jesus avoid the title Messiah at first?","What would it mean for you to call Jesus \"Lord\" in practice?"],act:"Make a four-column chart: Jesus / Christ / Son of God / Lord. Under each, write its meaning, one Bible passage, and one way to pray with it."},
 saints:[["St Bernard of Clairvaux (Sermon 15 on the Song of Songs)","Jesus is honey in the mouth, music in the ear, a song of joy in the heart."],["St Paul (Phil 2:10–11)","At the name of Jesus every knee should bow… and every tongue confess that Jesus Christ is Lord."]],
 more:[CQ("What is at the heart of catechesis, according to § 426?",["A set of rules","A Person: Jesus of Nazareth","The Church's history"],1,"§ 426."),CQ("What does \"Christ\" mean?",["Saviour","Anointed","Teacher"],1,"§ 436.")]});
cccD("c32",{paras:[["456–460","Why did the Word become flesh? With the Nicene Creed we answer: \"For us men and for our salvation.\" (1) To save us by reconciling us with God. (2) So that we might know God's love. (3) To be our model of holiness. (4) To make us \"partakers of the divine nature\": \"the Son of God became man so that we might become God\" (St Athanasius), sharing his life by grace."],["461–463","Taking up St John's expression \"the Word became flesh\", the Church calls \"Incarnation\" the fact that the Son of God assumed a human nature to accomplish our salvation in it. Belief in the true Incarnation is the distinctive sign of Christian faith: \"Every spirit which confesses that Jesus Christ has come in the flesh is of God.\""],["464–469","The Incarnation does not mean that Jesus is part God and part man, nor a confused mixture. He became truly man while remaining truly God. The Church had to defend this against heresies: Gnostic Docetism (denying his true humanity); Arianism (denying his divinity; Nicaea 325); Nestorianism (making Christ two persons; Ephesus 431 called Mary Theotokos, Mother of God); Monophysitism (merging the natures; Chalcedon 451: one Person \"in two natures, without confusion, change, division or separation\"). Constantinople II (553) confessed that \"one of the Trinity\" was crucified. Christ is \"one of the Trinity\"."],["470–474","Because the human nature was assumed, not absorbed, the Church has confessed the full reality of Christ's human soul, with its operations of intellect and will, and of his human body. Everything in his humanity is attributed to his divine Person. His human soul had true human knowledge, which could \"increase in wisdom\"; at the same time it knew intimately the Father and the eternal plans it came to reveal."],["475–478","Christ has two wills and two natural operations, divine and human, not opposed but cooperating, so that the human will freely follows his divine will (Constantinople III, 681). Christ's body was truly human: he can be portrayed (Nicaea II, 787, defended icons). Jesus knew and loved us each and all during his life, his agony and his Passion, and gave himself up for each one of us: \"The Son of God loved me and gave himself for me.\" His Sacred Heart is the sign and symbol of that love."]],
 mis:[["Jesus is half God and half man.","He is fully God and fully man, one divine Person in two natures (§ 464)."],["Jesus only seemed to be human (or only seemed to suffer).","This is Docetism; his humanity, body and soul, is fully real (§§ 465, 470)."],["Jesus did not have a real human mind or will.","He has a true human intellect and will, freely united to the divine will (§§ 472, 475)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants can state the doctrine of the Incarnation precisely, name the main heresies, and see why it matters for salvation.",discuss:["Why does it matter that Jesus is fully God? Fully human?","What does Athanasius mean by \"so that we might become God\"?","How does knowing Jesus loved you personally in his Passion (§ 478) change your prayer?"],act:"Heresy detective: give groups short statements (\"Jesus only appeared to be human\", \"Jesus was a created being\", \"Mary is only the mother of Jesus' humanity\") and ask them to name the error and the council that answered it."},
 saints:[["St Athanasius (On the Incarnation 54)","The Son of God became man so that we might become God."],["St Gregory of Nazianzus (Letter 101)","What has not been assumed has not been healed."]],
 more:[CQ("Which council confessed Christ as one Person in two natures, without confusion or separation?",["Nicaea","Chalcedon","Trent"],1,"§ 467."),CQ("How many wills does Christ have?",["One","Two, divine and human, in harmony","Three"],1,"§ 475.")]});
cccD("c33",{paras:[["484–486","The Annunciation inaugurates \"the fullness of time\": the Son is conceived by the Holy Spirit in Mary's womb. The Father sends the Spirit to sanctify her womb and make it fruitful. The Son's anointing by the Spirit begins at his conception."],["487–489","What the Catholic faith believes about Mary is based on what it believes about Christ, and in turn sheds light on faith in Christ. Mary was prepared by God throughout the Old Testament, in women who carried the promise (Eve, Sarah, Hannah, Deborah, Ruth, Judith, Esther)."],["490–493","To become the mother of the Saviour, Mary was \"enriched by God with gifts appropriate to such a role\": the angel greets her as \"full of grace\". Over the centuries the Church came to understand that she was redeemed from the moment of her conception: the Immaculate Conception, defined by Pius IX (1854), preserved her immune from all stain of original sin, by a singular grace in view of the merits of Jesus Christ. By grace she remained free of every personal sin her whole life long."],["494–495","At the Annunciation Mary gave the obedience of faith: \"Let it be to me according to your word.\" She became the mother of Jesus, embracing God's saving will wholeheartedly. As St Irenaeus said, \"being obedient she became the cause of salvation for herself and for the whole human race\": the knot of Eve's disobedience was untied by Mary's obedience. Called \"the mother of my Lord\" by Elizabeth, she is truly Mother of God (Theotokos), because the one she conceived as man is the eternal Son of the Father."],["496–498","From the first formulations of faith the Church confessed that Jesus was conceived solely by the power of the Holy Spirit, without a human father. The Fathers saw in the virginal conception the sign that it truly was the Son of God who came in a humanity like ours. Some see it as myth; but faith in the virginal conception was met with mockery from the beginning, and is accessible only to faith that understands the connection of the mysteries."],["499–507","The deepening of faith in the virginal motherhood led the Church to confess Mary's real and perpetual virginity, even in the act of giving birth. The Church's liturgy celebrates her as \"ever-virgin\". The \"brothers and sisters\" of Jesus mentioned in the Gospels are close relations, according to an Old Testament usage. Mary's virginity manifests God's absolute initiative in the Incarnation; it is a sign of her faith \"unadulterated by any doubt\" and of her undivided gift of herself to God. Mary is both virgin and mother because she is the figure and most perfect realisation of the Church."]],
 mis:[["The Immaculate Conception means Jesus was conceived without a human father.","That is the Virgin Birth; the Immaculate Conception concerns Mary's own conception, free from original sin (§ 491)."],["Mary didn't need a Saviour.","She was redeemed in an eminent way, in view of Christ's merits (§ 492)."],["Jesus had biological brothers and sisters.","The Gospels' \"brothers and sisters\" are close relatives, following Hebrew usage (§ 500)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants can explain the Immaculate Conception, the virginal conception, Mary as Mother of God and her perpetual virginity, all in relation to Christ.",discuss:["Why does what we believe about Mary always depend on what we believe about Jesus (§ 487)?","What does Irenaeus mean by Mary \"untying the knot\" of Eve?","How would you answer someone who says calling Mary \"Mother of God\" makes her a goddess?"],act:"Read Luke 1:26–45 together. Mark each title or description given to Mary, and say what it means."},
 saints:[["St Irenaeus (Against Heresies III.22.4)","The knot of Eve's disobedience was untied by Mary's obedience: what the virgin Eve bound by her unbelief, the Virgin Mary loosed by her faith."],["St Augustine (Sermon 186)","She remained a virgin in conceiving her Son, a virgin in giving birth to him, a virgin in carrying him, a virgin in nursing him."]],
 more:[CQ("Who defined the dogma of the Immaculate Conception, and when?",["Pius IX in 1854","Pius XII in 1950","John Paul II in 1987"],0,"§ 491."),CQ("Why is Mary called Mother of God?",["Because she created God","Because the one she conceived is the eternal Son of God","Because she is divine"],1,"§ 495.")]});
cccD("c34",{paras:[["512–513","The Creed speaks only of the Incarnation and of the Paschal mystery, but everything in Jesus' life should be seen as a sign of his mystery. The Catechism first considers what is common to all his mysteries, then the main mysteries of his hidden and public life."],["514–521","Many things about Jesus are not reported in the Gospels; what was written was set down \"that you may believe\". Christ's whole earthly life is a revelation of the Father: \"Whoever has seen me has seen the Father.\" His whole life is a mystery of redemption, which comes to us above all through his blood, but is at work throughout his life. It is a mystery of recapitulation: all he did, said and suffered restores fallen humanity to its original vocation. All that Christ lived, he lived for us, and he enables us to live in him what he lived."],["522–534","God's preparations: the Old Covenant and John the Baptist. Christmas: Jesus born in a humble stable, to poor shepherds, the first witnesses; to become a child in relation to God is the condition for entering the Kingdom. The mysteries of Jesus' infancy: circumcision, the Epiphany (Jesus manifested as Messiah of Israel, Son of God and Saviour of the world), the Presentation, the flight into Egypt. The hidden life at Nazareth: an ordinary life of manual work and obedience, a model for family life and sanctifying everyday life. The finding in the Temple: \"Did you not know that I must be in my Father's house?\""],["535–540","Jesus' public life begins with his baptism by John: he lets himself be numbered among sinners; the Spirit descends and the Father proclaims him his beloved Son. In Baptism the Christian is sacramentally assimilated to Jesus. After his baptism, Jesus is tempted in the desert: he is the new Adam who remains faithful where the first Adam gave in. His victory over the tempter in the desert anticipates his victory at the Passion."],["541–556","Jesus proclaims the Kingdom of God, inviting everyone, especially the poor and sinners, to enter by faith and conversion; parables reveal and veil it. Signs and miracles show that the Kingdom is present and that he is the promised Messiah, though they can also be occasions for \"offence\". He chooses the Twelve, and gives Peter the keys: authority to govern the house of God. At the Transfiguration, a foretaste of the Kingdom, the Father, Son and Spirit appear, and Jesus speaks of his \"departure\"."],["557–560","Jesus resolutely goes up to Jerusalem, ready to die there. His messianic entry into Jerusalem, on a donkey, manifests the coming of the King who will accomplish his work by the Passover of his death and Resurrection. The Church celebrates it on Palm Sunday."]],
 mis:[["Only Jesus' death matters for salvation; the rest of his life is just background.","His whole life is a mystery of redemption, revelation and recapitulation (§§ 516–518)."],["Miracles were just tricks to impress people.","They are signs that the Kingdom is present and invite faith (§§ 547–548)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants see Jesus' whole life as revealing the Father and as something we share in, and can connect the mysteries to the Rosary and the liturgical year.",discuss:["Which mystery of Jesus' life (birth, hidden life, baptism, temptation, miracles, Transfiguration) speaks most to your life now?","What does the hidden life at Nazareth teach about ordinary work and family?","How does the Rosary help us \"live in him what he lived\" (§ 521)?"],act:"Map the mysteries of the Rosary (Joyful, Luminous) onto the sections of §§ 522–560. Pray one decade on the mystery of the Baptism of the Lord."},
 saints:[["St John Eudes (On the Kingdom of Jesus)","We must continue to accomplish in ourselves the stages of Jesus' life and his mysteries, and often beg him to perfect and realise them in us and in his whole Church."],["St Paul VI (Nazareth address, 5 January 1964)","The home of Nazareth is the school where we begin to understand the life of Jesus: the school of the Gospel."]],
 more:[CQ("What three aspects are common to all Christ's mysteries (§§ 516–518)?",["Revelation, redemption, recapitulation","Teaching, healing, preaching","Birth, death, resurrection"],0,"§§ 516–518."),CQ("Who were the first witnesses of Christ's birth?",["The Magi","Poor shepherds","The priests of the Temple"],1,"§ 525.")]});
cccD("c35",{paras:[["571–573","The Paschal mystery of Christ's cross and Resurrection is at the centre of the Good News. The Gospels give much space to his Passion, and faith tries to understand the circumstances of his death, faithfully handed on by the Gospels and illuminated by other historical sources."],["574–576","From the beginning of Jesus' public ministry, certain Pharisees and partisans of Herod, together with priests and scribes, agreed together to destroy him, accusing him of expelling demons by Beelzebul, forgiving sins, healing on the Sabbath and associating with sinners. In the eyes of many in Israel Jesus seemed to act against essential institutions: obedience to the Law, the centrality of the Temple, and faith in the one God."],["577–582","Jesus and the Law: he did not come to abolish the Law but to fulfil it perfectly, which only he, the divine Legislator, could do. The Law's perfect fulfilment would require someone born under the Law to keep it perfectly, which Jesus did. He gave the Law its definitive interpretation as one with divine authority (\"But I say to you\"), and he disavowed certain human traditions of the Pharisees that were \"making void the word of God\"."],["583–586","Jesus and the Temple: he venerated the Temple, went up there for the feasts, and was zealous for it as his Father's house. He foretold its destruction, which some took as hostility, and presented himself as the definitive dwelling place of God among men: his body is the true Temple."],["587–591","Jesus and Israel's faith in the one God: the greatest scandal was his claim to act as God, especially by forgiving sins. His identification of himself with God was a real challenge: either he was blaspheming, or he was who he claimed to be. Faced with this, some leaders could only see blasphemy; others believed. Jesus asked for faith in his works; but the Sanhedrin judged him deserving of death for blasphemy, acting out of \"ignorance\" and the \"hardness\" of unbelief."]],
 mis:[["Jesus rejected the Jewish Law and the Temple.","He fulfilled the Law, venerated the Temple, and revealed himself as its fulfilment (§§ 577, 583–586)."],["\"The Jews\" killed Jesus.","The Church rejects collective blame; all sinners are the authors of Christ's Passion (§§ 597–598)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand why Jesus came into conflict with some leaders, and reject antisemitic misreadings of the Passion.",discuss:["Why was Jesus' forgiving sins so shocking (Mark 2:7)?","What does it mean that Jesus is the new Temple?","Why does the Church insist that our sins, not \"the Jews\", crucified Christ?"],act:"Read Mark 2:1–12. Role-play the scribes' reaction, then discuss: what are the only two options if Jesus really forgives sins?"},
 saints:[["Roman Catechism I.5.11 (quoted in CCC 598)","We must regard as guilty all those who continue to relapse into sin. Our crimes made our Lord Jesus Christ suffer the torment of the cross."],["St Francis of Assisi (Admonitions 5)","Nor did demons crucify him; it is you who have crucified him and crucify him still, when you delight in your vices and sins."]],
 more:[CQ("Did Jesus come to abolish the Law?",["Yes","No, to fulfil it","Only the moral law"],1,"§ 577; Matt 5:17."),CQ("What was the most scandalous claim for Jesus' opponents?",["That he healed people","That he acted as God, forgiving sins","That he ate with his disciples"],1,"§§ 587–591.")]});
cccD("c36",{paras:[["595–598","Among the religious authorities some did not oppose Jesus (Nicodemus, Joseph of Arimathea). The historical complexity of Jesus' trial is shown in the Gospels: personal sin of those involved is known to God alone. Jesus' death cannot be attributed to all the Jews then living, nor to Jews today; the Church rejects this. All sinners were the authors of Christ's Passion; Christians bear the graver responsibility, because \"we profess to know him\"."],["599–605","Jesus' violent death was not the result of chance; it belongs to the mystery of God's plan, as Peter explained at Pentecost: \"delivered up according to the definite plan and foreknowledge of God.\" This does not mean those who handed him over were passive actors in a scenario: God permitted their acts, which flowed from their blindness. Christ died \"for our sins in accordance with the Scriptures\": the suffering Servant (Isaiah 53). God took the initiative of universal redeeming love: \"he loved us and sent his Son to be the expiation for our sins.\" Christ's love excludes no one: there is not, never has been and never will be a single human being for whom Christ did not suffer."],["606–612","The Son of God came to do the Father's will; his whole life was a sacrifice for the sins of the world. Jesus embraced the Father's love freely: \"No one takes my life from me, but I lay it down of my own accord.\" At the Last Supper he expressed the free offering of himself: \"This is my body which is given for you.\" He made the Last Supper the memorial of his voluntary offering, and instituted his apostles as priests of the New Covenant. In the agony at Gethsemane, his human will accepted the Father's will: \"not as I will, but as you will.\""],["613–618","Christ's death is the Paschal sacrifice that accomplishes the definitive redemption of humanity, through \"the Lamb of God, who takes away the sin of the world\", and the sacrifice of the New Covenant, which restores communion between God and humanity. It is unique, completing and surpassing all other sacrifices. By his obedience unto death, Jesus substituted himself for us, making satisfaction for our sins: it is love \"to the end\" that gives his sacrifice its value. The Council of Trent: \"by his most holy Passion on the wood of the cross he merited justification for us.\" The cross is the unique sacrifice of Christ, but he calls his disciples to take up their cross and follow him; Mary was associated most intimately with it."],["624–630","\"By the grace of God he tasted death for everyone.\" God's saving plan included that his Son not only die but \"taste death\": experience the separation of his soul and body during the time between his death and Resurrection. Christ's body remained united to his divine Person during the time in the tomb; it did not see corruption. Baptism, by immersion, signifies the descent into the tomb by which the Christian dies to sin with Christ."]],
 mis:[["God the Father was angry and took it out on his innocent Son.","God took the initiative of redeeming love; Jesus freely embraced the Father's will out of love (§§ 604, 609)."],["Christ died only for some people.","There is no one for whom Christ did not suffer (§ 605)."],["Each Mass repeats Christ's sacrifice.","His sacrifice is unique and once for all; the Mass makes it present (§§ 614, 1366)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand Christ's death as God's loving plan, freely accepted, a unique sacrifice for all, and see their call to share it.",discuss:["Why is it important that Jesus freely laid down his life?","How does the Last Supper interpret the Cross?","What does it mean for you to \"take up your cross\" this week?"],act:"Pray the Stations of the Cross together, pausing at the 11th station (Jesus nailed to the cross) to read John 10:17–18."},
 saints:[["St Rose of Lima","Apart from the cross there is no other ladder by which we may get to heaven."],["St Thomas Aquinas (Collations on the Creed, art. 4)","No example of virtue is absent from the Cross: if you seek charity, patience, humility, obedience, or contempt for earthly things, you will find them there."]],
 more:[CQ("Was there anyone for whom Christ did not suffer?",["Yes, the damned","No; there is no human being for whom Christ did not suffer","Only those before his time"],1,"§ 605."),CQ("At which event did Jesus express in advance the free offering of himself?",["The Transfiguration","The Last Supper","The wedding at Cana"],1,"§ 610.")]});
cccD("c37",{paras:[["631–635","\"He descended into hell\": the apostolic preaching affirms that Jesus, like all people, experienced death and went to the realm of the dead. But he went there as Saviour, proclaiming the Good News to the spirits imprisoned. \"Hell\" (Sheol, Hades) here means the abode of the dead, deprived of the vision of God, where the just awaited their Redeemer. Jesus did not descend to deliver the damned, nor to destroy the hell of damnation, but to free the just who had gone before him. The descent is the last phase of his messianic mission: the spread of his redemptive work to all people of all times and places."],["638–644","\"We bring you the good news that what God promised to the fathers, this he has fulfilled to us their children by raising Jesus.\" The Resurrection of Jesus is the crowning truth of our faith. It is a real event, with historically verifiable manifestations: the empty tomb, the first sign; and the appearances: to Mary Magdalene and the holy women, the first messengers; to Peter; to the Twelve; and to more than five hundred at once. The disciples' faith was not invented: they were discouraged and frightened, and did not believe at first. Their faith was born of their experience of the risen Lord."],["645–647","By touching and eating with them, Jesus showed that he was not a ghost: his risen body is the same body that was tortured and crucified, bearing the marks of his Passion. Yet this authentic, real body possesses the new properties of a glorious body, not limited by space and time. Christ's Resurrection was not a return to earthly life like that of Lazarus; it is an essentially different reality. No one witnessed the Resurrection itself; it remains at the very heart of the mystery of faith, as something that transcends history."],["648–650","The Resurrection is the work of the Holy Trinity: the Father raised Christ; the Son rose by his own power; the Spirit gave life to his body. The Fathers contemplate it from the divine Person of Christ, who remained united to both his soul and body even when separated by death."],["651–655","The Resurrection confirms all that Christ did and taught; fulfils the promises of the Old Testament and of Jesus himself; confirms his divinity (\"When you have lifted up the Son of Man, then you will know that I am he\"); and is the principle of our justification: it opens the way to new life and makes us brothers and sisters of Christ. Finally, it is the source of our own future resurrection: \"Christ has been raised from the dead, the first fruits of those who have fallen asleep.\""]],
 mis:[["The Resurrection is a symbol for the disciples' renewed hope.","It is a real event with historically attested signs, though it also transcends history (§§ 639, 647)."],["Jesus came back to the same earthly life, like Lazarus.","His risen body is real but glorious, an essentially different reality (§ 646)."],["The descent into hell means Jesus suffered among the damned.","He went to the realm of the dead as Saviour, to free the just (§§ 632–633)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand the descent to the dead and the Resurrection as real, transformative events, and can present the evidence and its meaning.",discuss:["Why is the empty tomb not enough on its own?","What do the disciples' initial doubts tell us about the Resurrection's credibility?","What difference does the Resurrection make to how you face death and suffering?"],act:"Read 1 Corinthians 15:3–8 and 12–20. Make two lists: the evidence Paul gives, and what follows if Christ is not raised."},
 saints:[["Ancient homily for Holy Saturday (Office of Readings)","Something strange is happening: there is a great silence on earth… The King is asleep. God has died in the flesh and hell trembles with fear… \"Awake, O sleeper, and rise from the dead.\""],["St John Paul II (Adelaide, 1986), echoing St Augustine","We are an Easter people, and Alleluia is our song."]],
 more:[CQ("Who were the first messengers of the Resurrection?",["The apostles","Mary Magdalene and the holy women","The Roman guards"],1,"§ 641."),CQ("Is Christ's Resurrection like the raising of Lazarus?",["Yes, exactly","No; Christ's risen body is glorious, an essentially different reality","Lazarus was never raised"],1,"§ 646.")]});
cccD("c38",{paras:[["659–664","\"So then the Lord Jesus, after he had spoken to them, was taken up into heaven, and sat down at the right hand of God.\" Christ's body was glorified from the moment of his Resurrection; but for forty days he ate and drank familiarly with his disciples. His final appearance ends with the irreversible entry of his humanity into divine glory, symbolised by the cloud and by heaven. Left to its own natural powers, humanity does not have access to the Father's house; only Christ can open it, so that we, his members, may confidently hope to go where he, our Head, has preceded us. As high priest of the New Covenant he intercedes constantly for us. Being seated at the Father's right hand means the inauguration of the Messiah's kingdom."],["668–672","Christ already reigns through the Church; the Kingdom is mysteriously present in her. But it is not yet fulfilled \"with power and great glory\" by the King's return to earth. It is still under attack by evil powers. Until then the Church on earth suffers the birth pangs of the new creation; that is why Christians pray, above all in the Eucharist, \"Marana tha\", \"Come, Lord Jesus!\""],["673–677","Since the Ascension, Christ's coming in glory has been imminent, though \"it is not for you to know the times or seasons\". His glorious coming will be accomplished after the recognition of the Messiah by \"all Israel\". Before it, the Church must pass through a final trial that will shake many believers' faith: a religious deception offering an apparent solution to problems at the price of apostasy from the truth, the \"Antichrist\". The Church will enter the glory of the Kingdom only through this final Passover, following her Lord in his death and Resurrection."],["678–682","Following the prophets and John the Baptist, Jesus announced the judgement of the Last Day. Then the conduct of each one and the secrets of hearts will be brought to light; attitude to one's neighbour will reveal acceptance or refusal of grace and divine love: \"As you did it to one of the least of these my brethren, you did it to me.\" Christ is Lord of eternal life; full right to judge definitively belongs to him as Redeemer of the world. But the Son did not come to judge, but to save; by rejecting grace in this life, one already judges oneself."]],
 mis:[["The Ascension means Jesus left and is now absent.","He reigns, intercedes for us, and is present in the Church, the sacraments and the Spirit (§§ 662–669)."],["Christians should calculate the date of the end of the world.","\"It is not for you to know the times or seasons\" (§ 673)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand Christ's Ascension, reign and return, and the meaning of judgement as the revelation of love accepted or refused.",discuss:["Why does Christ's entry into heaven with his humanity give us hope?","How does \"already but not yet\" describe the Kingdom?","How does Matthew 25:31–46 shape the way we live now?"],act:"Read Matthew 25:31–46. Each person chooses one of the works of mercy listed and commits to doing it this week."},
 saints:[["St Augustine (Sermon on the Ascension, 98)","Christ is now exalted above the heavens, but he still suffers on earth all the pains that we, his members, endure."],["St John of the Cross (Dichos 64)","In the evening of life, we will be judged on love alone."]],
 more:[CQ("What does Christ do now at the Father's right hand, according to § 662?",["He rests","He intercedes for us as high priest","He waits passively"],1,"§ 662."),CQ("Before Christ's return, what must the Church pass through?",["A golden age","A final trial","Nothing"],1,"§ 675.")]});

// ---------- Catechism notes: "Go deeper" layer, §§ 683–1065 (the Holy Spirit, the Church, the last things) ----------
cccD("c39",{paras:[["683–686","\"No one can say 'Jesus is Lord' except by the Holy Spirit.\" To be in touch with Christ, we must first be touched by the Spirit. He goes before us and awakens faith in us. Through Baptism he gives us new birth in the Father's Son. The Spirit is the first to be revealed in action in our lives, though the last Person of the Trinity to be fully revealed; he is at work with the Father and the Son from the beginning to the completion of God's plan."],["687–690","\"No one comprehends the thoughts of God except the Spirit of God.\" The Spirit who reveals God makes known to us Christ, his Word, but does not speak of himself. We know him only in the movement by which he reveals the Word to us and disposes us to welcome him. The Church is the place where we know the Spirit: in Scripture, Tradition, the Magisterium, the sacraments, prayer, charisms and ministries, the apostolic life and the witness of the saints. The mission of the Son and the Spirit is joint and inseparable: whenever the Father sends his Word, he always sends his Breath."],["691–701","\"Holy Spirit\" is the proper name of the third Person. Jesus also calls him the Paraclete (\"he who is called to one's side\", advocate, consoler) and the Spirit of truth. St Paul speaks of the Spirit of the promise, of adoption, of Christ, of the Lord, of God. The symbols: water (new birth in Baptism); anointing (the Christ, the \"anointed\"; chrism); fire (transforming energy, Elijah, Pentecost); cloud and light (Sinai, the Transfiguration); the seal (the indelible character); the hand (laying on of hands); the finger (\"by the finger of God I cast out demons\"); the dove (at the Flood and at Jesus' baptism)."],["702–716","From the beginning until the fullness of time, the Spirit prepared for the Messiah. He was at work in creation (\"the Spirit of God was moving over the waters\"); in the promise to Abraham; in the theophanies and the Law; in the kingdom and the exile; and in the prophets, who announced a Messiah on whom the Spirit would rest (Isaiah 11 and 61) and a new heart and new spirit for God's people (Ezekiel 36)."],["717–730","In the fullness of time, John the Baptist is \"filled with the Holy Spirit\" from his mother's womb: Elijah who is to come. Mary is the masterwork of the Spirit's mission in the fullness of time: prepared by him, made fruitful by him, the burning bush of definitive theophany, the new Eve. Christ Jesus is anointed with the Spirit; he reveals the Spirit gradually, and on the day of his Resurrection breathes on the apostles: \"Receive the Holy Spirit.\""],["731–741","On the day of Pentecost Christ's Passover is fulfilled in the outpouring of the Spirit, manifested, given and communicated as a divine Person. The Spirit is God's gift of love, the first gift, which contains all others; the love \"poured into our hearts\". By his power the children of God bear fruit: \"love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, faithfulness, gentleness, self-control\". The Spirit builds, animates and sanctifies the Church, and makes her the sacrament of communion; he helps us in our weakness, interceding with sighs too deep for words."]],
 mis:[["The Holy Spirit is a force or energy, not a Person.","He is a divine Person, the third Person of the Trinity, given at Pentecost as a Person (§§ 689, 731)."],["The Spirit only appears at Pentecost.","He was at work from creation, through the prophets, and in Christ's whole life (§§ 702–730)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants know who the Holy Spirit is, his names and symbols, and how he acts in the Church and in each Christian.",discuss:["Why do you think the Spirit is the \"hidden\" Person of the Trinity?","Which symbol of the Spirit speaks to you most?","Where have you seen the fruit of the Spirit (Gal 5:22–23) in someone's life?"],act:"Set out seven symbols (water, oil, a candle, a cloud picture, a seal or stamp, an open hand, a paper dove). In groups, match each to a Bible passage and say what it teaches about the Spirit."},
 saints:[["St Basil the Great (On the Holy Spirit 15.36)","Through the Holy Spirit we are restored to paradise, led back to the Kingdom of heaven, and adopted as children."],["St Seraphim of Sarov (conversation with Motovilov)","The true aim of the Christian life is the acquisition of the Holy Spirit of God."]],
 more:[CQ("What does \"Paraclete\" mean?",["Messenger","He who is called to one's side: advocate, consoler","Fire"],1,"§ 692."),CQ("Who is called the masterwork of the Spirit's mission in the fullness of time?",["John the Baptist","Mary","Peter"],1,"§ 721.")]});
cccD("c40",{paras:[["748–750","\"Christ is the light of humanity\" (Lumen Gentium 1); that light is reflected on the face of the Church. The article on the Church depends entirely on the article on Christ Jesus. The Church has no other light than Christ's. To believe that the Church is \"holy\" and \"catholic\" is inseparable from belief in God the Father, Son and Spirit: we believe the Church, not in the Church, to avoid confusing God with his works."],["751–752","The word \"Church\" (Latin ecclesia, from the Greek ek-kalein, \"to call out of\") means a convocation or assembly. In the Old Testament it was used for the chosen people gathered before God, especially at Sinai. The first Christians called themselves \"Church\". In Christian usage \"church\" means the liturgical assembly, the local community, and the whole universal community of believers. These three cannot be separated."],["753–757","Scripture gives many images of the Church: the sheepfold (Christ the gate and shepherd); the cultivated field and vineyard (Christ the true vine); the building of God, with Christ as cornerstone, God's household, the temple; our mother, the Jerusalem above; and the spotless bride of the spotless Lamb."],["758–769","The Church's origin, foundation and mission: the Church was in God's plan from the beginning, \"prefigured from the world's beginning\". It was prepared in the Old Covenant, in the calling of Abraham and Israel. It was instituted by Christ: he inaugurated it by preaching the Kingdom, choosing the Twelve with Peter as their head, and above all by his total self-giving on the Cross; the Church was born from his pierced side, \"as Eve was formed from the sleeping Adam's side\". It was revealed by the Holy Spirit at Pentecost, and will be perfected in glory at the end of time."],["770–776","The Church is in history but transcends it: only the eyes of faith see its spiritual reality. It is at once visible and spiritual, a hierarchical society and the Mystical Body of Christ, an earthly and a heavenly reality. The Church is the universal sacrament of salvation: the sign and instrument of intimate union with God and of the unity of the whole human race. In the Church this communion is already begun; it is the means and goal of God's plan."]],
 mis:[["Jesus didn't intend to found a Church; it was a later human invention.","Christ inaugurated the Church, chose the Twelve with Peter, and gave it life from the Cross; the Spirit revealed it at Pentecost (§§ 763–767)."],["The Church is just its buildings, or just the clergy.","\"Church\" means the whole people God calls together, in liturgy, locally and universally (§ 752)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand what the Church is, where it comes from, and why it is both visible and spiritual.",discuss:["Which image of the Church (flock, vine, building, mother, bride) helps you most? Why?","What does it mean that the Church was \"born from Christ's side\"?","How can the Church be holy and full of sinners at the same time?"],act:"Each person draws one of the five biblical images of the Church and explains what it teaches about their own place in the Church."},
 saints:[["St Augustine (Sermon 341)","The whole Christ, head and body: Christ and the Church."],["St Joan of Arc (at her trial, 1431)","About Jesus Christ and the Church, I simply know they're just one thing, and we shouldn't complicate the matter."]],
 more:[CQ("Where was the Church born, according to the Fathers?",["In Rome","From the pierced side of Christ on the Cross","At the Council of Nicaea"],1,"§ 766."),CQ("The Church is the universal sacrament of…",["Rules","Salvation: union with God and unity of the human race","Culture"],1,"§§ 774–776.")]});
cccD("c41",{paras:[["781–786","God wills to make people holy and save them not as individuals without bond, but as a people. The People of God has distinct characteristics: it belongs to God; one becomes a member through faith and Baptism, \"born of water and the Spirit\"; its Head is Jesus Christ; its status is the dignity and freedom of the children of God; its law is the new commandment of love; its mission is to be the salt of the earth and light of the world; its destiny is the Kingdom of God. The whole people shares in Christ's three offices: priestly (offering themselves), prophetic (witnessing), and royal (serving, especially the poor)."],["787–796","From the beginning Jesus associated his disciples with his own life; he proclaimed an intimate communion: \"Abide in me, and I in you.\" By communicating his Spirit, Christ mystically constitutes as his body those brothers and sisters called from all nations. In this body there is a diversity of members and functions, all united to each other, especially to those who suffer and are persecuted. Christ is the Head of the body; the Church is also the Bride of Christ: \"the two shall become one flesh. This is a great mystery\"."],["797–801","\"What the soul is to the human body, the Holy Spirit is to the Body of Christ, the Church\" (St Augustine). The Spirit is the principle of every vital and saving action in each part of the body. He makes the Church the \"temple of the living God\". He gives charisms, extraordinary or simple, ordered to building up the Church and the good of others; they must be discerned by the Church's pastors, and always ordered to charity, the true measure of all charisms."],["802–810","In Brief: Christ gave himself to make a people his own; the Church is the People of God, the Body of Christ, the Temple of the Spirit, and the Bride of Christ; the Church is a people gathered by the unity of the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit."]],
 mis:[["Being a Christian is just me and Jesus.","God saves us as a people, a body; we belong to one another (§§ 781, 790–791)."],["Charisms are only spectacular gifts like healing or tongues.","Charisms can be extraordinary or simple and humble; all are for building up the Church in charity (§ 799)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand the three great images (People, Body, Temple) and their own share in Christ's priestly, prophetic and royal mission.",discuss:["How do you exercise Christ's priestly, prophetic and royal offices as a lay person?","\"If one member suffers, all suffer\" (1 Cor 12:26). Who is suffering in your community?","What charism might God have given you?"],act:"Body-part exercise: each person picks a part of the body (eyes, hands, feet, ears) and names a role in the parish that it represents, then thanks someone who does it."},
 saints:[["St Augustine (Sermon 267.4)","What the soul is to the human body, the Holy Spirit is to the Body of Christ, the Church."],["St Teresa of Avila (attributed)","Christ has no body now on earth but yours, no hands but yours, no feet but yours."]],
 more:[CQ("How does one become a member of the People of God?",["By birth into a Catholic family","By faith and Baptism","By attending Mass once"],1,"§ 782."),CQ("What is the true measure of all charisms?",["Their spectacular nature","Charity","Their number"],1,"§ 800.")]});
cccD("c42",{paras:[["811–812","\"This is the sole Church of Christ, which in the Creed we profess to be one, holy, catholic and apostolic.\" These four characteristics, inseparably linked, indicate essential features of the Church and her mission. The Church does not possess them of herself; it is Christ who, through the Spirit, makes her so, and calls her to realise each quality."],["813–822","The Church is one because of her source (the Trinity), her founder (Christ), and her soul (the Spirit). Bonds of unity: profession of one faith, common celebration of worship and the sacraments, and apostolic succession through Holy Orders. The sole Church of Christ subsists in the Catholic Church, governed by Peter's successor and the bishops in communion with him; yet many elements of sanctification and truth are found outside her visible confines: Scripture, grace, faith, hope, charity, the Spirit's gifts. Those born into communities separated from the Church cannot be charged with the sin of separation; they are rightly called Christians and accepted as brothers in the Lord. Christ always gives his Church the gift of unity, but she must pray and work for full unity (ecumenism): conversion of heart, prayer, mutual knowledge, dialogue and collaboration."],["823–829","The Church is holy: Christ loved her as his Bride and gave himself up to make her holy. She is \"the holy People of God\", and her members are called saints. Yet the Church, \"clasping sinners to her bosom\", is at once holy and always in need of purification. All members, ministers included, must acknowledge that they are sinners. By canonising saints, the Church recognises the power of the Spirit of holiness within her. Mary already embodies the Church's holiness."],["830–856","The Church is catholic, \"universal\": in her subsists the fullness of Christ's body, with the fullness of the means of salvation, and she has been sent out by Christ to the whole human race. Each particular Church (diocese) is catholic through communion with the Church of Rome. All people are called to belong to the People of God, in various ways: the Catholic faithful, other Christians, Jews (\"the first to hear the Word of God\"), Muslims (who \"profess to hold the faith of Abraham\"), and all who seek God. \"Outside the Church there is no salvation\" means, positively, that all salvation comes from Christ the Head through the Church; it does not apply to those who, through no fault of their own, do not know Christ but seek God sincerely and try to do his will. The Church still has the obligation and sacred right to evangelise all."],["857–870","The Church is apostolic: built on the foundation of the apostles; keeping and handing on their teaching with the Spirit's help; and taught, sanctified and guided by the apostles until Christ's return, through their successors, the bishops, assisted by priests, in union with the Pope. The whole Church is apostolic in that she is sent into the whole world; all members share in this mission, in various ways."]],
 mis:[["The Catholic Church says only Catholics can be saved.","All salvation comes from Christ through his Church, but those who through no fault of their own don't know Christ and seek God sincerely may be saved (§§ 846–848)."],["Other Christians are not really Christians.","They are rightly called Christians and accepted as brothers and sisters in the Lord (§ 818)."],["The Church is holy, so its members are not sinners.","The Church is holy and always in need of purification; all her members are sinners (§ 827)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants can explain each mark of the Church, especially \"subsists in\", ecumenism, and the true meaning of \"outside the Church no salvation\".",discuss:["What unites you with a Christian friend from another church? What still divides?","How would you explain \"outside the Church there is no salvation\" to an anxious friend?","Why does the Church still need to evangelise if others can be saved?"],act:"Four corners: label the room's corners One, Holy, Catholic, Apostolic. Read statements (e.g. \"the Pope succeeds Peter\", \"saints are canonised\") and have people move to the mark it illustrates."},
 saints:[["St Cyprian (On the Unity of the Church 6)","He cannot have God for his Father who does not have the Church for his mother."],["St Augustine (Sermon to the People of Caesarea 6)","Outside the Church one can have everything except salvation (said of those who knowingly separate themselves)."]],
 more:[CQ("Where does the one Church of Christ \"subsist\"?",["In all denominations equally","In the Catholic Church","Only in heaven"],1,"§ 816."),CQ("What does \"catholic\" mean?",["Roman","Universal","Ancient"],1,"§ 830.")]});
cccD("c43",{paras:[["871–873","\"The Christian faithful are those who, inasmuch as they have been incorporated into Christ through Baptism, have been constituted as the people of God.\" Among them there is a true equality of dignity and action, and a diversity of ministry. Clergy (the ordained) and laity both come from among the faithful, and some of both are called to consecrated life."],["874–896","Christ instituted the ministries in his Church for the good of the whole Body. The ordained receive a mission and power to act in the person of Christ the Head; this ministry is service. It has a collegial character (the apostles were a college with Peter as head) and a personal one (each is called by name). The Pope, Bishop of Rome and Peter's successor, is \"the perpetual and visible source and foundation of the unity both of the bishops and of the whole company of the faithful\", with full, supreme and universal power. The college of bishops has supreme authority only together with the Pope. Each bishop is the visible principle of unity in his particular Church. The offices of teaching (with the charism of infallibility in defining faith and morals), sanctifying and governing."],["897–913","The laity are all the faithful except those in Holy Orders and the religious state. Their vocation is to seek the Kingdom of God by engaging in temporal affairs and directing them according to God's will: initiative in discovering ways for Christian truth to permeate social, political and economic realities. They share in Christ's priestly office (offering their work, prayer, family life, even hardships), prophetic office (evangelising by word and life), and kingly office (overcoming sin in themselves, making the world's structures more just). They can cooperate in the exercise of the Church's power of governance and in many ministries."],["914–933","Consecrated life, characterised by the public profession of the evangelical counsels of poverty, chastity and obedience in a stable state of life recognised by the Church, is a gift to the Church. It takes many forms: hermits, consecrated virgins and widows, religious life (in monasteries and active congregations), secular institutes, and societies of apostolic life. Its deepest meaning is a more intimate consecration to God, a sign of the world to come, and service of the Church's mission."]],
 mis:[["The laity are second-class Catholics who just \"help Father\".","All the baptised share equal dignity; the laity have their own vocation to sanctify the world (§§ 872, 898)."],["Papal infallibility means the Pope never makes mistakes.","It is a limited charism: when the Pope, as supreme teacher, defines a doctrine of faith or morals; it doesn't cover personal opinions or sinlessness (§ 891)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand the Church's structure as service, the meaning of infallibility, and above all the lay vocation.",discuss:["What is the difference between infallibility and impeccability (sinlessness)?","Where is your \"field\" of mission as a lay person (§ 898)?","What sign does a religious sister or brother give to the world?"],act:"Each person writes three concrete ways they can bring Christ into their study, work or home this week (priestly, prophetic, kingly)."},
 saints:[["St Francis de Sales (Introduction to the Devout Life I.3)","Devotion must be practised differently by the gentleman, the worker, the servant, the prince, the widow, the young girl and the wife; it must be adapted to the strength, life and duties of each."],["St Gregory the Great (Letter to Eulogius)","The Pope is \"servant of the servants of God\"."]],
 more:[CQ("When does the Pope teach infallibly?",["Whenever he speaks","When he defines a doctrine of faith or morals as supreme teacher","Only on Sundays"],1,"§ 891."),CQ("What are the three evangelical counsels?",["Faith, hope, charity","Poverty, chastity, obedience","Prayer, fasting, almsgiving"],1,"§ 915.")]});
cccD("c44",{paras:[["946–948","After confessing the Church, the Creed adds \"the communion of saints\". In a sense this sums up the Church: \"What is the Church if not the assembly of all the saints?\" The communion of saints is the Church. The term has two closely linked meanings: communion in holy things (sancta) and among holy persons (sancti). \"Holy things for the holy\" (Byzantine liturgy)."],["949–953","Communion in spiritual goods: communion in the faith received from the apostles; communion of the sacraments, especially Baptism and the Eucharist; communion of charisms, given for the common good; \"they had everything in common\"; and communion in charity: \"none of us lives to himself\"; the least of our acts done in charity redounds to the profit of all."],["954–959","The three states of the Church: some disciples are pilgrims on earth; others have died and are being purified; still others are in glory. All are united by charity. The saints in heaven intercede for us with the Father; we do not venerate them only for their example, but because union with them strengthens the Church's unity in the Spirit. The Church has from the beginning honoured the memory of the dead and offered prayers for them. Our prayer for them can help them and make their intercession for us effective."],["960–962","The Church is a communion of saints: an image of the Body of Christ, united in one family. We believe in the communion of all the faithful of Christ: pilgrims on earth, the dead being purified, and the blessed in heaven, all together forming one Church."]],
 mis:[["Praying to saints takes away from God.","The saints intercede with God for us; their prayers lead us to Christ, the one Mediator (§ 956)."],["Once someone dies, we can do nothing for them.","We can pray for the dead, and our prayer can help them (§ 958)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand the communion of saints as one family across heaven, purgatory and earth, sharing spiritual goods.",discuss:["How can a small act of charity help the whole Church (§ 953)?","Which saint do you feel closest to? Why?","Who among your dead would you like to pray for?"],act:"Make a \"family tree\" of the communion of saints: your patron saint, a relative who has died, and someone on earth you pray for. Pray for each by name."},
 saints:[["St Dominic (on his deathbed)","Do not weep, for I shall be more useful to you after my death, and I shall help you then more effectively than during my life."],["St Thérèse of Lisieux (Last Conversations, 17 July 1897)","I want to spend my heaven doing good on earth."]],
 more:[CQ("What are the two meanings of \"communion of saints\"?",["Saints and angels","Communion in holy things and among holy persons","Past and future saints"],1,"§ 948."),CQ("Why do we honour the saints, besides their example?",["Because they are divine","Because union with them strengthens the Church's unity in the Spirit","Out of tradition only"],1,"§ 957.")]});
cccD("c45",{paras:[["963–965","After speaking of the Church, we turn to Mary, Mother of Christ and Mother of the Church. She is \"clearly the mother of the members of Christ\" because by her charity she joined in bringing about the birth of believers. Mary's role in the Church is inseparable from her union with Christ: from the virginal conception to his death, she stood by her Son, and at the Cross Jesus gave her to John: \"Woman, behold your son.\" After the Ascension she prayed with the apostles for the gift of the Spirit."],["966","\"Finally the Immaculate Virgin, preserved free from all stain of original sin, when the course of her earthly life was finished, was taken up body and soul into heavenly glory\" (Pius XII, 1950). The Assumption is a singular participation in her Son's Resurrection and an anticipation of the resurrection of other Christians. The Byzantine liturgy: \"In giving birth you kept your virginity; in your Dormition you did not leave the world, O Mother of God.\""],["967–970","By her complete adherence to the Father's will, to her Son's redemptive work, and to every prompting of the Spirit, Mary is the Church's model of faith and charity, the \"exemplary realisation\" of the Church. Her motherhood in the order of grace continues without interruption: taken up to heaven she continues by her intercession to bring us the gifts of salvation. So she is invoked as Advocate, Helper, Benefactress and Mediatrix. Her maternal role does not obscure or diminish Christ's unique mediation, but shows its power: all her saving influence flows from Christ's merits."],["971–975","\"All generations will call me blessed.\" The Church's devotion to Mary is intrinsic to Christian worship. It differs essentially from the adoration given to the incarnate Word, the Father and the Spirit, and greatly fosters it; it is expressed in liturgical feasts and in Marian prayer such as the Rosary, \"an epitome of the whole Gospel\". In Mary we contemplate what the Church already is in her mystery and what she will be in the homeland: an eschatological icon of the Church."]],
 mis:[["Catholics believe Mary is a goddess or an equal mediator with Christ.","Christ is the one Mediator; Mary's role flows entirely from his merits and leads to him (§ 970)."],["The Assumption means Mary didn't die.","The Church defines that she was taken up body and soul \"when the course of her earthly life was finished\"; it doesn't decide whether she died first (East: the Dormition) (§ 966)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand Mary's role as Mother of the Church, the Assumption, and the difference between veneration and worship.",discuss:["How is Mary a model for the whole Church?","How would you explain to a Protestant friend that Marian devotion leads to Christ?","What does the Assumption tell us about our own future?"],act:"Pray one decade of the Rosary together, choosing the mystery of the Assumption. Before each Hail Mary, name someone who needs a mother's care."},
 saints:[["St Louis de Montfort (True Devotion to Mary 120)","The more one is consecrated to Mary, the more one is consecrated to Jesus Christ."],["St Ambrose (Exposition on Luke II.7)","Mary is the type of the Church in the order of faith, charity and perfect union with Christ (quoted in Lumen Gentium 63)."]],
 more:[CQ("When was the Assumption defined, and by whom?",["Pius IX, 1854","Pius XII, 1950","John XXIII, 1962"],1,"§ 966."),CQ("Does Mary's role diminish Christ's unique mediation?",["Yes","No; it flows from and shows the power of his mediation","Only in the Eastern Churches"],1,"§ 970.")]});
cccD("c46",{paras:[["976","The Apostles' Creed links faith in the forgiveness of sins with faith in the Holy Spirit and in the Church. The risen Christ gave the apostles the Spirit and with him the power to forgive sins: \"Receive the Holy Spirit. If you forgive the sins of any, they are forgiven.\""],["977–980","Our Lord tied the forgiveness of sins to faith and Baptism: \"He who believes and is baptised will be saved.\" Baptism is the first and chief sacrament of forgiveness, uniting us to Christ who died and rose. In Baptism all sins are forgiven, original and personal, and all punishment for sin. But baptismal grace does not free human nature from weakness: we must still fight concupiscence. So the Church needed, beyond Baptism, the power to forgive the sins of the baptised: the sacrament of Penance, which the Fathers called \"a laborious kind of baptism\" and \"the second plank after the shipwreck of the loss of grace\"."],["981–983","After his Resurrection Christ sent the apostles to preach repentance and the forgiveness of sins to all nations. The apostles and their successors carry out this ministry of reconciliation through Baptism and Penance. There is no offence, however serious, that the Church cannot forgive. Christ wills that the gates of forgiveness should always be open to anyone who turns away from sin. Catechesis must strive to awaken and nourish faith in this immeasurable greatness of the gift. St Augustine: \"Were there no forgiveness of sins in the Church, there would be no hope of life to come.\""],["984–987","In Brief: the Creed links forgiveness of sins with the Spirit and the Church; Baptism is the first sacrament of forgiveness; by Christ's will the Church has power to forgive the sins of the baptised, exercised through bishops and priests in the sacrament of Penance; in forgiving sins, priests and sacraments are instruments that Christ, the only author of our salvation, uses to blot out our sins."]],
 mis:[["Only God can forgive sins, so priests can't.","Christ himself gave the apostles the power to forgive in his name; the priest is his instrument (§§ 976, 987)."],["Some sins are too great to be forgiven.","There is no offence the Church cannot forgive to someone truly repentant (§ 982)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand how forgiveness comes through Baptism and Penance, and are encouraged to trust God's mercy.",discuss:["Why did Christ link forgiveness to the Church and not only to private prayer?","Why is Penance called \"a second plank after shipwreck\"?","What stops people from going to Confession?"],act:"Read John 20:19–23 together. Then walk through how to make a good Confession, step by step, so that nobody feels anxious about the practicalities."},
 saints:[["St Augustine (Sermon 213.8)","Were there no forgiveness of sins in the Church, there would be no hope of life to come or of eternal liberation."],["Tertullian (On Penance 4), quoted in CCC 1446","Penance is the second plank of salvation after the shipwreck which is the loss of grace."]],
 more:[CQ("What is the first and chief sacrament of forgiveness?",["Penance","Baptism","The Eucharist"],1,"§ 977."),CQ("Is there any sin the Church cannot forgive to a repentant sinner?",["Yes, murder","No","Only sins of priests"],1,"§ 982.")]});
cccD("c47",{paras:[["988–991","The Creed culminates in the proclamation of the resurrection of the dead on the last day and in life everlasting. We firmly believe and hope that, just as Christ is truly risen and lives for ever, so after death the righteous will live for ever with the risen Christ and he will raise them up on the last day. Belief in the resurrection of the dead has been an essential element of Christian faith from its beginnings: \"How can some of you say that there is no resurrection of the dead?\""],["992–996","God revealed the resurrection of the dead to his people progressively: hope in the bodily resurrection grew from faith in the Creator of the whole person, soul and body, and in his faithfulness to his covenant (the martyrs of 2 Maccabees 7; Daniel 12). The Pharisees and many of Jesus' contemporaries hoped for it. Jesus teaches it firmly, links it to faith in himself (\"I am the resurrection and the life\"), and gives signs by raising the dead. From the beginning Christian faith in the resurrection met incomprehension and opposition: \"On no point does the Christian faith encounter more opposition than on the resurrection of the body\" (St Augustine)."],["997–1001","What is \"rising\"? In death the soul is separated from the body, which decays, while the soul goes to meet God, waiting to be reunited with its glorified body. God, in his almighty power, will definitively grant incorruptible life to our bodies by reuniting them with our souls through Jesus' Resurrection. Who will rise? All the dead: \"those who have done good, to the resurrection of life, and those who have done evil, to the resurrection of judgement\". How? Christ rose with his own body; we too shall rise with our own bodies, but transformed into a glorious, spiritual body. This \"how\" exceeds our understanding; it is accessible only to faith. Our participation in the Eucharist already gives us a foretaste. When? Definitively at the last day, at the end of the world."],["1002–1004","Risen with Christ: in a certain way we have already risen with Christ through Baptism, and our Christian life now shares in his risen life, hidden with Christ in God. Our bodies, members of Christ and temples of the Spirit, must be respected: this teaching inspires respect for one's own body and for others', especially the suffering."],["1005–1014","Dying in Christ Jesus: to rise with Christ, we must die with Christ. Death is the end of earthly life; it is a consequence of sin, but Christ transformed it: through Baptism the Christian has already sacramentally \"died with Christ\", and physical death completes this, \"going home to the Lord\". Death puts an end to our time of grace and mercy: \"it is appointed for men to die once\". There is no reincarnation after death. The Church encourages us to prepare for death, asking the Mother of God to pray for us \"now and at the hour of our death\", and to entrust ourselves to St Joseph, patron of a happy death."]],
 mis:[["Christians believe only that the soul survives death.","We believe in the resurrection of the body as well: our own bodies, glorified (§§ 990, 999)."],["Reincarnation is compatible with Christian faith.","There is no reincarnation after death; we die once (§ 1013)."],["The body is unimportant, since only the soul is saved.","The body is a temple of the Spirit and will rise; it deserves respect (§ 1004)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants can explain the resurrection of the body, the Christian meaning of death, and its implications for how we treat the body.",discuss:["Why do you think the resurrection of the body meets so much opposition (Augustine)?","How does the Eucharist give us a foretaste of the resurrection (§ 1000)?","How does belief in the resurrection change how we treat our bodies and the sick?"],act:"Read 1 Corinthians 15:35–44 and look at a seed beside a plant or flower. Discuss the image Paul uses: continuity and transformation."},
 saints:[["St Augustine (Enarrationes in Psalmos 88.2.5)","On no point does the Christian faith encounter more opposition than on the resurrection of the body."],["St Francis of Assisi (Canticle of the Creatures)","Praised be you, my Lord, through Sister Death, from whom no living person can escape."]],
 more:[CQ("Who will rise on the last day?",["Only the righteous","All the dead","Only the baptised"],1,"§ 998."),CQ("Is reincarnation compatible with Christian faith?",["Yes","No; we die once","Only for saints"],1,"§ 1013.")]});
cccD("c48",{paras:[["1020","The Christian who unites his own death to that of Jesus views it as a step towards him and an entrance into everlasting life. The Church speaks the words of commendation: \"Go forth, Christian soul, from this world… May you return to your Creator.\""],["1021–1022","Death puts an end to life as the time open to accepting or rejecting divine grace. Each person receives eternal retribution in his immortal soul at the very moment of death, in a particular judgement that refers his life to Christ: either entrance into heaven, through a purification or immediately, or immediate and everlasting damnation. St John of the Cross: \"At the evening of life, we shall be judged on our love.\""],["1023–1029","Those who die in God's grace and friendship and are perfectly purified live for ever with Christ; they see God \"face to face\". This perfect life with the Holy Trinity, this communion of life and love with the Trinity, the Virgin Mary, the angels and all the blessed is called heaven: the ultimate end and fulfilment of the deepest human longings, the state of supreme, definitive happiness. Because of his transcendence, God can be seen as he is only when he opens his mystery to our contemplation: the \"beatific vision\". Scripture speaks of it in images: life, light, peace, a wedding feast, wine of the Kingdom, the Father's house, the heavenly Jerusalem, paradise. \"No eye has seen, nor ear heard… what God has prepared for those who love him.\""],["1030–1032","All who die in God's grace and friendship, but still imperfectly purified, are indeed assured of their eternal salvation; but after death they undergo purification, to achieve the holiness necessary to enter the joy of heaven. The Church calls this final purification of the elect purgatory; it is entirely different from the punishment of the damned. The Church formulated it especially at Florence and Trent, referring to Scripture (2 Macc 12:46; 1 Cor 3:15). From the beginning the Church has honoured the dead and offered prayers for them, above all the Eucharistic sacrifice, and also almsgiving, indulgences and works of penance."],["1033–1037","We cannot be united with God unless we freely choose to love him. To die in mortal sin without repenting and accepting God's merciful love means remaining separated from him for ever by our own free choice. This state of definitive self-exclusion from communion with God and the blessed is called hell. Jesus often speaks of it. Immediately after death the souls of those who die in a state of mortal sin descend into hell, where they suffer its punishments, \"eternal fire\". Its chief punishment is eternal separation from God, in whom alone we can have the life and happiness for which we were created. The affirmations about hell are a call to responsibility and to urgent conversion. God predestines no one to go to hell; for this, a wilful turning away from God (a mortal sin) is necessary, and persistence in it until the end. The Church prays that no one should be lost."]],
 mis:[["Purgatory is a second chance after death, or a kind of temporary hell.","It is the purification of those already saved, entirely different from the punishment of the damned (§§ 1030–1031)."],["God sends people to hell against their will.","Hell is definitive self-exclusion; God predestines no one there (§§ 1033, 1037)."],["Heaven will be boring: clouds and harps.","Heaven is perfect communion of life and love with the Trinity and all the blessed, beyond all we can imagine (§§ 1024–1027)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants can explain the particular judgement, heaven, purgatory and hell accurately and hopefully.",discuss:["What does it mean that we will be \"judged on love\"?","How would you explain purgatory to a non-Catholic friend?","Why does the Church preach about hell, if she prays that no one is lost?"],act:"Read 1 Corinthians 13:12 and 1 John 3:2. Each person writes a short prayer asking for a holy death, and prays the Eternal Rest for someone who has died."},
 saints:[["St John of the Cross (Dichos 64)","At the evening of life, we shall be judged on our love."],["St Catherine of Genoa (Treatise on Purgatory)","The souls in purgatory have a joy so great that only the joy of the saints in heaven can compare with it; for they are certain of seeing God."]],
 more:[CQ("What is the \"beatific vision\"?",["A vision of angels","Seeing God as he is, face to face","A dream before death"],1,"§ 1028."),CQ("What is hell's chief punishment?",["Physical fire alone","Eternal separation from God","Boredom"],1,"§ 1035.")]});
cccD("c49",{paras:[["1038–1041","The resurrection of all the dead, \"of both the just and the unjust\", will precede the Last Judgement. Then Christ will come \"in his glory, and all the angels with him\", and all nations will be gathered before him. In his presence the truth of each person's relationship with God will be laid bare. The Last Judgement will come when Christ returns in glory; only the Father knows the day and the hour. Through his Son, he will pronounce the final word on all history; we will know the ultimate meaning of the whole work of creation and salvation, and the marvellous ways by which providence led everything to its final end. The Last Judgement will reveal that God's justice triumphs over all the injustices committed by his creatures, and that God's love is stronger than death. Its message calls us to conversion while God gives us \"the acceptable time\"."],["1042–1047","At the end of time the Kingdom of God will come in its fullness. The righteous will reign for ever with Christ, glorified in body and soul, and the universe itself will be renewed. Scripture calls this \"new heavens and a new earth\". For humanity, this consummation will be the final realisation of the unity of the human race; the Church will be \"the Bride, the wife of the Lamb\", no longer wounded by sin. For the cosmos, the visible universe itself is destined to be transformed, \"set free from its bondage to decay\"."],["1048–1050","We know neither the moment of the consummation of the earth nor the way the universe will be transformed. But far from diminishing our concern to develop this earth, the expectation of a new earth should spur us on, for it is here that the body of a new human family grows, foreshadowing the new age. Earthly progress must be distinguished from the growth of Christ's Kingdom; yet such progress is of vital concern to the Kingdom insofar as it contributes to better ordering human society. All the good fruits of our nature and enterprise, we will find again, cleansed and transfigured."],["1051–1060","In Brief: every person is judged at death; heaven, purgatory and hell are explained; the Church prays that no one is lost; at the end of time the Kingdom will come in fullness, and God will be \"all in all\"."],["1061–1065","\"Amen\". The Creed, like the last book of Scripture, ends with the Hebrew word Amen. In Hebrew it comes from the same root as \"believe\", expressing solidity, trustworthiness, faithfulness. God is the \"God of Amen\", always faithful. Jesus Christ himself is the \"Amen\", the definitive Amen of the Father's love. Our Amen at the end of the Creed repeats and confirms its first words: \"I believe.\""]],
 mis:[["Hope for heaven means we can ignore the problems of this world.","Expecting a new earth should spur us to work for justice now (§ 1049)."],["The world will simply be destroyed.","The universe will be transformed and renewed, \"set free from its bondage to decay\" (§ 1047)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand the Last Judgement, the new creation, and the meaning of \"Amen\", and connect Christian hope with action in the world.",discuss:["What injustices do you long to see God put right?","How does hope in a new creation shape care for the environment and for the poor?","What are you saying when you say \"Amen\" at Communion?"],act:"Read Revelation 21:1–5. Each person writes one thing they hope God will \"make new\", and one thing they can do about it now."},
 saints:[["St Augustine (City of God XXII.30)","There we shall rest and see, we shall see and love, we shall love and praise. Behold what will be at the end without end."],["St Augustine (Sermon 272)","Be what you see, and receive what you are. To \"the Body of Christ\" you reply \"Amen\"."]],
 more:[CQ("What will the Last Judgement reveal?",["Only our sins","That God's justice triumphs over injustice and his love is stronger than death","The date of the end"],1,"§ 1040."),CQ("Who is the definitive \"Amen\"?",["The Church","Jesus Christ","Mary"],1,"§ 1065.")]});

// ---------- Catechism notes: "Go deeper" layer, Part Two (§§ 1066–1690) ----------
cccD("c50",{paras:[["1066–1068","In the Creed the Church confesses the mystery of the Trinity and God's plan; in her liturgy she celebrates it, so that the faithful may live it. Christ accomplished the work of redemption principally by his Paschal mystery; the Church celebrates this mystery in the liturgy, so that the faithful may live from it and bear witness to it. \"Through the liturgy the work of our redemption is accomplished.\""],["1069–1075","\"Liturgy\" originally meant a public work or service done on behalf of the people. In Christian tradition it means the participation of God's people in the work of God: Christ continues the work of redemption in, with and through his Church. It includes proclamation and charity as well as worship. It is the summit towards which the Church's activity is directed and the font from which all her power flows. Liturgical catechesis (mystagogy) leads from the visible signs to the invisible mystery."],["1077–1083","The Father is the source and goal of the liturgy. \"Blessed be the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, who has blessed us with every spiritual blessing.\" From the beginning to the end of time God's whole work is a blessing. In the Church's liturgy the divine blessing is fully revealed and communicated: the Father is acknowledged and adored as the source of all blessings, and the Church, united with Christ, responds with blessing, thanksgiving and the offering of her own gifts."],["1084–1090","Christ's work in the liturgy: seated at the Father's right hand and pouring out the Spirit, Christ acts now through the sacraments he instituted to communicate his grace. His Paschal mystery, which happened once in history, does not pass away: it participates in the divine eternity and is made present in the liturgy. Christ is present in the sacrifice of the Mass (in the minister and above all under the Eucharistic species), in the sacraments, in his word, and when the Church prays and sings. The earthly liturgy is a foretaste of the heavenly liturgy."],["1091–1112","The Holy Spirit and the Church in the liturgy: the Spirit prepares the Church to encounter her Lord (as in the Old Testament, which the liturgy reads); recalls the mystery of Christ (especially in the Liturgy of the Word and the anamnesis, \"remembering\"); makes it present (the epiclesis, the invocation of the Spirit, at the heart of every sacrament, especially the Eucharist); and makes the Church a communion, bearing fruit in a life lived in him."]],
 mis:[["Liturgy is just the ceremonies and rules for Mass.","It is the participation of God's people in Christ's work of redemption, the work of the whole Trinity (§§ 1069, 1077–1109)."],["Each Mass is a new sacrifice.","Christ's Paschal mystery happened once for all and is made present in the liturgy, because it participates in God's eternity (§ 1085)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants see the liturgy as the work of the Trinity: the Father blesses, Christ acts, the Spirit makes it present, and we respond.",discuss:["What changes if we think of Mass as something God does before it is something we do?","Where is Christ present at Mass? Name four ways (§ 1088).","What do the anamnesis (remembering) and epiclesis (calling down the Spirit) do?"],act:"Bring a missal or Mass booklet. Find the epiclesis and the anamnesis in Eucharistic Prayer II and read them aloud together."},
 saints:[["St Leo the Great (Sermon 74.2)","What was visible in our Redeemer has passed over into his sacraments."],["Vatican II (Sacrosanctum Concilium 10)","The liturgy is the summit towards which the activity of the Church is directed; it is also the font from which all her power flows."]],
 more:[CQ("Which prayer invokes the Holy Spirit to sanctify the offerings?",["The anamnesis","The epiclesis","The doxology"],1,"§ 1105."),CQ("Why does Christ's Paschal mystery not pass away?",["Because it is repeated","Because it participates in the divine eternity","Because it is remembered emotionally"],1,"§ 1085.")]});
cccD("c51",{paras:[["1113–1116","The whole liturgical life of the Church revolves around the sacrifice of the Eucharist and the sacraments. There are seven: Baptism, Confirmation, Eucharist, Penance, Anointing of the Sick, Holy Orders and Matrimony. They are \"of Christ\": instituted by him. The mysteries of Christ's life are the foundations of what he now dispenses in the sacraments, through the ministers of his Church. Sacraments are \"powers that come forth\" from the Body of Christ."],["1117–1121","They are \"of the Church\": by the Spirit the Church has recognised this treasure and determined its \"dispensation\", discerning over the centuries that among her liturgical celebrations there are seven sacraments properly so called. They are \"by her\" (she is the sacrament of Christ's action) and \"for her\" (they make the Church). Baptism, Confirmation and Holy Orders confer a sacramental character, an indelible spiritual seal; they are never repeated."],["1122–1126","They are \"sacraments of faith\": they presuppose faith, and by words and objects they also nourish, strengthen and express it. The Church's faith precedes the faith of the believer. Lex orandi, lex credendi: the law of prayer is the law of faith. No sacramental rite may be changed or manipulated at will by the minister or the community."],["1127–1129","They are \"sacraments of salvation\": celebrated worthily in faith, they confer the grace they signify, because in them Christ himself is at work. They act ex opere operato, \"by the very fact of the action being performed\", by the power of Christ and the Spirit, not by the righteousness of the minister or recipient. Yet their fruits also depend on the disposition of the one who receives them. For believers the sacraments of the New Covenant are necessary for salvation."],["1130–1134","They are \"sacraments of eternal life\": the Church celebrates them as she awaits the Lord's return, \"Maranatha!\". They give a pledge of the glory to come."]],
 mis:[["Sacraments are just symbols that help us remember.","They are effective signs that really confer the grace they signify (§§ 1127–1131)."],["If the priest is a sinner, the sacrament doesn't work.","Sacraments act by Christ's power (ex opere operato), not by the minister's holiness (§ 1128)."],["Sacraments work like magic, regardless of the recipient.","Their fruit also depends on the recipient's dispositions (§ 1128)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants can define a sacrament, name the seven, and explain ex opere operato and the role of faith.",discuss:["Which of the seven sacraments have you received? What did each do?","How is a sacrament different from a symbol like a flag?","Why do Baptism, Confirmation and Orders happen only once?"],act:"Sacrament sorting: give each group cards with the seven sacraments; sort them into initiation, healing and service of communion, and name the matter and words of each."},
 saints:[["St Augustine (Tractates on John 80.3)","The word is added to the element, and it becomes a sacrament."],["St Thomas Aquinas (ST III q.62 a.1)","The sacraments of the New Law cause grace as instruments of Christ's divinity."]],
 more:[CQ("Which three sacraments confer an indelible character?",["Baptism, Eucharist, Marriage","Baptism, Confirmation, Holy Orders","Penance, Anointing, Orders"],1,"§ 1121."),CQ("What does lex orandi, lex credendi mean?",["Pray as you like","The law of prayer is the law of faith","Faith comes before prayer"],1,"§ 1124.")]});
cccD("c52",{paras:[["1136–1144","Who celebrates? The liturgy is the action of the whole Christ, head and body. Those who celebrate in heaven: the heavenly powers, all creation, the servants of the Old and New Covenants, the new People of God, the martyrs, the Mother of God, the Bride of the Lamb. On earth it is the whole community, the Body of Christ united with its Head; the baptised offer themselves as spiritual sacrifices. Not all members have the same function: some are called by Holy Orders to act in the person of Christ the Head; there are also particular ministries (servers, readers, commentators, choir)."],["1145–1162","How is it celebrated? Through signs and symbols drawn from human life (washing, anointing, breaking bread, sharing a cup), from creation (light, water, fire, oil, bread, wine), and from the Old Covenant (circumcision, anointing, the Passover). Christ himself used signs to heal and teach. Words and actions belong together: the Liturgy of the Word is an integral part of every sacrament. Singing and music are integral to solemn liturgy: \"he who sings prays twice\". Holy images (icons) of Christ, Mary, the angels and saints are justified by the Incarnation; contemplating them nourishes faith."],["1163–1178","When is it celebrated? The liturgical year unfolds the whole mystery of Christ. Sunday, the Lord's Day, \"the first day\" and \"the eighth day\", is the foundation and kernel of the whole liturgical year. Easter is the \"feast of feasts\", the \"solemnity of solemnities\". In the sanctoral cycle the Church venerates Mary and the saints. The Liturgy of the Hours, the public prayer of the Church, extends the Eucharist through the day; it is the prayer of the whole People of God."],["1179–1186","Where is it celebrated? Christian worship is not restricted to any one place: Christ is the true temple, and believers are living stones. But when religious freedom allows, Christians build churches: houses of prayer where the Eucharist is celebrated and reserved. Key places: the altar, the tabernacle, the chair, the ambo (lectern), the baptistery, and the place for the sacrament of Penance."],["1200–1209","Liturgical diversity and the unity of the mystery: from the first community of Jerusalem, the Church's liturgy has taken different forms in different places and cultures, expressing the one mystery of Christ. The liturgical traditions or rites in use today are the Latin (chiefly the Roman rite) and the Eastern rites: Byzantine, Alexandrian or Coptic, Syriac, Armenian, Maronite and Chaldean. The Church holds all lawfully recognised rites to be of equal right and dignity. The criterion is unity in the apostolic faith; liturgical diversity can enrich the Church."]],
 mis:[["Only the priest celebrates Mass; the people watch.","The whole assembly, the Body united with its Head, celebrates, each according to its role (§§ 1140–1144)."],["Icons and statues are idols.","Holy images are justified by the Incarnation and point beyond themselves to Christ and the saints (§§ 1159–1162)."],["Eastern Catholics are less Catholic than Latin Catholics.","All lawfully recognised rites have equal right and dignity (§ 1203)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand who celebrates, how signs work, the rhythm of the liturgical year, and the meaning of the church building.",discuss:["Which liturgical sign (water, light, oil, bread, incense) speaks to you most?","Why is Sunday the foundation of the liturgical year?","What would you show a visitor first in your parish church, and why?"],act:"Church tour: visit the church and identify the altar, ambo, chair, tabernacle, baptistery and confessional. Explain what happens at each."},
 saints:[["St Augustine (attributed, Enarrationes in Psalmos 72.1)","He who sings prays twice."],["St John Damascene (On Holy Images I.16)","I do not worship matter; I worship the Creator of matter, who became matter for my sake."]],
 more:[CQ("Which day is the foundation of the liturgical year?",["Christmas","Sunday","Ash Wednesday"],1,"§ 1193 (In Brief) and § 1166."),CQ("Which of these is an Eastern rite?",["Roman","Byzantine","Ambrosian"],1,"§ 1203.")]});
cccD("c53",{paras:[["1210–1212","The seven sacraments touch all the stages and important moments of Christian life. Baptism, Confirmation and the Eucharist lay the foundations: they are the sacraments of Christian initiation, by which we are born anew, strengthened, and nourished with the bread of eternal life."],["1213–1216","Baptism is the basis of the whole Christian life, the gateway to life in the Spirit, and the door to the other sacraments. Through it we are freed from sin, reborn as children of God, become members of Christ, and are incorporated into the Church. It is called Baptism (\"plunging\" or \"immersing\"), the washing of regeneration, and enlightenment."],["1217–1228","Prefigurations in the Old Covenant: the waters at creation, Noah's ark, the crossing of the Red Sea, the crossing of the Jordan. All are fulfilled in Christ, who began his public life by being baptised by John, and from whose pierced side flowed blood and water, types of Baptism and the Eucharist. From Pentecost the Church has celebrated and administered Baptism: \"Repent, and be baptised.\""],["1229–1245","How it is celebrated: in the early Church adult initiation (catechumenate) culminated in Baptism, Confirmation and Eucharist at the Easter Vigil; the RCIA has restored this. The rite: the sign of the cross; proclamation of the Word; exorcism and renunciation of Satan; profession of faith; blessing of the baptismal water; the essential rite, immersion three times or pouring of water three times on the head while saying \"N., I baptise you in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit\"; anointing with chrism; the white garment; the candle lit from the Easter candle."],["1246–1255","Who can receive Baptism? Every person not yet baptised. Adults go through the catechumenate. Children are baptised because, born with a fallen nature, they too need the new birth; the Church baptised infants from early times. Faith is required: the faith of the Church, in which the child is baptised, and the role of parents and godparents, who must be firm believers able to help the newly baptised on the road of Christian life."],["1256","Who can baptise? The ordinary ministers are the bishop and priest, and in the Latin Church also the deacon. In case of necessity, anyone, even a non-baptised person, with the required intention, can baptise, using the Trinitarian formula; the intention required is to will to do what the Church does."],["1257–1261","The necessity of Baptism: the Lord himself affirms that Baptism is necessary for salvation for those to whom the Gospel has been proclaimed and who have had the possibility of asking for it. \"God has bound salvation to the sacrament of Baptism, but he himself is not bound by his sacraments.\" Those who die for the faith (baptism of blood) and catechumens who die before Baptism (baptism of desire) are saved. Every person ignorant of the Gospel who seeks the truth and does God's will can be saved; they would have desired Baptism if they had known its necessity. Children who die without Baptism are entrusted to God's mercy; the Church allows us to hope for their salvation."],["1262–1274","The grace of Baptism: forgiveness of original sin, all personal sins and all punishment due to sin; new birth as a \"new creature\", an adopted child of God, a partaker of the divine nature, a member of Christ, a temple of the Spirit; sanctifying grace, the theological virtues and the gifts of the Spirit; incorporation into the Church; a sacramental bond of unity of Christians; and an indelible spiritual mark (character) consecrating the baptised for Christian worship."]],
 mis:[["Infant Baptism is meaningless because babies can't believe.","Children are baptised in the faith of the Church, with parents and godparents pledging to raise them in it (§§ 1250–1255)."],["Unbaptised babies who die are certainly lost.","The Church entrusts them to God's mercy and allows us to hope for their salvation (§ 1261)."],["Only a priest can ever baptise.","In necessity anyone can baptise, with the right intention and the Trinitarian formula (§ 1256)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand Baptism's prefigurations, rite, effects and necessity, and can explain infant Baptism and emergency Baptism.",discuss:["Which Old Testament prefiguration of Baptism helps you most?","What does each symbol of the rite (water, oil, white garment, candle) mean?","How would you respond to parents grieving a baby who died unbaptised?"],act:"Hold a baptismal \"walk-through\": with a bowl of water, oil, a white cloth and a candle, explain each step of the rite. Teach everyone how to baptise in an emergency."},
 saints:[["St Gregory of Nazianzus (Oration 40.3–4)","Baptism is the most beautiful and magnificent of God's gifts… We call it gift, grace, anointing, enlightenment, garment of immortality, bath of rebirth, seal."],["St Ambrose (On the Sacraments II.7.20, paraphrased)","You were asked: Do you believe in God the Father almighty? You said: I believe; and you were immersed, that is, you were buried."]],
 more:[CQ("What is the essential rite of Baptism?",["Anointing with oil","Immersion in, or pouring of, water with the Trinitarian formula","Lighting a candle"],1,"§§ 1239–1240."),CQ("What are baptism of blood and baptism of desire?",["Heresies","Ways those who die for the faith, or catechumens who die before Baptism, are saved","Ancient rites no longer used"],1,"§§ 1258–1259.")]});
cccD("c54",{paras:[["1285","Baptism, Eucharist and Confirmation together constitute the sacraments of Christian initiation. By Confirmation the baptised are more perfectly bound to the Church and enriched with a special strength of the Holy Spirit, obliged to spread and defend the faith by word and deed as true witnesses of Christ."],["1286–1292","In the Old Testament the prophets announced that the Spirit would rest on the Messiah. At Jesus' baptism the Spirit descended on him; Jesus promised the Spirit, and poured him out at Pentecost. From then on the apostles imparted the gift of the Spirit to the newly baptised by the laying on of hands. Very early, an anointing with perfumed oil (chrism) was added: \"Christian\" means \"anointed\". In the West, Confirmation came to be separated in time from Baptism so that the bishop could complete it; the East kept them together, with the priest confirming using chrism consecrated by the bishop."],["1293–1301","Signs and rite: anointing with oil is a sign of abundance and joy, cleansing, healing, and radiance. The person receives the \"mark\", the seal of the Spirit. The essential rite in the Latin Church is the anointing with sacred chrism on the forehead, done by the laying on of the hand, with the words \"Be sealed with the Gift of the Holy Spirit\". The sign of peace that concludes the rite signifies and demonstrates ecclesial communion with the bishop and the faithful."],["1302–1305","Effects: the full outpouring of the Holy Spirit, as at Pentecost. It roots us more deeply in divine filiation (\"Abba! Father!\"); unites us more firmly to Christ; increases the gifts of the Spirit in us; renders our bond with the Church more perfect; and gives special strength to spread and defend the faith and never to be ashamed of the Cross. Like Baptism, it imprints a character, and is given only once."],["1306–1311","Every baptised person not yet confirmed can and should receive it. In the Latin Church the \"age of discretion\" is the reference point, but in danger of death children should be confirmed even if they have not reached it. Preparation should lead to a more intimate union with Christ and a livelier familiarity with the Spirit. The candidate should be in a state of grace (going to Penance first) and should choose a sponsor, ideally a baptismal godparent."],["1312–1314","The original minister is the bishop, which expresses the link with the apostles and the Church's unity. In the East the priest confirms, using chrism consecrated by the bishop. A Latin bishop may, for grave reasons, grant priests the faculty to confirm; in danger of death any priest should confirm."]],
 mis:[["Confirmation is \"graduation\" from the Church.","It is a beginning: the strength of the Spirit to witness for life (§ 1303)."],["Confirmation is a sacrament where I confirm my faith.","It is first God's action, sealing us with the Spirit; our response follows (§§ 1293–1305)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand Confirmation's roots, rite and effects, and prepare to receive (or renew) it as a commission to witness.",discuss:["What does it mean to be \"sealed\" with the Spirit?","Which of the seven gifts of the Spirit do you most need?","Who could be a good sponsor, and why?"],act:"Each candidate researches a saint for a Confirmation name and shares one way that saint witnessed to Christ."},
 saints:[["St Ambrose (On the Mysteries 7.42)","Recall that you have received the spiritual seal, the spirit of wisdom and understanding, the spirit of counsel and strength… Guard what you have received."],["St Cyril of Jerusalem (Mystagogical Catecheses 3.1)","Having been made partakers of Christ, you are rightly called \"christs\", anointed ones."]],
 more:[CQ("What words accompany the anointing in the Latin rite?",["I baptise you…","Be sealed with the Gift of the Holy Spirit","Receive the Body of Christ"],1,"§ 1300."),CQ("What should a candidate do before Confirmation?",["Nothing special","Be in a state of grace, receiving Penance first","Fast for a week"],1,"§ 1310.")]});
cccD("c55",{paras:[["1322–1323","The Holy Eucharist completes Christian initiation. At the Last Supper, on the night he was betrayed, our Saviour instituted the Eucharistic sacrifice of his Body and Blood, to perpetuate the sacrifice of the Cross throughout the ages until he comes, and to entrust to his Bride, the Church, a memorial of his death and Resurrection: a sacrament of love, a sign of unity, a bond of charity, a Paschal banquet."],["1324–1327","The Eucharist is \"the source and summit of the Christian life\". The other sacraments, ministries and works of the apostolate are bound up with it and oriented to it. In the Eucharist is contained the whole spiritual good of the Church, Christ himself, our Pasch. It is the efficacious sign and sublime cause of communion in the divine life and of the unity of the People of God. \"Our way of thinking is attuned to the Eucharist, and the Eucharist in turn confirms our way of thinking\" (St Irenaeus)."],["1328–1332","Names: Eucharist (thanksgiving); the Lord's Supper; the Breaking of Bread; the Eucharistic assembly (synaxis); the memorial of the Lord's Passion and Resurrection; the Holy Sacrifice; the Holy and Divine Liturgy; Holy Communion; Holy Mass (from the dismissal, missio, sending the faithful out)."],["1333–1336","Signs of bread and wine: at the heart of the celebration are bread and wine that become Christ's Body and Blood. Prefigured by Melchizedek's offering, the bread and wine of the Passover, and the manna; fulfilled in the miracles of the loaves and the wine at Cana. The first announcement of the Eucharist (John 6) divided the disciples: \"This is a hard saying; who can listen to it?\" The Eucharist and the Cross are stumbling blocks."],["1337–1344","The institution: Jesus chose the Passover to fulfil what he had announced at Capernaum. \"This is my body which is given for you. Do this in memory of me… This cup is the new covenant in my blood.\" By celebrating the Last Supper with his apostles during the Passover meal, Jesus gave the Jewish Passover its definitive meaning. The command \"Do this\" is directed at the liturgical celebration of the memorial. From the beginning the Church has been faithful: \"They devoted themselves to the apostles' teaching and fellowship, to the breaking of bread and the prayers.\" Christians gathered especially on \"the first day of the week\", Sunday."]],
 mis:[["The Eucharist is one sacrament among others, no more important.","It is the source and summit: all the other sacraments are ordered to it (§ 1324)."],["\"Do this in memory of me\" means just remember Jesus.","It commands the liturgical celebration of the memorial that makes his sacrifice present (§ 1341)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand why the Eucharist is \"source and summit\", its names, prefigurations and institution.",discuss:["Which name for the Eucharist do you find most meaningful?","Why do you think some disciples left Jesus after John 6 (§ 1336)?","How does the Passover help us understand the Mass?"],act:"Read Exodus 12:1–14 and Luke 22:14–20 side by side. List the connections between the Passover and the Last Supper."},
 saints:[["St Irenaeus (Against Heresies IV.18.5)","Our way of thinking is attuned to the Eucharist, and the Eucharist in turn confirms our way of thinking."],["St Thomas Aquinas (Office of Corpus Christi, reading)","O precious and wonderful banquet, that brings us salvation and contains all sweetness!"]],
 more:[CQ("Where did Jesus first announce the Eucharist, dividing his disciples?",["At Cana","In the Bread of Life discourse (John 6)","On the Mount of Olives"],1,"§ 1336."),CQ("Where does the word \"Mass\" come from?",["The Hebrew for bread","The Latin dismissal (missio), sending out the faithful","A Greek word for meal"],1,"§ 1332.")]});
cccD("c56",{paras:[["1345–1355","The Mass of all ages: as early as the 2nd century, St Justin Martyr describes the basic structure still followed: readings from the apostles and prophets, the homily, prayers, the bringing of bread and wine, the Eucharistic prayer with the people's Amen, and Communion, with a collection for the needy. Two great parts form one act of worship: the Liturgy of the Word (readings, homily, Creed, prayers of the faithful) and the Liturgy of the Eucharist (presentation of gifts, the Eucharistic prayer with preface, epiclesis, institution narrative, anamnesis, intercessions, and Communion)."],["1356–1361","The sacramental sacrifice is first thanksgiving and praise to the Father, for creation, redemption and sanctification. It is the sacrifice of praise by which the Church sings the glory of God in the name of all creation."],["1362–1372","It is the memorial of Christ's sacrifice. In Scripture, \"memorial\" is not merely recalling past events but proclaiming God's mighty works, which become present and real. When the Church celebrates the Eucharist, Christ's Passover is made present: the sacrifice he offered once for all on the Cross remains ever present. \"The victim is one and the same: the same now offers through the ministry of priests, who then offered himself on the cross; only the manner of offering is different\" (Trent). Because it is the memorial of Christ's Passover, it is also a sacrifice, the sacrifice of the Church, which offers herself with him; it is offered for the living and the dead."],["1373–1381","The presence of Christ by the power of his word and the Holy Spirit: Christ is present in many ways (in his word, in the Church's prayer, in the poor, in the sacraments, in the minister), but \"most especially in the Eucharistic species\". In the Eucharist \"the body and blood, together with the soul and divinity, of our Lord Jesus Christ and, therefore, the whole Christ is truly, really, and substantially contained\". This presence is \"real\" in the fullest sense: a substantial presence. By consecration the whole substance of bread becomes the substance of Christ's Body, and the whole substance of wine the substance of his Blood: transubstantiation. Christ is present whole and entire in each species and each of its parts, as long as the species subsist. Adoration of the Blessed Sacrament inside and outside Mass expresses this faith."],["1382–1401","The Paschal banquet: the Mass is at once sacrifice and sacred banquet of communion. Christ invites us to receive him: \"Unless you eat the flesh of the Son of man… you have no life in you.\" To respond we must prepare: one who is conscious of grave sin must receive Penance before Communion. The Eucharistic fast (one hour) and bodily demeanour express reverence. The Church obliges the faithful to receive Communion at least once a year, if possible during Easter, and warmly recommends frequent, even daily, reception. Fruits of Communion: it augments our union with Christ; separates us from sin, wiping away venial sins and preserving from future mortal sins; makes the Church (\"because there is one bread, we who are many are one body\"); and commits us to the poor. Non-Catholic Christians: with the Orthodox, communion in certain circumstances is possible; for Protestant communities lacking valid Orders, Communion is not generally possible."],["1402–1405","The pledge of glory to come: the Eucharist anticipates the heavenly banquet. \"O sacred banquet, in which Christ is received… and a pledge of future glory is given to us.\" St Ignatius of Antioch called it \"the medicine of immortality, the antidote for death\"."]],
 mis:[["Catholics believe Christ is re-crucified at each Mass.","The one sacrifice of the Cross is made present, not repeated; only the manner of offering differs (§§ 1366–1367)."],["The Real Presence is symbolic or spiritual only.","Christ is truly, really and substantially present: transubstantiation (§§ 1374–1376)."],["Christ is only present during Mass, and receiving under one kind is half a Communion.","He remains present as long as the species subsist, and is whole and entire in each species (§ 1377)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand the structure of the Mass, the Eucharist as memorial-sacrifice, the Real Presence and transubstantiation, and how to receive Communion worthily.",discuss:["What surprises you about Justin Martyr's 2nd-century description of Mass (§ 1345)?","How would you explain transubstantiation simply and accurately?","What does Communion ask of us towards the poor (§ 1397)?"],act:"Hold a \"teaching Mass\" walk-through, or go through the parts of the Mass with a booklet. Then spend 15 minutes in silent adoration before the Blessed Sacrament."},
 saints:[["St Justin Martyr (First Apology 67, c. 155)","On the day called Sunday all who live in cities or in the country gather together… the memoirs of the apostles or the writings of the prophets are read… bread and wine and water are brought…"],["St Ignatius of Antioch (Letter to the Ephesians 20)","Breaking the one bread that provides the medicine of immortality, the antidote for death."],["St John Vianney (attributed)","If we really understood the Mass, we would die of joy."]],
 more:[CQ("Who described the structure of the Mass in the 2nd century?",["St Augustine","St Justin Martyr","St Thomas Aquinas"],1,"§ 1345."),CQ("How long does Christ's Eucharistic presence last?",["Only during Mass","As long as the Eucharistic species subsist","Only at the consecration"],1,"§ 1377."),CQ("What must someone conscious of grave sin do before Communion?",["Nothing","Receive the sacrament of Penance","Make a donation"],1,"§ 1385.")]});
cccD("c57",{paras:[["1420–1421","Through the sacraments of initiation we receive new life in Christ, but we carry it \"in earthen vessels\". This new life can be weakened and even lost by sin. The Lord Jesus, physician of our souls and bodies, willed that his Church continue his work of healing: this is the purpose of the two sacraments of healing, Penance and the Anointing of the Sick."],["1422–1426","It is called the sacrament of conversion (it makes sacramentally present Jesus' call to conversion), of Penance (a process of conversion, repentance and satisfaction), of confession (the disclosure of sins is an essential element, and also a \"confession\" of God's holiness and mercy), of forgiveness (God grants pardon and peace through the priest's absolution), and of Reconciliation (it imparts God's reconciling love). Why after Baptism? Baptismal grace does not abolish the frailty of human nature or the inclination to sin."],["1427–1433","The conversion of the baptised: Christ's call to conversion continues in Christians' lives: an uninterrupted task for the whole Church, which is \"at once holy and always in need of purification\". It is the movement of a \"contrite heart\" drawn by grace (Peter's conversion after his denial). Interior penance is a radical reorientation of our whole life, a return to God with all our heart, an end of sin, a turning away from evil, with repugnance towards the evil actions we have committed."],["1434–1439","Many forms of penance: Scripture and the Fathers insist above all on three: fasting, prayer and almsgiving, expressing conversion in relation to oneself, to God and to others. Also: reconciliation with one's neighbour, tears of repentance, concern for the salvation of others, the intercession of the saints, the practice of charity, revision of life, examination of conscience, spiritual direction, acceptance of suffering, enduring persecution for righteousness' sake. The seasons and days of penance (Lent, each Friday) are especially intense moments. The process of conversion is described in the parable of the prodigal son."],["1440–1449","The sacrament: sin is first an offence against God and a rupture of communion with him, and damages communion with the Church. Only God forgives sin; Jesus gave this power to the apostles: \"Receive the Holy Spirit; whose sins you forgive are forgiven.\" Reconciliation with the Church is inseparable from reconciliation with God. Over the centuries the form changed: in the early Church, public penance for grave sins, sometimes once in a lifetime; from the 7th century, Irish missionaries spread private penance, opening the way to regular celebration. Through the changes the same fundamental structure remains: the acts of the penitent and the action of God through the bishop or priest."],["1450–1460","The acts of the penitent: contrition, confession and satisfaction. Contrition is \"sorrow of the soul and detestation for the sin committed, together with the resolution not to sin again\". Perfect contrition arises from love of God above all; it remits venial sins, and also mortal sins if it includes the firm resolution to confess as soon as possible. Imperfect contrition (attrition) arises from the ugliness of sin or fear of punishment; it is enough for the sacrament. Preparation by an examination of conscience in the light of God's word. Confession of sins: all mortal sins of which one is conscious after diligent examination must be confessed, even the most secret. Confession of venial sins (everyday faults) is strongly recommended. Satisfaction (penance): absolution takes away sin but does not remedy all the disorders sin has caused; the penitent must do something more to make amends, \"do penance\", which helps configure us to Christ."],["1461–1467","The minister: bishops and priests, by virtue of Holy Orders, have the power to forgive sins, exercising it with the faculty granted by the Church. Certain particularly grave sins incur excommunication; absolution from them is reserved, but any priest can absolve in danger of death. The confessor is a sign and instrument of God's merciful love, not master of God's forgiveness; he must have knowledge, prudence, respect and discretion. Every priest who hears confessions is bound under very severe penalties to keep absolute secrecy: the \"sacramental seal\", which admits of no exceptions."],["1468–1470","Effects: reconciliation with God, by which the penitent recovers sanctifying grace; reconciliation with the Church; remission of the eternal punishment incurred by mortal sins; remission, at least in part, of temporal punishments; peace and serenity of conscience, and spiritual consolation; and an increase of spiritual strength for the Christian battle. In this sacrament the sinner, placing himself before God's merciful judgement, anticipates in a certain way the judgement at the end of his earthly life."]],
 mis:[["Why confess to a priest? I can confess directly to God.","Christ gave the apostles the power to forgive; sin also wounds the Church, and the sacrament reconciles us with both (§§ 1441–1445)."],["Imperfect contrition (fear of hell) isn't good enough.","Attrition is enough for forgiveness in the sacrament (§ 1453)."],["A priest could reveal my sins in some circumstances.","The sacramental seal admits of no exceptions (§ 1467)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand conversion, the history and structure of Penance, the acts of the penitent, the seal and the effects, and feel confident about going to Confession.",discuss:["Why does Jesus' call to conversion apply to people who are already Christian?","What is the difference between perfect and imperfect contrition?","What is the most common thing that keeps people away from Confession, and how would you answer it?"],act:"Read Luke 15:11–32 (the prodigal son) and map it onto the acts of the penitent: coming to himself (examination), returning and confessing, the father's embrace (absolution), the feast (reconciliation). Then walk through how to make a confession."},
 saints:[["St Ambrose (Letter 41.12)","There are water and tears: the water of Baptism and the tears of repentance."],["St John Vianney (attributed)","It is not the sinner who returns to God to beg his pardon, but God himself who runs after the sinner and makes him return to him."]],
 more:[CQ("What are the three acts of the penitent?",["Prayer, fasting, almsgiving","Contrition, confession, satisfaction","Faith, hope, love"],1,"§§ 1450–1460."),CQ("What does the sacramental seal require?",["Secrecy unless the law demands otherwise","Absolute secrecy, without exception","Secrecy for ten years"],1,"§ 1467."),CQ("Who spread the practice of private, repeatable confession from the 7th century?",["Roman popes","Irish missionaries","The Council of Trent"],1,"§ 1447.")]});
cccD("c58",{paras:[["1471–1473","An indulgence is \"a remission before God of the temporal punishment due to sins whose guilt has already been forgiven, which the faithful Christian who is duly disposed gains under certain prescribed conditions through the action of the Church\". Sin has a double consequence: grave sin deprives us of communion with God (eternal punishment); every sin, even venial, entails an unhealthy attachment to creatures, which must be purified here or after death (temporal punishment). These are not a vengeance inflicted from outside, but follow from the very nature of sin. Forgiveness of sin and restoration of communion entail remission of eternal punishment, but temporal punishment remains. The Christian should strive to accept this as a grace, through patient suffering, prayer and works of mercy, \"putting off the old man\"."],["1474–1477","In the communion of saints: the Christian is not alone. The life of each of God's children is joined in Christ with the lives of all. The \"treasury of the Church\" is the infinite value of Christ's merits before God, together with the prayers and good works of Mary and all the saints, who attained their own salvation and cooperated in the salvation of their brothers in the unity of the Mystical Body."],["1478–1479","Through indulgences the Church, by the power of binding and loosing granted by Christ, intervenes for individual Christians and opens to them the treasury of merits. She does this not only to aid them, but to spur them to works of devotion, penance and charity. Since the faithful departed being purified are also members of the same communion of saints, we can help them by obtaining indulgences for them."],["1480–1484","The celebration of Penance: like all sacraments it is a liturgical action, with a greeting and blessing, the reading of God's word, an exhortation to repentance, confession, the imposing and acceptance of a penance, the priest's absolution, a prayer of thanksgiving and dismissal. The absolution formula: \"God, the Father of mercies… I absolve you from your sins in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit.\" Individual, integral confession and absolution remain the only ordinary way to be reconciled with God and the Church; general absolution is permitted only in cases of grave necessity, with the obligation to confess grave sins individually afterwards."]],
 mis:[["Indulgences are a way to buy forgiveness of sins.","They remit temporal punishment for sins already forgiven, and cannot be bought; selling them was an abuse the Church condemned (§ 1471)."],["Temporal punishment means God is vindictive.","It follows from the nature of sin, as purification from unhealthy attachments (§ 1472)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand temporal punishment, the treasury of the Church, indulgences correctly understood, and the rite of Confession.",discuss:["Why might forgiveness still leave something to be healed? (Think of a broken window that is forgiven.)","How do indulgences show the communion of saints?","Why was selling indulgences in the 16th century an abuse?"],act:"Look at the indulgences shown on the site's Mass page for the coming weeks. Choose one to gain for someone who has died, and note the usual conditions (Confession, Communion, prayer for the Pope, freedom from attachment to sin)."},
 saints:[["Pope St Paul VI (Indulgentiarum Doctrina 8, 1967, paraphrased)","The aim of indulgences is not only to help the faithful expiate the punishment of sin, but also to urge them to perform works of devotion, penance and charity."],["Supplement to the Summa, compiled from St Thomas's Sentences commentary (q.25 a.1)","Indulgences have real value because of the unity of the Mystical Body, in which many have done more works of satisfaction than their own debts required."]],
 more:[CQ("Does an indulgence forgive mortal sins?",["Yes","No; it remits temporal punishment for sins already forgiven","Only on feast days"],1,"§ 1471."),CQ("What is the only ordinary way to be reconciled with God and the Church for grave sins?",["General absolution","Individual, integral confession and absolution","A good intention"],1,"§ 1484.")]});
cccD("c59",{paras:[["1499","\"By the sacred anointing of the sick and the prayer of the priests the whole Church commends those who are ill to the suffering and glorified Lord, that he may raise them up and save them.\""],["1500–1505","Illness and suffering have always been among the gravest problems in human life. Illness can lead to anguish, self-absorption, sometimes even despair and revolt against God; it can also make a person more mature, helping him discern what is not essential and turn towards God. In the Old Testament, illness is mysteriously linked to sin and evil, and faithfulness to God restores life. Christ, the physician, shows compassion towards the sick; his healings are signs of the coming of the Kingdom. By his Passion and death on the Cross he gave a new meaning to suffering: it can configure us to him and unite us with his redemptive Passion."],["1506–1513","\"Heal the sick!\" The Church has received this charge and strives to carry it out by caring for the sick and by intercessory prayer. James testifies to a rite for the sick: \"Is any among you sick? Let him call for the elders of the church, and let them pray over him, anointing him with oil in the name of the Lord.\" Tradition recognises this as one of the seven sacraments. Over time it came to be conferred more and more exclusively on the dying (\"Extreme Unction\"); the Church has restored its broader meaning."],["1514–1516","It is not a sacrament for those only who are at the point of death. The proper time is as soon as anyone begins to be in danger of death from sickness or old age. If a sick person recovers and then falls ill again, or the illness worsens, the sacrament can be received again. It is fitting before a serious operation, and for the elderly whose frailty becomes more pronounced. Only priests (bishops and presbyters) are ministers. Pastors should instruct the faithful on its benefits, and the faithful should encourage the sick to call for a priest."],["1517–1519","It is a liturgical and communal celebration, in the family, hospital or church, for one or several sick persons; fittingly within the Eucharist. If circumstances suggest, it can be preceded by Penance and followed by Communion. The essential rite: the priest lays hands on the sick, prays over them in the faith of the Church, and anoints the forehead and hands with oil blessed, if possible, by the bishop."],["1520–1523","Effects: a particular gift of the Spirit: strengthening, peace and courage to overcome the difficulties of serious illness or old age; renewal of trust in God against temptations to discouragement and anguish in the face of death; healing of the soul, and of the body if God wills; forgiveness of sins if the person could not obtain it through Penance. Union with the Passion of Christ: the sick person receives strength to unite himself more closely to Christ's Passion, so that suffering acquires new meaning. An ecclesial grace: the sick contribute to the good of the People of God. A preparation for the final journey."],["1524–1525","Viaticum: to those about to leave this life, the Church offers, besides the Anointing, the Eucharist as viaticum, \"food for the journey\": the seed of eternal life and the power of resurrection. Thus, just as Baptism, Confirmation and Eucharist form a unity, Penance, Anointing and Eucharist (as viaticum) form, at the end of life, \"the sacraments that prepare for our heavenly homeland\"."]],
 mis:[["Anointing is the \"last rites\", only for the dying.","It is for anyone in danger of death from illness or old age, can be repeated, and is fitting before serious surgery (§§ 1514–1515)."],["If someone is anointed, it means the family has given up hope.","It strengthens, brings peace and sometimes bodily healing; it is a sacrament of hope (§§ 1520–1523)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand the Christian meaning of illness, the Anointing of the Sick and Viaticum, and know when to call a priest.",discuss:["How can suffering bring someone closer to God, or push them away?","When should you ask for the Anointing for a family member?","What does \"food for the journey\" mean?"],act:"Visit (or write cards to) the sick or elderly of the parish. Discuss beforehand how to talk with a sick person about the sacrament."},
 saints:[["St Camillus de Lellis (to his brothers)","Put more heart into your hands."],["St John Paul II (Salvifici Doloris 19, 1984)","In the Cross of Christ not only is the Redemption accomplished through suffering, but also human suffering itself has been redeemed."]],
 more:[CQ("Who are the ministers of the Anointing of the Sick?",["Any baptised person","Priests and bishops only","Deacons"],1,"§ 1516."),CQ("Can the Anointing be received more than once?",["No","Yes, if the illness worsens or a new illness arises","Only twice"],1,"§ 1515.")]});
cccD("c60",{paras:[["1533–1538","Baptism, Confirmation and the Eucharist are the sacraments of initiation. Two further sacraments, Holy Orders and Matrimony, are directed towards the salvation of others; they confer a particular mission in the Church and serve to build up the People of God. Holy Orders is the sacrament through which the mission entrusted by Christ to his apostles continues to be exercised in the Church until the end of time: the sacrament of apostolic ministry. It includes three degrees: episcopate, presbyterate and diaconate. \"Order\" in Roman antiquity meant an established civil body; ordination means integration into an order."],["1539–1553","In the Old Covenant the chosen people was constituted a \"kingdom of priests\", and the tribe of Levi was set apart for liturgical service. These prefigure the priesthood of the New Covenant. Everything the priesthood of the Old Covenant prefigured finds its fulfilment in Christ Jesus, the \"one mediator between God and men\". There are two participations in Christ's one priesthood: the common priesthood of all the faithful (through Baptism and Confirmation), and the ministerial or hierarchical priesthood of bishops and priests. They differ essentially, not only in degree; the ministerial priesthood is at the service of the common priesthood. In the ordained minister it is Christ himself who is present to his Church as Head, acting in persona Christi Capitis. This does not mean the minister is preserved from all weakness, sin or error, but Christ's action is guaranteed in the sacraments."],["1554–1571","The three degrees: Episcopal ordination confers the fullness of the sacrament: the high priesthood, the summit of the sacred ministry. Bishops are successors of the apostles, in a college with the Pope; each bishop has, as vicar of Christ, the pastoral office of the particular Church entrusted to him. Priests are co-workers of the bishops, consecrated to preach the Gospel, shepherd the faithful and celebrate divine worship; they exercise their ministry above all in the Eucharist, in a presbyterium around their bishop. Deacons are ordained \"not unto the priesthood, but unto the ministry\": to assist the bishop and priests, especially in liturgy, preaching and charity. Since Vatican II the Latin Church has restored the permanent diaconate, which married men can receive."],["1572–1580","The essential rite of the sacrament of Holy Orders for all three degrees consists in the bishop's imposition of hands on the head of the ordinand and the specific consecratory prayer. Only validly ordained bishops, in apostolic succession, can validly confer the three degrees. Only a baptised man validly receives sacred ordination: the Lord Jesus chose men to form the college of the Twelve, and the apostles did the same; the Church recognises herself bound by this choice. No one has a right to receive the sacrament; it is a call from God. In the Latin Church, priests are normally chosen from among men of faith who live a celibate life and intend to remain celibate \"for the sake of the kingdom of heaven\"; in the Eastern Churches married men can be ordained priests and deacons, but bishops are chosen from celibates."],["1581–1589","Effects: the sacrament configures the recipient to Christ by a special grace of the Holy Spirit, so that he may serve as Christ's instrument. It confers an indelible spiritual character and cannot be repeated or conferred temporarily. The grace of the Spirit proper to it is configuration to Christ as Priest, Teacher and Pastor. St John Vianney: \"The priest continues the work of redemption on earth… If we really understood the priest on earth, we would die not of fright but of love.\""]],
 mis:[["The priest has more dignity than the laity.","All have equal dignity; the ministerial priesthood is at the service of the common priesthood (§§ 872, 1547)."],["If a priest is sinful, his Masses and absolutions are invalid.","Christ's action is guaranteed in the sacraments even if the minister is unworthy (§ 1550)."],["Deacons are just priests-in-training.","The permanent diaconate is a distinct degree of Orders, open to married men (§ 1571)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand the one priesthood of Christ shared in two ways, the three degrees of Orders, and the meaning of in persona Christi.",discuss:["What is the difference between the common priesthood and the ministerial priesthood?","What do bishops, priests and deacons each do in your diocese?","How can we support our priests and encourage vocations?"],act:"Invite a priest or deacon to speak briefly about his vocation and his day, or write thank-you cards to your clergy."},
 saints:[["St John Vianney (Curé of Ars)","The priesthood is the love of the heart of Jesus."],["St Ignatius of Antioch (Letter to the Smyrnaeans 8)","Let no one do anything concerning the Church without the bishop."]],
 more:[CQ("What is the essential rite of Holy Orders?",["Anointing with chrism","The bishop's laying on of hands with the consecratory prayer","Promising obedience"],1,"§ 1573."),CQ("Who can receive the permanent diaconate in the Latin Church?",["Only celibate men","Married and unmarried men","Women and men"],1,"§ 1571.")]});
cccD("c61",{paras:[["1601","\"The matrimonial covenant, by which a man and a woman establish between themselves a partnership of the whole of life, is by its nature ordered toward the good of the spouses and the procreation and education of offspring; this covenant between baptised persons has been raised by Christ the Lord to the dignity of a sacrament\" (Canon Law)."],["1602–1620","Marriage in God's plan: Scripture begins with the creation of man and woman in God's image and ends with \"the wedding feast of the Lamb\". The vocation to marriage is written in the very nature of man and woman as they came from the Creator. Marriage is not a purely human institution. Every person experiences evil, and marriage too is threatened by discord, domination, infidelity and jealousy: not from its nature but from sin. After the fall, marriage helps to overcome self-absorption. The Law, through the prophets, deepened understanding of its unity and indissolubility. Jesus begins his public life at the wedding at Cana; he teaches the original meaning of marriage: \"What God has joined together, let no one put asunder.\" Christian marriage becomes an efficacious sign of the covenant of Christ and the Church. Virginity for the sake of the Kingdom is an unfolding of baptismal grace; both esteem of virginity and Christian marriage are inseparable and reinforce each other."],["1621–1632","In the Latin rite marriage normally takes place during Mass. The spouses are the ministers of Christ's grace, conferring the sacrament on each other by expressing their consent before the Church. Consent is the indispensable element that \"makes the marriage\". It must be free: without coercion, not impeded by any natural or ecclesiastical law. If freedom is lacking, the marriage is invalid. Consent must be expressed according to the Church's form (normally before a priest or deacon and two witnesses). Preparation for marriage is of prime importance."],["1633–1637","Mixed marriages (Catholic and baptised non-Catholic) need particular attention: they require permission from Church authority; the couple must not ignore the difficulties, and the Catholic party has the duty to preserve their own faith and to do all in their power to have the children baptised and raised Catholic. Marriage with a non-baptised person (disparity of cult) requires a dispensation for validity. The Church can, for grave reasons, declare a marriage null: that it never existed (an annulment)."],["1638–1642","Effects: the marriage bond, established by God himself, so that a valid, consummated marriage between baptised persons can never be dissolved. The grace of the sacrament perfects the couple's love, strengthens their indissoluble unity, and helps them attain holiness in married life and in welcoming and educating children. Christ is the source of this grace: he dwells with them, gives them strength to take up their crosses, forgive one another, and bear one another's burdens."],["1643–1654","The goods and requirements of conjugal love: unity and indissolubility (polygamy is contrary to it; the spouses give themselves definitively and totally); fidelity (founded on God's fidelity to his covenant and Christ to his Church); and openness to fertility (children are the supreme gift of marriage and contribute greatly to the good of the parents). Couples to whom God has not granted children can have a conjugal life full of meaning, radiating fruitfulness of charity, hospitality and sacrifice. The divorced and civilly remarried remain members of the Church; they cannot receive Communion while the situation persists, but are to be accompanied, invited to Mass, prayer and works of charity."],["1655–1658","The domestic church: Christ chose to be born and grow up in the family of Joseph and Mary. The Church is God's family; from the beginning, whole households were converted. Today the family is a \"domestic church\", a community of grace and prayer, a school of human virtue and Christian charity: the first school of the Christian life. Single people too, especially those who have not chosen their state, must be welcomed into the \"great family\" of the Church."]],
 mis:[["An annulment is a Catholic divorce.","An annulment declares that a valid marriage never existed; divorce claims to dissolve one that did (§§ 1629, 2382–2385)."],["The priest marries the couple.","In the Latin Church the spouses are the ministers; the priest or deacon witnesses for the Church (§ 1623)."],["Couples without children have a lesser marriage.","Their marriage can be full of meaning and radiate fruitfulness in charity and service (§ 1654)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand marriage as God's plan, a covenant and a sacrament, with its essential properties, and the family as domestic church.",discuss:["Why do you think Jesus began his public ministry at a wedding?","What makes consent valid (§§ 1625–1628)?","What could make your family (now or in future) more of a \"domestic church\"?"],act:"Interview a couple married for many years (in person or by video) about what has kept their marriage strong, then discuss how their answers relate to unity, fidelity and fruitfulness."},
 saints:[["Tertullian (To His Wife II.8)","How can I ever express the happiness of the marriage joined by the Church, strengthened by an offering, sealed by a blessing, announced by angels and ratified by the Father?"],["St John Chrysostom (Homily on Ephesians 20)","I have taken you in my arms, and I love you, and I prefer you to my life itself… I place you before everything (advice to a young husband)."]],
 more:[CQ("What \"makes\" the marriage, according to § 1626?",["The wedding party","The consent of the spouses","The blessing of rings"],1,"§ 1626."),CQ("What are the essential properties of marriage?",["Romance and happiness","Unity and indissolubility","Wealth and children"],1,"§§ 1644–1645.")]});
cccD("c62",{paras:[["1667–1670","Holy Mother Church has instituted sacramentals: sacred signs which bear a resemblance to the sacraments; they signify effects, particularly of a spiritual nature, obtained through the Church's intercession. By them people are disposed to receive the chief effect of the sacraments, and various occasions in life are rendered holy. They derive from the baptismal priesthood: every baptised person is called to be a \"blessing\" and to bless. They do not confer the grace of the Spirit in the way the sacraments do, but by the Church's prayer they prepare us to receive grace and dispose us to cooperate with it."],["1671–1673","Among sacramentals, blessings (of persons, meals, objects and places) come first. Every blessing praises God and prays for his gifts. Some blessings have a lasting importance because they consecrate persons to God (abbots, consecrated virgins, readers, acolytes, catechists) or reserve objects and places for liturgical use (churches, altars, holy oils, vessels and vestments, bells). Exorcism: when the Church asks publicly and authoritatively in Jesus' name that a person or object be protected against the power of the Evil One. In a simple form it is performed at Baptism; a solemn (major) exorcism can be performed only by a priest with the bishop's permission, after ascertaining that it is truly a question of the presence of the Evil One and not of an illness, especially psychological illness, which is the domain of medical science."],["1674–1676","Popular piety: besides the liturgy, Christian life is nourished by forms of piety rooted in different cultures: the veneration of relics, visits to sanctuaries, pilgrimages, processions, the Stations of the Cross, religious dances, the Rosary, medals. These extend the Church's liturgical life but do not replace it; they should harmonise with the liturgical seasons and lead to it. Pastoral discernment is needed to support and purify them."],["1680–1683","Christian funerals: all the sacraments, especially those of initiation, have as their goal the last Passover of the child of God, which leads him into the life of the Kingdom. For the Christian, the day of death inaugurates, at the end of sacramental life, the fulfilment of his new birth begun at Baptism. The Church, who as mother has borne the Christian sacramentally in her womb during his earthly pilgrimage, accompanies him at his journey's end, to surrender him \"into the Father's hands\"."],["1684–1690","The funeral liturgy expresses communion with the deceased, the community's participation, and the proclamation of eternal life. It has four principal elements: the greeting of the community (a word of consolation); the Liturgy of the Word; the Eucharistic sacrifice, in which the Church expresses her efficacious communion with the departed; and the farewell (a dieu), commending the deceased to God."]],
 mis:[["Holy water, medals and rosaries are lucky charms.","Sacramentals work through the Church's prayer and our faith, preparing us for grace; treating them as magic is superstition (§§ 1670, 2111)."],["Anything strange is a sign of the devil, so call an exorcist.","The Church first ascertains that it is not an illness, especially psychological illness, which belongs to medicine (§ 1673)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand sacramentals, blessings and popular piety rightly, and the meaning of a Christian funeral.",discuss:["Which sacramentals do you use (holy water, medals, a crucifix)? What do they mean to you?","Why does the Catechism insist that psychological illness be ruled out before an exorcism?","How does a Catholic funeral differ from a \"celebration of life\"?"],act:"Bless each other with holy water, recalling your Baptism, and learn the blessing before meals. Optionally, bring rosaries or crosses for the priest to bless."},
 saints:[["St Teresa of Avila (Life 31.4)","From long experience I have learned that there is nothing like holy water to put devils to flight."],["St Monica (to Augustine, Confessions IX.11)","Lay this body anywhere; let not the care of it trouble you. This only I ask of you, that you remember me at the altar of the Lord, wherever you may be."]],
 more:[CQ("Who instituted the sacramentals?",["Christ directly","The Church","The apostles' families"],1,"§ 1667."),CQ("What must be ruled out before a major exorcism?",["Nothing","Illness, especially psychological illness","Lack of faith"],1,"§ 1673.")],psyNote:"§ 1673 is an important point of contact with psychology: distress that looks \"spiritual\" may be psychosis, dissociation, severe anxiety or depression, and needs proper clinical assessment first."});

// ---------- Catechism notes: "Go deeper" layer, Part Three, Section One (§§ 1691–2051) ----------
cccD("c63",{paras:[["1691–1698","\"Christian, recognise your dignity\" (St Leo the Great). Christians, made partakers of the divine nature, are called to lead a life \"worthy of the gospel of Christ\". The way of Christ \"leads to life\"; a contrary way \"leads to destruction\" (the \"two ways\" of the Didache). Catechesis of the \"newness of life\" in Christ should be: a catechesis of the Holy Spirit, of grace, of the beatitudes, of sin and forgiveness, of the human virtues, of the Christian virtues of faith, hope and charity, of the twofold commandment of charity, and an ecclesial catechesis. Its first and last point of reference is always Jesus Christ himself, \"the way, the truth and the life\"."],["1699–1709","Man, the image of God: life in the Spirit fulfils the vocation of man. The dignity of the human person is rooted in creation in God's image and likeness; it is fulfilled in the vocation to divine beatitude. Endowed with a spiritual and immortal soul, intellect and free will, the human person is from conception ordered to God. Disfigured by original sin, the image is restored in Christ; the one who believes in Christ becomes a son of God, and this adoption transforms him, enabling him to follow Christ's example."],["1710–1715","Man is called to seek and love what is true and good. Conscience, the moral law, freedom and grace are the themes of what follows. \"Christ… fully reveals man to himself and makes his supreme calling clear\" (Gaudium et Spes 22). The interior life, a life of prayer, enables us to discern and choose."]],
 mis:[["Catholic morality is a list of rules.","It begins with dignity and vocation: life in Christ, led by the Spirit, aiming at beatitude. The commandments serve that life (§§ 1691–1698)."],["Being made in God's image means looking like him.","It means having a spiritual soul with intellect and free will, made for communion with God (§§ 1703–1705)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants see Christian morality as the response to a dignity and a call, not as rule-keeping.",discuss:["What does St Leo's phrase \"recognise your dignity\" ask of you this week?","Why does the Catechism put Christ, the Spirit and grace before the commandments?","How does seeing others as made in God's image change how you treat them?"],act:"Read the opening of the Didache on the \"two ways\" (life and death) and compare it with Deuteronomy 30:15–20."},
 saints:[["St Leo the Great (Sermon 21.3, Christmas)","Christian, recognise your dignity, and now that you share in God's own nature, do not return to your former base condition."],["Didache 1.1 (1st century)","There are two ways, one of life and one of death, and there is a great difference between the two ways."]],
 more:[CQ("Who is the first and last point of reference for moral catechesis?",["The Ten Commandments","Jesus Christ","Conscience"],1,"§ 1698."),CQ("What does Gaudium et Spes 22 say Christ reveals?",["The date of the end","Man to himself and his supreme calling","The secrets of nature"],1,"§ 1701.")]});
cccD("c64",{paras:[["1716–1717","The Beatitudes are at the heart of Jesus' preaching. They take up the promises made to the chosen people since Abraham, and fulfil them by ordering them no longer merely to the possession of a territory but to the Kingdom of heaven. They depict the countenance of Jesus Christ and portray his charity; they express the vocation of the faithful associated with the glory of his Passion and Resurrection; they shed light on the actions and attitudes characteristic of the Christian life; they are the paradoxical promises that sustain hope in the midst of tribulations."],["1718–1719","The desire for happiness is of divine origin: God has placed it in the human heart in order to draw man to the One who alone can fulfil it. \"How is it, then, that I seek you, Lord? Since in seeking you, my God, I seek a happy life\" (Augustine). The Beatitudes reveal the goal of human existence, the ultimate end of human acts: God calls us to his own beatitude."],["1720–1724","The New Testament uses several expressions for this beatitude: the coming of the Kingdom, the vision of God, entering into the joy of the Lord, entering into God's rest. God has put us in the world to know, serve and love him, and so to come to paradise. Beatitude makes us \"partakers of the divine nature\" and of eternal life. It surpasses the understanding and powers of man; it comes from an entirely free gift of God: supernatural. The beatitude we are promised confronts us with decisive moral choices: it invites us to purify our hearts of bad instincts and to seek the love of God above all else. It teaches us that true happiness is not found in riches or well-being, in human fame or power, nor in any human achievement, however beneficial, but in God alone, the source of every good and of all love."]],
 mis:[["The Beatitudes are nice ideals but not really moral teaching.","They are the heart of Jesus' preaching and the charter of Christian life (§§ 1716–1717)."],["Wanting to be happy is selfish.","The desire for happiness is of divine origin; God placed it in us to draw us to himself (§ 1718)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants connect their own desire for happiness to the Beatitudes and to God as the only fulfilling end.",discuss:["Where do people around you look for happiness? What does § 1723 say about each?","Which Beatitude is hardest for you, and why?","How can the Beatitudes be \"promises that sustain hope\" in suffering?"],act:"Read Matthew 5:1–12 slowly. Each person picks one Beatitude and writes one concrete way to live it this week."},
 saints:[["St Augustine (Confessions X.20.29)","How is it, then, that I seek you, Lord? Since in seeking you, my God, I seek a happy life, let me seek you so that my soul may live."],["St Thomas Aquinas (ST I-II q.2 a.8)","Nothing can satisfy man's will except the universal good, which is found not in any creature but in God alone."]],
 more:[CQ("Where does the desire for happiness come from?",["Our selfishness","God, who placed it in our hearts","Society"],1,"§ 1718."),CQ("Where, according to § 1723, is true happiness found?",["Riches and fame","In God alone","Human achievement"],1,"§ 1723.")],psyNote:"Positive psychology distinguishes hedonic wellbeing (pleasure) from eudaimonic wellbeing (meaning, purpose, growth); meta-analytic work links purpose in life to lower mortality and better mental health (Cohen, Bavishi & Rozanski 2016, Psychosomatic Medicine). Aquinas's account of beatitude is eudaimonic: happiness is the possession of the highest good, not a feeling."});
cccD("c65",{paras:[["1730–1732","God created man a rational being, conferring on him the dignity of a person who can initiate and control his own actions. \"God willed that man should be left in the hand of his own counsel\" (Sirach 15:14). Freedom is the power, rooted in reason and will, to act or not to act, to do this or that, and so to perform deliberate actions on one's own responsibility. Freedom is a force for growth and maturity in truth and goodness; it attains its perfection when directed toward God, our beatitude. As long as freedom has not bound itself definitively to its ultimate good, there is the possibility of choosing between good and evil."],["1733–1738","The more one does what is good, the freer one becomes. There is no true freedom except in the service of what is good and just; choosing to disobey and do evil is an abuse of freedom and leads to \"the slavery of sin\". Freedom makes man responsible for his acts to the extent that they are voluntary. Imputability and responsibility for an action can be diminished or even nullified by ignorance, inadvertence, duress, fear, habit, inordinate attachments, and other psychological or social factors. Every human person has the natural right to be recognised as a free and responsible being; the right to the exercise of freedom, especially in moral and religious matters, must be recognised and protected by civil authority, within the limits of the common good and public order."],["1739–1742","Freedom and sin: man's freedom is limited and fallible. By refusing God's plan of love, he deceived himself and became a slave to sin. The exercise of freedom does not imply a right to say or do everything; it is false to maintain that man, \"the subject of this freedom\", is \"an individual who is fully self-sufficient and whose finality is the satisfaction of his own interests in the enjoyment of earthly goods\". Freedom and grace: by his glorious Cross Christ has won salvation for all men. The grace of Christ is not in the slightest way a rival of our freedom when this freedom accords with the sense of the true and the good that God has put in the human heart. The more docile we are to the promptings of grace, the more we grow in inner freedom."]],
 mis:[["Freedom means doing whatever I want.","Freedom is ordered to the good: the more one does good, the freer one becomes; evil enslaves (§ 1733)."],["Grace competes with freedom: more God means less me.","Grace is not a rival of freedom; docility to grace increases inner freedom (§ 1742)."],["Any factor like fear or habit means I'm never responsible.","Such factors can diminish or even remove imputability, but freedom remains real and we are responsible to the extent acts are voluntary (§§ 1734–1735)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand freedom as power for the good, the factors that diminish responsibility, and the harmony of grace and freedom.",discuss:["Can you think of a habit that has made you less free? One that has made you more free?","How does § 1735 help us judge others (and ourselves) with mercy without denying responsibility?","What is the difference between \"freedom from\" and \"freedom for\"?"],act:"Make two columns: \"things that enslave\" and \"things that set free\". Discuss how a virtue (like temperance) is a kind of freedom."},
 saints:[["St Irenaeus (Against Heresies IV.4.3)","Man is rational and therefore like God; he is created with free will and is master over his acts."],["St Augustine (City of God XIV.11)","The will is truly free when it is not the slave of vices and sins."]],
 more:[CQ("According to § 1733, what happens the more one does good?",["One becomes less free","One becomes freer","Nothing changes"],1,"§ 1733."),CQ("Which can diminish imputability?",["Ignorance, duress, fear, habit","Only physical force","Nothing"],0,"§ 1735.")],psyNote:"§ 1735's list (ignorance, duress, fear, habit, psychological factors) parallels how forensic and clinical psychology assess capacity and diminished responsibility. Self-determination theory (Ryan & Deci 2000) likewise distinguishes autonomous from controlled motivation: being driven by compulsion is not the same as acting freely."});
cccD("c66",{paras:[["1749–1750","Freedom makes man a moral subject. When he acts deliberately, man is, so to speak, the father of his acts. Human acts, that is, acts freely chosen in consequence of a judgement of conscience, can be morally evaluated: they are either good or evil. The morality of human acts depends on three \"sources\": the object chosen; the end in view or the intention; the circumstances of the action."],["1751–1754","The object chosen is a good toward which the will deliberately directs itself; it is the matter of a human act, and determines the morality of the act, as reason recognises it as being or not being in conformity with the true good. The intention is on the side of the acting subject: it is a movement of the will toward the end, and a key element in moral evaluation. One action can be inspired by several intentions. A good intention (for example, that of helping one's neighbour) does not make behaviour that is intrinsically disordered, such as lying and calumny, good or just. \"The end does not justify the means.\" A bad intention added to a good act (for example, almsgiving \"to be seen by men\") makes it evil. Circumstances, including consequences, are secondary elements: they contribute to increasing or diminishing the moral goodness or evil of acts (the amount of a theft), and can diminish or increase responsibility (acting out of fear of death); they cannot of themselves change the moral quality of acts, making good or right an action that is in itself evil."],["1755–1761","A morally good act requires the goodness of the object, of the end, and of the circumstances together. An evil end corrupts the action, even if its object is good in itself. The object of the choice can by itself vitiate an act in its entirety: there are some concrete acts, such as fornication, that it is always wrong to choose, because choosing them entails a disorder of the will, a moral evil. It is therefore an error to judge the morality of human acts by considering only the intention that inspires them or the circumstances which supply their context. There are acts which, in and of themselves, independently of circumstances and intentions, are always gravely illicit by reason of their object: blasphemy and perjury, murder and adultery. One may not do evil so that good may result from it."]],
 mis:[["If my intention is good, the act is good.","A good intention cannot make an intrinsically evil act good: the end does not justify the means (§§ 1753, 1756)."],["Morality depends entirely on consequences.","Circumstances and consequences can increase or reduce goodness or evil, but cannot make an evil act good (§ 1754)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants can apply the three sources of morality (object, intention, circumstances) to real cases.",discuss:["Apply the three sources to: giving money to charity to impress your boss.","Why can't a good intention justify lying?","What is the difference between an act's object and its consequences?"],act:"Case clinic: give groups three short cases (e.g. telling a lie to protect a friend's feelings; returning lost money; studying hard out of jealousy). Analyse each by object, intention and circumstances."},
 saints:[["St Thomas Aquinas (ST I-II q.18 a.4 ad 3)","An action is not good simply unless it is good in every respect: bonum ex integra causa, malum ex quocumque defectu (good from a complete cause, evil from any single defect)."],["St Paul (Romans 3:8)","Why not do evil that good may come? Their condemnation is just."]],
 more:[CQ("What are the three sources of morality?",["Feelings, rules, results","Object, intention, circumstances","Conscience, law, grace"],1,"§ 1750."),CQ("Can circumstances make an evil act good?",["Yes, if serious enough","No; they only increase or decrease goodness or evil","Only in emergencies"],1,"§ 1754.")]});
cccD("c67",{paras:[["1762–1766","The human person is ordered to beatitude by his deliberate acts; the passions or feelings he experiences can dispose him to it and contribute to it. \"Passions\" means emotions or movements of the sensitive appetite that incline us to act or not to act in regard to something felt or imagined to be good or evil. They form the passageway and ensure the connection between the life of the senses and the life of the mind. The most fundamental passion is love, aroused by the attraction of the good. Love causes a desire for the absent good and the hope of obtaining it; this movement finds completion in the pleasure and joy of the good possessed. The apprehension of evil causes hatred, aversion and fear of the impending evil; this movement ends in sadness at some present evil, or in the anger that resists it. \"To love is to will the good of another\" (Aquinas)."],["1767–1770","In themselves passions are neither good nor evil. They are morally qualified only to the extent that they effectively engage reason and will. Passions are said to be voluntary \"either because they are commanded by the will or because the will does not place obstacles in their way\". It belongs to the perfection of the moral or human good that the passions be governed by reason. Strong feelings are not decisive for the morality or holiness of persons; they are simply the inexhaustible reservoir of images and affections in which the moral life is expressed. Emotions and feelings can be taken up into the virtues or perverted by the vices. In the Christian life the Holy Spirit accomplishes his work by mobilising the whole being, with all its sorrows, fears and sadness, as is visible in the Lord's agony and passion. Moral perfection consists in man's being moved to the good not by his will alone, but also by his \"heart\"."]],
 mis:[["Feeling angry (or afraid, or attracted) is a sin.","Passions in themselves are neither good nor evil; they become moral only as they engage reason and will (§ 1767)."],["Holiness means having no strong emotions.","Strong feelings are not decisive for holiness; the goal is for the heart to be moved to the good too (§§ 1768–1770). Christ himself wept and was troubled."],["Emotions should simply be followed.","They are to be governed by reason and taken up into the virtues (§§ 1767–1768)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand the passions in Thomistic terms, their moral neutrality, and their integration into virtue.",discuss:["Name the eleven passions in Aquinas's scheme (love, hatred, desire, aversion, joy, sadness; hope, despair, fear, daring, anger). Which do you notice most?","When is anger good? When is it sinful?","What does it mean to be moved to the good \"by the heart\"?"],act:"Emotion journal: for three days, note one strong feeling each day, what good or evil it was responding to, and what reason said about how to act on it."},
 saints:[["St Thomas Aquinas (ST I-II q.26 a.4)","To love is to will the good of someone."],["St Augustine (City of God XIV.6)","If the will is perverse, the movements will be perverse; if it is right, they will be not only blameless but praiseworthy."]],
 more:[CQ("What is the most fundamental passion?",["Fear","Love","Anger"],1,"§ 1765."),CQ("When do passions become morally good or evil?",["Always","When they engage reason and will","Never"],1,"§ 1767.")],psyNote:"Aquinas's view that emotions are neither good nor bad in themselves, but are to be shaped by reason, closely parallels emotion-regulation research (Gross 1998, 2015): strategies like cognitive reappraisal are generally linked to better outcomes than suppression. CBT and DBT both teach that feelings are valid signals to be understood, not commands to be obeyed or enemies to be crushed."});
cccD("c68",{paras:[["1776–1778","\"Deep within his conscience man discovers a law which he has not laid upon himself but which he must obey. Its voice, ever calling him to love and to do what is good and to avoid evil, sounds in his heart… For man has in his heart a law inscribed by God… His conscience is man's most secret core and his sanctuary. There he is alone with God whose voice echoes in his depths\" (Gaudium et Spes 16). Moral conscience, present at the heart of the person, enjoins him at the appropriate moment to do good and avoid evil. Conscience is a judgement of reason whereby the human person recognises the moral quality of a concrete act he is going to perform, is in the process of performing, or has already completed. Conscience is \"a messenger of him, who, both in nature and in grace, speaks to us behind a veil\" (Newman)."],["1779–1782","It is important for every person to be sufficiently present to himself in order to hear and follow the voice of his conscience; this requirement of interiority is all the more necessary as life often distracts us from any reflection, self-examination or introspection. The dignity of the human person implies and requires uprightness of moral conscience. Man has the right to act in conscience and in freedom so as personally to make moral decisions. \"He must not be forced to act contrary to his conscience. Nor must he be prevented from acting according to his conscience, especially in religious matters.\""],["1783–1785","The formation of conscience: conscience must be informed and moral judgement enlightened. A well-formed conscience is upright and truthful; it formulates its judgements according to reason, in conformity with the true good willed by the wisdom of the Creator. The education of conscience is a lifelong task. It guards against fear, selfishness and pride, resentment arising from guilt, and feelings of complacency. In the formation of conscience the Word of God is the light for our path; we must also examine our conscience before the Lord's Cross, assisted by the gifts of the Spirit, the witness or advice of others, and the authoritative teaching of the Church."],["1786–1789","To choose in accord with conscience: some rules apply in every case. One may never do evil so that good may result from it. The Golden Rule: \"Whatever you wish that men would do to you, do so to them.\" Charity always proceeds by way of respect for one's neighbour and his conscience."],["1790–1794","Erroneous judgement: a human being must always obey the certain judgement of his conscience. If he were deliberately to act against it, he would condemn himself. Yet it can happen that conscience is in ignorance and makes erroneous judgements. This ignorance can often be imputed to personal responsibility: when a man \"takes little trouble to find out what is true and good, or when conscience is by degrees almost blinded through the habit of committing sin\". If the ignorance is invincible, or the judgement erroneous without the responsibility of the moral subject, the evil committed cannot be imputed to him; it nonetheless remains an evil, a privation, a disorder. One must therefore work to correct the errors of moral conscience. Sources of errors: ignorance of Christ and his Gospel, bad example, enslavement to one's passions, assertion of a mistaken notion of autonomy of conscience, rejection of the Church's authority and teaching, lack of conversion and charity."]],
 mis:[["Conscience means my feelings or personal opinion.","Conscience is a judgement of reason about the moral quality of a concrete act, which must be formed by truth (§§ 1778, 1783)."],["If I follow my conscience I am always right.","One must follow a certain conscience, but it can err; we have a duty to form it, and culpable ignorance does not excuse (§§ 1790–1791)."],["Forming my conscience by Church teaching means not thinking for myself.","The Church's teaching is one of the lights by which reason judges well, alongside Scripture, prayer, advice and the gifts of the Spirit (§ 1785)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand conscience as a judgement of reason, the duty to form it, and the difference between invincible and culpable ignorance.",discuss:["How would you explain to a friend the difference between conscience and feelings?","What forms (or deforms) consciences today: media, friends, habits?","What is the difference between invincible and culpable (vincible) ignorance?"],act:"Introduce the daily examen: five minutes at night to give thanks, review the day, notice where you followed or ignored conscience, ask forgiveness, and resolve. Practise it together."},
 saints:[["St John Henry Newman (Letter to the Duke of Norfolk 5)","Conscience is the aboriginal Vicar of Christ."],["St Thomas Aquinas (ST I q.79 a.13)","Conscience is not a power but an act: the application of knowledge to what we do."],["St Thomas More (letter to his daughter Margaret from the Tower, 1534, paraphrased)","I will not, for the whole world, set my conscience aside; in this matter I must look to my own soul."]],
 more:[CQ("What is conscience, according to § 1778?",["A feeling of guilt","A judgement of reason about the moral quality of a concrete act","Church law"],1,"§ 1778."),CQ("Is evil done from invincible ignorance imputable?",["Yes, fully","No, though it remains an evil to be corrected","Only partly"],1,"§ 1793."),CQ("Which is NOT listed as a source of erroneous conscience?",["Bad example","Enslavement to passions","Regular examination of conscience"],2,"§ 1792.")],psyNote:"Scrupulosity, religious or moral OCD, is a disorder where conscience-like anxiety becomes compulsive: repeated confessing, reassurance-seeking, intrusive fears of sin. The Catechism's picture of a well-formed conscience (upright, truthful, guarding against \"resentment arising from guilt\") is the healthy contrast. Exposure and response prevention (ERP) is the evidence-based treatment, often best done with a therapist and a confessor working together (Abramowitz & Jacoby 2014)."});
cccD("c69",{paras:[["1803–1804","\"Whatever is true, whatever is honourable… think about these things\" (Phil 4:8). A virtue is a habitual and firm disposition to do the good. It allows the person not only to perform good acts, but to give the best of himself. \"The goal of a virtuous life is to become like God\" (Gregory of Nyssa). Human virtues are firm attitudes, stable dispositions, habitual perfections of intellect and will that govern our actions, order our passions and guide our conduct according to reason and faith. They make possible ease, self-mastery and joy in leading a morally good life."],["1805–1809","The cardinal virtues: four virtues play a pivotal role (cardo = hinge). Prudence disposes practical reason to discern our true good in every circumstance and to choose the right means of achieving it; it is \"right reason in action\" and the \"charioteer of the virtues\", guiding the others. Justice is the constant and firm will to give God and neighbour their due (towards God, the \"virtue of religion\"). Fortitude ensures firmness in difficulties and constancy in the pursuit of the good; it strengthens resolve to resist temptations and overcome obstacles, and enables one to conquer fear, even fear of death. Temperance moderates the attraction of pleasures and provides balance in the use of created goods; it ensures the will's mastery over instincts and keeps desires within the limits of what is honourable."],["1810–1811","The human virtues are acquired by education, by deliberate acts, and by a perseverance ever renewed in repeated efforts; they are purified and elevated by divine grace. It is not easy for man, wounded by sin, to maintain moral balance. Christ's gift of salvation offers us the grace necessary to persevere in the pursuit of the virtues."],["1812–1829","The theological virtues relate directly to God: they have the One and Triune God for their origin, motive and object. They are infused by God into the souls of the faithful to make them capable of acting as his children and of meriting eternal life; they are the foundation of Christian moral activity and animate the human virtues. Faith: by which we believe in God and all he has revealed, and that the Church proposes; \"faith apart from works is dead\". Hope: by which we desire the Kingdom of heaven and eternal life as our happiness, placing our trust in Christ's promises and relying on the grace of the Spirit; it keeps man from discouragement and sustains him. Charity: by which we love God above all things for his own sake, and our neighbour as ourselves for the love of God; it is the \"bond of perfection\" and the form of all the virtues."],["1830–1845","The gifts and fruits of the Holy Spirit: the moral life of Christians is sustained by the gifts of the Spirit, permanent dispositions that make man docile in following his promptings. The seven gifts are wisdom, understanding, counsel, fortitude, knowledge, piety and fear of the Lord. The fruits are perfections the Spirit forms in us as the first fruits of eternal glory; tradition lists twelve: charity, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, generosity, gentleness, faithfulness, modesty, self-control, chastity."]],
 mis:[["Virtue means being prim or never having fun.","Virtue makes possible ease, self-mastery and joy in doing good (§ 1804)."],["We can become virtuous by willpower alone.","Human virtues are acquired by repeated effort but need grace to be purified and elevated; the theological virtues are infused (§§ 1810–1813)."],["Prudence means being cautious.","Prudence is right reason in action: seeing the true good and choosing the right means (§ 1806)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants can name and define the cardinal and theological virtues, gifts and fruits, and plan to grow in one virtue.",discuss:["Which cardinal virtue is your strongest? Your weakest?","Why is prudence called the \"charioteer\" of the virtues?","How do faith, hope and charity change the human virtues?"],act:"Virtue plan: choose one virtue and one small daily act that exercises it (e.g. fortitude: do the hardest task first). Track it for 30 days, like the site's challenges."},
 saints:[["St Gregory of Nyssa (On the Beatitudes 1)","The goal of a virtuous life is to become like God."],["St Augustine (On the Morals of the Catholic Church 25.46)","To live well is nothing other than to love God with all one's heart, with all one's soul and with all one's efforts."],["St Thomas Aquinas (ST II-II q.23 a.8)","Charity is the form of all the virtues."]],
 more:[CQ("Which virtue is the \"charioteer of the virtues\"?",["Justice","Prudence","Temperance"],1,"§ 1806."),CQ("How are the human virtues acquired?",["Only by infusion","By education, deliberate acts and perseverance, aided by grace","At birth"],1,"§ 1810."),CQ("Which is the greatest theological virtue?",["Faith","Hope","Charity"],2,"§ 1826, 1 Cor 13:13.")],psyNote:"Habit-formation research supports the Thomistic claim that virtues are acquired by repetition: in a naturalistic study, new behaviours took a median of about 66 days to become automatic, with wide variation (Lally et al. 2010, European Journal of Social Psychology, N=96). Peterson and Seligman's character-strengths project (2004) explicitly drew on the classical virtues, including the cardinal ones."});
cccD("c70",{paras:[["1846–1848","The Gospel is the revelation in Jesus Christ of God's mercy to sinners. \"You shall call his name Jesus, for he will save his people from their sins.\" To receive his mercy, we must admit our faults. \"If we say we have no sin, we deceive ourselves.\" Grace must uncover sin so as to convert our hearts: \"Where sin increased, grace abounded all the more.\""],["1849–1853","Sin is an offence against reason, truth and right conscience; it is failure in genuine love for God and neighbour caused by a perverse attachment to certain goods. It wounds human nature and injures human solidarity. It has been defined as \"an utterance, a deed, or a desire contrary to the eternal law\" (Augustine). Sin is an offence against God; it sets itself against God's love and turns our hearts away from it. Like the first sin, it is disobedience, a revolt against God through the will to become \"like gods\". In the Passion, where Christ's mercy vanquishes it, sin most clearly manifests its violence and its many forms. Sins can be distinguished according to their objects, the virtues they oppose, or the commandments they violate; by whether they concern God, neighbour or oneself; and as spiritual or carnal, in thought, word, deed or omission. The root of sin is in the heart of man."],["1854–1864","The gravity of sins: mortal and venial. Mortal sin destroys charity in the heart by a grave violation of God's law; it turns man away from God. Venial sin allows charity to subsist, even though it offends and wounds it. For a sin to be mortal, three conditions must together be met: grave matter (specified by the Ten Commandments), full knowledge, and deliberate consent. Unintentional ignorance can diminish or even remove imputability; feigned ignorance and hardness of heart do not. The promptings of feelings and passions, and external pressures or pathological disorders, can also diminish the voluntary and free character of the offence. Mortal sin, unrepented, causes exclusion from Christ's kingdom and the eternal death of hell, for our freedom has the power to make choices for ever. However, although we can judge that an act is in itself a grave offence, we must entrust judgement of persons to the justice and mercy of God. Venial sin weakens charity, manifests a disordered affection for created goods, impedes progress in virtue, and merits temporal punishment; deliberate and unrepented venial sin disposes us little by little to commit mortal sin. Blasphemy against the Spirit: there are no limits to God's mercy, but anyone who deliberately refuses to accept his mercy by repenting rejects forgiveness."],["1865–1869","The proliferation of sin: sin creates a proclivity to sin; it engenders vice by repetition. Vices can be classified according to the virtues they oppose, or linked to the capital sins: pride, avarice, envy, wrath, lust, gluttony, and sloth or acedia. Sins that \"cry to heaven\": the blood of Abel, the sin of the Sodomites, the cry of the oppressed people in Egypt, the cry of the foreigner, the widow and the orphan, injustice to the wage earner. We have responsibility for sins committed by others when we cooperate in them: participating directly and voluntarily, ordering, advising, praising or approving them, not disclosing or hindering them when obliged to, or protecting evil-doers. Sins give rise to social situations and institutions contrary to divine goodness: \"structures of sin\", an expression and effect of personal sins."]],
 mis:[["Any sin about a grave matter is automatically mortal.","Mortal sin requires grave matter, full knowledge and deliberate consent together (§ 1857)."],["We can know that a particular person is in hell.","We may judge acts, but judgement of persons belongs to God's justice and mercy (§ 1861)."],["Venial sins don't matter.","They wound charity, impede virtue, and, deliberate and unrepented, dispose us toward mortal sin (§ 1863)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand sin as an offence against love, the conditions for mortal sin, the capital sins, and our share in others' sins and social structures.",discuss:["Why does the Catechism place sin after mercy (§ 1846)?","Apply the three conditions for mortal sin to a real (anonymised) example.","Which capital sin shows up most in our culture? In your life?"],act:"Capital sins and contrary virtues: match each capital sin to its remedy (pride–humility, avarice–generosity, envy–kindness, wrath–patience, lust–chastity, gluttony–temperance, sloth–diligence) and plan one act of the opposite virtue."},
 saints:[["St Augustine (Against Faustus 22.27)","Sin is a word, deed or desire contrary to the eternal law."],["St Gregory the Great (Moralia 31.45)","Pride is the queen of vices; when she has conquered the heart she hands it over to the seven principal vices, her generals."]],
 more:[CQ("What three conditions are required for mortal sin?",["Grave matter, full knowledge, deliberate consent","Guilt, shame, regret","Public, frequent, harmful"],0,"§ 1857."),CQ("Which can diminish the voluntary character of a sin?",["Feigned ignorance","Pathological disorders and external pressures","Hardness of heart"],1,"§§ 1859–1860.")],psyNote:"The distinction between guilt (\"I did something bad\") and shame (\"I am bad\") matters here. Meta-analytic work associates shame-proneness, more than guilt-proneness, with depression and other symptoms (Kim, Thibodeau & Jorgensen 2011, Psychological Bulletin). The Catechism's order, mercy first and then sin, supports healthy guilt that leads to repair rather than global self-condemnation."});
cccD("c71",{paras:[["1877–1880","The vocation of humanity is to show forth the image of God and to be transformed into the image of the Father's only Son. This vocation takes a personal form but also concerns the human community as a whole. There is a certain resemblance between the union of the divine persons and the fraternity that men ought to establish among themselves. Love of neighbour is inseparable from love for God. The human person needs to live in society; through exchange with others, mutual service and dialogue he develops his potential. A society is a group of persons bound together organically by a principle of unity that goes beyond each one of them."],["1881–1885","Each community is defined by its purpose and obeys specific rules, but \"the human person… is and ought to be the principle, the subject and the end of all social institutions\". Certain societies, such as the family and the state, correspond more directly to human nature; they are necessary. Participation should be encouraged in voluntary associations and institutions. Socialisation also presents dangers: excessive intervention by the state can threaten personal freedom and initiative. The principle of subsidiarity: \"a community of a higher order should not interfere in the internal life of a community of a lower order, depriving the latter of its functions, but rather should support it in case of need and help to coordinate its activity with the activities of the rest of society\". God does not reserve to himself all exercise of power; he entrusts to every creature the functions it is capable of performing."],["1886–1889","Conversion and society: society is essential to the fulfilment of the human vocation. To attain this aim, respect must be accorded to the just hierarchy of values, which subordinates physical and instinctual dimensions to interior and spiritual ones. The inversion of means and ends leads to unjust structures. It is necessary to appeal to the spiritual and moral capacities of the person and to the permanent need for inner conversion, so as to bring about social changes that will really serve him. Charity is the greatest social commandment."]],
 mis:[["Faith is private and has nothing to say about society.","The human vocation is also communal; love of neighbour has a social form (§§ 1878–1879, 1889)."],["Subsidiarity means the state should do nothing.","It means higher bodies should not absorb what lower bodies can do, but should support and coordinate them (§ 1883)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand the social nature of the person, the principle of subsidiarity, and the link between inner conversion and social change.",discuss:["Which communities (family, parish, school, town) have shaped you most?","Give an example of subsidiarity working well, and of it being violated.","Why does the Catechism say social change requires inner conversion?"],act:"Map your communities from family to nation. For each, name one way you could serve it this month."},
 saints:[["Pope Pius XI (Quadragesimo Anno 79, 1931)","It is an injustice… to assign to a greater and higher association what lesser and subordinate organisations can do."],["St John Chrysostom (Homily on 1 Corinthians 25.3)","This is the rule of most perfect Christianity… to seek the common good."]],
 more:[CQ("What is the principle of subsidiarity?",["Higher bodies should do everything","Higher communities should not absorb lower ones' functions but support them","Everyone should act alone"],1,"§ 1883."),CQ("What is \"the greatest social commandment\"?",["Obedience","Charity","Prosperity"],1,"§ 1889.")]});
cccD("c72",{paras:[["1897–1904","Authority: human society can be neither well ordered nor prosperous unless it has some people invested with legitimate authority to preserve its institutions and devote themselves to the common good. Authority is the quality by virtue of which persons or institutions make laws and give orders, and expect obedience. Every human community needs an authority; its foundation lies in human nature. \"There is no authority except from God.\" The duty of obedience requires all to give due honour to authority. The choice of political regime and the appointment of rulers are left to the free decision of citizens. Authority does not derive its moral legitimacy from itself; it must act for the common good, employing morally licit means. If rulers enact unjust laws or take measures contrary to the moral order, such arrangements would not be binding in conscience. \"A human law has the character of law to the extent that it accords with right reason… insofar as it falls short of right reason it is said to be an unjust law, and so has not so much the nature of law as of a kind of violence\" (Aquinas). It is preferable that each power be balanced by other powers: the rule of law."],["1905–1912","The common good: \"the sum total of social conditions which allow people, either as groups or as individuals, to reach their fulfilment more fully and more easily\". It consists of three essential elements: respect for the person as such (fundamental and inalienable rights, freedom of conscience and religion); the social well-being and development of the group (making accessible what is needed to lead a truly human life: food, clothing, health, work, education and culture, information, the right to found a family); and peace, the stability and security of a just order. It is the purpose of the state to defend and promote the common good. Human interdependence is increasing: a universal common good concerns the whole human family."],["1913–1917","Responsibility and participation: participation is the voluntary and generous engagement of a person in social interchange. It is necessary that all participate, each according to his position and role, in promoting the common good. This obligation is inherent in the dignity of the human person. Participation is achieved first by taking charge of the areas for which one assumes personal responsibility: care for one's family, conscientious work. As far as possible citizens should take an active part in public life. As with any ethical obligation, participation requires conversion; fraud and other subterfuges by which some escape the constraints of law and civic obligation must be firmly condemned."]],
 mis:[["Christians must obey every law.","Unjust laws contrary to the moral order are not binding in conscience; legitimate authority must serve the common good (§§ 1902–1903)."],["The common good means the greatest good of the majority.","It is the conditions allowing everyone to flourish, starting with respect for each person's rights (§§ 1906–1907)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand the moral basis of authority, the three elements of the common good, and their duty to participate.",discuss:["How does Aquinas's definition of unjust law shed light on South African history (for example, apartheid laws)?","Which element of the common good is most under strain in your community?","What does responsible participation look like for a student or young worker?"],act:"Read Martin Luther King Jr's use of Aquinas in the Letter from Birmingham Jail (\"an unjust law is no law at all\"). Discuss how it applies § 1902."},
 saints:[["St Thomas Aquinas (ST I-II q.93 a.3 ad 2)","Human law has the nature of law insofar as it partakes of right reason… insofar as it deviates from reason it is called unjust law, and has the nature not of law but of violence."],["Letter of Barnabas 4.10 (2nd century)","Do not live entirely isolated, having retreated into yourselves… but gather instead to seek the common good together."]],
 more:[CQ("What are the three elements of the common good?",["Wealth, power, order","Respect for the person, social well-being and development, peace","Laws, police, courts"],1,"§§ 1907–1909."),CQ("Are unjust laws binding in conscience?",["Yes","No","Only if the majority voted for them"],1,"§ 1903.")]});
cccD("c73",{paras:[["1928–1933","Society ensures social justice when it provides the conditions that allow associations or individuals to obtain what is their due. Social justice can be obtained only in respecting the transcendent dignity of man. Respect for the human person entails respect for the rights that flow from his dignity; these rights are prior to society and must be recognised by it. Respect for the human person proceeds by way of respect for the principle that \"everyone should look upon his neighbour (without any exception) as 'another self'\". The duty of making ourselves a neighbour to others and actively serving them becomes even more urgent when it involves the disadvantaged. This same duty extends to those who think or act differently from us. The teaching of Christ goes so far as to require the forgiveness of offences; it extends the commandment of love to every enemy."],["1934–1938","Equality and differences: created in the image of the one God and equally endowed with rational souls, all men have the same nature and the same origin; all therefore enjoy an equal dignity. Every form of social or cultural discrimination in fundamental personal rights on the grounds of sex, race, colour, social conditions, language, or religion must be curbed and eradicated as incompatible with God's design. On coming into the world, man is not equipped with everything he needs: differences appear tied to age, physical abilities, intellectual or moral aptitudes, and the distribution of wealth. These differences belong to God's plan, who wills that each receive what he needs from others, and that those with particular talents share the benefits with those who need them. There also exist sinful inequalities that affect millions of men and women; they are in open contradiction of the Gospel."],["1939–1942","Human solidarity: the principle of solidarity, also articulated in terms of \"friendship\" or \"social charity\", is a direct demand of human and Christian brotherhood. Solidarity is manifested in the first place by the distribution of goods and remuneration for work. It presupposes the effort for a more just social order where tensions can be better resolved and conflicts settled by negotiation. Socio-economic problems can be resolved only with the help of all forms of solidarity: of the poor among themselves, between rich and poor, of workers among themselves, between employers and employees, among nations and peoples. Solidarity is also a Christian virtue: it practises the sharing of spiritual goods even more than material ones."]],
 mis:[["Social justice is a political ideology foreign to the Church.","The Catechism defines social justice as giving people what is their due, grounded in human dignity (§§ 1928–1930)."],["All inequality is unjust.","Some differences belong to God's plan and call for sharing; others are sinful inequalities that contradict the Gospel (§§ 1936–1938)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand social justice, equal dignity amid differences, and solidarity as a virtue.",discuss:["What does it mean to see your neighbour as \"another self\"?","How does § 1935 speak to South Africa's history of discrimination?","Name one form of solidarity you could practise this month."],act:"Solidarity audit: choose a local need (a soup kitchen, a children's home, a tutoring programme) and plan a concrete group response."},
 saints:[["St John Paul II (Sollicitudo Rei Socialis 38, 1987)","Solidarity is not a feeling of vague compassion… it is a firm and persevering determination to commit oneself to the common good."],["St Catherine of Siena (Dialogue 7)","I could well have supplied each of you with all your needs… but I wanted to make you dependent on one another, so that you would be my ministers to share the graces I have given you."]],
 more:[CQ("Which forms of discrimination does § 1935 say must be eradicated?",["Only racial","Discrimination on grounds of sex, race, colour, social conditions, language or religion","None"],1,"§ 1935."),CQ("What is another name for solidarity?",["Socialism","Friendship or social charity","Tolerance"],1,"§ 1939.")]});
cccD("c74",{paras:[["1949–1953","Called to beatitude but wounded by sin, man stands in need of salvation from God. Divine help comes to him in Christ through the law that guides him and the grace that sustains him. The moral law is the work of divine Wisdom: fatherly instruction, God's pedagogy. Law is \"an ordinance of reason for the common good, promulgated by the one who is in charge of the community\" (Aquinas). There are different expressions of the moral law, all interrelated: eternal law, the source of all law in God; natural law; revealed law (the Old Law and the New Law of the Gospel); and civil and ecclesiastical laws. The moral law finds its fullness and unity in Christ, \"the end of the law\"."],["1954–1960","The natural moral law: man participates in the wisdom and goodness of the Creator, who gives him mastery over his acts. The natural law expresses the original moral sense which enables man to discern by reason the good and the evil, the truth and the lie. \"The natural law is nothing other than the light of understanding placed in us by God; through it we know what we must do and what we must avoid\" (Aquinas). Its chief precepts are expressed in the Decalogue. It is called \"natural\" not in reference to irrational beings but because reason, which decrees it, properly belongs to human nature. It is universal: present in the heart of each man and established by reason. Its application varies greatly and requires reflection adapted to different conditions. It is immutable and permanent throughout history; even when it is rejected in its very principles, it cannot be destroyed or removed from the heart of man. The precepts of natural law are not perceived by everyone clearly and immediately; in man's present situation, sinful man needs grace and revelation so moral and religious truths may be known \"by everyone with facility, with firm certainty and with no admixture of error\"."],["1961–1964","The Old Law: God, our Creator and Redeemer, chose Israel for himself and revealed his Law to it. The Old Law is the first stage of revealed law; its moral prescriptions are summed up in the Ten Commandments. It is holy, spiritual and good, yet still imperfect: like a tutor, it shows what must be done, but does not of itself give the strength, the grace of the Spirit, to fulfil it. It is a preparation for the Gospel."],["1965–1974","The New Law or the Law of the Gospel is the perfection here on earth of the divine law, natural and revealed. It is the work of Christ and is expressed particularly in the Sermon on the Mount. It is also the work of the Holy Spirit: \"the grace of the Holy Spirit given to the faithful through faith in Christ\" (Aquinas). It works through charity; it uses the Sermon on the Mount to teach us what must be done, and the sacraments to communicate the grace to do it. It fulfils, refines, surpasses and leads the Old Law to its perfection. It is called a law of love (it makes us act out of love infused by the Spirit rather than fear), a law of grace (it confers the strength of grace), and a law of freedom (it sets us free from the ritual and juridical observances of the Old Law). Besides its precepts, the New Law includes the evangelical counsels (poverty, chastity, obedience), which aim to remove whatever might hinder the development of charity."]],
 mis:[["Natural law means whatever happens in nature (animals do it, so it's natural).","Natural law is the law known by human reason, which belongs to human nature (§ 1955)."],["The Old Testament law is abolished and irrelevant.","Its moral precepts (the Decalogue) remain; the New Law fulfils and perfects the Old (§§ 1962–1967)."],["The New Law is just a stricter rulebook.","It is principally the grace of the Holy Spirit given through faith in Christ: a law of love, grace and freedom (§§ 1966, 1972)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand the forms of law (eternal, natural, revealed, civil), especially natural law, and the New Law as the grace of the Spirit.",discuss:["Why is \"natural\" in natural law about reason, not biology?","How would you show someone who isn't religious that some moral truths are knowable by reason?","How is the New Law \"written on the heart\" (Jer 31:33)?"],act:"Read Romans 2:14–15 and Matthew 5:17–48. List how Jesus fulfils and deepens each Old Law teaching he mentions."},
 saints:[["St Thomas Aquinas (ST I-II q.90 a.4)","Law is nothing other than an ordinance of reason for the common good, promulgated by the one who has care of the community."],["St Thomas Aquinas (ST I-II q.106 a.1)","The New Law is chiefly the grace itself of the Holy Spirit, given to those who believe in Christ."],["St Augustine (Confessions II.4.9)","Theft is punished by your law, Lord, a law written in the hearts of men which iniquity itself cannot efface."]],
 more:[CQ("How does Aquinas define law?",["Whatever the ruler wants","An ordinance of reason for the common good, promulgated by the one in charge","A feeling of obligation"],1,"§ 1951."),CQ("What is the New Law principally?",["A set of new rules","The grace of the Holy Spirit given through faith in Christ","The Ten Commandments"],1,"§ 1966.")]});
cccD("c75",{paras:[["1987–1995","Justification: the grace of the Holy Spirit has the power to justify us, that is, to cleanse us from our sins and to communicate to us \"the righteousness of God through faith in Jesus Christ\" and through Baptism. Justification is first the work of conversion: man turns towards God and away from sin, and so accepts forgiveness and righteousness from on high. \"Justification is not only the remission of sins, but also the sanctification and renewal of the interior man\" (Trent). It detaches man from sin, and purifies his heart. It establishes cooperation between God's grace and man's freedom: man assents to the Word of God, and grace prevents (goes before), prepares and elicits this free response. Justification has been merited for us by the Passion of Christ. It is conferred in Baptism. It is \"the most excellent work of God's love\" (Augustine), greater even than the creation of heaven and earth."],["1996–2005","Grace: our justification comes from the grace of God. Grace is favour, the free and undeserved help that God gives us to respond to his call to become children of God, partakers of the divine nature and of eternal life. It is a participation in the life of God. The preparation of man for the reception of grace is already a work of grace. Sanctifying grace (habitual grace) is the gratuitous gift of his life that God makes to us, infused by the Spirit into our soul to heal it of sin and sanctify it: a stable and supernatural disposition. Actual graces are God's interventions, whether at the beginning of conversion or in the course of the work of sanctification. There are also sacramental graces, special graces (charisms) and graces of state. Grace, being supernatural, escapes our experience and cannot be known except by faith; we cannot rely on feelings to deduce that we are justified. But reflection on God's blessings in our life is a guarantee that grace is at work in us. St Joan of Arc, asked if she was in God's grace: \"If I am not, may it please God to put me in it; if I am, may it please God to keep me there.\""],["2006–2011","Merit: with regard to God, there is no strict right to any merit on the part of man; between God and us there is an immeasurable inequality. The merit of man before God in the Christian life arises from the fact that God has freely chosen to associate man with the work of his grace. The fatherly action of God is first on his own initiative, and then follows man's free acting through his collaboration. Since the initiative belongs to God in the order of grace, no one can merit the initial grace of forgiveness and justification. Moved by the Spirit and charity, we can then merit for ourselves and others the graces needed for sanctification and for eternal life. The charity of Christ is the source in us of all our merits. \"Our merits are God's gifts\" (Augustine). \"In the evening of this life, I shall appear before you with empty hands\" (Thérèse of Lisieux)."],["2012–2016","Christian holiness: \"we know that in everything God works for good with those who love him\". All Christians in any state or walk of life are called to the fullness of Christian life and to the perfection of charity. Spiritual progress tends toward ever more intimate union with Christ, sometimes called \"mystical\" union. The way of perfection passes by way of the Cross: there is no holiness without renunciation and spiritual battle. The children of our holy mother the Church rightly hope for the grace of final perseverance."]],
 mis:[["Catholics believe we earn salvation by good works.","No one can merit the initial grace of justification; it is God's free gift. Our merits are themselves God's gifts (§§ 2007–2011)."],["Justification is only a legal declaration; nothing changes inside us.","Justification is remission of sins and also interior sanctification and renewal (§ 1989)."],["If I feel close to God I'm in grace; if not, I'm not.","Grace escapes our experience and cannot be deduced from feelings (§ 2005)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand justification, grace (sanctifying and actual) and merit in Catholic teaching, and the universal call to holiness. This section is especially useful in conversations with evangelical Christians.",discuss:["How would you explain to an evangelical friend that Catholics believe salvation is by grace?","What is the difference between sanctifying and actual grace?","What do Thérèse's \"empty hands\" teach about merit?"],act:"Read the Joint Declaration on the Doctrine of Justification (Catholic-Lutheran, 1999), § 15: \"By grace alone, in faith in Christ's saving work and not because of any merit on our part, we are accepted by God.\" Discuss what Catholics and Lutherans agreed and where they still differ."},
 saints:[["St Augustine (Sermon 169.11.13)","God created us without us, but he did not will to save us without us."],["St Thérèse of Lisieux (Act of Offering to Merciful Love)","In the evening of this life, I shall appear before you with empty hands, for I do not ask you, Lord, to count my works."],["St Joan of Arc (at her trial)","If I am not, may it please God to put me in it; if I am, may it please God to keep me there."]],
 more:[CQ("Can anyone merit the initial grace of justification?",["Yes, by good works","No; it is a free gift of God","Only saints"],1,"§ 2010."),CQ("What is sanctifying grace?",["A feeling of peace","A stable, supernatural disposition infused by the Spirit that makes us holy","An occasional help"],1,"§§ 1999–2000."),CQ("Who is called to holiness?",["Priests and religious","All Christians in every state of life","Only adults"],1,"§ 2013.")]});
cccD("c76",{paras:[["2030–2033","It is in the Church, in communion with all the baptised, that the Christian fulfils his vocation. From the Church he receives the Word of God, the grace of the sacraments, and the example of holiness. The Church is the \"pillar and bulwark of the truth\". \"To the Church belongs the right always and everywhere to announce moral principles, including those pertaining to the social order, and to make judgements on any human affairs to the extent that they are required by the fundamental rights of the human person or the salvation of souls.\" The ordinary and universal Magisterium of the Pope and bishops in communion with him teach the faithful the truth to believe, the charity to practise, the beatitude to hope for."],["2034–2040","The authority of the Magisterium extends also to the specific precepts of the natural law, because their observance is necessary for salvation. The faithful have the right to be instructed in the divine saving precepts that purify judgement and, with grace, heal wounded human reason. They have the duty of observing the constitutions and decrees of the Church. The law of God entrusted to the Church is taught as the way of life and truth; it is the work of the Spirit in pastors and faithful alike. Personal conscience and reason should not be set in opposition to the moral law or the Magisterium. A true filial spirit toward the Church can develop among Christians."],["2041–2043","The precepts of the Church are set in the context of a moral life bound to and nourished by liturgical life. Their obligatory character is meant to guarantee to the faithful the indispensable minimum in the spirit of prayer and moral effort, in the growth in love of God and neighbour. (1) You shall attend Mass on Sundays and holy days of obligation and rest from servile labour. (2) You shall confess your sins at least once a year. (3) You shall receive the sacrament of the Eucharist at least during the Easter season. (4) You shall observe the days of fasting and abstinence established by the Church. (5) You shall help to provide for the needs of the Church. (The faithful also have the duty of providing for the material needs of the Church, each according to his abilities.)"],["2044–2051","Moral life and missionary witness: the fidelity of the baptised is a primordial condition for the proclamation of the Gospel and the Church's mission in the world. The witness of Christian life and good works done in a supernatural spirit have great power to draw people to the faith and to God. Because they are members of the Body whose Head is Christ, Christians contribute to building up the Church by the constancy of their convictions and their moral lives. The Church grows, matures and becomes holy through the holiness of her faithful."]],
 mis:[["The Church has no business teaching on morality, only faith.","She has the right to announce moral principles, including social ones, and her authority extends to the natural law (§§ 2032–2036)."],["The precepts of the Church are the whole of Catholic life.","They are the \"indispensable minimum\"; the goal is growth in love of God and neighbour (§ 2041)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand the Church as mother and teacher, the role of the Magisterium in moral life, and the five precepts.",discuss:["Why does the Church speak of herself as both \"mother\" and \"teacher\"?","Which precept of the Church is hardest for people today? Why?","How does a Christian's moral life become a form of witness?"],act:"Check the site's calendar for the next holy day of obligation and fast day, and plan how your group will keep them together."},
 saints:[["St Cyprian (On the Unity of the Church 6)","No one can have God for his Father who does not have the Church for his mother."],["St Ignatius of Loyola (Spiritual Exercises, Rules for Thinking with the Church 1)","Laying aside all judgement of our own, we ought to keep our minds ready and prompt to obey… the true Spouse of Christ our Lord, our holy Mother the hierarchical Church."]],
 more:[CQ("Which is a precept of the Church?",["Pray the Rosary daily","Confess your sins at least once a year","Go on pilgrimage"],1,"§ 2042."),CQ("Why are the precepts obligatory?",["To burden the faithful","To guarantee the indispensable minimum of prayer and moral effort","To raise money"],1,"§ 2041.")]});

// ---------- Catechism notes: "Go deeper" layer, Part Three, Section Two: the Ten Commandments (§§ 2052–2557) ----------
cccD("c77",{paras:[["2052–2055","\"Teacher, what good deed must I do, to have eternal life?\" Jesus answers: \"If you would enter life, keep the commandments\", and then, \"If you would be perfect, go, sell what you possess… and come, follow me.\" The commandments are the first step on the way to freedom; following Christ fulfils them. Jesus summed them up in the twofold commandment of love: \"You shall love the Lord your God with all your heart… and your neighbour as yourself. On these two commandments depend all the law and the prophets.\" The Decalogue must be interpreted in the light of this twofold and yet single commandment of love, the fullness of the Law."],["2056–2063","\"Decalogue\" means literally \"ten words\". God revealed them to his people on the holy mountain, written \"with the finger of God\". They are found in Exodus 20 and Deuteronomy 5. The Decalogue is understood first in the context of the Exodus, God's great liberating event: \"I am the Lord your God, who brought you out of the land of Egypt, out of the house of bondage.\" The commandments express the implications of belonging to God through the covenant. Moral existence is a response to the Lord's loving initiative."],["2064–2068","In fidelity to Scripture and Jesus' example, the Tradition of the Church has acknowledged the primordial importance and significance of the Decalogue. Since St Augustine, the Ten Commandments have occupied a predominant place in catechesis. The division and numbering have varied: the present catechism follows Augustine's division, traditional in the Catholic Church and also among Lutherans; the Greek Fathers worked out a slightly different division, found in the Orthodox Churches and Reformed communities. The Council of Trent teaches that the Ten Commandments are obligatory for Christians and that the justified man is still bound to keep them."],["2069–2082","The Decalogue forms a coherent whole: each \"word\" refers to each of the others and to all of them; to transgress one commandment is to infringe all the others. The first three concern love of God, the other seven love of neighbour. The Decalogue contains a privileged expression of the natural law. The commandments are fundamentally immutable and oblige always and everywhere; their obligations are grave in themselves, though obedience can involve light matter (an abusive word is forbidden by the fifth, but would be a grave offence only as a result of circumstances or intention). What God commands he makes possible by his grace: \"Apart from me you can do nothing.\""]],
 mis:[["Protestants and Catholics have different commandments.","The content is the same Exodus 20 and Deuteronomy 5 text; only the numbering differs (Augustine's division vs. the Greek Fathers') (§ 2066)."],["Christians are no longer bound by the Ten Commandments.","Trent and the whole Tradition teach that the justified are still bound by them; Christ fulfils them (§ 2068)."],["The commandments are mainly prohibitions.","They are the response to God's liberating love, interpreted by the twofold commandment of love (§§ 2055, 2061–2062)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand the Decalogue as covenant response, its unity, its numbering, and its link to the law of love.",discuss:["Why does the Decalogue begin by recalling the Exodus (\"I brought you out…\")?","What is the difference between keeping the commandments and following Christ (Matt 19:16–21)?","Why can't you break just one commandment?"],act:"Compare Exodus 20:1–17 with Deuteronomy 5:6–21 and note differences (such as the reason for the Sabbath). Then write the commandments in Catholic and Reformed numbering side by side."},
 saints:[["St Augustine (Sermon 33.2.2)","The ten commandments are written on two tablets… the first three concern God, the seven others the neighbour."],["St Irenaeus (Against Heresies IV.15.1)","From the beginning, God had implanted in the heart of man the precepts of the natural law. Then he was content to remind him of them. This was the Decalogue."]],
 more:[CQ("Whose division of the commandments does the Catholic Church follow?",["St Jerome's","St Augustine's","The Greek Fathers'"],1,"§ 2066."),CQ("How do the commandments relate to love?",["They oppose it","They are interpreted in the light of the twofold commandment of love","They replace it"],1,"§ 2055.")]});
cccD("c78",{paras:[["2083–2094","\"I am the Lord your God… You shall have no other gods before me.\" The first commandment embraces faith, hope and charity. Faith: we must nourish and protect it with prudence and vigilance, and reject everything opposed to it. Sins against faith: voluntary doubt (disregarding or refusing to hold as true what God has revealed); incredulity; heresy (obstinate post-baptismal denial of a truth that must be believed); apostasy (total repudiation of the faith); schism (refusal of submission to the Pope or communion with the Church). Hope: sins against it are despair (ceasing to hope for salvation or forgiveness) and presumption (hoping to be saved without effort or conversion, or without God's help). Charity: sins against it are indifference, ingratitude, lukewarmness, acedia (spiritual sloth) and hatred of God."],["2095–2109","The virtue of religion disposes us to give God what is his due. Adoration is the first act of religion: acknowledging God as Creator and Saviour, Lord and Master of everything that exists. Prayer: lifting the spirit to God is an expression of adoration. Sacrifice: outward sacrifice must express spiritual sacrifice; \"the sacrifice acceptable to God is a broken spirit\". Promises and vows: a vow is a deliberate and free promise made to God concerning a possible and better good. Social duty of religion and the right to religious freedom: all are bound to seek the truth, especially concerning God and his Church, and to embrace and hold it. The right to religious liberty means that no one is to be forced to act against his convictions, nor restrained from acting in accordance with conscience in religious matters, within due limits."],["2110–2117","Superstition is the deviation of religious feeling and practices; it can affect the worship we offer the true God (attributing an importance in some way magical to certain practices, even legitimate ones). Idolatry divinises what is not God: power, pleasure, race, ancestors, the state, money. Divination: all forms are to be rejected: recourse to Satan or demons, conjuring up the dead, horoscopes, astrology, palm reading, interpretation of omens and lots, clairvoyance, mediums. Magic and sorcery, by which one attempts to tame occult powers to have supernatural power over others, are gravely contrary to the virtue of religion. Wearing charms is reprehensible. Spiritism often implies divination or magical practices."],["2118–2128","Irreligion: tempting God (putting his goodness and power to the test), sacrilege (profaning sacraments, persons, things or places consecrated to God; especially grave against the Eucharist), and simony (buying or selling spiritual things). Atheism: rejecting or denying God's existence is a sin against the virtue of religion. Believers can have more than a little to do with the rise of atheism, by neglecting their education in the faith, misrepresenting doctrine, or by failings in religious, moral or social life. Agnosticism can sometimes include a search for God, but it can also mean indifferentism or a flight from the ultimate question of existence."],["2129–2141","\"You shall not make for yourself a graven image.\" The divine injunction included the prohibition of every representation of God by the hand of man. But God's Incarnation has introduced a new \"economy\" of images. Basing itself on the Incarnation, the Seventh Ecumenical Council (Nicaea II, 787) justified the veneration of icons of Christ, the Mother of God, the angels and the saints. \"The honour rendered to an image passes to its prototype.\" The veneration of sacred images is a \"respectful veneration\", not the adoration due to God alone."]],
 mis:[["Catholics worship statues.","Images receive respectful veneration that passes to the person represented; adoration is due to God alone (§§ 2131–2132)."],["Horoscopes and fortune-telling are harmless fun.","All forms of divination are to be rejected because they seek power over time and history apart from God (§ 2116)."],["Doubt is always sinful.","Voluntary doubt that refuses revealed truth is a sin; involuntary doubt (difficulty in believing) is different (§ 2088)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand the first commandment's demands of faith, hope, charity and religion, and the sins of superstition, idolatry, divination, irreligion and atheism.",discuss:["What are the most common \"idols\" in South African culture today (money, status, sport, politics, ancestors)?","How would you respond respectfully to a friend who visits a sangoma or consults horoscopes?","How might Christians contribute to atheism, according to § 2125?"],act:"Idol audit: each person writes privately what most competes with God for first place in their time, money and thoughts, then one concrete way to put God first this week."},
 saints:[["St Basil the Great (On the Holy Spirit 18.45)","The honour rendered to an image passes to its prototype."],["St Augustine (Confessions I.1)","You have made us for yourself, O Lord, and our heart is restless until it rests in you."],["St Thomas Aquinas (ST II-II q.94 a.1)","Idolatry gives the worship due to God to a creature."]],
 more:[CQ("Which council justified the veneration of icons?",["Trent","Nicaea II (787)","Vatican I"],1,"§ 2131."),CQ("Which is a sin against hope?",["Heresy","Presumption","Simony"],1,"§ 2092."),CQ("What is simony?",["Buying or selling spiritual things","Doubting the faith","Worshipping images"],0,"§ 2121.")]});
cccD("c79",{paras:[["2142–2149","\"You shall not take the name of the Lord your God in vain.\" The second commandment prescribes respect for the Lord's name. Like the first, it belongs to the virtue of religion and more particularly governs our use of speech in sacred matters. Among all the words of revelation, there is one which is unique: the revealed name of God. God confides his name to those who believe in him; he reveals himself to them in his personal mystery. The gift of a name belongs to the order of trust and intimacy. The sense of the sacred is part of the virtue of religion. Promises made to others in God's name engage the divine honour, fidelity, truthfulness and authority. Blasphemy is directly opposed to the second commandment: uttering against God, inwardly or outwardly, words of hatred, reproach or defiance; speaking ill of God; failing in respect toward him in speech; misusing God's name. It extends to language against the Church, the saints and sacred things. It is in itself a grave sin. Oaths which misuse God's name, though without the intention of blasphemy, show lack of respect for the Lord."],["2150–2155","Taking the name of God in vain: the second commandment forbids false oaths. Taking an oath or swearing is to take God as witness to what one affirms. Perjury is making a promise under oath with no intention of keeping it, or failing to keep a promise made under oath. Following St Paul, the Church has understood Jesus' words (\"Do not swear at all\") not to exclude oaths made for grave and right reasons, for example in court. Oaths may be refused when required by illegitimate civil authority or when contrary to human dignity."],["2156–2167","The Christian name: the sacrament of Baptism is conferred \"in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit\". In Baptism, the Lord's name sanctifies man, and the Christian receives his name in the Church. This can be the name of a saint, a disciple who has lived a life of exemplary fidelity to the Lord; the patron saint provides a model of charity and the assurance of his prayer. The \"baptismal name\" can also express a Christian mystery or virtue. The Christian begins his day, prayers and activities with the Sign of the Cross. God calls each one by name. Everyone's name is sacred. The name is the icon of the person. In the Kingdom, the mysterious and unique character of each person marked with God's name will shine forth in splendour."]],
 mis:[["The second commandment is only about swearing (bad language).","Its heart is reverence for God's name; it forbids blasphemy, false oaths and perjury, and casual misuse of his name (§§ 2142–2155)."],["Christians may never take an oath.","Oaths for grave and just reasons, such as testimony in court, are permitted (§ 2154)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants grow in reverence for God's name, understand blasphemy, oaths and perjury, and value their own Christian name.",discuss:["Why is it significant that God revealed his name (Exod 3:14)?","Why might \"OMG\" or using Jesus' name as an exclamation matter?","What does your baptismal or Confirmation name mean, and who is your patron?"],act:"Learn and pray the Divine Praises together (\"Blessed be God. Blessed be his holy Name…\"), traditionally said in reparation for blasphemy."},
 saints:[["St Augustine (Sermon on the Mount II.5.19)","\"Hallowed be thy name\" is asked not as if God's name were not holy, but that it may be held holy by men."]],
 more:[CQ("What is perjury?",["Swearing in court","Promising under oath with no intention of keeping it, or breaking such a promise","Using bad language"],1,"§ 2152."),CQ("Why does the Church give saints' names at Baptism?",["Tradition only","The saint offers a model of charity and the assurance of prayer","For legal reasons"],1,"§ 2156.")]});
cccD("c80",{paras:[["2168–2173","\"Remember the sabbath day, to keep it holy.\" Scripture recalls creation: \"in six days the Lord made heaven and earth… and rested the seventh day.\" It also recalls Israel's liberation: the sabbath is a memorial of deliverance from bondage in Egypt. God entrusted the sabbath to Israel as a sign of the irrevocable covenant. Rest is commanded so that humans, like God, can be refreshed. Jesus never failed to respect the holiness of this day; he gives it its authoritative interpretation: \"The sabbath was made for man, not man for the sabbath.\" The Son of Man is \"lord even of the sabbath\"."],["2174–2178","The Lord's Day: Jesus rose from the dead \"on the first day of the week\". Because it is the \"first day\", the day of Christ's Resurrection recalls the first creation; because it is the \"eighth day\" following the sabbath, it symbolises the new creation. For Christians it has become the first of all days, the first of all feasts: the Lord's Day (Dominica), Sunday. Sunday is distinguished from the sabbath, which it follows chronologically every week; for Christians its ceremonial observance replaces that of the sabbath. In Christ's Passover, Sunday fulfils the spiritual truth of the Jewish sabbath and announces man's eternal rest in God."],["2179–2188","The Sunday Eucharist: the Sunday celebration of the Lord's Day and his Eucharist is at the heart of the Church's life. \"Sunday is the day on which the paschal mystery is celebrated in light of the apostolic tradition and is to be observed as the foremost holy day of obligation.\" The parish is the place where all the faithful are gathered for the Sunday Eucharist. The Sunday obligation: \"On Sundays and other holy days of obligation the faithful are bound to participate in the Mass.\" The obligation is satisfied by attending Mass celebrated in a Catholic rite either on the holy day or on the evening of the preceding day. Those who deliberately fail in this obligation commit a grave sin, unless excused for a serious reason (illness, the care of infants) or dispensed by their own pastor. A day of grace and rest from work: Sunday is to be devoted to God, family, culture, the sick and the elderly, with rest from work that hinders worship, the joy proper to the Lord's Day, works of mercy, and appropriate relaxation of mind and body. Every Christian should avoid making unnecessary demands on others that would hinder them from observing the Lord's Day. Christians should seek recognition of Sundays and holy days as legal holidays."]],
 mis:[["Christians should keep Saturday, since that's the sabbath.","Sunday, the day of the Resurrection, fulfils the sabbath and replaces its ceremonial observance for Christians (§§ 2175–2176)."],["Missing Sunday Mass is a minor matter.","Deliberately failing the obligation without serious reason is a grave sin (§ 2181)."],["Rest on Sunday is optional; productivity matters more.","Rest from work that hinders worship and family is part of the commandment (§§ 2184–2187)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand the sabbath, why Sunday is the Lord's Day, the Sunday obligation, and Sunday as a day of rest and joy.",discuss:["How does the double meaning of Sunday (\"first day\" and \"eighth day\") help you?","What makes Sunday hard to keep holy in your life (sport, work, study)?","What could make Sunday a day of real joy and rest in your home?"],act:"Sunday plan: design one Sunday this month with Mass, a family meal, a visit to someone lonely or sick, and genuine rest from work and screens. Report back next session."},
 saints:[["St Ignatius of Antioch (Letter to the Magnesians 9.1)","Those who lived according to the old order have come to a new hope, no longer keeping the sabbath, but the Lord's Day, on which our life was raised up through him."],["St John Paul II (Dies Domini 7, 1998)","Do not be afraid to give your time to Christ!"],["Martyrs of Abitina (304, Acts of the Martyrs)","Without the Lord's Day we cannot live."]],
 more:[CQ("Why is Sunday called the \"eighth day\"?",["It is the last day of the week","It follows the sabbath and symbolises the new creation","It is eight days after Easter"],1,"§ 2174."),CQ("When can the Sunday obligation be fulfilled?",["Any day of the week","On Sunday or the evening of Saturday","Only Sunday morning"],1,"§ 2180.")],psyNote:"Regular rest and \"detachment\" from work are robustly linked to wellbeing: meta-analytic work on psychological detachment shows associations with lower exhaustion and better life satisfaction (Wendsche & Lohmann-Haislah 2017, Frontiers in Psychology). In the Nurses' Health Study, frequent religious-service attendance was prospectively associated with lower depression (Li, Okereke et al. 2016, JAMA Psychiatry) and lower all-cause mortality (Li, Stampfer et al. 2016, JAMA Internal Medicine), though such studies are observational."});
cccD("c81",{paras:[["2196–2200","\"Honour your father and your mother, that your days may be long.\" The fourth commandment opens the second tablet. It shows us the order of charity: God willed that, after him, we should honour our parents to whom we owe life. It is addressed expressly to children in relation to their parents, but also concerns relationships within the family, with elders, teachers, leaders, magistrates and those who govern. Observing it brings spiritual and temporal fruits of peace and prosperity."],["2201–2206","The family in God's plan: a man and a woman united in marriage, together with their children, form a family. This institution is prior to any recognition by public authority, which has an obligation to recognise it. The Christian family is a communion of persons, a sign and image of the communion of the Father and the Son in the Holy Spirit. It is called a domestic church. Family relationships entail particular affinity of feelings, affections and interests, arising above all from the members' respect for one another."],["2207–2213","The family and society: the family is the original cell of social life. Authority, stability and a life of relationships within the family constitute the foundations for freedom, security and fraternity within society. The family should live in such a way that its members learn to care and take responsibility for the young, the old, the sick, the handicapped and the poor. Where families cannot do this, other persons, families and society must provide for their needs. The political community has a duty to honour the family, to assist it, and to ensure: freedom to establish a family and raise children according to one's moral and religious convictions; protection of marriage; freedom to profess one's faith and pass it on; the right to private property, free enterprise, work and housing; medical care, assistance for the aged and family benefits; protection of security and health; freedom to form associations."],["2214–2220","Duties of children: filial respect (piety) is shown by true docility and obedience. As long as a child lives at home, he should obey his parents in all that they ask of him when it is for his good or that of the family. Grown children should give their parents material and moral support in old age and in times of illness, loneliness or distress. Respect for parents fosters harmony in all family life and among siblings. Christians owe special gratitude to those from whom they received the gift of faith."],["2221–2233","Duties of parents: the fecundity of conjugal love is not restricted solely to procreation but extends to children's moral education and spiritual formation. Parents have the first responsibility for the education of their children: creating a home where tenderness, forgiveness, respect, fidelity and disinterested service are the rule; educating in the virtues; giving good example; evangelising them; choosing schools that correspond to their convictions. When children become adults, they have the right and duty to choose their profession and state of life; parents should not exert pressure in choosing a profession or spouse. Family ties are important but not absolute: \"He who loves father or mother more than me is not worthy of me.\""],["2234–2246","Authorities in civil society: those who exercise authority should do so as a service, respecting fundamental human rights, and making a hierarchy of values that facilitates freedom and responsibility for all. Citizens' duties: to contribute to the good of society in a spirit of truth, justice, solidarity and freedom; to pay taxes, exercise the right to vote, defend one's country; to love and serve one's homeland. The rich nations are obliged to welcome the foreigner in search of security and livelihood, insofar as they are able; immigrants are obliged to respect the material and spiritual heritage of the host country. The citizen is obliged in conscience not to follow the directives of civil authorities when they are contrary to the moral order, the fundamental rights of persons or the teachings of the Gospel: \"We must obey God rather than men.\" Armed resistance to oppression is legitimate only under strict conditions."]],
 mis:[["The fourth commandment only applies to young children.","It concerns adult children (support in old age), siblings, and all relationships to legitimate authority (§§ 2199, 2218)."],["Honouring parents means obeying them in everything, even sin.","Children obey in what is for their good; adults choose their own state of life, and no one may obey commands contrary to the moral law (§§ 2217, 2230, 2242)."],["Family ties are absolute.","They are important but subordinate to the call of Christ (§ 2232)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand the family in God's plan, duties of children and parents, and the duties of authorities and citizens.",discuss:["What does honouring your parents look like at your current stage of life?","How can someone honour a parent who has hurt them deeply, without denying the harm?","Which civic duty in § 2240 do young South Africans most neglect?"],act:"Write a letter (sent or unsent) to a parent, grandparent or mentor, thanking them for one specific gift they gave you, especially the gift of faith."},
 saints:[["Sirach 3:12–13","My son, help your father in his old age, and do not grieve him as long as he lives; even if he is lacking in understanding, show forbearance."],["St John Chrysostom (Homily on Ephesians 21)","If we would have our children obey us, let us bring them up in the discipline and instruction of the Lord."]],
 more:[CQ("Who has the first responsibility for children's education?",["The state","Parents","Schools"],1,"§ 2223."),CQ("When may a citizen refuse to obey civil authority?",["Never","When directives are contrary to the moral order, human rights or the Gospel","When he disagrees with a policy"],1,"§ 2242.")],psyNote:"Honour does not require denying abuse or harm. Attachment theory (Bowlby; Ainsworth) shows how early caregiving shapes later relationships, and therapy for adults with painful family histories often works toward honest acknowledgement, boundaries and, where possible, forgiveness: none of which conflicts with the fourth commandment. Forgiveness interventions show moderate effects on wellbeing (Wade et al. 2014, Journal of Consulting and Clinical Psychology, meta-analysis)."});
cccD("c82",{paras:[["2258–2262","\"Human life is sacred because from its beginning it involves the creative action of God and it remains for ever in a special relationship with the Creator… God alone is the Lord of life from its beginning until its end: no one can under any circumstance claim for himself the right directly to destroy an innocent human being.\" Scripture tells of Cain's murder of Abel, showing the presence of anger and envy in man from the beginning. The covenant with Noah: \"Whoever sheds the blood of man, by man shall his blood be shed; for God made man in his own image.\" Jesus recalls \"You shall not kill\" and adds the prohibition of anger, hatred and vengeance, asking his disciples to love their enemies."],["2263–2267","Legitimate defence: the legitimate defence of persons and societies is not an exception to the prohibition of murder; it has a double effect: the preservation of one's own life, and the killing of the aggressor. \"The one is intended, the other is not\" (Aquinas). Love toward oneself remains a fundamental principle; legitimate defence can be not only a right but a grave duty for one responsible for the lives of others. The death penalty: in 2018 Pope Francis revised § 2267: \"the Church teaches, in the light of the Gospel, that 'the death penalty is inadmissible because it is an attack on the inviolability and dignity of the person', and she works with determination for its abolition worldwide.\""],["2268–2269","Intentional homicide: the fifth commandment forbids direct and intentional killing as gravely sinful. Infanticide, fratricide, parricide and the murder of a spouse are especially grave. It forbids doing anything with the intention of indirectly bringing about a person's death, and exposing someone to mortal danger without grave reason, or refusing assistance to a person in danger. Unintentional killing is not morally imputable, but one is not exonerated from grave offence if, without proportionate reasons, one has acted in a way that brings about someone's death."],["2270–2275","Abortion: human life must be respected and protected absolutely from the moment of conception. From the first moment of his existence, a human being must be recognised as having the rights of a person, among which is the inviolable right of every innocent being to life. Since the first century the Church has affirmed the moral evil of every procured abortion: \"You shall not kill the embryo by abortion\" (Didache). Direct abortion, willed either as an end or a means, is gravely contrary to the moral law; formal cooperation in abortion incurs excommunication (latae sententiae). The inalienable right to life of every innocent individual is a constitutive element of civil society. The embryo must be treated from conception as a person; prenatal diagnosis is permissible when it respects life and is directed toward safeguarding or healing; producing embryos as disposable biological material is immoral."],["2276–2279","Euthanasia: those whose lives are diminished or weakened deserve special respect; sick or handicapped persons should be helped to lead lives as normal as possible. Whatever its motives and means, direct euthanasia (putting an end to the lives of handicapped, sick or dying persons) is morally unacceptable. Discontinuing medical procedures that are burdensome, dangerous, extraordinary or disproportionate to the expected outcome can be legitimate: it is the refusal of \"over-zealous\" treatment. The decisions should be made by the patient if competent, or by those legally entitled. Ordinary care owed to a sick person cannot be legitimately interrupted. Painkillers to alleviate the sufferings of the dying, even at the risk of shortening their days, can be morally in conformity with human dignity if death is not willed. Palliative care is a special form of disinterested charity."],["2280–2283","Suicide: everyone is responsible for his life before God who has given it. We are stewards, not owners. Suicide contradicts the natural inclination to preserve life and is gravely contrary to the just love of self, of neighbour and of God. Voluntary cooperation in suicide is contrary to the moral law. \"Grave psychological disturbances, anguish, or grave fear of hardship, suffering, or torture can diminish the responsibility of the one committing suicide.\" \"We should not despair of the eternal salvation of persons who have taken their own lives. By ways known to him alone, God can provide the opportunity for salutary repentance. The Church prays for persons who have taken their own lives.\""]],
 mis:[["The Church teaches that everyone who dies by suicide goes to hell.","Psychological disturbance and anguish can diminish responsibility; we must not despair of their salvation, and the Church prays for them (§§ 2282–2283)."],["Refusing aggressive treatment at the end of life is euthanasia.","Discontinuing burdensome or disproportionate treatment can be legitimate; what is forbidden is intending death (§ 2278)."],["The Catechism still allows the death penalty.","Since 2018, § 2267 teaches that the death penalty is inadmissible (§ 2267, revised)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand the sanctity of life, legitimate defence, the revised teaching on the death penalty, abortion, euthanasia and suicide, with truth and compassion. Handle this session gently: some participants may have personal experience of abortion, suicide loss or end-of-life decisions.",discuss:["What does it mean that we are stewards, not owners, of our lives?","How would you explain the difference between letting die and killing (§ 2278)?","How can a parish support women in crisis pregnancies, and people grieving a suicide or an abortion?"],act:"Invite someone from a local pregnancy-care centre, hospice or bereavement ministry to speak about how they serve life practically. Share local help numbers with everyone (in South Africa, SADAG: 0800 567 567)."},
 saints:[["Didache 2.2 (1st century)","You shall not kill the embryo by abortion, nor cause the newborn to perish."],["St Thomas Aquinas (ST II-II q.64 a.7)","Nothing hinders one act from having two effects, only one of which is intended… the act of self-defence may have two effects: the saving of one's life and the slaying of the aggressor."],["St Teresa of Calcutta (attributed)","Love begins by taking care of the closest ones, the ones at home."]],
 more:[CQ("What does the revised § 2267 (2018) teach about the death penalty?",["It is always permitted","It is inadmissible, and the Church works for its abolition","It is required for murder"],1,"§ 2267."),CQ("Is giving painkillers that may shorten life always wrong?",["Yes","No, if death is not willed and the aim is to relieve suffering","Only if the patient asks"],1,"§ 2279."),CQ("What does the Church say about those who die by suicide?",["They are certainly lost","We should not despair of their salvation, and the Church prays for them","Nothing"],1,"§ 2283.")],psyNote:"§ 2282 recognises what psychology confirms: most people who die by suicide are suffering from a mental disorder, most often depression, and acute anguish narrows perception of options. Suicide is preventable; asking directly about suicidal thoughts does not increase risk (Dazzi et al. 2014, Psychological Medicine, systematic review). If you or someone you know is struggling, in South Africa contact the SADAG suicide crisis line, 0800 567 567, or speak to a doctor or priest."});
cccD("c83",{paras:[["2284–2287","Respect for the souls of others: scandal is an attitude or behaviour which leads another to do evil. The person who gives scandal becomes his neighbour's tempter. Scandal is grave when given by those who by nature or office are obliged to teach and educate others. \"Whoever causes one of these little ones who believe in me to sin, it would be better for him to have a great millstone fastened round his neck and to be drowned.\" Scandal can be provoked by laws or institutions, by fashion or opinion."],["2288–2291","Respect for health: life and physical health are precious gifts entrusted to us by God. We must take reasonable care of them, taking into account the needs of others and the common good. Society should help attain living conditions that allow people to grow and reach maturity: food and clothing, housing, health care, basic education, employment and social assistance. The virtue of temperance disposes us to avoid every kind of excess: the abuse of food, alcohol, tobacco or medicine. Those incur grave guilt who, by drunkenness or a love of speed, endanger their own and others' safety on the road, at sea or in the air. The use of drugs inflicts very grave damage on human health and life; except on strictly therapeutic grounds, it is a grave offence. Clandestine production of and trafficking in drugs are scandalous practices."],["2292–2301","Respect for the person and scientific research: scientific, medical or psychological experiments on human individuals or groups can contribute to healing the sick and advancing public health, but research must respect the person and the moral law. Research or experimentation on the human being cannot legitimate acts contrary to the dignity of persons; free and informed consent is required. Organ transplants are in conformity with the moral law if the physical and psychological dangers and risks to the donor are proportionate to the good sought; organ donation after death is a noble and meritorious act. Respect for bodily integrity: kidnapping and hostage-taking, terrorism, and torture are gravely against justice and charity. Except when performed for strictly therapeutic medical reasons, directly intended amputations, mutilations and sterilisations of innocent persons are against the moral law. Respect for the dead: the bodies of the dead must be treated with respect and charity, in faith and hope of the Resurrection; cremation is permitted provided it does not demonstrate a denial of faith in the resurrection of the body."],["2302–2317","Safeguarding peace: Jesus recalls \"You shall not kill\" and forbids anger, which is a desire for revenge. To desire vengeance in order to do evil to someone is illicit, but it is praiseworthy to impose restitution \"to correct vices and maintain justice\". Deliberate hatred is contrary to charity. Peace is not merely the absence of war; it is \"the tranquillity of order\" (Augustine), the work of justice and the effect of charity. Earthly peace is the image and fruit of the peace of Christ. Avoiding war: all citizens and governments are obliged to work for the avoidance of war. The strict conditions for legitimate defence by military force (\"just war\" doctrine): the damage inflicted by the aggressor must be lasting, grave and certain; all other means must have been shown impractical or ineffective; there must be serious prospects of success; the use of arms must not produce evils graver than the evil to be eliminated. The evaluation belongs to the prudential judgement of those responsible for the common good. Conscientious objectors should be provided for. The moral law remains valid during armed conflict; non-combatants, wounded soldiers and prisoners must be respected; genocide and indiscriminate destruction of cities are crimes against God and man. The arms race does not ensure peace; injustice, excessive economic or social inequalities, envy, distrust and pride threaten peace and cause wars."]],
 mis:[["The fifth commandment only forbids murder.","It also covers scandal, health, drugs, research ethics, bodily integrity, respect for the dead, anger, hatred and war (§§ 2284–2317)."],["Catholics may not be cremated.","Cremation is permitted if it does not express denial of the resurrection; ashes should be kept in a sacred place (§ 2301)."],["Peace simply means no war.","Peace is the tranquillity of order, the work of justice and the effect of charity (§ 2304)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants grasp the wide scope of the fifth commandment: scandal, health and addiction, research ethics, bodily integrity, anger and peace, and the just war conditions.",discuss:["How can social media be a source of scandal (leading others into sin)?","What does temperance look like with alcohol, food and screens in student life?","Apply the just war conditions (§ 2309) to a current or historical conflict."],act:"Peace practice: each person identifies one relationship where anger or resentment lingers and takes one concrete step toward peace this week (a prayer, a message, an apology)."},
 saints:[["St Augustine (City of God XIX.13)","Peace is the tranquillity of order."],["St Francis of Assisi (attributed, Peace Prayer)","Lord, make me an instrument of your peace."],["St Thomas Aquinas (ST II-II q.158 a.8, citing St John Chrysostom)","He who is not angry when he has cause to be, sins… for unreasonable patience is the hotbed of many vices."]],
 more:[CQ("What is scandal?",["A public embarrassment","Behaviour that leads another to do evil","Gossip"],1,"§ 2284."),CQ("Which is a condition of legitimate defence by military force?",["Any provocation","All other means have been shown impractical or ineffective","The enemy is a different religion"],1,"§ 2309."),CQ("Is cremation permitted?",["Never","Yes, if it does not express denial of the resurrection","Only for priests"],1,"§ 2301.")],psyNote:"§ 2291's concern about drugs fits the evidence: substance-use disorders are among the leading causes of disability worldwide. Temperance has a psychological analogue in self-regulation research. Treatment works: for alcohol-use disorder, medications such as naltrexone and acamprosate have meta-analytic support (Jonas et al. 2014, JAMA), as do psychosocial treatments such as CBT and motivational interviewing. In South Africa, SANCA offers treatment and support."});
cccD("c84",{paras:[["2331–2336","\"God is love and in himself he lives a mystery of personal loving communion. Creating the human race in his own image… God inscribed in the humanity of man and woman the vocation, and thus the capacity and responsibility, of love and communion.\" Sexuality affects all aspects of the human person in the unity of body and soul: especially the capacity to love and to procreate, and the aptitude for forming bonds of communion with others. Everyone, man and woman, should acknowledge and accept his sexual identity. Physical, moral and spiritual difference and complementarity are oriented toward the goods of marriage and the flourishing of family life. Man and woman are equal in dignity. Jesus came to restore creation to the purity of its origins; in the Sermon on the Mount he interprets God's plan strictly: \"Everyone who looks at a woman lustfully has already committed adultery with her in his heart.\""],["2337–2345","The vocation to chastity: chastity means the successful integration of sexuality within the person and thus the inner unity of man in his bodily and spiritual being. The chaste person maintains the integrity of the powers of life and love placed in him. Chastity includes an apprenticeship in self-mastery, which is a training in human freedom: \"Either man governs his passions and finds peace, or he lets himself be dominated by them and becomes unhappy.\" It requires self-knowledge, ascesis adapted to the situations one faces, obedience to God's commandments, the moral virtues, and prayer. Self-mastery is a long and exacting work, never acquired once and for all; it has laws of growth which progress through stages marked by imperfection and too often by sin. Chastity is a moral virtue, a gift from God, a grace, a fruit of spiritual effort. It blossoms in friendship, and shows the disciple how to follow and imitate Christ."],["2346–2350","Chastity is lived in various forms: all the baptised are called to it, according to their state of life: virginity or consecrated celibacy, married chastity, and the chastity of those who are engaged or single, living continence. \"There are three forms of the virtue of chastity: the first is that of spouses, the second that of widows, and the third that of virgins\" (Ambrose). Those who are engaged to marry are called to live chastity in continence, reserving for marriage the expressions of affection that belong to married love."],["2351–2356","Offences against chastity: lust is disordered desire for or inordinate enjoyment of sexual pleasure, sought for itself, isolated from its procreative and unitive purposes. Masturbation is an intrinsically and gravely disordered action; to form an equitable judgement about subjects' moral responsibility, one must take into account affective immaturity, force of acquired habit, conditions of anxiety or other psychological or social factors that lessen or even extenuate moral culpability. Fornication is carnal union between an unmarried man and an unmarried woman, gravely contrary to the dignity of persons and of human sexuality. Pornography perverts the conjugal act, does grave injury to the dignity of its participants (actors, vendors, the public), and immerses all in the illusion of a fantasy world; it is a grave offence, and civil authorities should prevent its production and distribution. Prostitution does injury to the dignity of the person who engages in it; the imputability of the offence can be attenuated by destitution, blackmail or social pressure. Rape is the forcible violation of the sexual intimacy of another person; it is an intrinsically evil act, and gravely so when committed against children by parents or educators."],["2357–2359","Chastity and homosexuality: homosexuality refers to relations between men or between women who experience an exclusive or predominant sexual attraction toward persons of the same sex. Its psychological genesis remains largely unexplained. Tradition has always declared that homosexual acts are intrinsically disordered: they close the sexual act to the gift of life and do not proceed from genuine affective and sexual complementarity. The number of men and women who have deep-seated homosexual tendencies is not negligible; this inclination constitutes for most of them a trial. They must be accepted with respect, compassion and sensitivity. Every sign of unjust discrimination in their regard should be avoided. They are called to fulfil God's will in their lives and, if Christians, to unite to the sacrifice of the Lord's Cross the difficulties they may encounter. Homosexual persons are called to chastity. By the virtues of self-mastery, by the support of disinterested friendship, by prayer and sacramental grace, they can and should gradually and resolutely approach Christian perfection."]],
 mis:[["The Church thinks sex is bad or dirty.","Sexuality is part of being made in God's image, ordered to love and communion; chastity integrates it, not suppresses it (§§ 2331–2337)."],["Chastity is only for unmarried people.","All the baptised are called to chastity according to their state, including married couples (§ 2348–2349)."],["The Church hates gay people.","Persons with same-sex attraction must be accepted with respect, compassion and sensitivity, and all unjust discrimination avoided; the teaching concerns acts, not persons' worth (§ 2358)."],["Anyone who struggles with a sexual habit is fully culpable every time.","Immaturity, habit, anxiety and other factors can lessen culpability (§ 2352)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand sexuality as part of the vocation to love, chastity as integration and freedom, its forms, and the offences against it, presented with truth and pastoral gentleness. Many participants carry wounds or struggles here; avoid shaming language.",discuss:["Why does the Catechism describe chastity as \"integration\" and as \"freedom\"?","How does pornography harm the viewer, the people filmed, and relationships (§ 2354)?","What would make our parish a place where people with same-sex attraction feel genuinely respected and welcomed (§ 2358)?"],act:"Practical chastity plan: discuss concrete helps (accountability friends, phone filters, avoiding near occasions, regular Confession, prayer, healthy friendships, exercise, sleep). Each person chooses one."},
 saints:[["St Augustine (Confessions X.29.40)","Through chastity we are gathered together and led back to the unity from which we were fragmented into multiplicity."],["St Ambrose (On Widows 23)","There are three forms of the virtue of chastity: that of spouses, that of widows, and that of virgins."],["St John Paul II (Love and Responsibility, 1960, paraphrased)","Only the chaste man and the chaste woman are capable of true love."]],
 more:[CQ("What is chastity, according to § 2337?",["Not having sex","The successful integration of sexuality within the person","Avoiding marriage"],1,"§ 2337."),CQ("What factors can lessen culpability for masturbation?",["None","Affective immaturity, habit, anxiety and other psychological or social factors","Only age"],1,"§ 2352."),CQ("How must persons with homosexual tendencies be treated?",["With suspicion","With respect, compassion and sensitivity; no unjust discrimination","They should be excluded"],1,"§ 2358.")],psyNote:"Self-regulation and habit research is directly relevant: compulsive behaviours are better changed by managing cues and environment, building replacement habits and addressing underlying stress, loneliness or anxiety than by shame. Shame is associated with more, not less, compulsive behaviour. Compulsive sexual behaviour disorder is recognised in ICD-11 (6C72). For someone stuck in a compulsive pattern, a therapist who respects his faith, a confessor and accountability can work together."});
cccD("c85",{paras:[["2360–2365","Sexuality is ordered to the conjugal love of man and woman. In marriage the physical intimacy of the spouses becomes a sign and pledge of spiritual communion. \"Sexuality… is realised in a truly human way only if it is an integral part of the love by which a man and woman commit themselves totally to one another until death.\" Tobias and Sarah's prayer expresses this. The spouses' union achieves the twofold end of marriage: the good of the spouses themselves and the transmission of life. These two meanings or values cannot be separated without altering the couple's spiritual life and compromising the goods of marriage and the future of the family. Fidelity expresses constancy in keeping one's given word; God is faithful."],["2366–2372","The fecundity of marriage: fecundity is a gift, an end of marriage, for conjugal love naturally tends to be fruitful. A child does not come from outside as something added on to mutual love; he springs from the very heart of that mutual giving. \"Each and every marriage act must remain open to the transmission of life\" (Humanae Vitae 11). Responsible parenthood: for just reasons, spouses may wish to space the births of their children; it is their duty to make certain that their desire is not motivated by selfishness but is in conformity with the generosity appropriate to responsible parenthood. Periodic continence, methods of birth regulation based on self-observation and the use of infertile periods (natural family planning), is in conformity with the objective criteria of morality. \"Every action which, whether in anticipation of the conjugal act, or in its accomplishment, or in the development of its natural consequences, proposes, whether as an end or as a means, to render procreation impossible\" is intrinsically evil. The state may legitimately inform and take measures to regulate population, but not by authoritarian or coercive means."],["2373–2379","The gift of a child: Scripture and the Church's practice see in large families a sign of God's blessing and the parents' generosity. Couples who discover they are sterile suffer greatly. Research aimed at reducing human sterility is to be encouraged on condition it is placed at the service of the human person. Techniques that entail the dissociation of husband and wife by the intrusion of a third person (donor sperm or egg, surrogate uterus) are gravely immoral. Techniques involving only the married couple (homologous IVF) are perhaps less reprehensible, yet remain morally unacceptable, because they dissociate the sexual act from the procreative act and entrust life and identity to the power of doctors and biologists. A child is not something owed to one, but is a gift. Couples who cannot have children can unite themselves with the Lord's Cross, give expression to their generosity by adopting abandoned children, or perform demanding services for others."],["2380–2391","Offences against the dignity of marriage: adultery is marital infidelity; it is an injustice and a grave sin. Divorce is a grave offence against the natural law: it claims to break the contract to which the spouses freely consented to live with each other till death. The separation of spouses while maintaining the marriage bond can be legitimate in certain cases provided for by canon law; if civil divorce remains the only possible way of ensuring certain legal rights, the care of children or the protection of inheritance, it can be tolerated and does not constitute a moral offence. It can happen that one spouse is the innocent victim of a divorce decreed by civil law; this spouse has not contravened the moral law. Polygamy is not in accord with the moral law; a polygamist who becomes Christian has a grave duty in justice to honour obligations contracted toward former wives and children. Incest, and sexual abuse perpetrated by adults on children or adolescents entrusted to their care, are gravely evil. Free union and \"trial marriage\" are contrary to the dignity of marriage."]],
 mis:[["The Church wants couples to have as many children as possible.","Spouses may, for just reasons, responsibly space births, using natural methods; generosity, not maximum numbers, is asked (§§ 2368–2370)."],["Natural family planning is just Catholic contraception.","NFP works with the body's natural cycle and keeps each act open to life; contraception renders procreation impossible (§ 2370)."],["A divorced Catholic is automatically excommunicated.","Divorce itself does not excommunicate; an innocent spouse may not have sinned at all; the difficulty arises with remarriage without annulment (§§ 2383–2386, 1650)."],["Infertile couples are second-class.","The Church encourages research to heal infertility and honours their many forms of fruitfulness, including adoption (§§ 2375, 2379)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand the unitive and procreative meanings of marriage, responsible parenthood and NFP, the gift of a child, and offences against marriage, with sensitivity to those who are divorced or infertile.",discuss:["Why does the Church hold that the unitive and procreative meanings must not be separated?","What does \"responsible parenthood\" mean in practice for a young South African couple?","How can a parish support couples facing infertility, or families affected by divorce?"],act:"Invite a married couple who teach natural family planning (for example the Billings or Creighton method, or a local Catholic family-life office) to explain how it works and why they chose it."},
 saints:[["Tobit 8:7","Grant that she and I may find mercy, and that we may grow old together."],["St Paul VI (Humanae Vitae 9, 1968)","This love is fully human… total… faithful and exclusive until death… and fecund."],["Sts Louis and Zélie Martin (letter of St Zélie, paraphrased)","When we had our children, our ideas changed: we lived only for them; it was our happiness."]],
 more:[CQ("What are the two inseparable meanings of the conjugal act?",["Pleasure and duty","Unitive and procreative","Social and legal"],1,"§§ 2363, 2366."),CQ("Is periodic continence (NFP) morally acceptable?",["No","Yes, for just reasons, in conformity with morality","Only after many children"],1,"§ 2370."),CQ("Is an innocent spouse in a civil divorce guilty of sin?",["Yes","No","Only if they remarry immediately"],1,"§ 2386.")]});
cccD("c86",{paras:[["2401–2406","\"You shall not steal.\" The seventh commandment forbids unjustly taking or keeping the goods of one's neighbour and wronging him in any way with respect to his goods. It commands justice and charity in the care of earthly goods and the fruits of men's labour. The universal destination of goods: in the beginning God entrusted the earth and its resources to the common stewardship of mankind. The appropriation of property is legitimate for guaranteeing freedom and dignity, but the right to private property does not do away with the original gift of the earth to the whole of mankind. \"Man should regard the external things that he legitimately owns not merely as exclusive to himself but common to others also\". Political authority has the right and duty to regulate the legitimate exercise of the right to ownership for the sake of the common good."],["2407–2418","Respect for persons and their goods requires temperance (moderating attachment to this world's goods), justice (preserving our neighbour's rights) and solidarity. Theft is usurping another's property against the reasonable will of the owner; there is no theft if consent can be presumed or if refusal is contrary to reason (urgent and essential need). Also contrary: deliberately keeping goods lent or lost; business fraud; paying unjust wages; forcing up prices by taking advantage of ignorance or hardship; corruption; appropriation of public goods; work poorly done; tax evasion; forgery of cheques and invoices; excessive expenses and waste; wilfully damaging property. Promises and contracts must be kept. Commutative justice obliges strictly and requires reparation; every injustice demands reparation (restitution). Games of chance and bets are not in themselves contrary to justice, but become morally unacceptable when they deprive someone of what is necessary to meet his needs and those of others; the passion for gambling risks becoming an enslavement. Enslaving human beings, buying and selling them as merchandise, is a sin against their dignity. Respect for the integrity of creation: animals, plants and inanimate beings are destined for the common good of past, present and future humanity. Animals are God's creatures and should be treated with kindness; it is contrary to human dignity to cause them to suffer or die needlessly, though it is legitimate to use them for food, clothing and medical research within reasonable limits."],["2419–2449","The social doctrine of the Church: the Church makes a moral judgement about economic and social matters when the fundamental rights of the person or the salvation of souls requires it. The economy is for man; it must be ordered to the service of persons and of the whole human community. The Church has rejected the totalitarian and atheistic ideologies associated with communism and socialism; she has also refused to accept, in the practice of capitalism, individualism and the absolute primacy of the law of the marketplace over human labour. Human work proceeds directly from persons created in God's image; work honours the Creator's gifts, and can be a means of sanctification. Everyone should be able to draw from work the means of providing for his life and that of his family. Access to employment must be open to all without unjust discrimination. A just wage is the legitimate fruit of work; refusing or withholding it can be a grave injustice. Recourse to a strike is morally legitimate when it cannot be avoided and is aimed at a proportionate benefit. Justice and solidarity among nations: rich nations have a grave moral responsibility toward those unable to ensure their own development. Love for the poor: the Church's love for the poor is part of her constant tradition; it inspires the corporal and spiritual works of mercy. \"Not to enable the poor to share in our goods is to steal from them\" (Chrysostom). \"When we attend to the needs of those in want, we give them what is theirs, not ours\" (Gregory the Great)."]],
 mis:[["The seventh commandment only forbids shoplifting and burglary.","It also covers wage theft, fraud, corruption, tax evasion, poor work, waste, broken contracts, and care for creation (§§ 2409–2418)."],["Catholic social teaching is socialist (or capitalist).","The Church rejects totalitarian socialism and also the absolute primacy of the market; the economy must serve the person (§ 2425)."],["Helping the poor is optional generosity.","Love for the poor is a matter of justice as well as charity: we give them what is theirs (§ 2446)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand the universal destination of goods, the many forms of theft, restitution, Catholic social teaching on work and the economy, and love for the poor.",discuss:["Which forms of theft in § 2409 are most common in South Africa (corruption, tender fraud, wage theft, \"borrowed\" goods)?","What would a just wage look like for domestic workers and farm workers?","How do the works of mercy (§ 2447) apply to students?"],act:"Learn the seven corporal and seven spiritual works of mercy. As a group, choose one corporal work to do together this month."},
 saints:[["St John Chrysostom (Homily on Lazarus 2.5)","Not to enable the poor to share in our goods is to steal from them and deprive them of life. The goods we possess are not ours, but theirs."],["St Gregory the Great (Pastoral Rule III.21)","When we attend to the needs of those in want, we give them what is theirs, not ours. More than performing works of mercy, we are paying a debt of justice."]],
 more:[CQ("What is the \"universal destination of goods\"?",["Everything belongs to the state","God gave the earth to all mankind; private property serves that original gift","All property is theft"],1,"§§ 2402–2403."),CQ("When does gambling become morally unacceptable?",["Always","When it deprives someone of what is necessary for his needs or others'","Never"],1,"§ 2413."),CQ("Does the Church accept the absolute primacy of the market?",["Yes","No","Only in developed countries"],1,"§ 2425.")],psyNote:"§ 2413's warning that gambling \"risks becoming an enslavement\" matches the clinical picture: gambling disorder is classified in DSM-5-TR alongside substance-related disorders because it activates similar reward pathways. CBT has the strongest evidence among treatments (Petry, Ginley & Rash 2017, Psychology of Addictive Behaviors, review). In South Africa, the National Responsible Gambling Programme offers free counselling (0800 006 008)."});
cccD("c87",{paras:[["2464–2470","\"You shall not bear false witness against your neighbour.\" The eighth commandment forbids misrepresenting the truth in our relations with others. Living in the truth: the Old Testament attests that God is the source of all truth. In Jesus Christ the whole of God's truth has been made manifest: \"I am the truth.\" Truthfulness is the virtue which consists in showing oneself true in deeds and truthful in words, and guarding against duplicity, dissimulation and hypocrisy. Men could not live with one another if there were not mutual confidence that they were being truthful. Truthfulness keeps to the just mean between what ought to be expressed and what ought to be kept secret: it entails honesty and discretion."],["2471–2474","Bearing witness to the truth: before Pilate, Christ proclaims that he \"has come into the world, to bear witness to the truth\". The Christian is not to \"be ashamed of testifying to our Lord\". Martyrdom is the supreme witness given to the truth of the faith: a witness even unto death. The Church has painstakingly collected the records of those who persevered to the end in witnessing to their faith: the acts of the martyrs."],["2475–2487","Offences against truth: false witness and perjury (a lie under oath) are particularly grave in court. Respect for the reputation of persons forbids rash judgement (assuming as true, without sufficient foundation, the moral fault of a neighbour), detraction (disclosing another's faults and failings to persons who did not know them, without objectively valid reason), and calumny (remarks contrary to the truth which harm the reputation of others). Every disciple should interpret a neighbour's words and deeds in a favourable way if possible (St Ignatius). Flattery, adulation or complaisance are forbidden when they encourage and confirm another in malicious acts. Boasting or bragging is an offence against truth; so is irony aimed at disparaging someone. Lying is the most direct offence against the truth: \"speaking a falsehood with the intention of deceiving\". By its very nature it is to be condemned. The gravity of a lie is measured by the nature of the truth it deforms, the circumstances, the intentions and the harm suffered by its victims. Every offence against justice and truth entails the duty of reparation, even if its author has been forgiven."],["2488–2492","Respect for the truth: the right to the communication of the truth is not unconditional. Charity and respect for the truth should dictate the response to every request for information. No one is bound to reveal the truth to someone who does not have the right to know it. The sacramental seal is inviolable. Professional secrets (of politicians, soldiers, doctors, lawyers) or confidential information given under the seal of secrecy must be kept, except in exceptional cases where keeping the secret is bound to cause very grave harm to the one who confided it, to the one who received it, or to a third party. Everyone should observe an appropriate reserve concerning persons' private lives."],["2493–2513","Use of the social communications media: information through the media is at the service of the common good; society has a right to information based on truth, freedom, justice and solidarity. Media users should practise moderation and discipline. Journalists should not offend against charity in disseminating information, and should avoid defamation. Truth, beauty and sacred art: truth is beautiful in itself. Art is a distinctively human form of expression; it bears a certain likeness to God's activity in creation. Sacred art is true and beautiful when its form corresponds to its particular vocation: evoking and glorifying, in faith and adoration, the transcendent mystery of God."]],
 mis:[["Only big lies matter.","Lying is by its nature to be condemned; gravity varies with the truth deformed, intent and harm (§§ 2484–2485)."],["Gossip is fine if what I say is true.","Disclosing someone's faults without valid reason is detraction, a sin even when true (§ 2477)."],["Keeping a confidence is always lying by omission.","No one is bound to reveal truth to someone without a right to know; professional secrets and confidences must generally be kept (§§ 2489–2491)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants grow in truthfulness and understand witness, the offences against truth and reputation (including detraction and calumny), confidentiality, and the ethics of media.",discuss:["What is the difference between detraction and calumny? Which is more common in WhatsApp groups?","How do Ignatius's words (\"interpret favourably\") change how we read others' posts?","When is keeping a secret right, and when must it be broken (§ 2491)?"],act:"A one-week \"speech fast\": no gossip, no exaggeration, no sarcasm aimed at others. At the next session, share what was difficult and what changed."},
 saints:[["St Augustine (On Lying 4.5)","A lie consists in speaking a falsehood with the intention of deceiving."],["St Ignatius of Loyola (Spiritual Exercises 22)","Every good Christian ought to be more ready to give a favourable interpretation to another's statement than to condemn it."],["St Ignatius of Antioch (Letter to the Romans 4.1)","Let me be food for the wild beasts, through whom I can reach God. I am God's wheat."]],
 more:[CQ("What is detraction?",["Telling lies about someone","Disclosing someone's true faults without valid reason to those who did not know them","Praising someone"],1,"§ 2477."),CQ("How does the Catechism (with Augustine) define lying?",["Any false statement","Speaking a falsehood with the intention of deceiving","Not saying everything"],1,"§ 2482."),CQ("Is a person always obliged to reveal the truth to anyone who asks?",["Yes","No; not to someone who has no right to know it","Only to family"],1,"§ 2489.")],psyNote:"Confidentiality in § 2491 closely parallels the ethics of psychologists. The HPCSA's ethical rules for psychology (Annexure 12 to the Ethical Rules of Conduct) require psychologists to keep confidences except where disclosure is required by law or needed to protect someone from serious harm: the same \"exceptional cases\" logic the Catechism uses."});
cccD("c88",{paras:[["2514–2516","St John distinguishes three kinds of covetousness or concupiscence: lust of the flesh, lust of the eyes and pride of life. Following the Catholic catechetical tradition, the ninth commandment forbids carnal concupiscence; the tenth forbids coveting another's goods. Etymologically, \"concupiscence\" can refer to any intense form of human desire; Christian theology has given it the particular meaning of the movement of the sensitive appetite contrary to the operation of reason. St Paul identifies it with the rebellion of the \"flesh\" against the \"spirit\". Concupiscence stems from the disobedience of the first sin; it unsettles man's moral faculties and, without being in itself an offence, inclines man to commit sins. In man, being composed of spirit and body, there already exists a certain tension, a certain struggle of tendencies between \"spirit\" and \"flesh\"."],["2517–2519","Purification of the heart: the heart is the seat of moral personality: \"Out of the heart come evil thoughts, murder, adultery, fornication.\" The struggle against carnal covetousness entails purifying the heart and practising temperance. The \"pure in heart\" are those who have attuned their intellects and wills to the demands of God's holiness, chiefly in three areas: charity, chastity or sexual rectitude, and love of truth and orthodoxy of faith. \"Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God.\" Purity of heart is the precondition of the vision of God; even now it enables us to see according to God, to accept others as \"neighbours\", and to perceive the human body as a temple of the Holy Spirit."],["2520–2527","The battle for purity: Baptism confers purification from all sins, but the baptised must continue to struggle against concupiscence of the flesh and disordered desires. With God's grace he will prevail: by the virtue and gift of chastity, by purity of intention, by purity of vision (discipline of feelings and imagination, refusing complicity in impure thoughts), and by prayer. Purity requires modesty, an integral part of temperance. Modesty protects the intimate centre of the person; it means refusing to unveil what should remain hidden; it is ordered to chastity, guides how one looks at others and behaves toward them, and protects the mystery of persons and their love. Forms of modesty vary from one culture to another, but everywhere it exists as an intuition of the spiritual dignity proper to man. Purity of heart demands a purification of the social climate: of the media, of permissiveness, of a mistaken notion of human freedom."]],
 mis:[["Having a sexual thought or temptation is a sin.","Concupiscence and temptation are not in themselves sins; sin lies in consenting, in \"complicity\" with impure thoughts (§§ 2515, 2520)."],["Modesty is only about women's clothing.","Modesty concerns everyone's gaze, speech, behaviour and dress; it protects the mystery of persons (§§ 2521–2524)."],["Purity of heart is only about sex.","It covers charity, chastity and love of truth (§ 2518)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand concupiscence, purity of heart in its three areas, the means of the battle for purity, and modesty as respect for the mystery of persons.",discuss:["What is the difference between a temptation and a sin?","How does purity of heart help us \"see according to God\" (§ 2519)?","What does modesty look like online (photos, messages, what we view)?"],act:"Custody of the eyes and heart: discuss practical ways to guard what enters through screens and imagination, and pray together the Beatitude \"Blessed are the pure in heart\" as a short daily prayer."},
 saints:[["St Augustine (Sermon on the Mount I.2.8)","The pure in heart are those who have a single, undivided heart: they shall see God."],["St Gregory of Nyssa (On the Beatitudes 6)","The Lord does not say it is blessed to know something about God, but to have God present within oneself."],["St Maria Goretti (to her attacker, 1902, as recorded)","No, it is a sin! God does not want it!"]],
 more:[CQ("Is concupiscence itself a sin?",["Yes","No; it inclines to sin but is not in itself an offence","Only in adults"],1,"§ 2515."),CQ("Which three areas does purity of heart concern?",["Food, drink, sleep","Charity, chastity, love of truth","Prayer, fasting, almsgiving"],1,"§ 2518.")],psyNote:"Clinically, intrusive unwanted sexual or blasphemous thoughts are common, and in obsessive-compulsive disorder they become a source of great distress. The Catechism's distinction between a thought arising and consenting to it is pastorally important: trying hard to suppress a thought tends to make it return more (Wegner's \"white bear\" studies; Wegner et al. 1987, Journal of Personality and Social Psychology). Acceptance-based approaches and ERP help."});
cccD("c89",{paras:[["2534–2537","\"You shall not covet your neighbour's house… or anything that is your neighbour's.\" The tenth commandment unfolds and completes the ninth, which is concerned with concupiscence of the flesh. It forbids coveting the goods of another, as the root of theft, robbery and fraud, which the seventh forbids. \"Lust of the eyes\" leads to the violence and injustice forbidden by the fifth. Cupidity has its origin, like fornication, in the idolatry prohibited by the first three commandments. The tenth commandment concerns the intentions of the heart; with the ninth, it summarises all the precepts of the Law. The sensitive appetite leads us to desire pleasant things we do not have; these desires are good in themselves, but often exceed the limits of reason and drive us to covet unjustly what belongs to another."],["2538–2540","Envy: the tenth commandment requires that envy be banished from the human heart. Envy can lead to the worst crimes: \"through the devil's envy death entered the world\". Envy is a capital sin: it refers to the sadness at the sight of another's goods and the immoderate desire to acquire them for oneself, even unjustly. When it wishes grave harm to a neighbour, it is a mortal sin. St Augustine saw envy as \"the diabolical sin\": from it are born hatred, detraction, calumny, joy caused by the misfortune of a neighbour, and displeasure caused by his prosperity. Envy represents a form of sadness and therefore a refusal of charity. The baptised person should struggle against it by exercising good-will. Envy often comes from pride; the baptised person should train himself to live in humility. \"Would you like to see God glorified by you? Then rejoice in your brother's progress and you will immediately give glory to God\" (Chrysostom)."],["2541–2550","The desires of the Spirit: the economy of law and grace turns men's hearts away from avarice and envy; it initiates them into desire for the Sovereign Good. Christ's faithful have \"crucified the flesh with its passions and desires\". Poverty of heart: Jesus enjoins his disciples to prefer him to everything and everyone, and bids them \"renounce all that they have\". \"Blessed are the poor in spirit\": abandonment to the providence of the Father in heaven frees us from anxiety about tomorrow. Trust in God is a preparation for the blessedness of the poor: they shall see God. \"I want to see God\": desire for true happiness frees man from his immoderate attachment to the goods of this world so that he can find his fulfilment in the vision and beatitude of God. \"There we shall rest and see, we shall see and love, we shall love and praise. Behold what will be at the end without end\" (Augustine, City of God XXII.30)."]],
 mis:[["The tenth commandment only matters if I act on my desire.","It concerns the intentions of the heart themselves; envy and covetousness are sins of the heart (§§ 2534–2535)."],["Wanting nice things is sinful.","Desires for pleasant things are good in themselves; the sin is coveting unjustly what belongs to another (§ 2535)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand covetousness and envy, poverty of heart, and how desire for God frees us from disordered desire.",discuss:["How does social media feed envy and covetousness?","Why does Chrysostom say rejoicing in another's progress glorifies God?","What would \"poverty of heart\" look like in your spending, studies or career plans?"],act:"Gratitude and rejoicing practice: for a week, write down three things you are grateful for each day, and one person whose good fortune you will deliberately rejoice in and pray for."},
 saints:[["St John Chrysostom (Homily on Romans 7.3)","Would you like to see God glorified by you? Then rejoice in your brother's progress and you will immediately give glory to God."],["St Augustine (City of God XXII.30)","There we shall rest and see, we shall see and love, we shall love and praise. Behold what will be at the end without end."],["St Teresa of Avila (\"Nada te turbe\")","Let nothing disturb you… whoever has God lacks nothing; God alone suffices."]],
 more:[CQ("What is envy?",["Wanting to improve oneself","Sadness at another's goods and immoderate desire to acquire them, even unjustly","Admiration"],1,"§ 2539."),CQ("How should the baptised fight envy?",["By avoiding successful people","By good-will and humility","By competing harder"],1,"§ 2540.")],psyNote:"Gratitude interventions, like the gratitude journal suggested here, show small but reliable improvements in wellbeing in meta-analyses (Davis et al. 2016, Journal of Counseling Psychology). Research on social-media use links passive scrolling and upward social comparison to envy and lower wellbeing (Verduyn et al. 2017, Social Issues and Policy Review), giving a modern face to the tenth commandment."});

// ---------- Catechism notes: "Go deeper" layer, Part Four: Christian prayer (§§ 2558–2865) ----------
cccD("c90",{paras:[["2558–2565","\"Great is the mystery of the faith!\" The Church professes it in the Creed, celebrates it in the liturgy, lives it in the commandments, and prays it: \"this mystery, then, requires that the faithful believe in it, that they celebrate it, and that they live from it in a vital and personal relationship with the living and true God\". This relationship is prayer. \"For me, prayer is a surge of the heart; it is a simple look turned toward heaven, it is a cry of recognition and of love, embracing both trial and joy\" (Thérèse). Prayer is the raising of one's mind and heart to God, or the requesting of good things from God. Humility is its foundation: \"we do not know how to pray as we ought.\" Prayer is a gift of God: \"If you knew the gift of God!\" The wonder of prayer is revealed beside the well where we come seeking water: there Christ comes to meet every human being; he seeks us first and asks us for a drink. \"God thirsts that we may thirst for him\" (Augustine). Prayer is covenant: it comes from the heart, the place of decision, of truth, of encounter. Prayer is communion: the life of prayer is the habit of being in the presence of the thrice-holy God and in communion with him."],["2566–2589","The universal call to prayer: man is in search of God. In the Old Testament, prayer is revealed through the covenant: Abraham (whose prayer is expressed first by deeds: he obeys, and then intercedes for Sodom); Jacob wrestling with the angel; Moses, the intercessor and friend of God (\"face to face, as a man speaks to his friend\"); David, the king \"after God's own heart\", and the Temple; Elijah and the prophets; and the Psalms, the masterwork of prayer in the Old Testament, which both nourish and express the prayer of the People of God gathered at the great feasts and the prayer of each person. They remain essential to the prayer of the Church."],["2598–2622","In the fullness of time: the drama of prayer is fully revealed in the Word made flesh. Jesus learned to pray with the words and rhythms of his people; he often prays alone, in solitude, especially before decisive moments (his baptism, the choice of the Twelve, the Transfiguration, his Passion). His prayer is a loving adherence of his human heart to the Father's will. His prayer before the raising of Lazarus begins with thanksgiving. At Gethsemane and on the Cross, his prayer takes up all the troubles of humanity. Jesus teaches us to pray: with a converted heart, with faith, with boldness, with watchfulness, persistence (the importunate friend, the unjust judge) and humility (the Pharisee and the tax collector). Jesus hears prayer: he answers prayer offered in faith. The prayer of the Virgin Mary: at the Annunciation (her fiat), at Cana (her intercession), at the foot of the Cross, and in the Magnificat, the song of the Mother of God and of the Church."]],
 mis:[["Prayer is mainly asking God for things.","Prayer is a living relationship with God: adoration, thanksgiving, contrition and petition, rooted in the heart (§§ 2558–2565)."],["We have to persuade an unwilling God by praying.","God seeks us first and thirsts for us; prayer is our response to his gift (§ 2560)."],["The Psalms are Old Testament and no longer for Christians.","They remain essential to the Church's prayer, and Jesus himself prayed them (§§ 2586–2587)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand prayer as gift, covenant and communion, and learn from the great prayers of Scripture, Jesus and Mary.",discuss:["Which definition of prayer in § 2558–2559 speaks most to you?","What do Abraham, Moses and Elijah teach about intercession?","Why do you think Jesus prayed before every major decision?"],act:"Pray Psalm 23 or Psalm 51 slowly together. Then each person writes their own short prayer in the style of a psalm (praise, lament, trust)."},
 saints:[["St Thérèse of Lisieux (Story of a Soul, Ms C 25r)","For me, prayer is a surge of the heart; it is a simple look turned toward heaven, a cry of recognition and of love, embracing both trial and joy."],["St John Damascene (On the Orthodox Faith III.24)","Prayer is the raising of one's mind and heart to God or the requesting of good things from God."],["St Augustine (Eighty-Three Questions 64.4)","God thirsts that we may thirst for him."]],
 more:[CQ("What is the foundation of prayer, according to § 2559?",["Eloquence","Humility","Knowledge"],1,"§ 2559."),CQ("Which Old Testament book is \"the masterwork of prayer\"?",["Proverbs","Psalms","Genesis"],1,"§ 2585–2589.")]});
cccD("c91",{paras:[["2623–2625","On the day of Pentecost, the Spirit of the promise was poured out on the disciples, \"gathered together in one place\". The Spirit who teaches the Church and recalls all that Jesus said is also the teacher of Christian prayer. In the first community of Jerusalem, believers \"devoted themselves to the apostles' teaching and fellowship, to the breaking of bread and the prayers\". The Church's prayer is founded on the apostolic faith, authenticated by charity, and nourished by the Eucharist."],["2626–2628","Blessing and adoration: blessing expresses the basic movement of Christian prayer: an encounter between God and man. God's gift and man's acceptance of it are united in dialogue. Because God blesses, the human heart can in return bless the One who is the source of every blessing. Adoration is the first attitude of man acknowledging that he is a creature before his Creator. It exalts the greatness of the Lord who made us and the almighty power of the Saviour who sets us free from evil. It is homage of the spirit to the \"King of glory\", respectful silence in the presence of the \"ever greater\" God."],["2629–2636","Petition: the vocabulary of supplication in the New Testament is rich in shades of meaning: ask, beseech, plead, invoke, entreat, cry out, even \"struggle in prayer\". By prayer of petition we express awareness of our relationship with God: as creatures, we are not our own beginning, nor master of adversity, nor our own last end. Asking forgiveness is the first movement of the prayer of petition (the tax collector: \"God, be merciful to me a sinner!\"). Christian petition is centred on the desire and search for the Kingdom to come. Every need can become the object of petition. Intercession is a prayer of petition which leads us to pray as Jesus did; it is characteristic of a heart attuned to God's mercy. Christian intercession participates in Christ's, and knows no boundaries: \"for all men, for kings and all who are in high positions\", for persecutors, for the salvation of those who reject the Gospel."],["2637–2649","Thanksgiving characterises the prayer of the Church which, in celebrating the Eucharist, reveals and becomes more fully what she is. Every event and need can become an offering of thanksgiving: \"Give thanks in all circumstances.\" Praise is the form of prayer which recognises most immediately that God is God. It lauds God for his own sake and gives him glory, quite beyond what he does, but simply because he is. It shares in the blessed happiness of the pure of heart who love God in faith before seeing him in glory. The Eucharist contains and expresses all forms of prayer: it is \"the pure offering\" of the whole Body of Christ to the glory of God's name."]],
 mis:[["Petition is a lower, selfish kind of prayer.","Petition expresses our creaturely dependence; Jesus commanded us to ask, and intercession participates in his own prayer (§§ 2629–2636)."],["Praise and thanksgiving are the same.","Thanksgiving thanks God for what he has done; praise glorifies God simply because he is (§§ 2638–2639)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants can name and practise the five forms of prayer: blessing and adoration, petition, intercession, thanksgiving, praise.",discuss:["Which form of prayer do you use most? Least?","Why does asking for forgiveness come first among petitions?","How does the Eucharist contain all the forms of prayer?"],act:"Teach the ACTS pattern (Adoration, Contrition, Thanksgiving, Supplication). Pray it together for ten minutes, one form at a time, with brief silence between."},
 saints:[["St Paul (1 Thessalonians 5:16–18)","Rejoice always, pray constantly, give thanks in all circumstances."],["St Augustine (Expositions on the Psalms 85.1)","He prays for us as our priest, prays in us as our head, and is prayed to by us as our God."],["St Ignatius of Loyola (Suscipe)","Take, Lord, and receive all my liberty, my memory, my understanding and my entire will."]],
 more:[CQ("Which form of prayer glorifies God simply because he is?",["Petition","Praise","Intercession"],1,"§ 2639."),CQ("What is the first movement of petition?",["Asking for health","Asking forgiveness","Asking for success"],1,"§ 2631.")],psyNote:"Different forms of prayer may affect wellbeing differently. Studies of prayer type suggest that adoration and thanksgiving are associated with greater wellbeing than petition-focused prayer alone (e.g. Whittington & Scher 2010, International Journal for the Psychology of Religion), and gratitude interventions show small but reliable benefits (Davis et al. 2016). Correlational findings like these cannot show that prayer \"works\" psychologically; the Christian reason to pray is God, not mood."});
cccD("c92",{paras:[["2650–2662","At the wellsprings of prayer: prayer cannot be reduced to the spontaneous outpouring of interior impulse; one must also learn how to pray. Through a living transmission (Tradition), the Holy Spirit teaches the children of God to pray. The Word of God: \"prayer should accompany the reading of Sacred Scripture, so that a dialogue takes place between God and man\" (\"We speak to him when we pray; we listen to him when we read the divine oracles\", Ambrose). Lectio divina: \"seek in reading and you will find in meditating; knock in mental prayer and it will be opened to you by contemplation\" (Guigo II). The liturgy of the Church. The theological virtues: entering into prayer is like entering into liturgy, through the narrow gate of faith. \"Today\": we learn to pray at certain moments by hearing the Word, but we pray in the events of each day."],["2663–2682","The way of prayer: prayer to the Father (through Jesus), to Jesus himself: the name of Jesus is at the heart of Christian prayer; the Jesus Prayer (\"Lord Jesus Christ, Son of God, have mercy on me, a sinner\") is a simple invocation that can be prayed constantly. Prayer to the Holy Spirit: \"Come, Holy Spirit.\" In communion with the holy Mother of God: Christian prayer goes to the Father through Christ in the Spirit, and Mary is the one who shows us the way (Hodegetria). The Hail Mary has two movements: magnifying the Lord for what he did in Mary (the angel's and Elizabeth's greetings), and entrusting ourselves to her (\"pray for us sinners, now and at the hour of our death\"). Medieval piety in the West developed the Rosary as a popular substitute for the Liturgy of the Hours."],["2683–2696","Guides for prayer: the cloud of witnesses (the saints, who share in the living tradition of prayer by the example of their lives and their prayer for us). Servants of prayer: the Christian family is the first place of education in prayer, the \"domestic church\" where God's children learn to pray daily. Ordained ministers, religious, catechists, prayer groups and spiritual direction. Places favourable for prayer: the church (the proper place for liturgical prayer), a \"prayer corner\" at home with Scripture and icons, monasteries, and pilgrimages."]],
 mis:[["Prayer to Mary and the saints takes away from prayer to God.","Christian prayer goes to the Father through Christ in the Spirit; Mary and the saints pray with us and for us, and lead us to Christ (§§ 2673–2683)."],["Real prayer must be spontaneous; set prayers are empty.","Prayer must also be learned; the Church transmits it through Scripture, the liturgy and the saints (§§ 2650–2651)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants learn the sources of prayer (Word, liturgy, virtues, daily life), its way (to the Father, Jesus and the Spirit, with Mary), and its guides and places.",discuss:["What is the difference between reading the Bible for information and praying with it?","How can the name of Jesus be a prayer in itself?","Where is the \"prayer corner\" in your home, or where could it be?"],act:"Practise lectio divina together with Luke 10:38–42 (Martha and Mary): read (lectio), reflect (meditatio), respond (oratio), rest (contemplatio). Close with one Hail Mary."},
 saints:[["St Ambrose (On the Duties of Ministers I.20.88)","We speak to him when we pray; we listen to him when we read the divine oracles."],["Guigo II the Carthusian (The Ladder of Monks 2)","Seek in reading and you will find in meditating; knock in mental prayer and it will be opened to you by contemplation."],["The Way of a Pilgrim (19th century, Russian)","Lord Jesus Christ, Son of God, have mercy on me, a sinner."]],
 more:[CQ("What are the four steps of lectio divina?",["Read, meditate, pray, contemplate","Sing, kneel, stand, sit","Fast, give, pray, confess"],0,"§ 2654 (with Guigo II)."),CQ("What are the two movements of the Hail Mary?",["Praise and petition","Magnifying the Lord for Mary, and entrusting ourselves to her","Confession and absolution"],1,"§§ 2675–2677.")]});
cccD("c93",{paras:[["2697–2699","Prayer is the life of the new heart. It ought to animate us at every moment. But we tend to forget him who is our life and our all. \"We must remember God more often than we draw breath\" (Gregory of Nazianzus). We cannot pray \"at all times\" if we do not pray at specific times, consciously willing it. The Church proposes certain rhythms of praying intended to nourish continual prayer: morning and evening prayer, grace before and after meals, the Liturgy of the Hours; Sundays centred on the Eucharist; the cycle of the liturgical year. The Christian tradition has retained three major expressions of prayer: vocal, meditative and contemplative. One thing is common to them: composure of heart."],["2700–2704","Vocal prayer: through his Word God speaks to man; by words, mental or vocal, our prayer takes flesh. Most important is that the heart be present to him to whom we are speaking. Jesus prayed vocally (the Our Father, his great priestly prayer). Vocal prayer is an essential element of the Christian life; it associates the body with interior prayer, and corresponds to our human nature. It is the prayer par excellence of crowds, and it can become an initial form of contemplative prayer."],["2705–2708","Meditation is above all a quest. The mind seeks to understand the why and how of the Christian life, in order to adhere and respond to what the Lord is asking. Helps: Scripture (especially the Gospels), holy icons, liturgical texts, the writings of the spiritual fathers, works of spirituality, the great book of creation and of history. To meditate on what we read helps us to make it our own by confronting it with ourselves. Methods of meditation are as diverse as spiritual masters; a method is only a guide, the important thing is to advance, with the Holy Spirit, along the one way of prayer: Christ Jesus. Meditation engages thought, imagination, emotion and desire, to deepen our convictions of faith, prompt conversion of heart, and strengthen our will to follow Christ. Christian prayer tries above all to meditate on the mysteries of Christ, as in lectio divina or the Rosary."],["2709–2724","Contemplative prayer: \"Contemplative prayer is nothing else than a close sharing between friends; it means taking time frequently to be alone with him who we know loves us\" (Teresa of Avila). Contemplative prayer seeks him \"whom my soul loves\". One does not undertake contemplative prayer only when one has the time: one makes time for the Lord, with the firm determination not to give up, no matter what trials and dryness one may encounter. Entering into it is like entering into the Eucharistic liturgy: we gather up the heart, recollect our whole being under the prompting of the Spirit. It is a gaze of faith fixed on Jesus: \"I look at him and he looks at me\" (the peasant of Ars, to St John Vianney). It is hearing the Word of God, silence, the \"symbol of the world to come\". It is a union with the prayer of Christ insofar as it makes us participate in his mystery; it is a communion of love bearing life for the multitude, a covenant relationship established by God within our hearts."]],
 mis:[["Vocal prayer is for beginners; real Christians only meditate.","Vocal prayer is essential; Jesus prayed vocally, and it can become contemplative (§§ 2700–2704)."],["Christian meditation is the same as emptying the mind.","Christian meditation engages thought, imagination and desire on the mysteries of Christ; contemplation is a loving gaze on a Person (§§ 2708, 2715)."],["Dryness in prayer means you're doing it wrong.","One makes time for the Lord whatever trials and dryness one encounters (§ 2710)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand and practise the three expressions of prayer: vocal, meditation and contemplation, and commit to a daily rhythm.",discuss:["What daily rhythm of prayer would be realistic for you (morning offering, grace, Angelus, night examen)?","How is Christian meditation different from mindfulness or Eastern meditation?","What does \"I look at him and he looks at me\" teach about contemplation?"],act:"Ten minutes of silent prayer before the Blessed Sacrament (or a crucifix): begin with a short vocal prayer, read a few verses of a Gospel, then simply remain with Jesus."},
 saints:[["St Teresa of Avila (Life 8.5)","Contemplative prayer, in my opinion, is nothing else than a close sharing between friends; it means taking time frequently to be alone with him who we know loves us."],["A peasant of Ars, to St John Vianney","I look at him and he looks at me."],["St Gregory of Nazianzus (Oration 27.4)","We must remember God more often than we draw breath."]],
 more:[CQ("What are the three major expressions of prayer?",["Morning, noon, night","Vocal, meditation, contemplation","Liturgy, Rosary, Adoration"],1,"§ 2699."),CQ("How did St Teresa define contemplative prayer?",["Emptying the mind","A close sharing between friends","Reading theology"],1,"§ 2709.")],psyNote:"Mindfulness-based interventions show moderate effects on anxiety, depression and stress (Goldberg et al. 2018, Clinical Psychology Review, meta-analysis). Christian contemplation shares practices with mindfulness (attention, stillness, non-judgemental awareness) but differs in its object: not awareness for its own sake but a relationship with a Person. Christian-accommodated versions of these programmes have also been developed and studied."});
cccD("c94",{paras:[["2725–2728","Prayer is both a gift of grace and a determined response on our part. It always presupposes effort. The great figures of prayer of the Old Covenant before Christ, as well as the Mother of God, the saints and Jesus himself, all teach us this: prayer is a battle. Against whom? Against ourselves and against the wiles of the tempter who does all he can to turn man away from prayer. We pray as we live, because we live as we pray. Objections to prayer: erroneous notions (prayer as psychological activity, or effort of concentration to reach a mental void, or ritual words and postures); mentalities of \"this world\" (only what is verifiable is true; productivity; sensuality and comfort; activism that sees prayer as a flight from the world); and discouragement during periods of dryness, sadness that we have not given everything, disappointment over not being heard according to our own will, wounded pride, our resistance to the idea that prayer is a free and unmerited gift."],["2729–2733","Humble vigilance of heart: the habitual difficulty in prayer is distraction. To set about hunting down distractions would be to fall into their trap, when all that is necessary is to turn back to our heart: a distraction reveals to us what we are attached to, and this humble awareness before the Lord should awaken our preferential love for him. Another difficulty, especially for those sincerely wanting to pray, is dryness: when the heart is separated from God, with no taste for thoughts, memories and feelings, even spiritual ones. This is the moment of sheer faith clinging faithfully to Jesus in his agony and in his tomb. \"Unless a grain of wheat falls into the earth and dies… it bears much fruit.\" The most common, yet most hidden, temptation is our lack of faith, revealed by our actual preferences. Acedia: a form of depression stemming from lax ascetical practice, decreasing vigilance and carelessness of heart. \"The spirit indeed is willing, but the flesh is weak.\""],["2734–2741","Filial trust: filial trust is tested when we feel that our prayer is not always heard. Why do we complain of not being heard? \"You ask and do not receive, because you ask wrongly.\" If we ask with a divided heart, God cannot answer. Our prayer is efficacious because it is united in faith with that of Jesus. \"Do not be troubled if you do not immediately receive from God what you ask him; for he desires to do something even greater for you, while you cling to him in prayer\" (Evagrius). \"God wills that our desire should be exercised in prayer, that we may be able to receive what he is prepared to give\" (Augustine)."],["2742–2758","Persevering in love: \"Pray constantly… always and for everything giving thanks.\" \"It is possible to offer fervent prayer even while walking in public or strolling alone, or seated in your shop… or while busy at your cooking\" (Chrysostom). Prayer and Christian life are inseparable, for they concern the same love and the same renunciation. \"He 'prays without ceasing' who unites prayer to works and good works to prayer\" (Origen). The prayer of the hour of Jesus: the priestly prayer of John 17, spoken at the hour of his Passover, sums up the whole economy of creation and salvation, and inspires the great petitions of the Our Father."]],
 mis:[["If prayer is hard, I'm not meant to pray.","Prayer is a battle for everyone, including the saints and Jesus himself (§ 2725)."],["Distractions ruin prayer; I must eliminate them first.","Hunting distractions is a trap; turning back to the Lord is enough, and distractions show what we are attached to (§ 2729)."],["Unanswered prayer means God isn't listening.","God may wish to give something greater; we must ask with an undivided heart, in union with Jesus' prayer (§§ 2735–2741)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand prayer as a battle, recognise the objections and difficulties (distraction, dryness, acedia, unanswered prayer), and resolve to persevere.",discuss:["Which objection to prayer in § 2727 do you hear most from friends?","How do you usually deal with distractions? What does § 2729 suggest instead?","Have you experienced \"unanswered\" prayer? How does § 2737 help?"],act:"Prayer battle plan: name your biggest obstacle (time, distraction, dryness, phone), and choose one concrete remedy (a fixed time, a set place, the phone in another room, a short prayer when you notice distraction). Review in two weeks."},
 saints:[["Evagrius Ponticus (On Prayer 34)","Do not be troubled if you do not immediately receive from God what you ask him; for he desires to do something even greater for you, while you cling to him in prayer."],["St John Chrysostom (Ecloga de oratione 2)","It is possible to offer fervent prayer even while walking in public or strolling alone, or seated in your shop, while buying or selling, or even while cooking."],["St Augustine (Letter 130, to Proba, 8.17)","God wills that our desire should be exercised in prayer, that we may be able to receive what he is prepared to give."]],
 more:[CQ("What is the habitual difficulty in prayer?",["Lack of words","Distraction","Lack of time"],1,"§ 2729."),CQ("What is acedia?",["A form of spiritual depression from lax practice and carelessness of heart","A kind of prayer","Fasting"],0,"§ 2733.")],psyNote:"§ 2733 calls acedia \"a form of depression\" stemming from spiritual laxity. Clinically it is important not to confuse the two: acedia is a spiritual condition of the will, whereas major depressive disorder (DSM-5-TR) is an illness with low mood or loss of interest for at least two weeks plus symptoms such as sleep and appetite change, guilt, and difficulty concentrating. Depression can make prayer feel dry or impossible; it is not a spiritual failure and calls for proper treatment. Spiritual direction and clinical care can work together."});
cccD("c95",{paras:[["2759–2764","\"He was praying at a certain place, and when he ceased, one of his disciples said to him, 'Lord, teach us to pray.'\" In response, the Lord entrusts the fundamental Christian prayer, the Our Father. Luke gives five petitions; Matthew gives seven, the version the liturgical tradition of the Church has retained. The Lord's Prayer is \"truly the summary of the whole gospel\" (Tertullian); \"the most perfect of prayers\" (Aquinas). It is at the centre of the Scriptures: the Sermon on the Mount is teaching for life, the Our Father is prayer; in both the Spirit gives new form to our desires."],["2765–2776","\"The Lord's Prayer\": it comes to us from the Lord Jesus, the master and model of our prayer. He gives us both the words of his own filial prayer and the Spirit by whom these become \"spirit and life\" in us. The prayer of the Church: it is an integral part of the major hours of the Divine Office and of the sacraments of Christian initiation (Baptism, Confirmation, Eucharist). In the Eucharistic liturgy it sums up the anaphora's intercessions and knocks at the door of the Banquet of the Kingdom. Its eschatological character: it is the prayer of the \"last days\", awaiting the Lord's return."],["2777–2785","\"We dare to say\": in the Roman liturgy the Eucharistic assembly is invited to pray with filial boldness (parrhesia): straightforward simplicity, filial trust, joyous assurance, humble boldness, the certainty of being loved. \"Abba, Father\": we can invoke God as \"Father\" because he is revealed to us by his Son become man and because his Spirit makes him known to us. Praying to our Father should develop in us two fundamental dispositions: the desire to become like him, and a humble and trusting heart. \"When we pray to the Father, we are in communion with him and with his Son, Jesus Christ.\" The Fatherhood of God surpasses human fatherhood (and motherhood), though it is their origin and standard: \"no one is father as God is Father\". \"We must remember… and know that when we call God 'our Father' we ought to behave as sons of God\" (Cyprian)."],["2786–2802","\"Our\" Father: \"our\" expresses not possession but an entirely new relationship with God: the New Covenant in Jesus Christ. It is the prayer of the whole Church; it leaves no room for individualism; it expresses the baptised's common prayer, and calls for the communion of all Christians. \"Who art in heaven\": this biblical expression does not mean a place (\"space\") but a way of being; it refers not to God's distance but to his majesty. \"Heaven\" is also where believers find their true homeland: \"our commonwealth is in heaven.\""]],
 mis:[["The Our Father is just a children's prayer.","It is \"the summary of the whole Gospel\" and \"the most perfect of prayers\" (§§ 2761, 2763)."],["\"Heaven\" in the Our Father means God is far away in the sky.","It refers to God's majesty and way of being, not a distant place (§ 2794)."],["Calling God \"Father\" means God is male.","God transcends human fatherhood and motherhood; he is their origin and standard (§§ 239, 2779)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand the place of the Lord's Prayer in the Gospel and the liturgy, and the meaning of its address: \"Our Father, who art in heaven\".",discuss:["What does Tertullian mean by calling the Our Father \"the summary of the whole gospel\"?","What changes when we pray \"our\" instead of \"my\"?","How might someone with a difficult earthly father approach praying to God as Father (§ 2779)?"],act:"Pray the Our Father very slowly together, pausing after each phrase for 30 seconds of silence. Afterwards, share which phrase struck each person."},
 saints:[["Tertullian (On Prayer 1)","The Lord's Prayer is truly the summary of the whole gospel."],["St Thomas Aquinas (ST II-II q.83 a.9)","The Lord's Prayer is the most perfect of prayers… in it we ask not only for all the things we can rightly desire, but also in the sequence that they should be desired."],["St Cyprian (On the Lord's Prayer 11)","We must remember… that when we call God 'our Father' we ought to behave as sons of God."]],
 more:[CQ("Which Gospel's version of the Our Father has the liturgy retained?",["Luke (five petitions)","Matthew (seven petitions)","John"],1,"§ 2759."),CQ("What does \"who art in heaven\" refer to?",["A place in the sky","God's majesty and way of being","The angels"],1,"§ 2794.")],psyNote:"Research on attachment to God suggests that people often relate to God in ways that mirror, or compensate for, their attachment to their parents (Granqvist & Kirkpatrick; Granqvist 2020, Attachment in Religion and Spirituality). For someone wounded by an earthly father, § 2779's call to purify our images of fatherhood is pastorally important, and therapy and spiritual direction can help to heal both."});
cccD("c96",{paras:[["2803–2806","After placing ourselves in the presence of God our Father to adore, love and bless him, the Spirit of adoption stirs up in our hearts seven petitions. The first three, more theological, draw us toward the glory of the Father; the last four, as ways toward him, commend our wretchedness to his grace. \"Deep calls to deep.\" The first series carries us toward him, for his own sake: thy name, thy kingdom, thy will. The second series offers him our expectations: they concern our life, to nourish it or heal it of sin, and our struggle for the victory of good over evil."],["2807–2827","\"Hallowed be thy name\": not that we make God holy, but that we recognise his name as holy and that it be hallowed in us and by us, in our lives and actions. \"Thy kingdom come\": the Kingdom of God has been coming since the Last Supper and in the Eucharist; we pray for its final coming with Christ's return, while working for justice and peace now. It does not divert the Church from her mission in the present world but commits her to it. \"Thy will be done on earth as it is in heaven\": the Father's will is \"that all men be saved and come to the knowledge of the truth\"; we ask that our will be united to the Son's, as in Gethsemane: \"not my will, but thine, be done.\""],["2828–2837","\"Give us this day our daily bread\": the trust of children who look to their Father for everything; and the bread of the Word and the Body of Christ. The petition carries a responsibility: the drama of hunger in the world calls Christians to effective solidarity. \"Pray as if everything depended on God and work as if everything depended on you\" (attributed to St Ignatius). \"Daily\" (Greek epiousios, found nowhere else) means \"today\", \"what is necessary\", and \"super-essential\": the Eucharist."],["2838–2845","\"And forgive us our trespasses, as we forgive those who trespass against us\": this petition is astonishing. The outpouring of mercy cannot penetrate our hearts as long as we have not forgiven those who have trespassed against us. It is not in our power not to feel or to forget an offence; but the heart that offers itself to the Holy Spirit turns injury into compassion and purifies the memory by transforming the hurt into intercession. Forgiveness is the high point of Christian prayer; it bears witness that, in our world, love is stronger than sin."],["2846–2865","\"And lead us not into temptation\": we ask God not to allow us to take the way that leads to sin, and to give us the Spirit of discernment and strength. God tempts no one; we ask not to give in to temptation. \"But deliver us from evil\": evil is not an abstraction but refers to a person, Satan, the Evil One, the angel who opposes God. Victory over the \"prince of this world\" was won once for all at the hour when Jesus freely gave himself up to death. In this final petition, the Church brings before the Father all the distress of the world. The final doxology (\"for the kingdom, the power and the glory are yours\") takes up the first three petitions. \"Amen\": \"So be it\", ratifying what is contained in the prayer God taught us."]],
 mis:[["\"Lead us not into temptation\" implies God tempts us.","God tempts no one; we ask not to enter into or give in to temptation (§ 2846). (Some translations now say \"do not let us fall into temptation\".)"],["Forgiving means feeling fine about the hurt or forgetting it.","It is not in our power not to feel or forget an offence; forgiveness turns the hurt into compassion and intercession (§ 2843)."],["The doxology (\"for thine is the kingdom\") is a Protestant addition.","It is an ancient liturgical ending (Didache 8.2) used in the Mass after the embolism (§ 2855)."]],
 teach:{aim:"Participants understand each of the seven petitions and the doxology, and pray the Our Father more consciously.",discuss:["Which petition is hardest for you to pray honestly? Why?","How can we forgive without pretending the hurt didn't happen (§ 2843)?","What responsibility does \"give us this day our daily bread\" give us toward the hungry?"],act:"Petition by petition: assign each group one petition. They read its Catechism paragraphs, summarise its meaning in one sentence, and suggest one way to live it this week. Close by praying the Our Father together, slowly."},
 saints:[["St Augustine (Letter 130, to Proba, 12.22)","Run through all the words of the holy petitions, and I do not think you will find anything in them that is not contained and included in the Lord's Prayer."],["St Cyprian (On the Lord's Prayer 23)","God does not accept the sacrifice of a sower of disunion… our peace and brotherly concord is the greater sacrifice to God."],["St Ambrose (On the Sacraments V.4.30)","The Lord who has taken away your sin and pardoned your faults also protects you and keeps you from the wiles of your adversary the devil."]],
 more:[CQ("How many petitions does the Our Father have (Matthew's version)?",["Five","Seven","Ten"],1,"§ 2803."),CQ("What does \"evil\" refer to in the last petition?",["Bad luck","A person, Satan, the Evil One","Sickness only"],1,"§ 2851."),CQ("What is the meaning of \"Amen\"?",["Goodbye","So be it","Holy"],1,"§ 2856.")],psyNote:"§ 2843 matches what forgiveness research distinguishes: \"decisional\" forgiveness (choosing to release revenge) and \"emotional\" forgiveness (replacing negative emotions over time) (Worthington 2006). Forgiveness interventions show moderate benefits for depression, anxiety and hope (Wade et al. 2014, meta-analysis). Forgiveness is not reconciliation, nor staying in an unsafe situation."});

;PACK_DONE("ccc");
